window.DATA={"items":[{"kind":"lesson","title":"WATCH THIS FIRST!","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"How the Dropshipping Inner Circle course is organized, and how to choose the learning style that works for you.","points":["The full ~18-hour module is also split into separate sections (Intro, Logistics, Website, Marketing, Product Selection, Scaling). It's the same content either way.","Watch the whole thing straight through, or go section by section if you prefer things more organized. You don't need to watch both.","Time per section reflects importance: Intro is short (~5 min), Logistics ~1 hour, Website ~4 hours, Marketing ~6 hours, Product Selection is shorter, and Scaling is ~2 hours (about 22 hours total).","The sections that make the money (website and marketing) are the longest, so give them the most attention.","Modules are kept as tight as possible to save you time and energy, and they're updated regularly."],"topics":["Getting started","Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_E3WlyuZhNB9Mm/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"WATCH THIS NEXT","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"What the Dropshipping Inner Circle is and where it fits in your learning path.","points":["Dropshipping is a fulfillment model (how orders get shipped), not a business model on its own.","These are the advanced dropshipping modules, and everything is taught in a way that ties into MEO (multi-store e-commerce).","Make sure you understand the beginner material first, then complete this Inner Circle section.","Only after finishing here should you move on to MEO, which covers scaling, advanced tactics and exiting (selling) your store.","Module requests are welcome."],"topics":["Getting started","Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_yjmeETPcDWT60/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to be organized before you start!","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"Organization z1. Organization z1. What this video covers: How to stay organized while working through the advanced modules so you actually apply everything.","points":["There are two ways to work: take action as you watch, or take notes first and act after. Either is fine as long as you take action.","If you act as you go: at the end of each category (Website, Product, Marketing, etc.), go back through every module and check that your store has what each one says.","If you take notes first: make sure you have notes on every module in the category before you start implementing.","Taking notes is recommended either way.","Don't skip or skim. Small tips in these videos can save you time, money and mistakes, and can help scale your store."],"topics":["Getting started"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_rBmb77MPDYlah/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Looking at comp to maximize website","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"A teardown of a successful competitor's website to show what makes a store convert, and how to borrow those ideas for your own.","points":["Study competitors' sites, not just your own. Successful stores show what customers respond to.","Value up front: a welcome discount offer, plus small discounts on \"hot\" products to create urgency. Focus discounts on your most profitable products.","Large catalogs across many niches only work once you've already blown up. When starting, keep the product count small (this store has only 29).","Keep the design clean: a simple white background, no crazy colors.","Trust builders: customer contact info, a thorough FAQ (~13 questions), an order tracking page, and clear shipping, refund and privacy policies.","Set expectations: show delivery times and a return policy (use the supplier's terms if they offer them).","Selling the same products on TikTok Shop can be a big extra revenue stream.","Match your design to your audience: this site suits an older (40–65) audience, while younger audiences need a more modern, organized look.","Organic marketing kept this store growing. They still post a lot even after blowing up."],"topics":["Product research","Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_YVnRRRHdu8W7Q/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Abandoned cart. (SYSTEM)","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"What an abandoned cart is and how to set up an automated recovery email that brings shoppers back.","points":["An abandoned cart (a \"soft order\") happens when someone likes the product, adds it to cart, then leaves. It's usually shipping cost, price, or simply getting distracted, not because they don't want it.","Never assume they lost interest. Many just forgot, so a reminder recovers real sales.","Automate it with Shopify Flow/workflows, Klaviyo or Postscript so the email goes out automatically when a cart is abandoned.","Don't use a generic AI email. Write casually, like a person (\"We see you left your cart hanging\"), not stiff and corporate.","The winning formula is an offer plus scarcity: include a coupon code (the offer), then a real time limit to act on it (the scarcity).","Keep the window realistic. 10–15 minutes works. 30 seconds is too short to act on, and a long window removes the urgency. Make sure the coupon actually expires when you say it does.","Done consistently, this raises your store's overall conversion rate.","Tip: keep your notes in Google Docs."],"topics":["Email & SMS"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_NY8sidFzhRZgO/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Sales Funnel","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"A step-by-step breakdown of the customer journey (sales funnel) from first seeing your product to checkout, and what your store needs at each stage.","points":["Browsing: customers find you via Google, Instagram, TikTok, Facebook, Pinterest, YouTube and so on, then land on your landing page.","Landing page must-haves: a pop-up that collects contact info (email or phone, not both, to keep it light) so you can follow up, plus an urgency notice in a banner (e.g. sale ending soon, limited-time offer, recent purchases). Most people won't buy on their first visit.","Potential Initial Product (PI): shoppers often aren't sure which product they want, so offer a few options. Start with 1 proven winner or 3–4 products, and no more than ~5. Too many choices leads to no choice.","Add to cart, then view cart: most people browse more before checking out, so make the cart easy to review.","Potential Upsell (PU): suggest complementary products at checkout, ideally with a discount (e.g. a matching beanie with a hoodie, or a buy-one-get-one deal) to raise order value.","Checkout: shipping and payment info, then order confirmation. Use Shopify (or Wix/Weebly) so failed payments can be retried.","Why people don't check out: shipping cost, second thoughts on spending, comparison shopping, or simply forgetting (the most common).","That's why collecting contact info is critical: it lets you bring non-buyers back with follow-up flows (covered in later modules).","Reach out to the coaches if anything is unclear."],"topics":["Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_469QtIvcbtlzhvhOX5w7HP/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to find font for logo","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"How to find the right font for your logo and brand, and how branding differs for dropshipping stores vs. clothing brands.","points":["Browse a free font library (like DaFont) by category (old school, Western, groovy, distorted, etc.) and download fonts that fit your vibe.","Match the font to your niche: clean and professional for fitness or professional products, more expressive (vintage, distressed, blurred) for streetwear.","Always check a font's license before using it commercially.","Prioritize readability. A cool font that's hard to read won't stick in people's minds.","Dropshipping stores: a simple, professional logo is enough (AI-made is fine). Use it on your profile picture, website and emails. The goal is a brand image so the store doesn't look like a generic dropshipping site, which builds trust.","Clothing brands: invest more in a unique logo and put it everywhere. It should be seen, not hidden.","Test print quality with your supplier, since detailed effects (like blur) may not print well."],"topics":["Website","Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_3vYUyiVU2StZi2nqQw5hXM/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"A good website from mentee!","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"A review of a high-converting mentee website (a hair care brand), showing what makes shoppers trust it and buy.","points":["Show how you're different: a comparison section (\"Our product vs. Others\") with checkmarks. Don't name specific competitor brands, which could create legal issues.","Explain what it does and why it's different. Those two things win people over.","Show what it doesn't contain as well as what it does (e.g. free of certain ingredients), plus the ingredients and the science behind them.","Testimonials and results throughout the page build trust fast.","Trust signals: About Us, Contact Us, FAQ, easy returns, and messaging that shows where the brand is based (shoppers feel safer about delivery).","Product page: strong images (including one listing everything it includes), how-to-use instructions in the description, and clear return info.","A site built like this converts well, especially once you start running ads. Aim for this standard whatever your product."],"topics":["Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_rtjyBRdZ78xPX/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"NURTURE CUSTOMERS","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"Why your website should nurture customers (build desire and trust) before pushing the product, shown through a skincare brand's site.","points":["The hero section shows the logo and a real person using the product in a bathroom, so visitors picture it in their own home, rather than a plain product shot.","Sell a feeling, not a product: the headline is about \"confidence,\" not \"buy our serum.\"","Trust stack as you scroll: \"as featured in\" logos, best sellers, then testimonials.","Testimonials should match your target audience. This brand targets older customers, so its reviewers are older people describing results that matter to them.","Mix product images ~50/50 with photos of people using the product.","The Shop Now button can be small. The page's job is to show why buying benefits the customer, with science, results and an About section.","Brands often push their highest-profit product, not necessarily their best seller.","You don't have to copy this layout, but your site should nurture visitors on why your product is worth it."],"topics":["Website","Email & SMS"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_MrKSRU6ykBhEy/"},{"kind":"pdf","title":"Best suppliers","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"A quick list of the go-to suppliers for dropshipping.","points":["Zendrop","AutoDS","CJ Dropshipping","Alibaba (best for private suppliers and custom or bulk orders)"],"topics":["Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_Pn1jQ5YKPJjW3/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Reviews","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"Why you should add a review app (Judge.me) to your Shopify store and how it helps sales.","points":["Judge.me collects reviews mainly from verified buyers, so ratings come from real customers.","Good reviews build trust passively and raise your click-through and conversion rates, so make sure the product itself is good.","Display reviews with eye-catching widgets and testimonial sliders on your site.","Encourage reviews and repeat sales by sending a coupon or referral reward for leaving one (ask for honest reviews, not just 5 stars).","Sync reviews to Google and Meta so they show up when people search for you.","Reviews double as feedback: customers will tell you what to improve or add (e.g. a new color), which helps you build better products."],"topics":["Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_ZHRfJEUJy7OyH/"},{"kind":"pdf","title":"Meaning of stuff","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"A glossary of the ecommerce and ad terms used throughout the course, in plain English.","points":["CTR (click-through rate), CPA (cost per acquisition) and ROAS (return on ad spend), with a worked example: spend $100, make $500 = 5x ROAS","AOV (average order value), conversion rate and upsells","Front end vs. back end: the offer that gets the first sale vs. the money made after it through emails and subscriptions","Fulfillment, chargebacks, scaling, organic traffic, LTV (lifetime value) and KPIs"],"topics":[],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_mpa0NoTHHMMps/","also":["Clothing Brand"]},{"kind":"lesson","title":"ADD THESE APPS!","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"The essential Shopify apps to add to your store, what each one does, and when to add them.","points":["Your store is more than a storefront. The right apps (shipping, marketing, engagement, upsells, reviews, optimization) separate stagnant stores from growing ones. Add them from the Apps section in Shopify.","Klaviyo: email and SMS automation flows. Free up to 250 contacts.","Postscript: SMS marketing with extras like gifts to make customers feel welcome.","Yotpo: loyalty, rewards and referral programs for repeat purchases (free under a certain order volume).","Loox: automatically requests photo and video reviews after purchase.","Session-recording/analytics apps: see what customers actually do on your site.","Gorgias: all-in-one customer support help desk. Wait until you've scaled; a simple contact page works at first.","Upsell and bundle apps (AI product recommendations, Wiz, UpCart): raise average order value. Add these once you have more products; start by focusing on selling.","Marketplace Connect: list your products on Amazon and other marketplaces.","PageFly (no-code landing pages) and Tapcart (turn your store into a mobile app): for later, as you grow.","Shopify Flow (free): no-code automations (trigger → condition → action) like restock alerts, follow-up emails, or pausing ads when inventory runs low.","Vitals: an all-in-one app (reviews, bundles, urgency, currency). Convenient, but many owners prefer separate apps.","Next steps: find where your store is \"leaking,\" test apps, remove what doesn't help, and double down on what works.","Top picks: Klaviyo/Postscript plus a good bundle app."],"topics":["Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_76PB7wN9Lo1iW/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Website Maxxing","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"A website-maximizing call focused on collecting customer contacts (CRM), building trust, and thinking long-term so your store is worth more when you sell it.","points":["A real offer beats a plain newsletter sign-up: of 1,000 visitors, a discount or giveaway pop-up might capture ~300 contacts vs. ~25 from \"join our newsletter.\" Giving up 10% to build a big email list pays off over time.","A strong CRM (email/SMS list) can significantly increase what your store sells for. Always ask \"how can I collect contacts?\" in your ads and on your site.","Trust factors: genuine testimonials and reviews (use Judge.me to collect them from real buyers), presented in a clean, organized, aesthetic way.","Put the product first, then reviews below it. People see the product, get interested, then look for proof they can trust you.","Sites with no reviews and just a product look like \"burner\" stores and make people question whether they're legit.","Focus on skills, not just the next sale: CRM, offers, sales psychology and trust-building create longevity and carry over to any business.","The bigger payday is often selling the store (commonly a multiple of annual revenue), so build systems that make it valuable.","Q&A: you can export contacts and import them into a new store's email tool. Keep stores in the same niche where possible, and make sure your sign-up form tells customers they're agreeing to promotional emails."],"topics":["Calls","Website","Email & SMS"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_ObequJcz7HSjx/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"WEBSITE REVIEW!","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"A simple overview of the full MEO process, so you know where everything fits. (Despite the title, this video is a roadmap rather than a website review.)","points":["The process: find a product, start your store (yourself or with the done-for-you agency), find a good way to market it, hire people to handle what you do, then sell the store.","Repeat the cycle as many times as you want, depending on your goals.","It sounds quick, but there are many small steps in between that make the difference, so watch every module thoroughly and take notes.","If you feel confused about where to start: find a product and get your store up first. Everything else is taught step by step."],"topics":["Website","Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_U6hn2tVDUbXqz/"},{"kind":"pdf","title":"Different payment","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"Which payment processors to use on your store, and which ones to start with.","points":["Best picks: Shopify Payments and Stripe; PayPal adds trust for many customers","Square is common if you sell both online and in person","Other options: Authorize.net, Braintree, Adyen, Worldpay, Checkout.com, 2Checkout (Verifone)","Typical Shopify setup: Shopify Payments + PayPal; use Stripe on a custom store or non-Shopify platform"],"topics":[],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_3JJulN0Z9CZVe/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Mindset break","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"A mindset break about replacing a scarcity mindset with an abundance mindset in e-commerce and in life.","points":["There are billions of people in the world. Reaching even a tiny fraction of them with a product is enough to build a very successful business.","Even big brands you see everywhere have reached a tiny percentage of the world, so there's room for you even if others sell similar products.","Watch your words: swap \"I don't know if I can do it\" for \"how can I do more?\"","Focus on making more rather than only saving. Treat money spent on samples or startup costs as an investment that motivates you to earn it back.","Abundance doesn't mean being careless. Think big, but stay smart with decisions.","Results aren't guaranteed, but a scarcity mindset only holds you back."],"topics":["Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_Bmf2k7TmpaNqx/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Website maxxing (call)","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"The non-negotiables every store's landing page needs to build trust and convert, plus Q&A on reviews, AI in ads, suppliers and testing creatives.","points":["Testimonials/social proof: show that other people use the product and it works. You don't need hundreds; 3 or so on the homepage is enough to start.","About Us page: give your brand a deeper meaning. People buy the persona, not just the product (Nike sells feeling fit). Explain what problem you solve and why you exist.","FAQ: answer the questions people would ask (what it does, shipping time, where it ships from). Five or six good ones save time and speed up purchases.","Contact page: shows you're a real business, not a quick cash grab.","What the product solves: address objections head-on (e.g. a grip product that prevents blisters).","Real urgency when you're running a sale (e.g. free shipping for the next 24–48 hours).","Keep your refund/return policy in the footer.","Don't obsess over colors and wording. Once the essentials are in place, marketing is what drives sales, and a website is useless without traffic.","Reviews when you're new: collect genuine reviews from people who've actually used the product. Never make reviews up.","AI in ads: keep paid ads mostly real content (roughly 25% AI max). Never use celebrities' or athletes' likeness with AI without permission, as you can get sued.","Strategy: use organic content to validate, then put money behind what works in paid ads. You don't need to buy followers.","Testing creatives: launch several around the same time, track analytics, then raise the budget on winners and replace losers.","Suppliers: order samples from your actual supplier so you see what customers get. For dropshipping, Zendrop or CJ usually beat Alibaba.","Business setup (LLC vs. sole proprietorship) varies by state, so talk to a tax advisor."],"topics":["Calls","Website","Paid ads","AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_NlQFZJmvSZKjf/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Different ways to use base 44 (CALL)","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"A masterclass on Base44, an AI tool that builds website front ends and custom software, plus Q&A on faceless branding, selling a store, and AI-driven dropshipping.","points":["What Base44 does: builds the front end of your store (homepage/landing page) from a text prompt and integrates with Shopify or Wix, which handle the back end (checkout, payments).","Shopify vs. Wix: both work as the back end. Shopify has a free trial, so test both and pick what you're comfortable with.","Custom software: Base44 can build internal tools, e.g. a daily task calendar that texts reminders (via WhatsApp) and rolls unfinished tasks over, or alerts for late orders.","Product research alerts: you can have Base44 build a system that notifies you of new products in your niche every couple of weeks.","Use it to mock up design ideas quickly before building on Shopify. It's one of the more affordable and beginner-friendly AI builders.","Faceless branding: keep your face off your main brand account to avoid copycats. A separate personal account can promote the product instead.","Suppliers: AutoDS and Zendrop for dropshipping, Alibaba for bulk/B2B (covered in the school modules).","Selling a store: branded stores are worth more if you hold and scale them for at least a year. Buyers often pay a multiple of annual revenue, and a broker helps find buyers. Covered in the multi-store modules.","Growth comes from fixing small leaks: email flows, CTAs, paid ads, UGC creators and TikTok Shop all compound.","AI dropshipping: emotional/story-driven AI videos can convert well for quick revenue, but they don't build a lasting brand. Use them sparingly alongside real content.","When to avoid AI: products people consume or that require high trust are better served by real people and UGC creators."],"topics":["Calls","Website","AI tools","Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_iW8u0yTeIc1eD/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Website Audit!","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"Live audits of real dropshipping stores found on TikTok, pointing out what they do well and what they're missing.","points":["Link in bio: once you pass 1,000 followers on TikTok, add your store link to your bio. Not having one leaves sales on the table.","Good: an Add to Cart button right on the landing page, FAQs, shipping and refund info, and the winning product featured big at the top.","Announcement banner: free shipping, returns and similar perks at the top answer objections before shoppers ask.","Common miss: no pop-up to collect emails. Without it you lose the chance to build a customer list (CRM).","Gadget stores can make money fast, but a focused niche gives more longevity.","If you have one winning product, link your bio straight to that product page.","Marketing tips from these stores: reply to (hate) comments with videos showing how the product works, and add a CTA like \"follow for more\" in captions.","Make sure your store's language matches your audience. A mismatch kills sales.","Winning stores have pop-ups and discounts, so take notes.","Be creative with product choice instead of picking generic items. Use early profits to bring in UGC creators or AI content."],"topics":["Website","Organic marketing","Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_H3e36WFeq8p99/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Website (masterclass)","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"A masterclass on setting up the right customer funnel so every website visit builds your contact list (CRM) and relationship, plus Q&A on ads, video length and email frequency.","points":["Step 1, pop-up first: the first thing visitors should see is a pop-up that collects their email or phone. Prioritize long-term contacts over a quick sale.","A strong offer drives sign-ups: a percentage discount (e.g. 10–20% off) usually works best, but free shipping or a giveaway can be tested. A/B test offers week to week.","Step 2, welcome message: right after sign-up, automatically send a welcome email/SMS (with the coupon if it isn't shown instantly) and tell them what your brand is about.","Step 3, post-purchase: send an order confirmation with a thank-you and a discount on their next order with a real expiration (e.g. 7 days) to encourage repeat purchases.","Tools: Klaviyo for pop-ups and email, and an SMS app for texts.","Email frequency: when starting out, about every other day (some brands send daily). Not every email needs a discount; just promote products in a relevant way. AI can help write them.","TikTok vs. Facebook ads: both sell well. Facebook skews older, TikTok younger, so lean into wherever your audience is.","Organic video length: any length works as long as it's worth watching all the way through.","Store building: the done-for-you agency (replies within ~24–48 hours), AI tools, or build it yourself to learn the skill.","MEO modules: wait until your store is running before watching them. Locked school modules unlock by leveling up in the community."],"topics":["Calls","Website","Paid ads","Email & SMS"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_KVNcggJhYQf0D/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"raising AOV with your website","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"Offer types you can use on your website to raise average order value (AOV), so each customer spends more while getting more value.","points":["Buy more, save more: tiered pricing (e.g. 1 for $40, 2 for $70, 3 for $90) nudges buyers toward the bigger bundle.","Buy 2, get 1 free: works well when your product cost is low. You can raise the price slightly to cover the free unit.","Bundle and save: pair Product A + Product B at a discount (e.g. $80 value for $59). Great for moving a second product.","Free gift threshold: spend a certain amount (e.g. $75) and get a gift or gift card.","Complete the set: main product plus a matching accessory for a small add-on price (e.g. hoodie plus matching pants).","Mix and match: pick any 3 for a set price.","Mystery gift: buy 2 and get a free mystery item. Best if you have a larger catalog.","Premium bundle: everything included for the biggest savings.","Simple and effective for small stores (2–6 products): free shipping when they buy 2 or spend over $50–$75."],"topics":["Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_xxS26f5Mkb1yA/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Winning products (Fitness Niche)","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"A deep dive into the fitness niche: why it's a big opportunity, which products are winning, how to validate them, and how to market them.","points":["The opportunity: fitness is a huge, growing global market (the video cites ~$300B). Buyers now want gym-quality results at home, plus tech, tracking and good looks.","The niche is broad: home workout gear, recovery tools (massage guns, foam rollers), wearables, supplements, fitness apparel, content-friendly accessories and multifunctional products.","Know your buyer: beginners want simple, cheap and motivating; athletes want performance and recovery; home users want compact and easy. Speak clearly to one group.","What buyers want now: digital features (tracking, gamification, sharing), a recovery focus, and aesthetic appeal (it has to look good on social).","Winning-product checklist: demand without overwhelming competition, solves a problem, evergreen or trending, profitable and light to ship, video-friendly. If it checks 3+ boxes, test it.","Examples: adjustable dumbbells and massage guns (high volume), smart jump ropes, mini steppers and ankle weights (trending), grip trainers and shaker bottles (niche).","Validate before investing: Google Trends for steady interest, TikTok (\"TikTok made me buy it\"), and Amazon/Reddit reviews for complaints you can solve.","Marketing: demo and testimonial videos, transformation and challenge content, and content matched to each platform (TikTok, IG Reels, YouTube Shorts).","Micro-influencers (10K–100K followers) and customer UGC drive far more engagement than polished brand ads.","Store-building cheat code: motivational branding, smart bundles to raise AOV, and free digital add-ons (e.g. a 30-day workout PDF, which AI can help make).","Action plan: research → shortlist → source (Zendrop, AutoDS, CJ) → launch, test, and double down on winners.","Sell the transformation (strength, confidence, progress), not just the tool."],"topics":["Product research"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_dGXoCki0zemD4bGQe2Vj5/"},{"kind":"pdf","title":"Focusing on 2-3 products per niche","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"Why a store with 2–4 focused products beats a store with hundreds, and how to build around one hero product.","points":["Fewer products = higher conversions; too many choices causes decision fatigue and no sale","A tight lineup looks like a real brand, not a random catalog, so trust goes up","Ads get cheaper and clearer: you know what to test, creatives are focused and the data is clean","You can properly optimize product pages, upsells and bundles","Fewer suppliers, fewer fulfillment and support issues, faster scaling","Easy to swap out a losing product; start with 1 hero product and expand based on data (Amazon is the exception, not the model)"],"topics":["Product research"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_EEtEjgh2nCged/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Mindset break!","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"A mindset break for anyone doubting themselves or overthinking whether they're on the right track.","points":["You're on the right track. Most people your age aren't even thinking this way, and that makes you different.","The question isn't \"will it work?\" (the business model works) but \"will you work for it?\"","Overthinking and feeling isolated are normal in the early entrepreneur phase. Don't let them make you quit.","Find a big enough reason (family, your future kids, generational change). A strong purpose kills the \"what if it fails\" thinking; selfish goals like cars or flexing usually lead to quitting fast.","Ask \"what if it works?\" instead of \"what if it fails?\"","There's beauty in consistency. Results rarely come in a day, and giving up early means giving up on who you could become.","Keep the faith. Nobody else will want it as badly as you do."],"topics":["Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_i5NAITeqWPpzU/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Using google trend for $$","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"How to use Google Trends to spot what people are searching for and build product ideas around those trends.","points":["Check Google Trends' \"Trending now\" regularly (daily or every couple of days) to see what's hot, e.g. big games, events, viral foods.","Look for topics trending over the past 7 days (and past 48 hours), not just a 24-hour spike. Lasting trends are safer bets.","Use the past 4–24 hours to catch early trends before everyone else.","Filter by category (sports, entertainment, business, food) to find trends that match your store's niche.","Turn a trend into a product angle: e.g. sports-fan products around big games, or event-related items a couple of weeks before (like the Super Bowl).","Never use trademarked logos or copyrighted material. Get close to the trend without infringing.","Once you spot a trend, find a related product on your supplier (e.g. Zendrop) and start promoting it immediately, organically or with Facebook ads."],"topics":["Product research"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_TkckgnLBAZMDB/"},{"kind":"pdf","title":"best niche/ examples of products in niche!!","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"A guide to the best niches for dropshipping and MEO, with example products, the MEO angle for each, and niches to avoid.","points":["Beauty & self-care: emotional, impulse buys with repeat customers (LED masks, ice rollers, hair growth devices, teeth whitening); split into skin, hair and grooming stores","Fitness & body aesthetics: evergreen, easy UGC (home workout gear, posture correctors, massage guns); 1–2 hero products per store","Pet products: owners overspend and cute videos sell (shedding removers, smart toys, grooming tools); dog, cat and grooming stores","Home improvement & organization: visible problems and easy before/afters (storage hacks, cleaning gadgets, motion lights)","Pain relief / \"fix my problem\": urgent buyers who don't price-shop (posture correctors, neck traction, foot rollers, lumbar cushions)","Gaming & desk setups: aesthetic impulse buys (RGB lights, cable management, monitor risers)","Avoid: phone cases (oversaturated), diffusers (not a need, outdated), and plain clothes without design (returns, sizing)"],"topics":["Product research","Suppliers & shipping","Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_4I44Q0D9vxnnQ/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Leveraging trends","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"How to ride trending videos by \"stitching\" them on TikTok to promote your product.","points":["Find trending videos (millions of views) in a category related to your product.","Use TikTok's Stitch feature (Share → Stitch) to put the viral clip first, then show how your product solves the problem in it.","Example: someone slips in a viral clip, and you stitch it showing grippy shoes that would have prevented it.","This lets you borrow the attention of a video that's already blowing up instead of starting from zero.","Keep claims honest. Show what your product actually does."],"topics":["Product research","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_iiPtLhel9eT52/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"WHY WE DO ORGANIC!!","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"Why you shouldn't skip organic marketing, even if you plan to run paid ads.","points":["Don't want to show your face? Hire a UGC creator (e.g. on Fiverr) or have a friend promote the product. There's no excuse to skip it.","Reason 1, find your target audience: comments and engagement show who's actually interested (e.g. a hoodie video drew mostly women, which shaped future content).","Reason 2, brand recognition: organic content isn't only about immediate sales. It gets your product seen.","People who have already seen your product are far more likely to buy when they later see a paid ad. Familiarity raises conversion rates.","Big brands do the same thing: Coca-Cola billboards just show the product and logo, not \"buy now.\" Subtle, repeated exposure works.","You don't need a fancy setup. Early content was filmed in a small bathroom for hours after school. If you really want it, you'll find a way to post."],"topics":["Organic marketing","Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_htUvE66G134Ve/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How I do organic marketing","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"A walkthrough of DiMaggio's own brand page, showing real organic marketing examples and the ideas behind them.","points":["Organic content built the brand's audience (100K followers). The goal is reaching your target audience and building contacts.","Once you have contacts, you can email them weekly or monthly and keep selling to the same customers.","Simple content works: videos filmed at home got tens of thousands of views. You don't need a studio.","Content ideas that performed: wearing or showing the product in everyday settings, product shots with a nice backdrop (like a sunset), outfit/styling videos, AI-edited product clips, seasonal angles (back to school, Christmas), and \"we're dropping this for $X\" hooks.","A big creator or streamer wearing your product can drive huge attention.","Engagement tip: comments and replies boost reach, so engage with your audience.","One free viral video (600K views) can be worth a lot in orders and new contacts.","As you grow, combine organic posting with Facebook ads and email marketing.","The point is constant reminders: when people see your product repeatedly, they eventually buy."],"topics":["Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_3aTZeC1WkNO50BxXZ9Qy2Q/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"More in depth with Organic Marketing","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"A deeper framework for organic marketing: how to make content people engage with, how to analyze results, and how to turn viewers into customers.","points":["Use the 3 W's in every video: What can people comment on? What will they get from the product? Why should they buy (from you)?","Spark comments: ask questions (\"would you wear this?\"), run comment giveaways, or do something unexpected with the product that gets people talking.","Show the benefit, not just the product: what problem does it solve or what experience does it give (e.g. a water toy that cures summer boredom)? Show it rather than plastering text.","If a competitor sells the same product, use a different marketing angle to reach a different audience.","Analyze every post with the \"3 major keys.\" If it got engagement, look at what people are talking about and make more content around that.","If it didn't get engagement, check analytics (watch-through rate), list 5 reasons it may have flopped, and honestly ask whether you'd engage with it. Then revise.","Reply to comments, positive or negative. Negative comments still push your content out. Answer objections and point people to your store (e.g. with a code).","Make your page and landing page obvious so interested viewers can buy easily.","Whether they buy or not, collect their info. Follow up non-buyers with a discount through email/SMS automations (Klaviyo, Postscript)."],"topics":["Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_2L17b8RtwxHVhN6zr75Z8U/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"OM Social Media","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"Pros and cons of the top social platforms for organic marketing (TikTok, Instagram, YouTube Shorts) and how often to post.","points":["TikTok cons: you need ~1,000 followers before adding a bio link, early posts may draw bot comments, and videos can take weeks to pop. Never delete videos.","TikTok pros: very interactive audience (lots of comments), viral posts build followers and fans, and detailed analytics (active times, locations, trends).","Instagram cons: slower traction, lighter analytics, and possible hate comments (which still boost reach, so don't let them get to you).","Instagram pros: bio link from day one, pinned comments (e.g. \"go to my page to shop\"), all engagement counts toward reach, and post scheduling once your account is established.","YouTube Shorts cons: no DMs to build relationships, no stories, and people rarely share Shorts.","YouTube Shorts pros: very fast early traction, engaged commenters, and longer videos later.","Post the same video on every platform. Don't make new content for each one.","Frequency: start with ~2 posts a day per platform and don't exceed ~5, or you may look like spam or a bot.","It's free promotion that takes minutes a day, so don't let the cons discourage you."],"topics":["Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_3rufYj4TZRTPHF4sci9o68/","also":["Clothing Brand"]},{"kind":"lesson","title":"me doing OM!!","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"A live demo of how quick and simple organic marketing videos can be, using a hoodie and a diffuser as examples (the \"starter organic method\").","points":["It doesn't need to be perfect. Each example video took about 3 minutes to make.","On TikTok, tap + and pick a trending/recommended sound, then film the product simply (e.g. lay a hoodie on the floor and pan over details like a custom chain).","Even simple videos need the 3 W's. The caption does the heavy lifting early on, e.g. \"Have you ever seen a hoodie with a custom chain?\" or \"POV: this was your night light growing up.\"","Give people something to comment on. Doing something unexpected with the product drives comments (a friend got huge views letting commenters suggest what to put in a diffuser).","Film in your phone's camera app instead of inside TikTok so you can reuse the same clip across platforms with different captions.","ChatGPT can help generate captions.","15 minutes a day can produce ~5 videos, which means 5 chances to go viral. Consistency matters more than effort per video.","The goal isn't only sales: it's getting your product familiar in potential customers' heads."],"topics":["Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_6CMTA8ARTdIgABYtbLoMZD/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to do Market Research","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"How to do market research on TikTok to see how others market products in your niche and get ideas for your own content.","points":["Search \"TikTok made me buy it\" or \"buy now\" + your niche (e.g. \"buy now fitness\") to find products and see how they're being marketed.","Study what grabs attention: strong hooks, POV-style videos, quick demos (e.g. testing a product underwater), and replying to comments with videos.","Note which videos get lots of comments, even negative ones like \"that's useless.\" Comments mean reach.","What not to do: screen-recording your website as \"organic\" content performs poorly.","Be skeptical of self-proclaimed \"Shopify experts\" in comment sections.","Engage with these videos (like, comment, watch) so the algorithm fills your For You page with similar stores, giving you a constant stream of trends and marketing ideas.","You'll notice sellers copying each other's winning formats (like the same demo style). That's market research in action."],"topics":["Product research","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_6ZVLmnmqqE1zWnsSVRnk7T/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Scaling with FB ads (the MEO own way)","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"How to scale with Facebook/Meta ads the MEO way: validate first, then test with data and scale only proven winners.","points":["Meta (Facebook/Instagram) is one of the biggest ad platforms. Learn how it works before spending.","Non-negotiables before scaling: a validated product and audience, solid organic marketing (~100–120 posts, just 2–3 a day), some testimonials, and a product price that leaves room for ad costs (ideally $35+).","Rule 1, data first, budget later: start with 3–5 ads, test one interest per ad set (e.g. gym-goers), and begin low at ~$5–$10/day.","Key metrics: CPM (cost per 1,000 impressions, lower is better), CTR (aim for 2%+; low CTR means the wrong audience or a weak creative, so hook people in the first 2 seconds), and add-to-cart vs. checkout (many add-to-carts but few purchases points to pricing, shipping or checkout issues; use discount codes and follow-up emails).","Rule 2, scale winners, not hopes: if one product gets clearly more organic traction, put ad money behind that one. Don't reinvent what's working.","Targets: cost per purchase low relative to your price (roughly $5–$15) and ROAS around 1.3–3x to start.","Scaling: duplicate winning ad sets (ABO), use campaign budget (CBO) or Advantage+ shopping, and raise budgets only 20–30% every 24 hours. Never double overnight.","Refresh creatives every 3–5 days to avoid ad fatigue, and monitor results closely in the first 72 hours.","Mindset: the first hurdle is the hardest. Stay disciplined with organic before ads, and grow in every area of life, not just money."],"topics":["Paid ads","Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_4wFq6wGj2qXsNH6QX45Twk/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Utilizing FB groups","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"How to use Facebook groups for community-driven, genuine marketing of your store.","points":["Why it works: groups are already niched down, so you reach people likely to care about your product. Facebook skews older, and that audience tends to buy what they need.","Find groups: search niche keywords (e.g. home decor, fitness, pets, moms), pick active groups (1,000+ members who post regularly), join 10–15 to start, and read each group's rules carefully.","Follow the 80/20 rule: spend ~80% of your time adding real value (answering questions, helpful tips) and only ~20% promoting.","Mention products naturally when they genuinely solve someone's problem, be upfront that it's your store, and offer a link if they want it.","Make sure you have stock and can deliver before promoting.","Content that converts: product-in-use demos, honest reviews, problem → solution stories, educational posts (e.g. ab workouts), and genuine limited-time offers.","Suggested timeline: weeks 1–2 pure value, week 3 casually share your story, week 5+ share products when relevant. Build relationships, not just sales.","Avoid: promoting right after joining, copy-paste posts that look like a bot, breaking group rules, and being pushy.","Track engagement (likes, comments, shares), conversations and website clicks (Shopify shows traffic sources)."],"topics":["Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_6RBDPBoS1UtwO5KSw2t5Ud/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"OM is not hard, fam","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"Proof that organic marketing isn't hard: a scroll through DiMaggio's old account, where he posted constantly before outsourcing.","points":["For one to two years he posted relentlessly (sometimes ~11 times a day), always featuring his own products (hoodies, shirts, hats, shorts) and tagging them in captions.","He did this while in school, working, reselling shoes and mowing lawns, often filming in a hot, cramped bathroom. Time and setup aren't real excuses.","Using a small personal brand to promote your store can kick-start sales much faster.","Today his marketing is outsourced, but you have to put in the work before you can get there.","You don't need 12 posts a day. 2–3 a day is enough. Record videos in your camera app so you can make several quickly.","Don't be scared of the work. Find a way to get it done."],"topics":["Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_4zHCSrUeqKFjK/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"The point: Marketing","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"Why marketing is the whole point: success comes from getting your store in front of as many eyes as possible, through many channels.","points":["Zero traffic means zero sales. Unlike a physical store, nobody stumbles onto your website, so you have to build momentum.","Early traction creates social proof: the more places people see your brand, the more trustworthy it looks.","Cast a wide net, then double down on whichever channel or audience responds best.","Start with your personal network: post on your personal socials, share your story, and ask friends and family to share (maybe for a small gift). Don't worry about what people think.","Old-school still works: flyers on community boards (coffee shops, libraries, campus), business cards, local events, car magnets, and branded stickers (only where permitted).","Digital: business accounts posting daily, email marketing, SEO (show up for niche search terms), and helpful posts in Reddit/Facebook communities.","Partnerships: send product to micro-influencers with a promo code, cross-promote with non-competing brands, and run affiliate programs.","Content: blog posts that answer niche questions and link to your product (e.g. \"how to sleep better\"), demos, unboxings, tutorials, testimonials, podcasts and UGC.","Track and optimize: try at least 3 channels, check analytics and comments daily to see who's engaging, and adjust.","Action plan: pick 3 tactics, market at least 30 minutes a day, and keep going. Every successful store started at zero."],"topics":[],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_36B3irDDNDgJ5Oy7ATf7BC/","also":["Clothing Brand"]},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to STALK Competitors!","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"The \"burner account\" method for watching competitors and filling your feed with product-marketing ideas.","points":["Make a new TikTok account and only interact with videos that promote products: follow, like and comment on them.","Within a short time, your For You page fills with other sellers' product videos, even from accounts you never followed.","Study how they promote: hooks, trends they're riding, who they're targeting (e.g. a mini laundry machine aimed at college students).","One viral video can put a store on the map and bring in contacts and sales for a long time.","Viral product videos are often simple and quick (5–15 minutes to make) and don't need to be professional.","Use these insights to make videos in a similar style for your own product."],"topics":["Product research"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_1VGvM66diWe93QjACEhQvF/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Why ORGANIC before PAID?","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"Why you should do organic marketing before running paid ads.","points":["Posting a few videos won't bring instant sales; it takes consistency","Organic content on TikTok and Instagram shows you who your audience really is","Learn your analytics and target demographics before spending on ads so you don't waste money","Knowing your audience helps you scale and raises your store's value if you sell it (MEO)","Turn your best-performing organic content into paid ads"],"topics":["Organic marketing","Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_T1mN5ShhzmiFK/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"WHAT EACH MARKETING IS FOR!","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"The four goals of marketing and why each one matters as much as sales.","points":["Sales: people visiting your site and buying.","Traffic: getting people onto your website.","Contacts: getting visitors to leave their email or phone number.","Awareness: people becoming familiar with your brand.","They all depend on each other. You can't get sales without traffic and awareness, or contacts without traffic.","The biggest mistake is going into e-commerce focused only on sales. Treat it like a real business and work on all four.","Act with gratitude for progress (e.g. 100 sessions is a win). Visitors today may come back and buy later, especially if you collected their contact info."],"topics":[],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_Z5pLIGTtOaagg/","also":["Dropshipping · Marketing"]},{"kind":"lesson","title":"SCROLL DOWN","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"if you are in a niche and trying to figure out what to post. LOOK UP YOUR NICHE such as (fintess)","points":["then \"good product”","so it can be “good product for fitness” or whatever your niche is.","or it can be “fitness, buy now” and once you do that, you will see a lot of other ideas to use for product.","Dee."],"topics":["Product research","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_AWNcV4OuRTvDLabnEPoR1/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"OM>> paid ads","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"Why you should do organic marketing before paid ads.","points":["Running paid ads first means guessing and burning money. You don't yet know who actually wants your product.","Before running ads, know at least 3 things about your audience: where they are (country/state/city), when they're active, and their age bracket.","Organic marketing gives you those analytics for free. Switch to a creator or business account to see the same kind of data Facebook ads would give you.","The people engaging with your organic posts are your buyers. Trust the algorithm's targeting.","Use organic to fund your ads with both knowledge (who to target, what to make) and money (early sales).","Use the push-and-go method from the marketing modules: validate with organic, then push winners with ads.","Post about 3–5 times a day. It only takes around 20 minutes."],"topics":["Organic marketing","Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_1mgEzeO4WPeyhe7wIuIZN9/","also":["Clothing Brand"]},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Examples of organic marketing/ How to find more WINNING products","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"Real examples of viral organic marketing for dropshipping products, and what made them work.","points":["Example: a dashboard de-icing gadget marketed to people in cold climates. The videos showed it in use on a frosty morning, which made the niche instantly relatable.","Show what the product does, not just the product. Demonstrations get far more engagement than simply holding it up.","Target your niche directly (e.g. people dealing with cold weather) and use relatable everyday scenarios.","Hooks that spark debate drive comments and reach, but avoid anything that disrespects or alienates part of your audience.","Timely hooks like \"best product of [current year]\" and putting the store link in the comments (not just \"link in bio\") can help.","Volume matters: the creator's first big viral video came after roughly 50–100+ posts. Keep posting.","Once traffic comes in, it's all about getting more people to the site, like adding more customers to a restaurant that's already built."],"topics":["Product research","Organic marketing","Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_7ghn0lGiVyB3W/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Running FB ads","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"A step-by-step walkthrough of setting up a Facebook/Meta sales ad campaign in Ads Manager.","points":["In Ads Manager, click Create and choose the Sales objective. Traffic, awareness and engagement are mainly for hyping a drop before launch.","Choose manual setup (or Advantage+), and set the conversion event to Purchase.","Connect your Meta Pixel through the Facebook & Instagram app in Shopify.","Audience size: avoid too narrow or too broad. Use what organic marketing taught you about your audience.","Locations: commonly the US, UK, Canada and Australia for English stores. Target specific regions only for seasonal reasons (e.g. summer clothes in the opposite hemisphere).","Budget: start small (~$5/day) until you know your analytics.","Schedule: start at 12 a.m. the next day (gives Meta time to process) and set an end date 7–10 days out so you don't overspend.","Only scale budget once an ad is clearly outperforming. Skip Meta's auto-generated ads.","Connect your brand's Instagram and Facebook pages.","Creative: a single image or video works best (Canva can help with design). Primary text, headline and description should be short and clear (e.g. restock news, limited stock, a first-orders discount).","Use a Shop Now call to action, add your promo code, and use a clean display link (your domain)."],"topics":["Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_4EfpvK9AC2u0ukb27oGRdq/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"CROSS PLATFORM, EARLY MEO","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"Cross-promoting multiple stores in your content, an early step toward MEO (multi-store e-commerce).","points":["While promoting one product, subtly feature a product from another store in the background (e.g. wearing your hoodie while demoing a different product).","Viewers who don't want the main product may notice and want the background item instead.","This is subtle marketing: like brand-recognition ads (e.g. soda ads that just show the logo), the goal is familiarity, not an immediate sale.","People who've seen a product before trust it more and are more likely to buy it later.","Only do this once your main product is getting traction. Don't promote too many products at once or push the second product directly.","Works for organic content and ads alike (e.g. promoting camera glasses while wearing your hoodie)."],"topics":["Organic marketing","Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_sGnjM4E55Cq5V/","also":["Clothing Brand"]},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Mistakes When Organically Marketing","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"The biggest mistake in organic marketing, which is not doing enough of it, and why marketing deserves most of your effort.","points":["No marketing means zero sales. Even Jeff Bezos wouldn't make sales from a store nobody knows about.","Marketing should get the majority (~80%) of your attention in e-commerce, not a token 1% effort.","Posting once a week and expecting big results won't work. Consistency is key.","Big brands (e.g. Balenciaga) market everywhere, all year: Instagram, TikTok, Pinterest and Snapchat posts, influencers, paid ads on multiple platforms, plus email and SMS.","If the biggest brands do all of this constantly, a new store can't expect success from minimal effort.","Bottom line: if nothing is being pushed out to people, they can't buy your product."],"topics":["Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_4GXjAQWUHRA2GrOrvpoSBP/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Mindset break","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"A mindset break on failure: the only real way to fail is to give up.","points":["Success in business means little if your mindset is weak, which is why mindset gets so much focus here.","Trying something that doesn't work yet isn't failing. Failing is stopping. People who say \"it doesn't work\" usually just quit.","If others in the same (or worse) position have succeeded, it can work for you too. E-commerce works, just like the gym works.","Be grateful for your resources (a laptop, phone, Wi-Fi). Many people around the world would love the same opportunity.","Everyone has a doubtful inner voice that whispers \"should I quit?\" Recognize it and keep going.","Results aren't guaranteed, but consistency puts you in position to benefit when the next big opportunity or breakthrough comes. Those who quit miss it."],"topics":["Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_qawPZrOV8iUTS/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"ORGANIC OUTREACH!","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"Why you shouldn't skip organic marketing, even if you plan to run paid ads.","points":["Don't want to show your face? Hire a UGC creator (e.g. on Fiverr) or have a friend promote the product. There's no excuse to skip it.","Reason 1, find your target audience: comments and engagement show who's actually interested (e.g. a hoodie video drew mostly women, which shaped future content).","Reason 2, brand recognition: organic content isn't only about immediate sales. It gets your product seen.","People who have already seen your product are far more likely to buy when they later see a paid ad. Familiarity raises conversion rates.","Big brands do the same thing: Coca-Cola billboards just show the product and logo, not \"buy now.\" Subtle, repeated exposure works.","You don't need a fancy setup. Early content was filmed in a small bathroom for hours after school. If you really want it, you'll find a way to post."],"topics":["Organic marketing","Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_8U17aW6HXqz1K/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Algorithm for OM (Best Times to Post)","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"How social media algorithms work and the best times and frequency to post on each platform for organic marketing.","points":["Why timing matters: early engagement (velocity) drives reach. Posting at 3 a.m. for a US audience pushes your video to the wrong time zones first.","Recency matters: fresh content gets prioritized, often with a burst of views in the first 30–60 minutes while the algorithm tests it.","Vary your content. Repetitive-looking videos can get suppressed and look like spam.","Each platform suits different content and ages: YouTube Shorts and Snapchat Spotlight skew younger (fast-paced, heavily edited), Pinterest and Facebook skew older, and LinkedIn is for older or professional audiences only.","General best windows: Tuesday–Thursday, early morning and evening (before and after work). Match your audience's routine.","Facebook: Tuesdays and Wednesdays, roughly 9 a.m.–1 p.m. Older users love community and local news angles. Share IG posts to Facebook and Threads with one tap.","Instagram: anytime works, but early-week posts often sell better.","TikTok: rewards consistency. Views may dip after a week before rising again if you keep going. Mornings and late evenings work well.","YouTube: post a few hours before prime time. Pinterest: post a day ahead and let it build.","Frequency: post 2–3 times a day per platform until you get real traction (it's like buying more lottery tickets). Once you get consistent 10–20K-view videos, you can slow down. Space posts at least 1–2 hours apart.","Use the audience data you gather (age, interests, platform) to target paid ads, and always ask whether you'd be entertained by the video yourself."],"topics":["Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_pGWRkD1ZpwiUO/"},{"kind":"pdf","title":"Best caption CTA's for social media oM","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"Ready-to-use caption CTAs for organic social posts, split by goal: followers, sales and comments.","points":["Follower CTAs, e.g. \"Follow to see part 2\" and \"More tips like this tomorrow, follow so you don't miss it\"","Sales CTAs, e.g. \"Tap the link and try it for yourself\" and \"If you've been thinking about getting it, this is your sign\"","Comment CTAs, e.g. \"Comment YES or NO\", \"What would you rate this 1–10?\", \"Tag someone who needs to see this\"","Comment-bait questions that boost engagement and push the post further"],"topics":["Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_6RiK210tlCsB7/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Key to organic!!","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"The key to organic marketing: don't be a perfectionist, and post everywhere to reach as many people as possible.","points":["Don't overthink each post. Get content out as much as possible.","Post on every platform: TikTok, Instagram, YouTube, Facebook, Snapchat, Pinterest and more.","You don't need different videos for each platform. Make one video and post it everywhere, and use Instagram/Facebook cross-posting.","A worse-looking store can outsell yours simply by reaching more people. Reach is the game.","More eyes means more sales: 1 million viewers beats 10,000.","With organic, reaching a broad audience costs nothing. Narrow niche targeting matters most when you're paying for ads."],"topics":["Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_CyZxfxJ6JHPKf/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"include ai with your OM","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"Using AI-generated videos in your organic marketing, especially if you want to stay faceless.","points":["AI video tools (e.g. Sora, or video generators you prompt via ChatGPT or Claude) can create short, entertaining clips in seconds.","Example: simple AI pet videos (e.g. a talking dog) promoting pet products have reached over a million views.","You could post a few AI videos a day just by pasting prompts, which is a great way to get your brand out there.","Use AI videos mainly for awareness and organic reach, not as your main paid ads, since they can come across as less trustworthy when selling.","Works for any niche (pets, fitness, etc.). Find creators doing it well and model the style.","Once your store takes off, you may lean more on paid ads, but organic (including AI content) saves money early on."],"topics":["Organic marketing","AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_ncirixwqIAzR5/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Type of ads/marketing!","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"A rundown of the main types of marketing and ads you can use for your store, and which ones are worth your time.","points":["Organic content: make your own videos, with or without showing your face (hands-only on a tripod, or have a friend film), and later hire creators on Fiverr","Showing your face early helps build a brand and trust; you can step back from the camera as the store grows","Use or wear your product in public so it works like a walking billboard","Influencers: DM micro-influencers (some will post for free product), negotiate low and be willing to walk away","Bigger influencers on a contract (e.g. keep videos up for 6 months) can pay off long term through the customer contacts you collect","Paid platforms: Pinterest (niche/lifestyle), Snapchat (younger audience), Google Ads (better for big brands, focus on SEO instead), Meta ads (Facebook, Instagram, Threads/Messenger) mainly for sales, and TikTok ads for repeated exposure across platforms","Main recommendation: start with organic videos plus influencers, and use Facebook and TikTok for paid ads"],"topics":["Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_dVABVe20Cp9bp/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Pretesting Paid/OM","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"How to pre-test a product for real demand before you put any money into ads.","points":["Base decisions on actual demand, not just a gut feeling","Check search volume and Google Trends to see when a product or keyword is trending and which season it peaks in","Look for social proof density: are multiple unrelated creators already posting about this type of product with real engagement? If so, the product already has awareness and you just need to sell it","Use your organic videos as a test. If even one comment asks \"where can I get this?\", that creative is showing demand","Organic can be more experimental, but paid ads should clearly show the problem and the solution","Validate on TikTok too, not just Google, by seeing whether creators are talking about it more","A brand-new product in a winning niche (e.g. looksmaxxing) may show little demand at first only because nobody knows about it yet; the niche itself can create the demand"],"topics":["Organic marketing","Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_3n87gLUA2P8eP/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"HOW TO MAKE OM easier pt.1","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"How to make organic marketing easier and save time by scheduling your posts ahead of time.","points":["Work smart and hard: scheduling keeps you consistent without having to stop and post manually every day","Platforms to schedule on: TikTok Studio, Instagram and YouTube","TikTok: download the TikTok Studio app, go to Post, then Schedule Post (it works from the app, not the computer)","You can schedule up to about 7 days out. For example, sit down on Friday, make 7–8 posts and schedule them across the next few days","Use the time you save to grow your brand and find better products","Instagram and YouTube may need a short wait before scheduling unlocks, while TikTok Studio lets you schedule right away","If scheduled posts aren't getting consistent views, switch back to posting manually"],"topics":["Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_CKRhx41jUlx1t/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Kalodata to find Products","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"How to use Kalodata to find winning products and proven video ideas from TikTok Shop data.","points":["Kalodata shows the hottest-selling TikTok Shop products and how much revenue they're making (it has a free trial)","Go to Explore, filter by date range (e.g. last 30 days) and by category to see the top sellers in your niche","Don't stop at the revenue list: check the Trends section to spot products that are spiking right now (e.g. a cordless pressure washer)","Sort by the highest-revenue videos for a product, study what makes them entertaining, and find the patterns","Use those patterns as inspiration for your own creatives (including AI-made videos) without copying them outright","Instead of selling on TikTok Shop, list the product on your own site and run ads to it","If you already sell a product, search for it to find video ideas that are already proven to work"],"topics":["Product research","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_ManoEW99lz3kU/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Testing Products","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"A live masterclass with Q&A on a new way to test products using AI-generated videos, compared with ordering a physical sample.","points":["Q&A: skincare creatives should show the solution and before/after results; products with color variants need the product page before checkout, which is normal","Offer incentives tied to the product (free shipping over a set amount, a free gift or mystery box) rather than giveaway-style prizes, and avoid discounts that drag down your average order value","Option 1, physical sample: order from your supplier or Amazon (can take 3+ weeks), then film unlimited videos yourself for just the cost of the sample","Option 2, AI videos: put a product screenshot into an AI video tool like Higgsfield or Arcads, with a prompt written by Claude describing the product, to get realistic videos (roughly $25–$125/month for a limited number of videos)","Repurpose AI videos by cutting and remixing them, so one video can become around 10 posts","Best of both: start AI content while your sample ships, and run one AI account plus one account with you using the real product","Downside of skipping the sample: you don't know the quality. Products that need trust or real results (supplements, skincare) do better with real people or UGC creators; simple products (water bottles, ab rollers, headsets) work well with AI","If AI videos show demand, use those sales to order the sample and go all in","Q&A on competitors offering to collab: ask for proof of their numbers before anything, don't merge a store that's still growing fast, and if anything, test the partnership on a separate new store","Q&A on suppliers and fulfillment: build supplier relationships to get better pricing, and move to a 3PL (about $2 per package) or warehouse once order volume gets high"],"topics":["Calls","Product research","AI tools","Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_l99elxFJDrEgl/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"What different types of marketing is good for!","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"A breakdown of what each type of marketing is best for, rated on awareness, contacts, website traffic and potential sales.","points":["Organic marketing: highest for awareness. Contacts, traffic and sales are medium and depend on consistency, going viral and having a link in your bio. Use it to build awareness and fund your ads, not mainly for sales","Seeing your product organically gives people a break from obvious ads and makes them more likely to buy later","UGC creators: top-tier awareness because their audience already trusts them. Traffic is lower, but conversion is very high. Think of it as organic marketing on steroids","Email/SMS: very high traffic and potential sales, since these people already know your brand and are being reminded to buy","Paid ads: not mainly for awareness, but highest for traffic and direct sales","Summary: organic = awareness, UGC = half awareness and half sales, email/SMS and paid ads = sales","Awareness is the most underrated one. It builds longevity and makes your store worth more if you ever sell it"],"topics":["Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_OfmSyTd14mBq0/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"watch competitors ads!","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"Using TikTok to research products and study what competitors are posting.","points":["TikTok is one of the best product research tools. Search your niche (e.g. \"fitness items\", \"workout items\") and scroll through the videos","Example math: if just 1% of a viral video's viewers buy a ~$37 product, that could be tens of thousands in sales from one post","Treat organic posts like lottery tickets: post every day and eventually some will hit","Organic first is good practice for learning your targeting and how to make good ads, but you can start ads whenever you feel ready (it just costs more)","Creators who promote products in your niche are worth DMing for promotion","Successful pages often reuse the same footage to make many different videos, so it's not as hard as it looks","Once a page finds its target audience organically, it usually moves into running ads","Keep researching on TikTok to find new product ideas, even if you have to customize them for your niche"],"topics":["Product research","Organic marketing","Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_9VrhiEL1CZd6f/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Watch their meta ads!","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"How to use the Facebook (Meta) Ad Library to see what ads your competitors are running and get ideas for your own.","points":["Open the Facebook Ad Library, select \"All ads\", and search keywords for your niche (e.g. fitness, skincare)","Check how long an ad has been running: one that's been live for months is probably a winning, profitable ad","You only need one great ad or marketing video that you can keep running","Make ads in a similar style for your own product, but don't copy or take their ads directly (for legal reasons)","Look for common patterns across winning ads in your niche and write them down","Ads using real people tend to outperform obvious AI ads","Do this research for any niche, even if you're building a brand"],"topics":["Product research","Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_83LhX6E1KpC9m/","also":["Clothing Brand"]},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Having AI help you with organic marketing","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"How to use AI (Claude or ChatGPT) to come up with organic marketing video ideas when you're running out of them.","points":["Start with a clear prompt: tell the AI you do organic marketing for your brand, which platforms you post on (YouTube Shorts, Snapchat Spotlight, etc.) and that you need ideas","Explain what your brand is, what it does and who your target audience is (e.g. streetwear for ages 16–25)","If the first ideas aren't good, ask it to make them more engaging","Describe or share examples of videos that work (yours or competitors'), such as B-roll of the product with engaging captions like \"which fit would you wear?\"","If the AI drifts off and starts writing scripts, bring it back to your original prompt","Ask it to turn your best ideas into 25 similar content ideas, and paste in your top 5–10 performing videos for reference","Be patient while it works, then pick the ideas that fit your brand (e.g. \"I wore this to...\" or \"rate my fit\" style videos)"],"topics":["Organic marketing","AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_GEyq04PXxBKYK/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Patterns in winning ads","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"How to spot the patterns that winning competitor ads have in common, with real examples from the skincare and travel niches.","points":["Watch several competitor ads in your niche back to back to find what they share","Example: a disposable face towel ad opens with the problem (bacteria in reused towels), backs it up with facts, explains how the product was developed, then shows the solution and ends with a discount code offer","Example: a travel product ad opens by calling out a relatable problem (\"raise your hand if your airport experience...\")","The key pattern: winning ads present the problem first, then show your product as the solution","Just showing the product only works for huge brands. Smaller stores need to show how the product is different and why people need it","Keep checking competitor ads regularly to spot new patterns in your niche"],"topics":["Product research","Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_QMEfyCatfSZOZ/","also":["Clothing Brand"]},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Purpose of organic","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"The real purpose of organic marketing, shown with analytics from one of the mentor's own stores.","points":["Organic brings a lot of sessions but fewer direct sales, because it's mainly for awareness","Visitors from organic often leave their contact info and come back later to buy after seeing you over and over","It took hundreds of posts, not 10–20, before organic started driving consistent sales","Example: around $30,000 from organic traffic in about two and a half weeks","Organic is one of the best forms of outreach because it's free: why pay $50 to reach 1,000 people with ads when 10 extra minutes can reach 2,000?","Even a video with 500 views is reaching potential buyers","Use organic to find your target audience and build awareness, then run Facebook ads to drive more sales"],"topics":["Mindset","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_n1Kkw8Vgd6TPG/","also":["Clothing Brand"]},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to Use ai for organic!","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"How to use AI to create UGC-style organic marketing videos for your product.","points":["Keep filming your own content too, for extra videos and to save money, but AI UGC can add a lot of volume","Search TikTok for UGC creator videos in your niche (e.g. \"UGC creator protein powder\") to find examples you like","Download the video, or watch it and describe it to Claude in detail, scene by scene with timestamps (what's shown, the setting, the hands, how the product is used)","Ask Claude to generate a script for your own product based on that description that you can paste into an AI video tool (Claude is recommended over ChatGPT for scripts)","In the AI UGC software, choose an avatar (avoid overused ones), upload a picture of your product and generate the video","You can also pick existing templates, click recreate with your product, and chop up different creatives into new videos"],"topics":["Organic marketing","AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_mhNRW1XWWB9ja/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to do email SMS marketing (Klaviyo)","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"A walkthrough of setting up Klaviyo for email and SMS marketing and sending your first campaign.","points":["Sign up, answer the setup questions (your business, where your store is hosted) and add your business address","Set the sender email your customers will see","Choose your channels: email and SMS (WhatsApp and push notifications are also available)","Start on the free plan until you're sure you like it, and connect your Shopify store","The home dashboard shows how your emails and SMS are performing, and the built-in guides help with things like sign-up forms","To create a campaign: choose email, select or import your audience list, then write the subject line and preview text","The subject line is what catches their eye, so make it strong. The preview text shows underneath it","Pick a template, edit it, and send it to your list"],"topics":["Paid ads","Email & SMS"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_i9Tw3Q5vdTZDa/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Different ways to create content","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"All the different ways you can get content for your product, so you're not limited to just two or three sources.","points":["Film your own 5–15 second clips with a sample (from Amazon, Alibaba, etc.), with decent quality and a clean background","Ask your supplier for permission to use their product videos, or request custom shots","Find TikTok videos, Instagram Reels and YouTube reviews/unboxings of the product, and always get the creator's permission before using them","Collect customer reviews and videos with a review app like Judge.me, or offer customers a discount on their next order for an honest video","Hire UGC creators (Fiverr, Billo, Insense) or pay micro-influencers, who already have a niche audience","Use affiliate creators who earn a commission on sales","Use AI video tools (e.g. CapCut) or 3D renders (e.g. Blender) for product visuals","Film in relatable locations like the gym, car, kitchen or office for authenticity","Invite happy past buyers to become brand ambassadors with a coupon code, but keep it a simple ambassador option, not an MLM-style scheme","Best content stack: supplier footage (fastest), UGC creators (most scalable), self-shot footage, and customer videos for social proof"],"topics":["Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_aFbsyEwhV62HR/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Mastering FB Ads","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"An in-depth walkthrough of Facebook (Meta) Ads Manager, from reading your dashboard to setting up and publishing a campaign.","points":["Opportunity score: Meta's points rating for your ad setup. Under 60 is bad, and about 73–75 is the sweet spot","Campaign dashboard: turn campaigns on and off, and name each one after the product and budget so tests stay organized","Avoid duplicating winning ads unless you have a big budget, because Meta will push you to spend more. Create new ones instead","A/B testing runs two creatives against each other for a set time and keeps the winner","Key columns: results, cost per result (CPR), amount spent, impressions and reach. Keep attribution on a 7-day click","Campaign goals: awareness (before launch), traffic, engagement, leads (collect contacts) and sales (once the product is live)","Budgets: daily or lifetime. New ad accounts should start small (about $5/day per test) so Meta trusts the account","Ad sets act like folders inside a campaign (e.g. an image version and a video version of the same product)","Targeting: narrow by location, age and interests, using what your organic marketing showed you about who your audience really is","Placements: choose where the ad shows and exclude platforms that don't fit your audience","Creative: single image, video or carousel. Add your website URL, and use instant forms if you're collecting leads","After you publish, the ad may sit in draft/pending while Meta reviews it, which is normal"],"topics":["Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_2b2YQHrihlhej/","also":["Dropshipping · Marketing"]},{"kind":"lesson","title":"More with fb ads","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"More on Facebook (Meta) ads: how to set up multiple creatives within a campaign so you can test them against the same audience and budget.","points":["Click Create and keep the buying type on Auction (Reservation is for more advanced, predictable buys)","Review the campaign objectives (awareness, traffic, engagement, app promotion, leads, sales) and pick the one that matches your goal, e.g. Sales","Advantage+ lets Meta's AI help with targeting if you want it","Think of the campaign as a folder: duplicate ad sets inside the original campaign to run different creatives side by side","Name everything clearly (e.g. \"Water gun – testing creatives\", \"Water gun 2\") so you can tell your tests apart","Use A/B (creative) testing to compare versions. An incomplete ad shows a warning marker on the side","Use the ad preview to check how it will look, and connect both your Facebook and Instagram profiles for more reach","Choose the right call to action for your goal (e.g. Shop Now or Buy Now for sales, sign-up for leads), and turn on partnership ads if you're running with creators"],"topics":["Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_01wj9tOHVda60/","also":["Clothing Brand"]},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Marketing masterclass (call)","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"A live marketing masterclass with member Q&A on fulfillment, store setup, brand recognition and how to find and scale winning ad creatives.","points":["Q&A: if a new supplier's version looks slightly different but works the same, email customers to let them know before it ships","Q&A: for a chargeback over a defective item, ask for photo evidence, then contact your supplier (or Alibaba) for a replacement or refund","Q&A: get a professional business email (e.g. support@yourstore.com) through GoDaddy. Routing it into Gmail is fine","Q&A: with AliExpress, send order details to the supplier manually, or automate it with AutoDS","Must-have automations: abandoned cart, welcome email, and a post-purchase thank-you with a coupon for the next order","Sold out? Keep posting and add a pop-up to collect emails and phone numbers so you can text and email everyone when you restock. Reinvest profits into more inventory","Organic marketing builds recognition (like Sprite billboards), which makes people far more likely to choose your brand and raises conversion rates","In ads, focus on the creatives (video/image) and text. The ad setup itself is mostly automated now","A winning ad shows a high click-through rate. Clicks and site visits show interest even before sales follow","Once a creative wins, don't reinvent it: recreate it with a new setting or person, and put more budget behind it","Test 2–3 creatives at about $5/day each for 3–5 days, then scale the one with the most clicks. Raising the budget reaches more people in that niche","Avoid \"first 10 orders get X% off\" messaging, which can make it look like nobody is buying. A time-limited offer creates urgency without that downside","For pre-orders, state the shipping timeline clearly in the title, description and emails"],"topics":["Calls","Paid ads","Suppliers & shipping","Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_BT8COlu1cLRdd/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"New Meta Ads Manager Walkthrough (AI Update)","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"A tour of Facebook (Meta) Ads Manager after its newer AI-powered update, and how to build a sales campaign step by step.","points":["The structure: a campaign is the folder, ad sets are the pages inside it, and each ad is one specific page","Create a campaign on Auction with the Sales objective, give the folder a clear name, and leave the default settings","Start with about a $5 budget to warm up a new ad account","Turn on A/B testing to compare up to seven creatives over about seven days and see which performs best","At the ad set level, aim to maximize conversions (or website visits), and optionally let Meta adjust media and text for different customers","Set an end date or leave it running, and build or adjust your audience and demographics","Ad transparency isn't necessary. Use placements to focus on platforms like Instagram if you want","Use the ad preview, add your website URL, then upload your creative. Image ads suit branded stores, while video ads suit dropshipping or problem-solving products","Meta's new AI features can generate extra creative versions from your images","Don't stress if the campaign score is a bit low, as long as everything else is set up correctly"],"topics":["Paid ads","AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_IW059u9IflYzv/"},{"kind":"pdf","title":"Different ai's for different things","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"Which AI tool to use for each marketing job, from scripts to AI video.","points":["ChatGPT: scripts, hooks, captions, ad copy, emails, product descriptions and turning one idea into 20 variations","ChatGPT images + Canva for ad pictures and product creatives","CapCut for TikTok/Reels editing; OpusClip to cut long videos into Shorts; Repurpose AI to rewrite content across platforms","Runway for AI video and B-roll; ElevenLabs for AI voiceovers","HeyGen or Captions for talking AI presenters and UGC-style videos"],"topics":["AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_vxWsVzhD44J87/"},{"kind":"pdf","title":"Best time to run ads!","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"The best hours to run and test ads on each platform.","points":["Facebook/Instagram: run 24/7; if restricting, 7 AM–11 PM with focus on 7–10 AM, 12–2 PM and 6–11 PM","TikTok: 24/7 at first; if dayparting, 12 PM–12 AM, especially 5–11 PM","Google Search/Shopping: usually 24/7 since buyers search at all hours","YouTube: 12–11 PM, priority 5–10 PM; Snapchat: 3 PM–12 AM, especially 7–11 PM"],"topics":["Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_Da1yVB4948Nw7/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Email content","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"How to create good email marketing content, using the Shopify Messaging app (the same ideas work in Klaviyo).","points":["Install the free Shopify Messaging app to practice email campaigns before moving to Klaviyo","Brand every email: add your logo and store name, pick a clean, readable font, set your colors, and add a footer with your address and social links (TikTok, IG)","For content ideas, subscribe to other brands' emails (e.g. GLD) and study how they market, from product promos to deals like \"2 for 1 Tuesday\"","Not every email needs a discount. Think of emails as reminders for people who meant to come back or never checked out","You can paste a competitor's email into ChatGPT and ask it to rewrite the idea in your own words for your product","Every email should have a clickable button (e.g. \"Shop Now\") and a product image","Simple email ideas: \"We just restocked\", \"Sale ending soon\", or seasonal hooks like \"Feeling cold?\""],"topics":["Website","Organic marketing","Email & SMS"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_dBWJ4S625xQaK/","also":["Dropshipping · Marketing"]},{"kind":"pdf","title":"PRE_TESTING","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"How to validate demand for a product before you spend anything on ads.","points":["Don't trust one signal like TikTok views; cross-check at least 3","Google Trends: look for steady or rising interest, not a dead spike","Keyword tool: decent search volume with low CPC = underpriced attention","Social proof: multiple unrelated creators posting about it organically with real engagement","Did it show demand in your own organic marketing (100+ videos)?"],"topics":["Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_J5EgG3W9qmdtm/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Which AI Tools to Use (Masterclass Call)","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"A live masterclass with Q&A on which AI tools to use for different parts of your e-commerce business, plus member wins and shipping troubleshooting.","points":["Q&A: when a Zendrop order stops updating in transit, contact support, wait up to a full 30 days, then reship or refund the customer if it still hasn't arrived","Refunds still apply when a customer never received their product","Know which AI fits which job, instead of using one tool for everything","Higgsfield: videos (UGC, organic content, some paid ads)","Claude: website coding, email copy, video scripts, hooks and content ideas","ChatGPT: images (ad images, product photo shoots), prompts, copywriting and general business questions","Lovable: mainly front-end websites and landing pages, and it can connect to Shopify","Base44: front-end websites plus custom software (e.g. inventory or UGC creator tracking) that can connect to Shopify or Wix","Using AI the right way saves time and money compared with paying people for copywriting, prompts or UGC","Member win: adding clear CTAs in captions quickly turned a stagnant store into daily sales. Tell viewers where to buy","Post on Snapchat and YouTube Shorts too, and add bundle or add-on products (no more than 5–6) to raise average order value"],"topics":["Calls","AI tools","Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_l9jsCk1aMUVjz/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Good example of AI marketing","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"A real example of a store that grew fast using AI-generated marketing videos, and what you can learn from it.","points":["A crochet product store whose TikTok videos are almost all AI-generated, with some reaching around a million views","The store reportedly did thousands of orders in about a month from this approach","They use a bundle offer to raise order value. The main thing missing is an email pop-up","Lesson: find a good product, figure out how to make that style of AI video for it, and post consistently","Use stores like this for inspiration only. Don't copy their product or ads"],"topics":["AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_cfL0cS3Hindke/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Influencers are your best friends","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"You need to make sure you include these in all your organic marketing videos! A call to action: a call to action is something like. “Follow for more”, “Get it now”, “tag a friend who needs this”","points":["All these things^ will make people interact and it will be easier to market and blow up your videos for your drop shipping store","Be creative: sit down and write as many ideas of the product you are promoting and do every single one of those and post it on every platform you can think of. Entrepreneurs, don’t pay for things with time, they pay for things by being creative. the more creative you can get the more money you will make.","Good lighting and quality-  Ofc like I say don’t go too crazy on your first posts and try to hard but definitely make sure it has processional background & good lighting to run ads","Make sure you have processional dashboard/creator mode turned on so you can see insights (this will let you see your taerget audience)","Something entertains to keep viewer engaged and make them comment.","If y’all have any extra questions, text the shark admins or me!","What this video covers: Why influencers are your best friends when starting out, and how to reach out and partner with them.","Search TikTok for influencers in your niche (e.g. \"fitness influencers\", \"skincare tips\", \"pet advice\") and look for creators who post consistently","DM them as a company: introduce your brand and what you specialize in, and offer a sponsorship","Many influencers want to earn more but don't have their own products, so there are thousands open to deals","You don't have to pay up front. Give them a custom Shopify discount code and a percentage of revenue (e.g. 25%) from sales made with it","Or offer a flat rate per video (e.g. $50 for five videos, or about $25 per video)","ChatGPT can help write outreach messages, but tailor the script to each person (a dedicated script module is coming)","The bigger goal isn't only sales: a trusted influencer in your niche builds credibility, so people trust your other marketing more"],"topics":["Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_6lSy1KqC22FfYrC9O8WsWh/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to use ai to help you reach out to creators","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"How to use AI (ChatGPT or Claude) to write outreach messages to creators so they'll promote your product.","points":["Prompt the AI to write a text, DM or email asking a creator to promote your product, and tell it what you're offering","Offer ladder: start with free product for a video, then money plus product, then commission plus product. Big creators may want payment up front","Ask the AI to make it eye-catching and natural, keep it short, and revise it until it sounds right","Explain what your brand stands for and why it fits the creator's audience","Use \"we\"/\"our\" if you have a team, which can make the outreach feel more established","Don't name an exact price or commission rate in the first message. Say you're open to paying up front or offering commission, then negotiate once they reply","Mention the long-term opportunity if their content performs well, and end with \"reply back if interested\"","An intriguing opener (e.g. \"Did you hear about this?\") can get more creators to open the DM"],"topics":["Organic marketing","AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_JoUDWiLw7jHrX/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to get ai UGC","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"How to use AI to create UGC-style organic marketing videos for your product.","points":["Keep filming your own content too, for extra videos and to save money, but AI UGC can add a lot of volume","Search TikTok for UGC creator videos in your niche (e.g. \"UGC creator protein powder\") to find examples you like","Download the video, or watch it and describe it to Claude in detail, scene by scene with timestamps (what's shown, the setting, the hands, how the product is used)","Ask Claude to generate a script for your own product based on that description that you can paste into an AI video tool (Claude is recommended over ChatGPT for scripts)","In the AI UGC software, choose an avatar (avoid overused ones), upload a picture of your product and generate the video","You can also pick existing templates, click recreate with your product, and chop up different creatives into new videos"],"topics":["Organic marketing","AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_ITyQJFJXaQrUb/","also":["Dropshipping · Scaling"]},{"kind":"lesson","title":"CLV","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"Mastering customer lifetime value (CLV) and retention so your store earns sustainable income instead of constantly chasing new customers.","points":["The common mistake: spending heavily on ads for one-time buyers who never return, like filling a leaky bucket","Keeping a customer is 5–25x cheaper than getting a new one, and repeat customers drive most revenue","CLV = average order value × purchase frequency × customer lifespan (e.g. $50 × 3/year × 2 years = $300)","Knowing your CLV lets you confidently spend more to acquire a customer, even breaking even on the first sale","Strategy 1, upsells and cross-sells: volume discounts, bundles, premium versions, and post-purchase one-click add-ons (apps like CartHook, Zipify OCU, or Shopify's built-in feature)","Strategy 2, subscriptions: find a consumable product in your niche (treats, refills, supplies) and offer subscriber perks with easy skip or pause options (Recharge, Bold)","Strategy 3, email/SMS: welcome series, abandoned cart, post-purchase and win-back flows, and review requests 7–10 days after delivery. Keep texts short, time-sensitive and easy to opt out of","Strategy 4, community: a private Facebook group, Discord or Instagram broadcast list with tips, Q&As, early access and UGC contests","Tools: Klaviyo/Omnisend (email), Postscript/Attentive (SMS), Smile.io/LoyaltyLion/Yotpo (loyalty), and GA4 or Triple Whale (analytics)","90-day roadmap: month 1, analytics, email flows and bundles; month 2, upsells, loyalty and SMS tests; month 3, subscriptions, community and a value ladder","Focus on one retention strategy at a time, and test and measure everything"],"topics":["Email & SMS"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_ts54vaJX0qVYAbR5IB1hQ/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Creating A PB for trust","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"How to build a personal brand that creates trust and helps scale your dropshipping store.","points":["Trust beats reach: 1,000 people who trust you are worth more than a million who don't. Shoppers are skeptical of faceless stores with generic supplier videos","Put a real person behind the store: your face, your hands or your voice, plus genuinely engaging with comments","Content that converts: problem/solution stories, behind-the-scenes (testing, packing, quality checks), honest before/afters and free educational tips that feature your product","Short-form video (TikTok, Reels, Shorts) gives huge free reach. Spend 30 minutes a day making 3–5 videos of 15–60 seconds. Longer YouTube videos can tell your story and build deeper trust","Be relatable and raw: minimal editing, a conversational tone, and talk to viewers like friends","Transparency is your secret weapon: unboxings, honest reviews that mention flaws or shipping times, and showing how you resolve customer issues","Trust path: discovery, then visiting your profile, connecting, following, purchasing and coming back","30-day launch: week 1, find your niche and film 20 pieces of content; week 2, post daily across platforms; week 3, refine what works; week 4, scale with UGC, email and micro-influencers","Set up a personal account that promotes your store (face photo, clear bio, call to action linking to the store), respond to every comment, and post even if it feels uncomfortable at first"],"topics":["Website","Organic marketing","Suppliers & shipping","Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_2mxWIN6dX9bZ32ETWlqYLp/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Mixing Personal Brand with your store🔥","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"How to mix a personal brand with your store using a two-account strategy to build trust.","points":["Most shoppers need to trust a brand before they buy. A human face (yours, a friend's, or even just your hands and voice) separates you from faceless stores","Two-account strategy: a personal account that humanizes the business, plus a professional brand account (logo, product showcases, promotions)","Personal account setup: a clear face photo, a 2–3 sentence authentic bio about what you help people with, and a link to your store","Content that connects: life stories, your workspace and packing, business wins and losses (e.g. switching to a better supplier), and everyday moments","Platform tips: Instagram for lifestyle posts with some business, TikTok for unpolished authentic content, X for quick thoughts that can go viral, LinkedIn for business connections","Subtle selling: mention your store naturally (\"just added this product I can't stop using\"), with about 90% authentic content and 10% business","Timeline: month 1, just share your story and post consistently; months 2–3, start weaving in your business; after that, promote more openly","Action steps: set up the account, post 4–5 times daily, and genuinely engage with 10+ people in your niche every day"],"topics":["Website","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_632FYuK5FJkkuoX1oruxrK/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Don't Put Too Much Energy INTO THESE!!","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"The things people waste too much energy on when starting or running an e-commerce store.","points":["Over-designing your website: customers come for the product, not your color scheme. Keep the site clean and functional and move on","Over-perfecting organic content: spending 15+ minutes on a 5-second video leads to burnout. Focus on pushing out consistent, valuable content instead of perfect videos","Over-perfecting your product: make sure the design and quality are good, but don't obsess over tiny details. Your energy is better spent elsewhere","Listing too many products: you only need 1–3 products. Stores that sell everything (dog beds, treadmills, etc.) burn out before they ever get to marketing","Upselling is worth your energy, especially when you're first starting out"],"topics":[],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_chC7eb3gq5ZwdB8D8IzNK/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Scaling With OM","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"The biggest mistakes people make with organic marketing, and how one viral post can scale your store when the right funnels are in place.","points":["Mistake 1, expecting fast results: treat each post like a lottery ticket. The more consistently you post, the better your odds","One video with a million views, plus email/SMS funnels to capture contacts and bring buyers back, can turn into six figures over time","Mistake 2, over-producing posts: spend no more than about 15 minutes per video. An hour on one video could have been 10 videos","Mistake 3, very low-quality posts: blurry screenshots from Google, Alibaba or Zendrop won't go viral. Aim for decent, not perfect","Mistake 4, not using the \"three W's\": make content people can comment on, react to and interact with, like the top videos on your own feed","Mistake 5, not posting enough: post 3–5 decent videos a day. A member who stayed consistent for over a month with no results eventually had a video take off and changed his business","Mistake 6, too many hashtags: stick to 3–5 relevant ones (e.g. fitness, viral, fyp, gym). Avoid tags that make your product easy for competitors to find"],"topics":["Website","Organic marketing","Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_71uZhZJF6SZxsIN16wmzdT/","also":["Clothing Brand"]},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Don't make these 3 mistakes!!","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"Three e-commerce deal breakers that kill sales, and how to fix each one.","points":["Mistake 1, ignoring your audience: organic marketing is for finding your audience. Focus on one niche so customers feel connected to your brand, like Nike or Jordan","Broad targeting leads to low click-through and conversion rates, while competitors win by targeting one audience well (even Amazon started with just books)","Be an audience detective: use Facebook ads data, Google Analytics and surveys to see where your buyers live, then focus influencers and ads on those areas","Build detailed customer personas from the profiles of people engaging with your content (age, location, interests, pain points). A personal brand in your niche helps here too","Mistake 2, an over-complicated checkout: too many products or unclear steps cause cart abandonment","Turn on as many payment options as possible in Shopify (PayPal, Apple Pay, etc.), allow guest checkout, and keep forms short","Mistake 3, neglecting mobile: most social traffic shops on phones, so check your site in mobile view, fix oversized text and unclear prices, and test the full checkout on your own phone"],"topics":[],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_QMKkIEPEgerPx/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Tracking audience with OM","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"How to figure out your target audience from the comments on organic marketing videos, plus creative marketing ideas from real examples.","points":["Look at who is commenting on organic videos for your product, or similar ones: names, profile pictures and how they write show their likely gender, age and lifestyle","Example: a self-care product's comments were mostly women around their 20s to middle age, likely city or apartment dwellers, so that's who to target with Facebook ads","Example: a protein/fitness product's comments skewed toward men","Use these patterns to confirm your audience before you spend money on ads","Clever trick: writing your shop name on your hand while demoing the product means viewers see your brand repeatedly in the video","Realistic-looking AI videos can work for organic marketing, but use real people for paid ads","Get creative with your hooks (e.g. calling out overpriced alternatives) to grab attention"],"topics":["Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_JkU5Ye6A12Tx6/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to Handle Customer Service","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"How to handle customer service for your store, from common tickets to response-time goals and SOPs.","points":["Customer service is about answering quickly, solving problems, preventing chargebacks and protecting your reputation","\"Where is my order?\" makes up about half of all tickets. Reply with a friendly template: the order is in transit, the tracking number, and a note to allow a few extra days","Damaged item: ask for photos of the packaging and product, then send a replacement or store credit","Refund requests: check whether it shipped and was delivered. If it's defective, get proof and send it to your supplier, then follow your store's policy","Always ask for the order number and proof before resolving an issue","Wrong address: if it hasn't shipped, update it right away. If it has, contact the carrier and explain the options, and still try to work it out to keep the customer","KPIs: respond within 12 hours (ideally under 4), resolve within 24–72 hours, keep chargebacks under 1%, and track complaints, reviews and repeat buyers","Write an SOP for each common issue (e.g. order not received: verify tracking, check the ship date, contact the carrier) so you can hand it to a customer service VA as you grow"],"topics":["Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_FYXcqoAesxwAj/","also":["Dropshipping · Scaling"]},{"kind":"lesson","title":"MORE pointers to winning product","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"More pointers on what makes a winning product, including the psychology behind why people buy.","points":["It's something people need, not just want. Market it as solving a real problem (e.g. a diffuser for congestion, or one that helps clear skin)","It makes someone's life better, like electric toothbrushes or hiring a maid. Highlight that on your site (FAQ, testimonials)","Even a winning product won't sell if you don't market how it improves people's lives","Small upgrades create loyalty. Example: adding a satin lining to hoodie hoods (protects hair) built a loyal fan base that keeps coming back and tells friends","It's hard to find in stores. If people can buy it at Walmart for a few dollars, it won't sell at a markup","It doesn't need to be totally unique, just a version people can't easily get (e.g. a teddy bear that records and plays back a loved one's voice)"],"topics":["Product research"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_6gnVc2LgKQ8zl/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Customizing your own product","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"How to customize products on Alibaba so you can build a more unique, branded store.","points":["Customizing often means a different fulfillment method: you order stock to yourself and ship it out, unless your supplier will brand and dropship it for you","Search your niche on Alibaba (e.g. fitness supplements, skincare) and scroll past the first few results to find less common products","Use \"Send inquiry\" (better than \"Chat now\") to ask about custom labels, flavors, packaging or adding your logo","Check the minimum order quantity (MOQ). Some products require around 1,000 units","Consider a separate Alibaba account for research so your main account's recommendations don't get cluttered","Alibaba's secure payment and money-back guarantee protect you if a supplier doesn't ship","Avoid generic items people can grab at Target (like basic pimple patches) unless you can market and brand them differently","For regular dropshipping, stick with Zendrop, CJ Dropshipping or AutoDS. Branded, customized products can help you grow faster"],"topics":[],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_7T9YuXiqaor5T/"},{"kind":"pdf","title":"Don't do too many products","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"Why a store with 2–4 focused products beats a store with hundreds, and how to build around one hero product.","points":["Fewer products = higher conversions; too many choices causes decision fatigue and no sale","A tight lineup looks like a real brand, not a random catalog, so trust goes up","Ads get cheaper and clearer: you know what to test, creatives are focused and the data is clean","You can properly optimize product pages, upsells and bundles","Fewer suppliers, fewer fulfillment and support issues, faster scaling","Easy to swap out a losing product; start with 1 hero product and expand based on data"],"topics":[],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_VZHoAUkI9svfu/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"HOW TO KNOW WINNING PRODUCT","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"How to tell whether a product is a hit or not, and why there's really no such thing as a pure \"loser\" product.","points":["Almost any product can win with the right business behind it. Big brands sell average products all the time. It's about how you market it","Traction isn't the same as sales: judge a product by engagement, hype and site sessions across your ads, organic posts and UGC","If engagement, sessions and hype are all low across every channel, that's a key warning sign","Give it a fair test: post 100+ organic videos across multiple platforms (TikTok, Snapchat, YouTube, IG), not just one. This is a business, not a get-rich-quick scheme","Read the comments: \"Where do I buy?\" or \"When is it dropping?\" are green flags, and any positive comment is a good sign you just need more reach","Silence (no comments at all) is a worse sign than hate","Don't get discouraged by negative comments. Hate often means you're doing something right"],"topics":["Product research"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_TRHkHbG1ddNQ2/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Niches For Products (BEST ONES)","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"The best product niches to dominate now and for years to come, with example products for each.","points":["Pick products that improve people's lives or meet a real need, ideally with repeat purchases (like a pharmacy's refills)","Health and wellness: growing every year as people age. Examples: posture correctors, wellness journals, sleep optimization products. Pair with small influencers and authentic testimonials","Pets: owners buy repeatedly and will spend on premium products for their pets. Examples: interactive smart feeders, pet cameras with treat dispensers, eco-friendly or dental toys","Tech accessories: always in demand (gaming setups, work-from-home). Examples: MagSafe accessories, wireless chargers with a niche twist","Home fitness: many people want to work out but avoid the gym. Look for unique at-home equipment beyond basic dumbbells, since thousands of untapped products exist on AliExpress, Zendrop, AutoDS and CJ","Beauty, skincare and eco-friendly: shoppers want natural products that work better than store brands, which drives repeat buys","Home decor and hobbies: people constantly upgrade their spaces (aesthetic lights, posters, decor)","Fashion/streetwear: sell upgraded versions of everyday items (e.g. shorts with an inside pocket so your phone doesn't fall out)","Parenting and kids: parents spend heavily on educational and learning products year-round"],"topics":["Product research"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_3cT6gN4R3Vjj0T80Hmascr/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Scaling Products","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"How to spot viral trends and use them to promote your products.","points":["Stay on top of trends; brands have blown up by tapping into what's viral (e.g. back-to-school content)","Make a separate account just for tracking trends","Think about how your product fits into popular videos","Example: if your product solves a school problem, stitch a viral video and show it off","Get creative and use trends to go viral yourself"],"topics":["Product research","Organic marketing","Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_HOzdup1VrpgGL/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Using TikTok for product research","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"Using TikTok to research products and study what competitors are posting.","points":["TikTok is one of the best product research tools. Search your niche (e.g. \"fitness items\", \"workout items\") and scroll through the videos","Example math: if just 1% of a viral video's viewers buy a ~$37 product, that could be tens of thousands in sales from one post","Treat organic posts like lottery tickets: post every day and eventually some will hit","Organic first is good practice for learning your targeting and how to make good ads, but you can start ads whenever you feel ready (it just costs more)","Creators who promote products in your niche are worth DMing for promotion","Successful pages often reuse the same footage to make many different videos, so it's not as hard as it looks","Once a page finds its target audience organically, it usually moves into running ads","Keep researching on TikTok to find new product ideas, even if you have to customize them for your niche"],"topics":["Product research","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_BZs7QHuD7Kcwf/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"WINNING PRODUCT CRITERIA!","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"The criteria for a winning product, rated by how important each one is, plus red flags to avoid.","points":["Solves a problem (very important): makes life easier, removes frustration, or saves time or money (e.g. a one-step apple slicer, a grippier pen)","Wow factor: something people have never seen before makes them stop scrolling and is easier to market","Impulse buy (less important): products under about $50–$100 that don't need much research are easier to buy on impulse, and price and offer drive this","Broad but niche audience: e.g. fitness is niche but open to anyone who works out. Avoid going so niche that your market is capped","Good profit margin: aim for 2–4x your product cost. 35%+ margin is decent when you're starting out","Easy to explain (very important): customers should understand the benefit within about 35 seconds, and you can show the problem and solution visually","Passion or pain market (very important): health, pets, beauty, home improvement, parenting, fitness, hobbies","Not easily found in local stores (mildly important): gives people a reason to order online","Red flags: fragile items, super-low margins, copyrighted or trademarked items (anything with someone else's logo), and products that need extensive customer education","Seasonal products can work. Ride the season, then evolve the store into a related year-round niche"],"topics":["Product research"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_TLH854miVxEZA/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Riding QUAD trend","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"How to spot and ride trends early in your niche, and how to test and launch trend products without overspending.","points":["Successful entrepreneurs spot trends 3–6 months early. Being first gives you an edge, better supplier pricing and higher margins before prices rise","Building on a trend with a twist (e.g. a new beanie style during a beanie trend) can make you an authority in that space","Trend-watch systems: burner TikTok accounts that follow 100+ accounts in your niche, monitoring hashtags, tracking competitor launches, and tools like Exploding Topics or BuzzSumo","Look at seasonal and long-term growth patterns so you can launch before competitors catch on","Join communities in your niche to hear about trends early","Post on every platform, but focus on value. After 100–140 posts, start improving quality","Partner with 10–20 smaller niche creators with strong engagement before celebrities, and send them samples before launch","Test in small batches (50–100 units or pre-orders) and aim to sell out quickly once your marketing foundation is set","Watch your KPIs: sell-out rate, profit margin and return rate (under 10%), and gather customer feedback","Make content shareable: quick unboxings, show the product in use so viewers can picture owning it, offer multiple colors, and film from flattering angles","If you're still on your first product, focus on mastering product research, your store and marketing before using these advanced strategies"],"topics":["Product research"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_6cp8ffRXIk3o7HO8KLwrUj/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"watch your competitors","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"Why and how to watch your competitors in e-commerce, using them for motivation and opportunities rather than envy.","points":["Why: know where your pricing stands (don't massively underprice or overprice compared with competitors), and notice when competitors shift products","Keep an abundance mindset: \"if they can do it, I can too.\" Use competitors as motivation, never jealousy","Find gaps you can improve on, like extra colors, better materials or features customers complain about in their reviews","Defensive strategy: check their website regularly (e.g. screen-record it every couple of weeks) to spot new sales, offers or changes, and act fast when something is working","Know your direct competitors (same product, check them 3–4 times a week), indirect competitors (similar marketing or products) and fast-rising new ones","Track prices, SEO keywords, reviews and social conversations (e.g. Reddit). This can be done manually in about an hour a week, or delegated","Five things to monitor: pricing, site experience (e.g. pop-ups collecting email, phone and name), shipping speed and costs, product selection, and how they promote","Example: Target closely tracks Walmart's pricing to stay competitive","Stay legal and ethical: don't copy logos or trademarked designs, impersonate employees or bribe anyone for information. Learn from their strategy and make it your own"],"topics":["Mindset","Product research"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_64Wmu0X2rrs0JHnTBW5yQK/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Mind games: How Wording Affects Sales","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"How the wording on your store, down to a single word, affects buying psychology, using a real store's homepage banner as the example.","points":["After Black Friday, the store's banner changed from a fall sale to a seasonal \"winter cozy sale\" that matches its best-selling hoodies","The wording should match the feeling of the product you're featuring. A \"summer sale\" next to hoodies would feel wrong to shoppers","\"Winter\" alone makes people think of puffer coats, so combining it with \"cozy\" fits hoodies better","One wrong word (e.g. naming a style or feature some shoppers don't want) can make people scroll past everything","Sell the feeling behind the product, not just the item, and keep your best sellers front and center with messaging that fits the season"],"topics":["Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_QBpWKtnkzSoRT/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Understand \"who is on what?\"","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"Which age groups are on which social platforms, so you know where to push hardest for your product's audience.","points":["TikTok: reaches both very young and older audiences","Snapchat: younger to middle-aged audience","Pinterest: young adults (around 21–23)","Facebook and Messenger: mainly older users (around 40–50+)","Threads and YouTube Shorts: mainly teens and younger users","Instagram: a middle mix of ages","Match your niche to the platforms where its audience is strongest (e.g. fitness leans middle ages, skincare reaches both young and older)","Still post every video on all platforms, since anyone can be anywhere and a video might go viral on one platform but not another. It only takes seconds to repost"],"topics":[],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_8h7iM6MrFyMYX/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Shipping rates","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"A quick example of how to set up simple shipping rates for your store.","points":["Instead of complex calculated shipping, you can set up simple shipping zones and flat rates","Example setup: free shipping on orders of $150+, economy (1–5 lbs) at $12, economy (5–70 lbs) at $21, and international economy at $43","Around $43 is a reasonable average for international shipping given current costs"],"topics":["Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_l9t4zjOhRoBd9/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to verify supplier are legit","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"","points":[],"topics":["Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_sZD2XrWiWtmYL/","pending":true},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Test supplier on zendrop","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"How to check and test suppliers on Zendrop before adding their products to your store.","points":["Search your niche on Zendrop and scroll past the first results to find products","Open a product and check its details, especially the shipping country (e.g. United States) and average shipping time (e.g. 2 days)","Tip: create a separate Zendrop account for product research so you see fresh product suggestions","Zendrop suppliers are generally legit, since they want repeat business","Red flag: \"suppliers\" who message you out of nowhere on WhatsApp, with no platform behind them to verify they're real","Zendrop and AutoDS are B2C dropshipping (they ship straight to your customer), while Alibaba is mostly B2B (shipped to you first), though some Alibaba suppliers also dropship"],"topics":["Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_CWG7EmXEg5rpu/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to hire safely & smart","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"How to hire people for your store safely and smartly, and what to look out for.","points":["You can hire on Fiverr or similar sites, or hire family and friends, but above all make sure they're good, trustworthy people","Don't give full access right away. Start with limited permissions and increase them only after people prove themselves","Customer service hires: look for clear communication, quick responses and the ability to stay calm. Ask how they'd handle an angry customer whose package is late","Media buyers: ask what budgets and niches they've managed, their testing strategies and how they handle losing campaigns. Watch for people who blame the algorithm for everything","Start with a small paid test task (product research, competitor research, data entry or customer service). A one-hour test can save months of headaches","Look for reliability: fast responses, showing up on time and following instructions exactly","Protect your store with contracts, NDAs and confidentiality agreements","Use separate logins for each person, and remove access or change passwords when someone leaves"],"topics":["Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_JyQbVHNtXoGsv/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"HIRE ANYONE!","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"A quick look at using Fiverr to hire people for almost any task in your business.","points":["Fiverr is one of the best places to find people for quick tasks","Search for UGC creators and you'll find tons of options for product videos","You can hire Shopify customer service help for as little as about $5","There are niche services too (e.g. funny pet or animal content for a pet store)","Whatever you need done, search Fiverr first before doing it all yourself"],"topics":["Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_fli9Z4QYXdNLp/","also":["Dropshipping · Logistics"]},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Which ai is best for what (masterclass)","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"A live masterclass on which AI tool is best for each part of your e-commerce business, plus how much AI to use in ads versus organic content.","points":["Q&A: you can run your store while living abroad. The main change is your posting schedule, and check with your bank about any differences","Use AI where it helps, but keep a real person and personality behind your brand so customers trust you","Marketing scripts and prompts: Claude (more in-depth and doesn't just tell you what you want to hear)","Captions and email copywriting: ChatGPT (sounds more human and is faster)","Product research: Claude does deeper research, though ChatGPT has improved a lot","Images: ChatGPT by far (Claude doesn't generate images)","Videos: Higgsfield, with Claude writing the video scripts or prompts (the two can be connected)","Organic content: AI UGC works great, so consider a separate account just for AI UGC, and turn viral AI videos into paid ads","Paid ads: keep them mostly human (about 75% human, 25% AI), since too much obvious AI can hurt conversions and may get flagged by Meta","Store product photos, banners and site text can be AI-generated to save time and money","Ads and creatives are separate skills, and existing marketing modules cover Facebook ads in depth"],"topics":["Calls","Organic marketing","Paid ads","AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_fnkmv6UOwygUl/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to embed Base44 in Shopify","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"How to embed something you built on Base44 into your Shopify store.","points":["In Shopify, create a new page (or section) where you want the Base44 build to show up","Ask Claude or ChatGPT to create an iframe embed code for you","Copy the link to your Base44 project and put it into the iframe code","Paste the iframe code into Shopify, save, and click preview to check that it shows up","If it glitches, delete the code and paste it in again","If you get a \"not available\" error, go to Base44's dashboard, then Security settings, and turn off the setting that blocks embedding, then reload","Base44 can't be added as a Shopify app (they're likely competitors), so embedding is the workaround. Wix makes embedding easier"],"topics":["Website","AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_ZWgF2HyDrMgvz/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to use ChatGPT 6 Astra","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"An overview of what the new ChatGPT model (referred to in the video as \"ChatGPT 6 Astra\") can do for your e-commerce store.","points":["Websites: it can build front-end landing pages (FAQs, product options, cart design), though it's not the same as an agency building the back end too","Ads: it can develop concepts, headlines, primary text and image variations. Use competitor ads as inspiration, not to replicate them","Numbers: it helps you understand your ad spend versus revenue (ROAS) and why ads are over- or under-spending","Store improvements: send screenshots of your store to get suggestions for better product images and FAQs","Email: it can write campaigns and automations like abandoned cart and restock announcements","Operations: it helps organize tasks for VAs and employees","Use your own thinking first, and treat AI as a helper rather than something that does everything for you. More detailed how-to videos are coming"],"topics":["AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_T0UspYpdt5EXm/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to make a website on ChatGPT 6 Astra","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"A walkthrough of building a full e-commerce website with the new ChatGPT model (\"ChatGPT 6 Astra\") from a short prompt.","points":["Start with a website-building prompt and fill in your details: brand name, product (e.g. bath bombs) and target customer","Knowing your target customer from organic marketing helps here (e.g. women or moms on Facebook)","Ask for a polished, established-looking site with a home page, strong headline and persuasive copy","The prompt doesn't need to be extremely long to get a good result","In ChatGPT, select the new model, paste the prompt, and let it build","In about 10 minutes it generated a complete site with product images and an FAQ section, and you can change images or add more afterward","It's a free way to build a website, and more prompts are coming in the next module"],"topics":["Website","AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_zrNR6WBIxIY10/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Good use of ai!","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"A real example of a store that grew fast using AI-generated marketing videos, and what you can learn from it.","points":["A crochet product store whose TikTok videos are almost all AI-generated, with some reaching around a million views","The store reportedly did thousands of orders in about a month from this approach","They use a bundle offer to raise order value. The main thing missing is an email pop-up","Lesson: find a good product, figure out how to make that style of AI video for it, and post consistently","Use stores like this for inspiration only. Don't copy their product or ads"],"topics":["AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_ZJLYJr31WXM4f/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"5 biggest mistakes in MEO","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"The 5 biggest mistakes people make when starting (or running) their e-commerce store.","points":["Mistake 1, not sleeping on your ideas: take action, but before you finalize a logo, name or product (ordering samples, launching marketing), wait overnight and re-check it with fresh eyes","Mistake 2, asking \"will it work?\" The real question is \"will you work?\" With 8 billion people in the world, even a tiny fraction is a huge potential audience","Mistake 3, a limited mindset: no brand has sold to everyone, and new online shoppers appear every day. Don't be scared off because a big brand sells something similar. Out-market them instead","Mistake 4, focusing too much on how your store looks: when starting, you just need a decent store, a pop-up to collect contact info and a good domain","Mistake 5, limiting your marketing: post on every platform you can and do anything that puts your product in front of more people"],"topics":["Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_BsBmtUoy46UtX/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Fix ur mistakes!","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"An intro to the \"mistakes\" section of the course and how to get the most out of it.","points":["The upcoming videos focus on common mistakes, some the mentor made himself and some he sees members make often","A big part of mentorship is learning what not to do, saving you time, money and energy","Avoiding these mistakes helps your store succeed faster","Watch all the videos and take notes, adding a \"don't do this\" note under the matching section (website, marketing, etc.)"],"topics":[],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_IMRrHMJ8ejlA2/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Mistakes to avoid in ECOM! (call)","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"A live call on four big mistakes to avoid in e-commerce, plus Q&A on CTAs, suppliers and scaling ads.","points":["Mistake 1, spending too much time on website looks: focus on systems instead. Put your product on the home page, use clear Add to Cart/Buy Now buttons and a fast checkout, and add a pop-up. Marketing gets the sales, not fancy design (look at how simple Amazon makes buying)","Mistake 2, being inconsistent with organic: it's free ads (1,000 views is roughly $1,500 of ad spend saved), it shows you your audience, and it's practice for making good creatives before paid ads. You don't need to show your face","Mistake 3, putting off CRM: collect emails and phone numbers from day one. Big brands use pop-ups, and even 1% of a 10,000-person list buying adds up. Klaviyo gives you 250 contacts free. Test emails landing in junk is normal","Mistake 4, comparing yourself to others: \"don't let your plate get cold looking at someone else's food.\" Learn from competitors, but don't jump between products or business ideas chasing what others are doing","Q&A: add a private supplier as a fulfillment location in Shopify's shipping profiles (no plan upgrade needed)","Q&A: put CTAs in your bio and comments (e.g. reply to a top comment with \"link in bio\") rather than heavily in captions, which platforms may suppress","Q&A: a ~5% click-through rate on Meta is excellent (average is about 1.2%). Scale the budget by no more than 10% per day and test more creatives with different models","Set goals around freedom and value, not just a number, and get wins outside of money too (gym, faith, personal growth)"],"topics":["Calls","Paid ads","Suppliers & shipping","Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_Tt4fxic6Iun0S/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"What to do if problems come up!","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"What to do when technical issues or other problems come up while running your store.","points":["The mentor's personal first step is to pray, then work out what kind of problem it is","Decide whether it's a technical issue (e.g. Shopify showing an error when adding payments or an app) or a personal/account issue (e.g. you got logged out or forgot your password)","Technical issue: contact the company involved, such as Shopify, your payment processor, or your supplier app (Zendrop, AutoDS)","Personal or setup issue: ask your personal coach or post in the Discord, since someone has likely dealt with it before","Fastest fix: do both. Contact the company's support and post in the Discord at the same time","The team will help research unusual issues, but they're business mentors, not tech support, so some problems will still need the platform's support team"],"topics":[],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_dfqKBjbP8l8aY/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Daily store requirements!","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"The daily tasks you should do to run your store, which take about an hour and 20 minutes a day.","points":["Push out marketing and check on it (~35 min): post organic videos, DM creators, run Facebook ads, then see which creators replied, who your organic content is reaching, and which ads perform best","Stay updated on products and trends (~30 min): scroll a burner TikTok account whose feed is full of other dropshippers and brands, and look for trending topics you can tie into your store (e.g. looksmaxxing, or a hat with a fan when everyone's talking about the heat)","Check your email and watch competitors (Facebook Ad Library, etc.). If they jump on a new product or marketing trend, you should too","Customer service: answer customer emails quickly, such as \"where's my order?\" (AI can help draft replies)","Check Shopify (~5–10 min) to make sure orders are shipping","Top two priorities: pushing out marketing and staying on top of trends and products, since these drive sales and scaling","At minimum, do the free stuff daily (organic content and DMing creators). Getting a sample to film your own videos helps a lot"],"topics":[],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_LTe1ZNL9AZgWC/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"What's branding?","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"What branding your dropshipping store and products means, along with the pros, cons and when to do it.","points":["Branding makes your store look and feel like a real business. At minimum, your store should have a name and logo","Branding the product means having your supplier put your logo on the item itself","Branding the product isn't mandatory, but branding your store is pretty necessary","Pros: people recognize your product in ads, you build a stronger brand presence (like Gatorade), a more loyal fan base, less competition, and more money","Cons: it can cost a little more per piece (e.g. ~$0.10–$0.20), and you may have to hold inventory if your supplier won't brand and dropship","Message your supplier (e.g. on Zendrop) to ask if they can add your logo and how much extra it costs per piece. The added value also lets you raise your price","Before branding, make sure it's the right product by getting some wins first (sales or strong interest like \"where can I get this?\")","Ask yourself if you're ready to hold inventory. If your supplier can't brand it, you may need to source the same product on Alibaba"],"topics":["Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_NaliiGh2rnhKS/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to brand it!","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"How to customize and brand a product as your own through an Alibaba supplier.","points":["Do this after you've found a winning product. It adds personality and a brand to your store","Find the product on Alibaba, click \"Chat now\", and ask if they'll let you customize it and what the price difference is","Be patient, since suppliers are often in very different time zones","Ask for their MOQ (minimum order quantity). When starting out, look for the lowest MOQ so you can sell at your own pace (e.g. clothing is often around 50)","Customization MOQs are usually higher than regular ones, but you can negotiate (e.g. \"Can I start with 100 since I'm just starting out?\")","Decide your max MOQ in advance and compare suppliers until you find one that fits. Check their reviews too","Have your logo and brand name ready for them to put on the product","Always order a sample first before placing a bulk order"],"topics":["Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_N6uKsaaya90jO/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to embed Base44 with Wix","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"How to embed something you built on Base44 into a Wix website.","points":["Copy the link to your Base44 project","In Wix, click Edit Site to open the editor (Wix's built-in AI chat can navigate you there if you can't find it)","Click Add (top left), then Elements, then Embed Code","Choose \"Embed a site\" (easier than pasting code), then paste your Base44 link","Add it, then resize and position it however you want, even full-page","This walkthrough is for Wix. A separate video covers embedding Base44 into Shopify"],"topics":["Website","AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_6MUQ23OpcQb47/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"ideas for Base44","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"Different ways to use Base44 for your e-commerce business beyond just building a website.","points":["Websites: build a polished front-end landing page, then connect it to Shopify or Wix (separate videos cover how)","Base44 gives a better real-world layout than asking ChatGPT or Claude to design a website","Content planner: ask it to build a weekly content planning system tailored to your business","Inventory tracker: create a more advanced system that warns you when stock is low or when to restock","Influencer portal: let influencers submit their videos (and even sign contracts) in one organized place","It's strong at building custom software and apps that keep your business organized","Built-in AI systems can raise your store's value if you ever sell it, since you're selling the systems along with the store"],"topics":["Website","AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_JlZ6DUHcNOLnd/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to scale store HIGHER!","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"","points":[],"topics":["Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_45ButAEIh3GjzXi5uaRazv/","pending":true},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Tips for u and ur bro","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"","points":[],"topics":[],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_3oY6d2KfPTJs3/","pending":true},{"kind":"lesson","title":"1 (more info on MEO)","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"","points":[],"topics":["Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_L2FC7uoBaeRcz/","pending":true},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Getting rich is NOT hard","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"","points":[],"topics":[],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_cmbyTZSLhebNH/","pending":true},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Change ur life!!","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"","points":[],"topics":[],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_rkvNhVzRvGx1o/","pending":true},{"kind":"lesson","title":"ATTRACTED","where":"Dropshipping","summary":"","points":[],"topics":[],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_4zBGAwqkc64ACfUWpiHn1U/lessons/lesn_kdApXqB8Y4riM/","pending":true},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Watch this FIRST!","where":"Dropshipping · Intro","summary":"How the Dropshipping INNER CIRCLE modules are organized and how to choose the way you want to learn.","points":["The big \"Inner circle DROPSHIPPING\" course has all the modules in one place (about 18+ hours)","The same videos are also split into organized sections: Intro, Logistics, Website Maxxing, Marketing, Product Selection and Scaling","You don't need to watch both, since they're the same modules. Watch the full course or the separated sections, whichever fits how you learn","Intro is short (under ~5 minutes) and covers what the program is about and what to do first","Logistics is around an hour and covers the details people often forget","Website is around 4 hours and Marketing around 6 hours. These are the most important, money-making sections","Product Selection is shorter: find a good product and build a niche around it","Scaling is around 2 hours, for about 22 hours total including the recorded calls (plus PDF lessons)","More modules will keep being added and updated over time"],"topics":["Getting started","Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_EHSYxMip9NdjG/lessons/lesn_WcwiQTAmJ3mp3/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"What the Dropshipping Inner Circle Is","where":"Dropshipping · Intro","summary":"What the Dropshipping Inner Circle is and where it fits in your learning path.","points":["Dropshipping is a fulfillment model (how orders get shipped), not a business model on its own.","These are the advanced dropshipping modules, and everything is taught in a way that ties into MEO (multi-store e-commerce).","Make sure you understand the beginner material first, then complete this Inner Circle section.","Only after finishing here should you move on to MEO, which covers scaling, advanced tactics and exiting (selling) your store.","Module requests are welcome."],"topics":["Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_EHSYxMip9NdjG/lessons/lesn_ZJWaJ4qDbMZNx/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Organization- before you watch modules","where":"Dropshipping · Intro","summary":"How to stay organized while working through the advanced modules so you actually apply everything.","points":["There are two ways to work: take action as you watch, or take notes first and act after. Either is fine as long as you take action.","If you act as you go: at the end of each category (Website, Product, Marketing, etc.), go back through every module and check that your store has what each one says.","If you take notes first: make sure you have notes on every module in the category before you start implementing.","Taking notes is recommended either way.","Don't skip or skim. Small tips in these videos can save you time, money and mistakes, and can help scale your store."],"topics":[],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_EHSYxMip9NdjG/lessons/lesn_bfvyMCADAbz7i/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Before watching IC (PRODUCTS)","where":"Dropshipping · Product Selection","summary":"What you need to know before starting the product section: pick your path and know what a product and a winning product are.","points":["Find your product (and niche) before building the website","Two paths: learn first then do, or do as you go; if doing as you go, have a first product ready to test","If you don't know what a product is yet, go back to the beginner group first","Some beginner material is repeated here as a refresher"],"topics":["Product research"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_WXIXCTeTsX0Ir/lessons/lesn_lwrsOOsuXcbu7/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"What is a \"Winning\" Product?","where":"Dropshipping · Product Selection","summary":"More pointers on what makes a winning product, including the psychology behind why people buy.","points":["It's something people need, not just want. Market it as solving a real problem (e.g. a diffuser for congestion, or one that helps clear skin)","It makes someone's life better, like electric toothbrushes or hiring a maid. Highlight that on your site (FAQ, testimonials)","Even a winning product won't sell if you don't market how it improves people's lives","Small upgrades create loyalty. Example: adding a satin lining to hoodie hoods (protects hair) built a loyal fan base that keeps coming back and tells friends","It's hard to find in stores. If people can buy it at Walmart for a few dollars, it won't sell at a markup","It doesn't need to be totally unique, just a version people can't easily get (e.g. a teddy bear that records and plays back a loved one's voice)"],"topics":["Product research"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_WXIXCTeTsX0Ir/lessons/lesn_l3ZLUYcKV2mqa/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Signs if Your Product is/isn't a winner","where":"Dropshipping · Product Selection","summary":"How to tell whether a product is a hit or not, and why there's really no such thing as a pure \"loser\" product.","points":["Almost any product can win with the right business behind it. Big brands sell average products all the time. It's about how you market it","Traction isn't the same as sales: judge a product by engagement, hype and site sessions across your ads, organic posts and UGC","If engagement, sessions and hype are all low across every channel, that's a key warning sign","Give it a fair test: post 100+ organic videos across multiple platforms (TikTok, Snapchat, YouTube, IG), not just one. This is a business, not a get-rich-quick scheme","Read the comments: \"Where do I buy?\" or \"When is it dropping?\" are green flags, and any positive comment is a good sign you just need more reach","Silence (no comments at all) is a worse sign than hate","Don't get discouraged by negative comments. Hate often means you're doing something right"],"topics":[],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_WXIXCTeTsX0Ir/lessons/lesn_y2jz9vIPNjlDs/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"The Criteria for a Winning Product","where":"Dropshipping · Product Selection","summary":"The criteria for a winning product, rated by how important each one is, plus red flags to avoid.","points":["Solves a problem (very important): makes life easier, removes frustration, or saves time or money (e.g. a one-step apple slicer, a grippier pen)","Wow factor: something people have never seen before makes them stop scrolling and is easier to market","Impulse buy (less important): products under about $50–$100 that don't need much research are easier to buy on impulse, and price and offer drive this","Broad but niche audience: e.g. fitness is niche but open to anyone who works out. Avoid going so niche that your market is capped","Good profit margin: aim for 2–4x your product cost. 35%+ margin is decent when you're starting out","Easy to explain (very important): customers should understand the benefit within about 35 seconds, and you can show the problem and solution visually","Passion or pain market (very important): health, pets, beauty, home improvement, parenting, fitness, hobbies","Not easily found in local stores (mildly important): gives people a reason to order online","Red flags: fragile items, super-low margins, copyrighted or trademarked items (anything with someone else's logo), and products that need extensive customer education","Seasonal products can work. Ride the season, then evolve the store into a related year-round niche"],"topics":["Product research"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_WXIXCTeTsX0Ir/lessons/lesn_Q347ECPqtbEIb/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Finding the winning products","where":"Dropshipping · Product Selection","summary":"A live masterclass on what a winning product really is, how to tell if you should switch products, and how to make a product stand out.","points":["There's no magic \"winning product\": you make a product win through marketing, your website and outreach (e.g. ambassadors). Even a toothbrush can be a best-seller with the right skills","Practice your skills with a simple product first, because a great product won't sell if you can't market it","Saturated products (sold at Walmart or Target) can still sell, but you're competing with huge brands, so you'll need to market much harder","Your first 2–3 products may not hit. Keep testing and don't give up after a week","Signs to switch: after real effort (ads plus organic on all platforms), there's no interest, no comments, not even haters, and a very low click-through rate even after trying new creatives","Site visits show interest. If people visit but don't buy, look at your site, price or offer before blaming the product","Upgrade proven products to stand out (e.g. a hoodie with a satin-lined hood so it doesn't mess up your hair), or find a supplier selling an upgraded version","Good product checklist: large market, room for margin, an emotional \"I need that\" trigger, a benefit you can show in 10–20 seconds, and a clear difference from competitors","Paid ads should show the problem then the solution so viewers picture it fixing their own problem","Look for offer potential: bundles, quantity breaks or subscriptions"],"topics":["Calls","Product research"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_WXIXCTeTsX0Ir/lessons/lesn_xaeCAssGVV11K/"},{"kind":"pdf","title":"Print this out and check it off","where":"Dropshipping · Product Selection","summary":"A printable product research checklist: tick at least 4–5 boxes before you test a product.","points":["Solves a problem or creates strong desire, and you can say who wants it in one sentence","Easy to demonstrate in a short video","Real evidence of demand: recent reviews, buyer questions, multiple sellers (views and ads alone don't prove sales)","A reason to buy from you, room for profit after product, shipping, fees, returns and ads","Reliable supplier (order a sample), practical to ship and support, can be marketed honestly","Quick profit check example: $35 price − $10 product − $5 shipping − $2 fees = $18 before ads; at $12 per purchase, $6 left"],"topics":["Product research"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_WXIXCTeTsX0Ir/lessons/lesn_9488cm9YKBzQs/"},{"kind":"pdf","title":"Winning Product Scorecard","where":"Dropshipping · Product Selection","summary":"Score a product before you spend money testing it: margin, price, wow factor, problem solved.","points":[],"topics":["Product research"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_WXIXCTeTsX0Ir/lessons/lesn_pBjXxlZcACPEq/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to Pump Test + When to Kill a Product (Call)","where":"Dropshipping · Product Selection","summary":"A masterclass on when to kill a product, why you should run a niche store, and how to give every product a real test before giving up on it.","points":["Killing a product just means you stop promoting it; you can keep it on the site to help raise AOV","Never name your store after one product (e.g. \"White Humidifier\"); build a niche store (e.g. a wellness store) so you can swap products without rebuilding your domain and logo","Don't judge on one signal (SEO, Google Trends or competitors alone); marketing is the real test","Organic: post 25–50 videos and look for comments, DMs and follows asking where to get it, not just sales","Ads: run 3–5 creatives; a CTR above ~1% means people are interested. Ignore conversion rate while testing, since that's a website problem","\"Treat it like a winner until you find out it isn't\": don't go in expecting your first product to fail, but keep testing others too","Ads need budget, organic needs posts, not time; a UGC creator posting about it is a cheap fast test","Q&A: put the product on your niche store first, then market it; suppliers like Zendrop/CJ fulfill orders automatically once connected; private suppliers vs. apps; you need a real domain, not a .myshopify.com link"],"topics":["Calls","Product research"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_WXIXCTeTsX0Ir/lessons/lesn_8a0lu5k7Lx2K2/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Using TikTok to Research Products","where":"Dropshipping · Product Selection","summary":"Using TikTok to research products and study what competitors are posting.","points":["TikTok is one of the best product research tools. Search your niche (e.g. \"fitness items\", \"workout items\") and scroll through the videos","Example math: if just 1% of a viral video's viewers buy a ~$37 product, that could be tens of thousands in sales from one post","Treat organic posts like lottery tickets: post every day and eventually some will hit","Organic first is good practice for learning your targeting and how to make good ads, but you can start ads whenever you feel ready (it just costs more)","Creators who promote products in your niche are worth DMing for promotion","Successful pages often reuse the same footage to make many different videos, so it's not as hard as it looks","Once a page finds its target audience organically, it usually moves into running ads","Keep researching on TikTok to find new product ideas, even if you have to customize them for your niche"],"topics":["Product research","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_WXIXCTeTsX0Ir/lessons/lesn_iBi5Qd0rTGJ34/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Finding Products That Are Scaling","where":"Dropshipping · Product Selection","summary":"How to spot viral trends and use them to promote your products.","points":["Stay on top of trends; brands have blown up by tapping into what's viral (e.g. back-to-school content)","Make a separate account just for tracking trends","Think about how your product fits into popular videos","Example: if your product solves a school problem, stitch a viral video and show it off","Get creative and use trends to go viral yourself"],"topics":["Product research","Organic marketing","Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_WXIXCTeTsX0Ir/lessons/lesn_j4oD8dLD9gDnb/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Leveraging Trends to Find Best Products","where":"Dropshipping · Product Selection","summary":"How to ride trending videos by \"stitching\" them on TikTok to promote your product.","points":["Find trending videos (millions of views) in a category related to your product.","Use TikTok's Stitch feature (Share → Stitch) to put the viral clip first, then show how your product solves the problem in it.","Example: someone slips in a viral clip, and you stitch it showing grippy shoes that would have prevented it.","This lets you borrow the attention of a video that's already blowing up instead of starting from zero.","Keep claims honest. Show what your product actually does."],"topics":["Product research","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_WXIXCTeTsX0Ir/lessons/lesn_1R20z3ZQKsaTZ/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Using Google Trends to Find Winning Prods","where":"Dropshipping · Product Selection","summary":"How to use Google Trends to spot what people are searching for and build product ideas around those trends.","points":["Check Google Trends' \"Trending now\" regularly (daily or every couple of days) to see what's hot, e.g. big games, events, viral foods.","Look for topics trending over the past 7 days (and past 48 hours), not just a 24-hour spike. Lasting trends are safer bets.","Use the past 4–24 hours to catch early trends before everyone else.","Filter by category (sports, entertainment, business, food) to find trends that match your store's niche.","Turn a trend into a product angle: e.g. sports-fan products around big games, or event-related items a couple of weeks before (like the Super Bowl).","Never use trademarked logos or copyrighted material. Get close to the trend without infringing.","Once you spot a trend, find a related product on your supplier (e.g. Zendrop) and start promoting it immediately, organically or with Facebook ads."],"topics":["Product research"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_WXIXCTeTsX0Ir/lessons/lesn_6bA5yGz8Q47WC/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to Customize Your Product","where":"Dropshipping · Product Selection","summary":"How to customize products on Alibaba so you can build a more unique, branded store.","points":["Customizing often means a different fulfillment method: you order stock to yourself and ship it out, unless your supplier will brand and dropship it for you","Search your niche on Alibaba (e.g. fitness supplements, skincare) and scroll past the first few results to find less common products","Use \"Send inquiry\" (better than \"Chat now\") to ask about custom labels, flavors, packaging or adding your logo","Check the minimum order quantity (MOQ). Some products require around 1,000 units","Consider a separate Alibaba account for research so your main account's recommendations don't get cluttered","Alibaba's secure payment and money-back guarantee protect you if a supplier doesn't ship","Avoid generic items people can grab at Target (like basic pimple patches) unless you can market and brand them differently","For regular dropshipping, stick with Zendrop, CJ Dropshipping or AutoDS. Branded, customized products can help you grow faster"],"topics":[],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_WXIXCTeTsX0Ir/lessons/lesn_flCSRLP9Jhdjv/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"The Best Niches W/ The Best Products","where":"Dropshipping · Product Selection","summary":"The best product niches to dominate now and for years to come, with example products for each.","points":["Pick products that improve people's lives or meet a real need, ideally with repeat purchases (like a pharmacy's refills)","Health and wellness: growing every year as people age. Examples: posture correctors, wellness journals, sleep optimization products. Pair with small influencers and authentic testimonials","Pets: owners buy repeatedly and will spend on premium products for their pets. Examples: interactive smart feeders, pet cameras with treat dispensers, eco-friendly or dental toys","Tech accessories: always in demand (gaming setups, work-from-home). Examples: MagSafe accessories, wireless chargers with a niche twist","Home fitness: many people want to work out but avoid the gym. Look for unique at-home equipment beyond basic dumbbells, since thousands of untapped products exist on AliExpress, Zendrop, AutoDS and CJ","Beauty, skincare and eco-friendly: shoppers want natural products that work better than store brands, which drives repeat buys","Home decor and hobbies: people constantly upgrade their spaces (aesthetic lights, posters, decor)","Fashion/streetwear: sell upgraded versions of everyday items (e.g. shorts with an inside pocket so your phone doesn't fall out)","Parenting and kids: parents spend heavily on educational and learning products year-round"],"topics":["Product research"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_WXIXCTeTsX0Ir/lessons/lesn_2NbKtgJRWxtyB/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Winning Products in Fitness","where":"Dropshipping · Product Selection","summary":"A deep dive into the fitness niche: why it's a big opportunity, which products are winning, how to validate them, and how to market them.","points":["The opportunity: fitness is a huge, growing global market (the video cites ~$300B). Buyers now want gym-quality results at home, plus tech, tracking and good looks.","The niche is broad: home workout gear, recovery tools (massage guns, foam rollers), wearables, supplements, fitness apparel, content-friendly accessories and multifunctional products.","Know your buyer: beginners want simple, cheap and motivating; athletes want performance and recovery; home users want compact and easy. Speak clearly to one group.","What buyers want now: digital features (tracking, gamification, sharing), a recovery focus, and aesthetic appeal (it has to look good on social).","Winning-product checklist: demand without overwhelming competition, solves a problem, evergreen or trending, profitable and light to ship, video-friendly. If it checks 3+ boxes, test it.","Examples: adjustable dumbbells and massage guns (high volume), smart jump ropes, mini steppers and ankle weights (trending), grip trainers and shaker bottles (niche).","Validate before investing: Google Trends for steady interest, TikTok (\"TikTok made me buy it\"), and Amazon/Reddit reviews for complaints you can solve.","Marketing: demo and testimonial videos, transformation and challenge content, and content matched to each platform (TikTok, IG Reels, YouTube Shorts).","Micro-influencers (10K–100K followers) and customer UGC drive far more engagement than polished brand ads.","Store-building cheat code: motivational branding, smart bundles to raise AOV, and free digital add-ons (e.g. a 30-day workout PDF, which AI can help make).","Action plan: research → shortlist → source (Zendrop, AutoDS, CJ) → launch, test, and double down on winners.","Sell the transformation (strength, confidence, progress), not just the tool."],"topics":["Product research"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_WXIXCTeTsX0Ir/lessons/lesn_CQVL2wNjp3sY6/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Testing product sauce (CALL)","where":"Dropshipping · Product Selection","summary":"A live masterclass with Q&A on a new way to test products using AI-generated videos, compared with ordering a physical sample.","points":["Q&A: skincare creatives should show the solution and before/after results; products with color variants need the product page before checkout, which is normal","Offer incentives tied to the product (free shipping over a set amount, a free gift or mystery box) rather than giveaway-style prizes, and avoid discounts that drag down your average order value","Option 1, physical sample: order from your supplier or Amazon (can take 3+ weeks), then film unlimited videos yourself for just the cost of the sample","Option 2, AI videos: put a product screenshot into an AI video tool like Higgsfield or Arcads, with a prompt written by Claude describing the product, to get realistic videos (roughly $25–$125/month for a limited number of videos)","Repurpose AI videos by cutting and remixing them, so one video can become around 10 posts","Best of both: start AI content while your sample ships, and run one AI account plus one account with you using the real product","Downside of skipping the sample: you don't know the quality. Products that need trust or real results (supplements, skincare) do better with real people or UGC creators; simple products (water bottles, ab rollers, headsets) work well with AI","If AI videos show demand, use those sales to order the sample and go all in","Q&A on competitors offering to collab: ask for proof of their numbers before anything, don't merge a store that's still growing fast, and if anything, test the partnership on a separate new store","Q&A on suppliers and fulfillment: build supplier relationships to get better pricing, and move to a 3PL (about $2 per package) or warehouse once order volume gets high"],"topics":["Calls","Product research","AI tools","Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_WXIXCTeTsX0Ir/lessons/lesn_ycywZEy7TXnpU/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Criteria before testing (sauce) :","where":"Dropshipping · Product Selection","summary":"The \"test before the test\": the boxes a product must check before you spend any time or money on it.","points":["It needs a wow factor (fast money) or to solve a problem (longevity)","Hard to find in local stores, or clearly better than what Walmart sells (e.g. a spatula that auto-flips pancakes)","Leave room for margin: sell at about 3x your landed cost and don't go below ~35% profit margin","Check demand: Google Trends, Amazon reviews and monthly sales, and Facebook Ad Library","If a competitor has been pumping ads on the same product for a long time, it's likely profitable"],"topics":[],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_WXIXCTeTsX0Ir/lessons/lesn_qH2hMjjmxIyw2/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"First step to testing","where":"Dropshipping · Product Selection","summary":"Step one of testing a product: always order a sample first.","points":["Check quality and packaging yourself so you know what customers will get","Your own footage of the sample beats reused supplier videos","Samples can take 2–3 weeks; that's better than 1,000 angry customers","Sample several suppliers: quality and price vary, and a $2 cheaper supplier adds up to real profit at volume","You can skip samples only with a private supplier you've used many times"],"topics":["Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_WXIXCTeTsX0Ir/lessons/lesn_uk2BvcjLqM7b3/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"second step to testing (GOOD sauce)","where":"Dropshipping · Product Selection","summary":"Step two of testing: put the product on its own page in a niche store, then run a small, properly funded ad test.","points":["Build a niche store (e.g. \"Ski Life\", not \"Ski Boots\") so you can test many products in one niche","Create a dedicated product page in Shopify and send ad traffic there, not to the home page","Make 3–5 creatives per ad set; creative matters more than almost anything (demo, problem/solution, unboxing, reaction)","Test on Meta and TikTok: $5/day on a tight budget, ideally $50–100/day; Facebook needs budget, not time","Judge on CTR and landing page clicks first; put about $200 into a campaign before killing a product","Don't blame the product for bad creatives or too little budget"],"topics":["Product research"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_WXIXCTeTsX0Ir/lessons/lesn_QJcV3J0T4zGS7/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to pump test ($$)","where":"Dropshipping · Product Selection","summary":"A masterclass on when to kill a product, why you should run a niche store, and how to give every product a real test before giving up on it.","points":["Killing a product just means you stop promoting it; you can keep it on the site to help raise AOV","Never name your store after one product (e.g. \"White Humidifier\"); build a niche store (e.g. a wellness store) so you can swap products without rebuilding your domain and logo","Don't judge on one signal (SEO, Google Trends or competitors alone); marketing is the real test","Organic: post 25–50 videos and look for comments, DMs and follows asking where to get it, not just sales","Ads: run 3–5 creatives; a CTR above ~1% means people are interested. Ignore conversion rate while testing, since that's a website problem","\"Treat it like a winner until you find out it isn't\": don't go in expecting your first product to fail, but keep testing others too","Ads need budget, organic needs posts, not time; a UGC creator posting about it is a cheap fast test","Q&A: put the product on your niche store first, then market it; suppliers like Zendrop/CJ fulfill orders automatically once connected; private suppliers vs. apps; you need a real domain, not a .myshopify.com link"],"topics":["Calls","Product research"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_WXIXCTeTsX0Ir/lessons/lesn_CRFc7p4UK9D8Q/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"WATCH THIS FIRST","where":"Dropshipping · Website Maxxing","summary":"What you need before starting the website section.","points":["A basic understanding of what a website is (rewatch the beginner group if not)","Understand what a niche is, and ideally know yours","Know your way around your platform, e.g. Shopify","If you're doing as you go, have your site built or being built so you can apply each lesson"],"topics":["Getting started","Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_ACnEQbqVtBOxl/lessons/lesn_FAc8MPXFy652l/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Learning a Website Sales Funnel","where":"Dropshipping · Website Maxxing","summary":"A step-by-step breakdown of the customer journey (sales funnel) from first seeing your product to checkout, and what your store needs at each stage.","points":["Browsing: customers find you via Google, Instagram, TikTok, Facebook, Pinterest, YouTube and so on, then land on your landing page.","Landing page must-haves: a pop-up that collects contact info (email or phone, not both, to keep it light) so you can follow up, plus an urgency notice in a banner (e.g. sale ending soon, limited-time offer, recent purchases). Most people won't buy on their first visit.","Potential Initial Product (PI): shoppers often aren't sure which product they want, so offer a few options. Start with 1 proven winner or 3–4 products, and no more than ~5. Too many choices leads to no choice.","Add to cart, then view cart: most people browse more before checking out, so make the cart easy to review.","Potential Upsell (PU): suggest complementary products at checkout, ideally with a discount (e.g. a matching beanie with a hoodie, or a buy-one-get-one deal) to raise order value.","Checkout: shipping and payment info, then order confirmation. Use Shopify (or Wix/Weebly) so failed payments can be retried.","Why people don't check out: shipping cost, second thoughts on spending, comparison shopping, or simply forgetting (the most common).","That's why collecting contact info is critical: it lets you bring non-buyers back with follow-up flows (covered in later modules).","Reach out to the coaches if anything is unclear."],"topics":["Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_ACnEQbqVtBOxl/lessons/lesn_BtZcPUe1qDNqH/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"The Steps to a Profitable Website","where":"Dropshipping · Website Maxxing","summary":"A simple overview of the full MEO process, so you know where everything fits. (Despite the title, this video is a roadmap rather than a website review.)","points":["The process: find a product, start your store (yourself or with the done-for-you agency), find a good way to market it, hire people to handle what you do, then sell the store.","Repeat the cycle as many times as you want, depending on your goals.","It sounds quick, but there are many small steps in between that make the difference, so watch every module thoroughly and take notes.","If you feel confused about where to start: find a product and get your store up first. Everything else is taught step by step."],"topics":["Website","Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_ACnEQbqVtBOxl/lessons/lesn_cjaNDBbLBNwKG/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Niche website!","where":"Dropshipping · Website Maxxing","summary":"Why your store should be built around a niche, not one product.","points":["Put your name, logo and domain around a niche (e.g. a wellness store), never a single product","Your winner might be your 2nd, 3rd or 7th product, so you need room to test many in one store","A niche name helps SEO so you show up for searches in that space","A broad brandable name also works if you might pivot later (Amazon started with books)","Only name it after one product if you're pumping and dumping, which isn't recommended"],"topics":["Product research","Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_ACnEQbqVtBOxl/lessons/lesn_EIET6238H07pO/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Reviewing a Profiting Website","where":"Dropshipping · Website Maxxing","summary":"A review of a high-converting mentee website (a hair care brand), showing what makes shoppers trust it and buy.","points":["Show how you're different: a comparison section (\"Our product vs. Others\") with checkmarks. Don't name specific competitor brands, which could create legal issues.","Explain what it does and why it's different. Those two things win people over.","Show what it doesn't contain as well as what it does (e.g. free of certain ingredients), plus the ingredients and the science behind them.","Testimonials and results throughout the page build trust fast.","Trust signals: About Us, Contact Us, FAQ, easy returns, and messaging that shows where the brand is based (shoppers feel safer about delivery).","Product page: strong images (including one listing everything it includes), how-to-use instructions in the description, and clear return info.","A site built like this converts well, especially once you start running ads. Aim for this standard whatever your product."],"topics":["Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_ACnEQbqVtBOxl/lessons/lesn_WHNjzvTlbRh1T/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Finding a Font For Logo","where":"Dropshipping · Website Maxxing","summary":"How to find the right font for your logo and brand, and how branding differs for dropshipping stores vs. clothing brands.","points":["Browse a free font library (like DaFont) by category (old school, Western, groovy, distorted, etc.) and download fonts that fit your vibe.","Match the font to your niche: clean and professional for fitness or professional products, more expressive (vintage, distressed, blurred) for streetwear.","Always check a font's license before using it commercially.","Prioritize readability. A cool font that's hard to read won't stick in people's minds.","Dropshipping stores: a simple, professional logo is enough (AI-made is fine). Use it on your profile picture, website and emails. The goal is a brand image so the store doesn't look like a generic dropshipping site, which builds trust.","Clothing brands: invest more in a unique logo and put it everywhere. It should be seen, not hidden.","Test print quality with your supplier, since detailed effects (like blur) may not print well."],"topics":["Website","Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_ACnEQbqVtBOxl/lessons/lesn_iri8vqPT3PNtO/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"good Apps for Your Site","where":"Dropshipping · Website Maxxing","summary":"The essential Shopify apps to add to your store, what each one does, and when to add them.","points":["Your store is more than a storefront. The right apps (shipping, marketing, engagement, upsells, reviews, optimization) separate stagnant stores from growing ones. Add them from the Apps section in Shopify.","Klaviyo: email and SMS automation flows. Free up to 250 contacts.","Postscript: SMS marketing with extras like gifts to make customers feel welcome.","Yotpo: loyalty, rewards and referral programs for repeat purchases (free under a certain order volume).","Loox: automatically requests photo and video reviews after purchase.","Session-recording/analytics apps: see what customers actually do on your site.","Gorgias: all-in-one customer support help desk. Wait until you've scaled; a simple contact page works at first.","Upsell and bundle apps (AI product recommendations, Wiz, UpCart): raise average order value. Add these once you have more products; start by focusing on selling.","Marketplace Connect: list your products on Amazon and other marketplaces.","PageFly (no-code landing pages) and Tapcart (turn your store into a mobile app): for later, as you grow.","Shopify Flow (free): no-code automations (trigger → condition → action) like restock alerts, follow-up emails, or pausing ads when inventory runs low.","Vitals: an all-in-one app (reviews, bundles, urgency, currency). Convenient, but many owners prefer separate apps.","Next steps: find where your store is \"leaking,\" test apps, remove what doesn't help, and double down on what works.","Top picks: Klaviyo/Postscript plus a good bundle app."],"topics":["Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_ACnEQbqVtBOxl/lessons/lesn_jyQ91pLg0C5Xs/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Good website systems (CALL)","where":"Dropshipping · Website Maxxing","summary":"A live masterclass on the systems your website needs, especially CRM (customer relationship management), and why marketing matters more than a perfect-looking site.","points":["Marketing and reaching people gets sales, not a beautiful website. Big stores have made $100K+/month with average-looking sites. Each day, ask \"did I do all my marketing?\" before tweaking the site","A decent website (looks fine) is different from having the right systems (how you capture contacts and turn them into buyers)","CRM isn't just collecting emails. It's building a relationship: welcome emails, thank-you emails after orders, and messages about what your brand stands for","Great customer service (order updates, tracking, quick replies) is part of CRM and brings people back, like Amazon or a store where staff are friendly","Make your sign-up offer so good people would feel silly not to join. The better the offer, the higher the sign-up rate. Mention it in your video captions too","If TikTok won't let you add a link yet, send people to your Instagram, where they can click through or DM to order","Write emails yourself in Klaviyo, or have ChatGPT draft them and personalize them. Write a week of emails on Saturday and schedule them about 4 times per week","Use one automation (a welcome email at sign-up), then send scheduled bulk emails so subscribers don't get doubled-up messages","Start with email. Add SMS once customers know your brand, since unexpected texts feel more intrusive","Subscribe to competitors' emails (e.g. GLD) with a burner email for ideas, and tie emails to trends and seasons"],"topics":["Calls","Website","Email & SMS"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_ACnEQbqVtBOxl/lessons/lesn_4OPFXHwGM0ESU/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Don't make these mistakes with your website!","where":"Dropshipping · Website Maxxing","summary":"A beginner-friendly masterclass on common website mistakes that hurt trust and sales.","points":["Mistake 1, no custom domain: a \"yourstore.myshopify.com\" address hurts trust and is hard to remember. Buy a domain on GoDaddy (or Shopify), and use endings like .shop or .apparel if .com is taken","Mistake 2, no clear path to checkout: put your product on the home page, add a \"Shop Now\" button in the hero banner, and an add-to-cart button below the product. Don't make people dig for it","Test it: if a kid under 10 can't figure out how to buy from your site, simplify it. Shopify Sidekick can help you put the product and add-to-cart button on the home page","Mistake 3, an unprofessional look: stick to 2–3 consistent colors (e.g. black/white/red) that suit your audience, and use professional-looking product photos","Mistake 4 (more advanced), no incentive to buy now: offer a reason to check out, like a discount code from a pop-up, free shipping or buy-one-get-one, so shoppers don't put it off","Feature your winning product on the home page (1–3 products). If you have many products, organize them into categories"],"topics":["Calls","Getting started","Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_ACnEQbqVtBOxl/lessons/lesn_x6kOcoIT1IOJt/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"adding product page to test products","where":"Dropshipping · Website Maxxing","summary":"How to add a product page in Shopify and why you send test traffic straight to it.","points":["Products → Add product: name, description and images; Shopify generates the product page link","Open the product on your live store and copy that link","When testing, point ads at the product page, not the home page, or people browse around and you can't tell if the product converts","Once you have a winning store, you can send traffic to the home page"],"topics":["Product research","Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_ACnEQbqVtBOxl/lessons/lesn_QKR7TLacOns7R/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"SECRET Systems pt.2 (CALL)","where":"Dropshipping · Website Maxxing","summary":"What a winning homepage or landing page should look like from top to bottom, with real store examples.","points":["Hero section (top banner): show your current offer (e.g. \"Buy 2, get 30% off\"), a background image of someone using the product, and a \"Shop Now\" button","Right below: your winning products, especially the one people are coming to your site for","Next: models and testimonials (e.g. 5-star reviews saying \"fit perfectly\") so shoppers can see it works and looks good on real people","Then: category sections (if you sell multiple product types), plus perks like free shipping, fast delivery and warranties","Influencers or well-known creators using the product build trust. If you don't have those yet, use UGC creators or testimonials","Footer: an email sign-up (e.g. \"Join the family\") to capture contacts","Most shoppers want to check out quickly, so make the path to buy obvious while answering objections as they scroll","Q&A: link your bio to the product page if you promote one product, or to the homepage if you promote several","Q&A: AI-generated models can work for showing how a product is used, as long as they look realistic"],"topics":["Calls","Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_ACnEQbqVtBOxl/lessons/lesn_vwkVJQd21XSKR/"},{"kind":"pdf","title":"What do do now CHECK--LIST","where":"Dropshipping · Website Maxxing","summary":"A short \"what do I do now?\" checklist for the website section.","points":["Doing it as you go: website completed, a pop-up on the site, a countdown timer or urgency notice, and your product added","Learning first: take notes as you watch the modules","Keep up with the modules, since each step is taught above"],"topics":["Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_ACnEQbqVtBOxl/lessons/lesn_WDS5q37AsJ5K5/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"(COACH VID) Using Shopify AI Block Generator for Premium Pages","where":"Dropshipping · Website Maxxing","summary":"Coach video: using Shopify's Sidekick AI block generator to make a premium-looking homepage, and fixing the bug where AI sections land on the wrong page.","points":["Edit theme → open Sidekick → ask it to use the AI block generator to recreate your homepage with a more premium, luxurious look","It writes the code; pick your hero image and drop the new section in","Common issue: an AI block made for another page (like the catalog) shows up at the bottom of the homepage","Fix: on the right page, Add section → AI generated → pick the one it made, move it into place, then delete the stray copies from the homepage","Lets you brand the site while still using Shopify integrations (less luxurious than Base44, but one prompt)"],"topics":["Website","AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_ACnEQbqVtBOxl/lessons/lesn_JQBFYOaiLA4j5/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"5 Website Must-Haves That Convert","where":"Dropshipping · Website Maxxing","summary":"The 5 things high-converting stores (some doing $1M+/month) all have on their website, and how to add each one.","points":["Announcement bar: a sale or offer at the very top (e.g. \"Halloween Sale – 50% off\", \"Free shipping on all orders\"); you can stack two","Bundles: \"1 for $40 or 2 for $70\" changes the question from \"do I buy?\" to \"one or two?\"; make the discount big enough to matter; Kaching Bundles has a built-in AI helper","FAQ: answer what people actually ask in your comments and competitors' comments, not just generic shipping/returns questions","\"Us vs. others\" comparison: show why yours beats the cheaper versions at Walmart or Target, using the features that matter for your product","Image reviews: reviews with real customer photos build far more trust than text-only ones; 6–8 photo reviews is plenty"],"topics":["Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_ACnEQbqVtBOxl/lessons/lesn_PRTy86w4d5Ogb/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"(COACH VID) How to optimize Cart to make more $$)","where":"Dropshipping · Website Maxxing","summary":"Coach video: how to optimize your cart drawer to raise AOV and stop add-to-carts from dropping off.","points":["Use a slide cart app (e.g. Kaching Cart) with a free shipping progress bar","Set the threshold just above one item so \"add $5 more for free shipping\" nudges people to the 2-pack bundle (e.g. 1 for $40 + shipping, 2 for $55 with free shipping)","Rewards can be free shipping, store credit, a discount or a free gift","Offer optional shipping protection for $3–5; many customers add it and it raises AOV (never force it on)","Show payment method icons (Klarna, PayPal, Visa…) for credibility and match the cart colors to your brand"],"topics":["Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_ACnEQbqVtBOxl/lessons/lesn_NoruNnhKmMCWb/"},{"kind":"pdf","title":"Store Launch Checklist","where":"Dropshipping · Website Maxxing","summary":"Everything your store needs before you send traffic: homepage, trust pages, checkout, email flows (welcome, abandoned cart), pixel.","points":[],"topics":["Website","Email & SMS"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_ACnEQbqVtBOxl/lessons/lesn_yLSVOq7NQD1un/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Using Competition to Make the Best Website","where":"Dropshipping · Website Maxxing","summary":"A teardown of a successful competitor's website to show what makes a store convert, and how to borrow those ideas for your own.","points":["Study competitors' sites, not just your own. Successful stores show what customers respond to.","Value up front: a welcome discount offer, plus small discounts on \"hot\" products to create urgency. Focus discounts on your most profitable products.","Large catalogs across many niches only work once you've already blown up. When starting, keep the product count small (this store has only 29).","Keep the design clean: a simple white background, no crazy colors.","Trust builders: customer contact info, a thorough FAQ (~13 questions), an order tracking page, and clear shipping, refund and privacy policies.","Set expectations: show delivery times and a return policy (use the supplier's terms if they offer them).","Selling the same products on TikTok Shop can be a big extra revenue stream.","Match your design to your audience: this site suits an older (40–65) audience, while younger audiences need a more modern, organized look.","Organic marketing kept this store growing. They still post a lot even after blowing up."],"topics":["Product research","Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_ACnEQbqVtBOxl/lessons/lesn_eL8bFOQLJlvUX/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Website Reviews of Comp","where":"Dropshipping · Website Maxxing","summary":"Live audits of real dropshipping stores found on TikTok, pointing out what they do well and what they're missing.","points":["Link in bio: once you pass 1,000 followers on TikTok, add your store link to your bio. Not having one leaves sales on the table.","Good: an Add to Cart button right on the landing page, FAQs, shipping and refund info, and the winning product featured big at the top.","Announcement banner: free shipping, returns and similar perks at the top answer objections before shoppers ask.","Common miss: no pop-up to collect emails. Without it you lose the chance to build a customer list (CRM).","Gadget stores can make money fast, but a focused niche gives more longevity.","If you have one winning product, link your bio straight to that product page.","Marketing tips from these stores: reply to (hate) comments with videos showing how the product works, and add a CTA like \"follow for more\" in captions.","Make sure your store's language matches your audience. A mismatch kills sales.","Winning stores have pop-ups and discounts, so take notes.","Be creative with product choice instead of picking generic items. Use early profits to bring in UGC creators or AI content."],"topics":["Website","Organic marketing","Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_ACnEQbqVtBOxl/lessons/lesn_1rHTAKyuQiWEK/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Preventing Abandoned Carts","where":"Dropshipping · Website Maxxing","summary":"What an abandoned cart is and how to set up an automated recovery email that brings shoppers back.","points":["An abandoned cart (a \"soft order\") happens when someone likes the product, adds it to cart, then leaves. It's usually shipping cost, price, or simply getting distracted, not because they don't want it.","Never assume they lost interest. Many just forgot, so a reminder recovers real sales.","Automate it with Shopify Flow/workflows, Klaviyo or Postscript so the email goes out automatically when a cart is abandoned.","Don't use a generic AI email. Write casually, like a person (\"We see you left your cart hanging\"), not stiff and corporate.","The winning formula is an offer plus scarcity: include a coupon code (the offer), then a real time limit to act on it (the scarcity).","Keep the window realistic. 10–15 minutes works. 30 seconds is too short to act on, and a long window removes the urgency. Make sure the coupon actually expires when you say it does.","Done consistently, this raises your store's overall conversion rate.","Tip: keep your notes in Google Docs."],"topics":["Email & SMS"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_ACnEQbqVtBOxl/lessons/lesn_QmdyyM0mgr8er/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Nurturing Customers -> Sales","where":"Dropshipping · Website Maxxing","summary":"Why your website should nurture customers (build desire and trust) before pushing the product, shown through a skincare brand's site.","points":["The hero section shows the logo and a real person using the product in a bathroom, so visitors picture it in their own home, rather than a plain product shot.","Sell a feeling, not a product: the headline is about \"confidence,\" not \"buy our serum.\"","Trust stack as you scroll: \"as featured in\" logos, best sellers, then testimonials.","Testimonials should match your target audience. This brand targets older customers, so its reviewers are older people describing results that matter to them.","Mix product images ~50/50 with photos of people using the product.","The Shop Now button can be small. The page's job is to show why buying benefits the customer, with science, results and an About section.","Brands often push their highest-profit product, not necessarily their best seller.","You don't have to copy this layout, but your site should nurture visitors on why your product is worth it."],"topics":["Website","Email & SMS"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_ACnEQbqVtBOxl/lessons/lesn_wzXgMKJLOEOII/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Getting Reviews For Your Products","where":"Dropshipping · Website Maxxing","summary":"Why you should add a review app (Judge.me) to your Shopify store and how it helps sales.","points":["Judge.me collects reviews mainly from verified buyers, so ratings come from real customers.","Good reviews build trust passively and raise your click-through and conversion rates, so make sure the product itself is good.","Display reviews with eye-catching widgets and testimonial sliders on your site.","Encourage reviews and repeat sales by sending a coupon or referral reward for leaving one (ask for honest reviews, not just 5 stars).","Sync reviews to Google and Meta so they show up when people search for you.","Reviews double as feedback: customers will tell you what to improve or add (e.g. a new color), which helps you build better products."],"topics":["Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_ACnEQbqVtBOxl/lessons/lesn_s6mpFWOT5aexp/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to Maximize AOV on Website","where":"Dropshipping · Website Maxxing","summary":"Offer types you can use on your website to raise average order value (AOV), so each customer spends more while getting more value.","points":["Buy more, save more: tiered pricing (e.g. 1 for $40, 2 for $70, 3 for $90) nudges buyers toward the bigger bundle.","Buy 2, get 1 free: works well when your product cost is low. You can raise the price slightly to cover the free unit.","Bundle and save: pair Product A + Product B at a discount (e.g. $80 value for $59). Great for moving a second product.","Free gift threshold: spend a certain amount (e.g. $75) and get a gift or gift card.","Complete the set: main product plus a matching accessory for a small add-on price (e.g. hoodie plus matching pants).","Mix and match: pick any 3 for a set price.","Mystery gift: buy 2 and get a free mystery item. Best if you have a larger catalog.","Premium bundle: everything included for the biggest savings.","Simple and effective for small stores (2–6 products): free shipping when they buy 2 or spend over $50–$75."],"topics":["Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_ACnEQbqVtBOxl/lessons/lesn_FDlJ1EjtevMhF/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to use ai to make you money with MEO-.. (call)","where":"Dropshipping · Website Maxxing","summary":"A live masterclass with Q&A on which AI tools to use for different parts of your e-commerce business, plus member wins and shipping troubleshooting.","points":["Q&A: when a Zendrop order stops updating in transit, contact support, wait up to a full 30 days, then reship or refund the customer if it still hasn't arrived","Refunds still apply when a customer never received their product","Know which AI fits which job, instead of using one tool for everything","Higgsfield: videos (UGC, organic content, some paid ads)","Claude: website coding, email copy, video scripts, hooks and content ideas","ChatGPT: images (ad images, product photo shoots), prompts, copywriting and general business questions","Lovable: mainly front-end websites and landing pages, and it can connect to Shopify","Base44: front-end websites plus custom software (e.g. inventory or UGC creator tracking) that can connect to Shopify or Wix","Using AI the right way saves time and money compared with paying people for copywriting, prompts or UGC","Member win: adding clear CTAs in captions quickly turned a stagnant store into daily sales. Tell viewers where to buy","Post on Snapchat and YouTube Shorts too, and add bundle or add-on products (no more than 5–6) to raise average order value"],"topics":["Calls","AI tools","Suppliers & shipping","Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_ACnEQbqVtBOxl/lessons/lesn_XSZ8ovyrVyU2Z/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Base44 Review, Powerful Tool","where":"Dropshipping · Website Maxxing","summary":"A masterclass on Base44, an AI tool that builds website front ends and custom software, plus Q&A on faceless branding, selling a store, and AI-driven dropshipping.","points":["What Base44 does: builds the front end of your store (homepage/landing page) from a text prompt and integrates with Shopify or Wix, which handle the back end (checkout, payments).","Shopify vs. Wix: both work as the back end. Shopify has a free trial, so test both and pick what you're comfortable with.","Custom software: Base44 can build internal tools, e.g. a daily task calendar that texts reminders (via WhatsApp) and rolls unfinished tasks over, or alerts for late orders.","Product research alerts: you can have Base44 build a system that notifies you of new products in your niche every couple of weeks.","Use it to mock up design ideas quickly before building on Shopify. It's one of the more affordable and beginner-friendly AI builders.","Faceless branding: keep your face off your main brand account to avoid copycats. A separate personal account can promote the product instead.","Suppliers: AutoDS and Zendrop for dropshipping, Alibaba for bulk/B2B (covered in the school modules).","Selling a store: branded stores are worth more if you hold and scale them for at least a year. Buyers often pay a multiple of annual revenue, and a broker helps find buyers. Covered in the multi-store modules.","Growth comes from fixing small leaks: email flows, CTAs, paid ads, UGC creators and TikTok Shop all compound.","AI dropshipping: emotional/story-driven AI videos can convert well for quick revenue, but they don't build a lasting brand. Use them sparingly alongside real content.","When to avoid AI: products people consume or that require high trust are better served by real people and UGC creators."],"topics":["Calls","Website","AI tools","Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_ACnEQbqVtBOxl/lessons/lesn_j4YWeXL0oLLCt/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to get Base44 with Wix","where":"Dropshipping · Website Maxxing","summary":"How to embed something you built on Base44 into a Wix website.","points":["Copy the link to your Base44 project","In Wix, click Edit Site to open the editor (Wix's built-in AI chat can navigate you there if you can't find it)","Click Add (top left), then Elements, then Embed Code","Choose \"Embed a site\" (easier than pasting code), then paste your Base44 link","Add it, then resize and position it however you want, even full-page","This walkthrough is for Wix. A separate video covers embedding Base44 into Shopify"],"topics":["Website","AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_ACnEQbqVtBOxl/lessons/lesn_E2qHUFEsupys7/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Getting Base44 On Shopify","where":"Dropshipping · Website Maxxing","summary":"How to embed something you built on Base44 into your Shopify store.","points":["In Shopify, create a new page (or section) where you want the Base44 build to show up","Ask Claude or ChatGPT to create an iframe embed code for you","Copy the link to your Base44 project and put it into the iframe code","Paste the iframe code into Shopify, save, and click preview to check that it shows up","If it glitches, delete the code and paste it in again","If you get a \"not available\" error, go to Base44's dashboard, then Security settings, and turn off the setting that blocks embedding, then reload","Base44 can't be added as a Shopify app (they're likely competitors), so embedding is the workaround. Wix makes embedding easier"],"topics":["Website","AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_ACnEQbqVtBOxl/lessons/lesn_64QcbxUZefq9r/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Ways to use Base44","where":"Dropshipping · Website Maxxing","summary":"Different ways to use Base44 for your e-commerce business beyond just building a website.","points":["Websites: build a polished front-end landing page, then connect it to Shopify or Wix (separate videos cover how)","Base44 gives a better real-world layout than asking ChatGPT or Claude to design a website","Content planner: ask it to build a weekly content planning system tailored to your business","Inventory tracker: create a more advanced system that warns you when stock is low or when to restock","Influencer portal: let influencers submit their videos (and even sign contracts) in one organized place","It's strong at building custom software and apps that keep your business organized","Built-in AI systems can raise your store's value if you ever sell it, since you're selling the systems along with the store"],"topics":["Website","AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_ACnEQbqVtBOxl/lessons/lesn_f71DbgLMF2w8J/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Complete Website Masterclass","where":"Dropshipping · Website Maxxing","summary":"A masterclass on setting up the right customer funnel so every website visit builds your contact list (CRM) and relationship, plus Q&A on ads, video length and email frequency.","points":["Step 1, pop-up first: the first thing visitors should see is a pop-up that collects their email or phone. Prioritize long-term contacts over a quick sale.","A strong offer drives sign-ups: a percentage discount (e.g. 10–20% off) usually works best, but free shipping or a giveaway can be tested. A/B test offers week to week.","Step 2, welcome message: right after sign-up, automatically send a welcome email/SMS (with the coupon if it isn't shown instantly) and tell them what your brand is about.","Step 3, post-purchase: send an order confirmation with a thank-you and a discount on their next order with a real expiration (e.g. 7 days) to encourage repeat purchases.","Tools: Klaviyo for pop-ups and email, and an SMS app for texts.","Email frequency: when starting out, about every other day (some brands send daily). Not every email needs a discount; just promote products in a relevant way. AI can help write them.","TikTok vs. Facebook ads: both sell well. Facebook skews older, TikTok younger, so lean into wherever your audience is.","Organic video length: any length works as long as it's worth watching all the way through.","Store building: the done-for-you agency (replies within ~24–48 hours), AI tools, or build it yourself to learn the skill.","MEO modules: wait until your store is running before watching them. Locked school modules unlock by leveling up in the community."],"topics":["Calls","Website","Paid ads","Email & SMS"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_ACnEQbqVtBOxl/lessons/lesn_PKBS5EF7I5uUh/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Website Maximization pt. 1","where":"Dropshipping · Website Maxxing","summary":"The non-negotiables every store's landing page needs to build trust and convert, plus Q&A on reviews, AI in ads, suppliers and testing creatives.","points":["Testimonials/social proof: show that other people use the product and it works. You don't need hundreds; 3 or so on the homepage is enough to start.","About Us page: give your brand a deeper meaning. People buy the persona, not just the product (Nike sells feeling fit). Explain what problem you solve and why you exist.","FAQ: answer the questions people would ask (what it does, shipping time, where it ships from). Five or six good ones save time and speed up purchases.","Contact page: shows you're a real business, not a quick cash grab.","What the product solves: address objections head-on (e.g. a grip product that prevents blisters).","Real urgency when you're running a sale (e.g. free shipping for the next 24–48 hours).","Keep your refund/return policy in the footer.","Don't obsess over colors and wording. Once the essentials are in place, marketing is what drives sales, and a website is useless without traffic.","Reviews when you're new: collect genuine reviews from people who've actually used the product. Never make reviews up.","AI in ads: keep paid ads mostly real content (roughly 25% AI max). Never use celebrities' or athletes' likeness with AI without permission, as you can get sued.","Strategy: use organic content to validate, then put money behind what works in paid ads. You don't need to buy followers.","Testing creatives: launch several around the same time, track analytics, then raise the budget on winners and replace losers.","Suppliers: order samples from your actual supplier so you see what customers get. For dropshipping, Zendrop or CJ usually beat Alibaba.","Business setup (LLC vs. sole proprietorship) varies by state, so talk to a tax advisor."],"topics":["Calls","Website","Paid ads","AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_ACnEQbqVtBOxl/lessons/lesn_5L8ZEUovhM3bb/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Website Maximization pt. 2","where":"Dropshipping · Website Maxxing","summary":"A website-maximizing call focused on collecting customer contacts (CRM), building trust, and thinking long-term so your store is worth more when you sell it.","points":["A real offer beats a plain newsletter sign-up: of 1,000 visitors, a discount or giveaway pop-up might capture ~300 contacts vs. ~25 from \"join our newsletter.\" Giving up 10% to build a big email list pays off over time.","A strong CRM (email/SMS list) can significantly increase what your store sells for. Always ask \"how can I collect contacts?\" in your ads and on your site.","Trust factors: genuine testimonials and reviews (use Judge.me to collect them from real buyers), presented in a clean, organized, aesthetic way.","Put the product first, then reviews below it. People see the product, get interested, then look for proof they can trust you.","Sites with no reviews and just a product look like \"burner\" stores and make people question whether they're legit.","Focus on skills, not just the next sale: CRM, offers, sales psychology and trust-building create longevity and carry over to any business.","The bigger payday is often selling the store (commonly a multiple of annual revenue), so build systems that make it valuable.","Q&A: you can export contacts and import them into a new store's email tool. Keep stores in the same niche where possible, and make sure your sign-up form tells customers they're agreeing to promotional emails."],"topics":["Calls","Website","Email & SMS"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_ACnEQbqVtBOxl/lessons/lesn_j5hWmky4FfnNw/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"The Entire Point of Marketing","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"Why marketing is the whole point: success comes from getting your store in front of as many eyes as possible, through many channels.","points":["Zero traffic means zero sales. Unlike a physical store, nobody stumbles onto your website, so you have to build momentum.","Early traction creates social proof: the more places people see your brand, the more trustworthy it looks.","Cast a wide net, then double down on whichever channel or audience responds best.","Start with your personal network: post on your personal socials, share your story, and ask friends and family to share (maybe for a small gift). Don't worry about what people think.","Old-school still works: flyers on community boards (coffee shops, libraries, campus), business cards, local events, car magnets, and branded stickers (only where permitted).","Digital: business accounts posting daily, email marketing, SEO (show up for niche search terms), and helpful posts in Reddit/Facebook communities.","Partnerships: send product to micro-influencers with a promo code, cross-promote with non-competing brands, and run affiliate programs.","Content: blog posts that answer niche questions and link to your product (e.g. \"how to sleep better\"), demos, unboxings, tutorials, testimonials, podcasts and UGC.","Track and optimize: try at least 3 channels, check analytics and comments daily to see who's engaging, and adjust.","Action plan: pick 3 tactics, market at least 30 minutes a day, and keep going. Every successful store started at zero."],"topics":[],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_QBGGLv3wB1URi/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"The Different Types of Marketing","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"A rundown of the main types of marketing and ads you can use for your store, and which ones are worth your time.","points":["Organic content: make your own videos, with or without showing your face (hands-only on a tripod, or have a friend film), and later hire creators on Fiverr","Showing your face early helps build a brand and trust; you can step back from the camera as the store grows","Use or wear your product in public so it works like a walking billboard","Influencers: DM micro-influencers (some will post for free product), negotiate low and be willing to walk away","Bigger influencers on a contract (e.g. keep videos up for 6 months) can pay off long term through the customer contacts you collect","Paid platforms: Pinterest (niche/lifestyle), Snapchat (younger audience), Google Ads (better for big brands, focus on SEO instead), Meta ads (Facebook, Instagram, Threads/Messenger) mainly for sales, and TikTok ads for repeated exposure across platforms","Main recommendation: start with organic videos plus influencers, and use Facebook and TikTok for paid ads"],"topics":["Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_n5SAjRfar7KX3/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Why We Use Each Type of Marketing","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"A breakdown of what each type of marketing is best for, rated on awareness, contacts, website traffic and potential sales.","points":["Organic marketing: highest for awareness. Contacts, traffic and sales are medium and depend on consistency, going viral and having a link in your bio. Use it to build awareness and fund your ads, not mainly for sales","Seeing your product organically gives people a break from obvious ads and makes them more likely to buy later","UGC creators: top-tier awareness because their audience already trusts them. Traffic is lower, but conversion is very high. Think of it as organic marketing on steroids","Email/SMS: very high traffic and potential sales, since these people already know your brand and are being reminded to buy","Paid ads: not mainly for awareness, but highest for traffic and direct sales","Summary: organic = awareness, UGC = half awareness and half sales, email/SMS and paid ads = sales","Awareness is the most underrated one. It builds longevity and makes your store worth more if you ever sell it"],"topics":["Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_MVQqRs3ZR74LF/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Different Ways to Market Your Brand","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"All the different ways you can get content for your product, so you're not limited to just two or three sources.","points":["Film your own 5–15 second clips with a sample (from Amazon, Alibaba, etc.), with decent quality and a clean background","Ask your supplier for permission to use their product videos, or request custom shots","Find TikTok videos, Instagram Reels and YouTube reviews/unboxings of the product, and always get the creator's permission before using them","Collect customer reviews and videos with a review app like Judge.me, or offer customers a discount on their next order for an honest video","Hire UGC creators (Fiverr, Billo, Insense) or pay micro-influencers, who already have a niche audience","Use affiliate creators who earn a commission on sales","Use AI video tools (e.g. CapCut) or 3D renders (e.g. Blender) for product visuals","Film in relatable locations like the gym, car, kitchen or office for authenticity","Invite happy past buyers to become brand ambassadors with a coupon code, but keep it a simple ambassador option, not an MLM-style scheme","Best content stack: supplier footage (fastest), UGC creators (most scalable), self-shot footage, and customer videos for social proof"],"topics":["Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_gvHZvAPRipVoV/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Complete Marketing Masterclass","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"A live marketing masterclass with member Q&A on fulfillment, store setup, brand recognition and how to find and scale winning ad creatives.","points":["Q&A: if a new supplier's version looks slightly different but works the same, email customers to let them know before it ships","Q&A: for a chargeback over a defective item, ask for photo evidence, then contact your supplier (or Alibaba) for a replacement or refund","Q&A: get a professional business email (e.g. support@yourstore.com) through GoDaddy. Routing it into Gmail is fine","Q&A: with AliExpress, send order details to the supplier manually, or automate it with AutoDS","Must-have automations: abandoned cart, welcome email, and a post-purchase thank-you with a coupon for the next order","Sold out? Keep posting and add a pop-up to collect emails and phone numbers so you can text and email everyone when you restock. Reinvest profits into more inventory","Organic marketing builds recognition (like Sprite billboards), which makes people far more likely to choose your brand and raises conversion rates","In ads, focus on the creatives (video/image) and text. The ad setup itself is mostly automated now","A winning ad shows a high click-through rate. Clicks and site visits show interest even before sales follow","Once a creative wins, don't reinvent it: recreate it with a new setting or person, and put more budget behind it","Test 2–3 creatives at about $5/day each for 3–5 days, then scale the one with the most clicks. Raising the budget reaches more people in that niche","Avoid \"first 10 orders get X% off\" messaging, which can make it look like nobody is buying. A time-limited offer creates urgency without that downside","For pre-orders, state the shipping timeline clearly in the title, description and emails"],"topics":["Calls","Paid ads","Suppliers & shipping","Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_0RWWREqGViwMm/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Marketing Research","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"How to do market research on TikTok to see how others market products in your niche and get ideas for your own content.","points":["Search \"TikTok made me buy it\" or \"buy now\" + your niche (e.g. \"buy now fitness\") to find products and see how they're being marketed.","Study what grabs attention: strong hooks, POV-style videos, quick demos (e.g. testing a product underwater), and replying to comments with videos.","Note which videos get lots of comments, even negative ones like \"that's useless.\" Comments mean reach.","What not to do: screen-recording your website as \"organic\" content performs poorly.","Be skeptical of self-proclaimed \"Shopify experts\" in comment sections.","Engage with these videos (like, comment, watch) so the algorithm fills your For You page with similar stores, giving you a constant stream of trends and marketing ideas.","You'll notice sellers copying each other's winning formats (like the same demo style). That's market research in action."],"topics":["Product research","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_TKimskVhfaeEI/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to Stalk Competition","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"The \"burner account\" method for watching competitors and filling your feed with product-marketing ideas.","points":["Make a new TikTok account and only interact with videos that promote products: follow, like and comment on them.","Within a short time, your For You page fills with other sellers' product videos, even from accounts you never followed.","Study how they promote: hooks, trends they're riding, who they're targeting (e.g. a mini laundry machine aimed at college students).","One viral video can put a store on the map and bring in contacts and sales for a long time.","Viral product videos are often simple and quick (5–15 minutes to make) and don't need to be professional.","Use these insights to make videos in a similar style for your own product."],"topics":["Product research"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_4kRpDucu6UrmR/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Watching The Ads of Competition","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"Using TikTok to research products and study what competitors are posting.","points":["TikTok is one of the best product research tools. Search your niche (e.g. \"fitness items\", \"workout items\") and scroll through the videos","Example math: if just 1% of a viral video's viewers buy a ~$37 product, that could be tens of thousands in sales from one post","Treat organic posts like lottery tickets: post every day and eventually some will hit","Organic first is good practice for learning your targeting and how to make good ads, but you can start ads whenever you feel ready (it just costs more)","Creators who promote products in your niche are worth DMing for promotion","Successful pages often reuse the same footage to make many different videos, so it's not as hard as it looks","Once a page finds its target audience organically, it usually moves into running ads","Keep researching on TikTok to find new product ideas, even if you have to customize them for your niche"],"topics":["Product research","Organic marketing","Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_LfaqGpYtCe91c/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Reviewing Patterns in Winning Ads","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"How to spot the patterns that winning competitor ads have in common, with real examples from the skincare and travel niches.","points":["Watch several competitor ads in your niche back to back to find what they share","Example: a disposable face towel ad opens with the problem (bacteria in reused towels), backs it up with facts, explains how the product was developed, then shows the solution and ends with a discount code offer","Example: a travel product ad opens by calling out a relatable problem (\"raise your hand if your airport experience...\")","The key pattern: winning ads present the problem first, then show your product as the solution","Just showing the product only works for huge brands. Smaller stores need to show how the product is different and why people need it","Keep checking competitor ads regularly to spot new patterns in your niche"],"topics":["Product research","Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_ZybcbjEMQw8Ru/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Examples of AI Marketing","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"A real example of a store that grew fast using AI-generated marketing videos, and what you can learn from it.","points":["A crochet product store whose TikTok videos are almost all AI-generated, with some reaching around a million views","The store reportedly did thousands of orders in about a month from this approach","They use a bundle offer to raise order value. The main thing missing is an email pop-up","Lesson: find a good product, figure out how to make that style of AI video for it, and post consistently","Use stores like this for inspiration only. Don't copy their product or ads"],"topics":["AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_nJTjUXoGW7ALP/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Pretesting Your Marketing","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"How to pre-test a product for real demand before you put any money into ads.","points":["Base decisions on actual demand, not just a gut feeling","Check search volume and Google Trends to see when a product or keyword is trending and which season it peaks in","Look for social proof density: are multiple unrelated creators already posting about this type of product with real engagement? If so, the product already has awareness and you just need to sell it","Use your organic videos as a test. If even one comment asks \"where can I get this?\", that creative is showing demand","Organic can be more experimental, but paid ads should clearly show the problem and the solution","Validate on TikTok too, not just Google, by seeing whether creators are talking about it more","A brand-new product in a winning niche (e.g. looksmaxxing) may show little demand at first only because nobody knows about it yet; the niche itself can create the demand"],"topics":["Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_SBda5sX9HQcrV/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"The Purpose of OM","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"The real purpose of organic marketing, shown with analytics from one of the mentor's own stores.","points":["Organic brings a lot of sessions but fewer direct sales, because it's mainly for awareness","Visitors from organic often leave their contact info and come back later to buy after seeing you over and over","It took hundreds of posts, not 10–20, before organic started driving consistent sales","Example: around $30,000 from organic traffic in about two and a half weeks","Organic is one of the best forms of outreach because it's free: why pay $50 to reach 1,000 people with ads when 10 extra minutes can reach 2,000?","Even a video with 500 views is reaching potential buyers","Use organic to find your target audience and build awareness, then run Facebook ads to drive more sales"],"topics":["Mindset","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_8dep84QShAQVL/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Why ORGANIC Ads Matter","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"Why you shouldn't skip organic marketing, even if you plan to run paid ads.","points":["Don't want to show your face? Hire a UGC creator (e.g. on Fiverr) or have a friend promote the product. There's no excuse to skip it.","Reason 1, find your target audience: comments and engagement show who's actually interested (e.g. a hoodie video drew mostly women, which shaped future content).","Reason 2, brand recognition: organic content isn't only about immediate sales. It gets your product seen.","People who have already seen your product are far more likely to buy when they later see a paid ad. Familiarity raises conversion rates.","Big brands do the same thing: Coca-Cola billboards just show the product and logo, not \"buy now.\" Subtle, repeated exposure works.","You don't need a fancy setup. Early content was filmed in a small bathroom for hours after school. If you really want it, you'll find a way to post."],"topics":["Organic marketing","Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_4GTHr5YnLvDgQ/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Organic Marketing Breakdown","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"A deeper framework for organic marketing: how to make content people engage with, how to analyze results, and how to turn viewers into customers.","points":["Use the 3 W's in every video: What can people comment on? What will they get from the product? Why should they buy (from you)?","Spark comments: ask questions (\"would you wear this?\"), run comment giveaways, or do something unexpected with the product that gets people talking.","Show the benefit, not just the product: what problem does it solve or what experience does it give (e.g. a water toy that cures summer boredom)? Show it rather than plastering text.","If a competitor sells the same product, use a different marketing angle to reach a different audience.","Analyze every post with the \"3 major keys.\" If it got engagement, look at what people are talking about and make more content around that.","If it didn't get engagement, check analytics (watch-through rate), list 5 reasons it may have flopped, and honestly ask whether you'd engage with it. Then revise.","Reply to comments, positive or negative. Negative comments still push your content out. Answer objections and point people to your store (e.g. with a code).","Make your page and landing page obvious so interested viewers can buy easily.","Whether they buy or not, collect their info. Follow up non-buyers with a discount through email/SMS automations (Klaviyo, Postscript)."],"topics":["Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_VVNqCgZ45GltP/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"The KEY to OM","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"The key to organic marketing: don't be a perfectionist, and post everywhere to reach as many people as possible.","points":["Don't overthink each post. Get content out as much as possible.","Post on every platform: TikTok, Instagram, YouTube, Facebook, Snapchat, Pinterest and more.","You don't need different videos for each platform. Make one video and post it everywhere, and use Instagram/Facebook cross-posting.","A worse-looking store can outsell yours simply by reaching more people. Reach is the game.","More eyes means more sales: 1 million viewers beats 10,000.","With organic, reaching a broad audience costs nothing. Narrow niche targeting matters most when you're paying for ads."],"topics":["Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_2LKnidlukD1t5/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Algorithm for OM","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"How social media algorithms work and the best times and frequency to post on each platform for organic marketing.","points":["Why timing matters: early engagement (velocity) drives reach. Posting at 3 a.m. for a US audience pushes your video to the wrong time zones first.","Recency matters: fresh content gets prioritized, often with a burst of views in the first 30–60 minutes while the algorithm tests it.","Vary your content. Repetitive-looking videos can get suppressed and look like spam.","Each platform suits different content and ages: YouTube Shorts and Snapchat Spotlight skew younger (fast-paced, heavily edited), Pinterest and Facebook skew older, and LinkedIn is for older or professional audiences only.","General best windows: Tuesday–Thursday, early morning and evening (before and after work). Match your audience's routine.","Facebook: Tuesdays and Wednesdays, roughly 9 a.m.–1 p.m. Older users love community and local news angles. Share IG posts to Facebook and Threads with one tap.","Instagram: anytime works, but early-week posts often sell better.","TikTok: rewards consistency. Views may dip after a week before rising again if you keep going. Mornings and late evenings work well.","YouTube: post a few hours before prime time. Pinterest: post a day ahead and let it build.","Frequency: post 2–3 times a day per platform until you get real traction (it's like buying more lottery tickets). Once you get consistent 10–20K-view videos, you can slow down. Space posts at least 1–2 hours apart.","Use the audience data you gather (age, interests, platform) to target paid ads, and always ask whether you'd be entertained by the video yourself."],"topics":["Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_WQN9rZFsta2KR/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Copying my Organic Marketing Scheme","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"A walkthrough of DiMaggio's own brand page, showing real organic marketing examples and the ideas behind them.","points":["Organic content built the brand's audience (100K followers). The goal is reaching your target audience and building contacts.","Once you have contacts, you can email them weekly or monthly and keep selling to the same customers.","Simple content works: videos filmed at home got tens of thousands of views. You don't need a studio.","Content ideas that performed: wearing or showing the product in everyday settings, product shots with a nice backdrop (like a sunset), outfit/styling videos, AI-edited product clips, seasonal angles (back to school, Christmas), and \"we're dropping this for $X\" hooks.","A big creator or streamer wearing your product can drive huge attention.","Engagement tip: comments and replies boost reach, so engage with your audience.","One free viral video (600K views) can be worth a lot in orders and new contacts.","As you grow, combine organic posting with Facebook ads and email marketing.","The point is constant reminders: when people see your product repeatedly, they eventually buy."],"topics":["Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_cjZmvfZlZD2Wz/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Showing my Organic Marketing Recording Process","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"A live demo of how quick and simple organic marketing videos can be, using a hoodie and a diffuser as examples (the \"starter organic method\").","points":["It doesn't need to be perfect. Each example video took about 3 minutes to make.","On TikTok, tap + and pick a trending/recommended sound, then film the product simply (e.g. lay a hoodie on the floor and pan over details like a custom chain).","Even simple videos need the 3 W's. The caption does the heavy lifting early on, e.g. \"Have you ever seen a hoodie with a custom chain?\" or \"POV: this was your night light growing up.\"","Give people something to comment on. Doing something unexpected with the product drives comments (a friend got huge views letting commenters suggest what to put in a diffuser).","Film in your phone's camera app instead of inside TikTok so you can reuse the same clip across platforms with different captions.","ChatGPT can help generate captions.","15 minutes a day can produce ~5 videos, which means 5 chances to go viral. Consistency matters more than effort per video.","The goal isn't only sales: it's getting your product familiar in potential customers' heads."],"topics":["Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_OtCevIP1tkoM5/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Organic Marketing Social Medias","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"Pros and cons of the top social platforms for organic marketing (TikTok, Instagram, YouTube Shorts) and how often to post.","points":["TikTok cons: you need ~1,000 followers before adding a bio link, early posts may draw bot comments, and videos can take weeks to pop. Never delete videos.","TikTok pros: very interactive audience (lots of comments), viral posts build followers and fans, and detailed analytics (active times, locations, trends).","Instagram cons: slower traction, lighter analytics, and possible hate comments (which still boost reach, so don't let them get to you).","Instagram pros: bio link from day one, pinned comments (e.g. \"go to my page to shop\"), all engagement counts toward reach, and post scheduling once your account is established.","YouTube Shorts cons: no DMs to build relationships, no stories, and people rarely share Shorts.","YouTube Shorts pros: very fast early traction, engaged commenters, and longer videos later.","Post the same video on every platform. Don't make new content for each one.","Frequency: start with ~2 posts a day per platform and don't exceed ~5, or you may look like spam or a bot.","It's free promotion that takes minutes a day, so don't let the cons discourage you."],"topics":["Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_vGyIIsS3qj4c3/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Stop Overthinking Organic Marketing","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"Proof that organic marketing isn't hard: a scroll through DiMaggio's old account, where he posted constantly before outsourcing.","points":["For one to two years he posted relentlessly (sometimes ~11 times a day), always featuring his own products (hoodies, shirts, hats, shorts) and tagging them in captions.","He did this while in school, working, reselling shoes and mowing lawns, often filming in a hot, cramped bathroom. Time and setup aren't real excuses.","Using a small personal brand to promote your store can kick-start sales much faster.","Today his marketing is outsourced, but you have to put in the work before you can get there.","You don't need 12 posts a day. 2–3 a day is enough. Record videos in your camera app so you can make several quickly.","Don't be scared of the work. Find a way to get it done."],"topics":["Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_X2mjHCXhU9ImM/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"OM Before Paid Ads, here's why","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"Why you should do organic marketing before running paid ads.","points":["Posting a few videos won't bring instant sales; it takes consistency","Organic content on TikTok and Instagram shows you who your audience really is","Learn your analytics and target demographics before spending on ads so you don't waste money","Knowing your audience helps you scale and raises your store's value if you sell it (MEO)","Turn your best-performing organic content into paid ads"],"topics":["Organic marketing","Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_c4vF0fxpIFhFO/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Prioritize OM Over Paid Ads","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"Why you should do organic marketing before paid ads.","points":["Running paid ads first means guessing and burning money. You don't yet know who actually wants your product.","Before running ads, know at least 3 things about your audience: where they are (country/state/city), when they're active, and their age bracket.","Organic marketing gives you those analytics for free. Switch to a creator or business account to see the same kind of data Facebook ads would give you.","The people engaging with your organic posts are your buyers. Trust the algorithm's targeting.","Use organic to fund your ads with both knowledge (who to target, what to make) and money (early sales).","Use the push-and-go method from the marketing modules: validate with organic, then push winners with ads.","Post about 3–5 times a day. It only takes around 20 minutes."],"topics":["Organic marketing","Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_y2HXoJNTGDFs5/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Examples of OM and How to Find Inspo","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"Real examples of viral organic marketing for dropshipping products, and what made them work.","points":["Example: a dashboard de-icing gadget marketed to people in cold climates. The videos showed it in use on a frosty morning, which made the niche instantly relatable.","Show what the product does, not just the product. Demonstrations get far more engagement than simply holding it up.","Target your niche directly (e.g. people dealing with cold weather) and use relatable everyday scenarios.","Hooks that spark debate drive comments and reach, but avoid anything that disrespects or alienates part of your audience.","Timely hooks like \"best product of [current year]\" and putting the store link in the comments (not just \"link in bio\") can help.","Volume matters: the creator's first big viral video came after roughly 50–100+ posts. Keep posting.","Once traffic comes in, it's all about getting more people to the site, like adding more customers to a restaurant that's already built."],"topics":["Organic marketing","Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_F5SMJTUBjHpbt/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Cross Platform OM","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"Cross-promoting multiple stores in your content, an early step toward MEO (multi-store e-commerce).","points":["While promoting one product, subtly feature a product from another store in the background (e.g. wearing your hoodie while demoing a different product).","Viewers who don't want the main product may notice and want the background item instead.","This is subtle marketing: like brand-recognition ads (e.g. soda ads that just show the logo), the goal is familiarity, not an immediate sale.","People who've seen a product before trust it more and are more likely to buy it later.","Only do this once your main product is getting traction. Don't promote too many products at once or push the second product directly.","Works for organic content and ads alike (e.g. promoting camera glasses while wearing your hoodie)."],"topics":["Organic marketing","Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_kVWxr8AFSs9tt/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Mistakes Everyone Makes in OM","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"The biggest mistake in organic marketing, which is not doing enough of it, and why marketing deserves most of your effort.","points":["No marketing means zero sales. Even Jeff Bezos wouldn't make sales from a store nobody knows about.","Marketing should get the majority (~80%) of your attention in e-commerce, not a token 1% effort.","Posting once a week and expecting big results won't work. Consistency is key.","Big brands (e.g. Balenciaga) market everywhere, all year: Instagram, TikTok, Pinterest and Snapchat posts, influencers, paid ads on multiple platforms, plus email and SMS.","If the biggest brands do all of this constantly, a new store can't expect success from minimal effort.","Bottom line: if nothing is being pushed out to people, they can't buy your product."],"topics":["Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_6HPNP9pOPfplI/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Outreach for OM","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"Why you shouldn't skip organic marketing, even if you plan to run paid ads.","points":["Don't want to show your face? Hire a UGC creator (e.g. on Fiverr) or have a friend promote the product. There's no excuse to skip it.","Reason 1, find your target audience: comments and engagement show who's actually interested (e.g. a hoodie video drew mostly women, which shaped future content).","Reason 2, brand recognition: organic content isn't only about immediate sales. It gets your product seen.","People who have already seen your product are far more likely to buy when they later see a paid ad. Familiarity raises conversion rates.","Big brands do the same thing: Coca-Cola billboards just show the product and logo, not \"buy now.\" Subtle, repeated exposure works.","You don't need a fancy setup. Early content was filmed in a small bathroom for hours after school. If you really want it, you'll find a way to post."],"topics":["Organic marketing","Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_QWRvToQlTJPs0/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Using AI Within OM","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"Using AI-generated videos in your organic marketing, especially if you want to stay faceless.","points":["AI video tools (e.g. Sora, or video generators you prompt via ChatGPT or Claude) can create short, entertaining clips in seconds.","Example: simple AI pet videos (e.g. a talking dog) promoting pet products have reached over a million views.","You could post a few AI videos a day just by pasting prompts, which is a great way to get your brand out there.","Use AI videos mainly for awareness and organic reach, not as your main paid ads, since they can come across as less trustworthy when selling.","Works for any niche (pets, fitness, etc.). Find creators doing it well and model the style.","Once your store takes off, you may lean more on paid ads, but organic (including AI content) saves money early on."],"topics":["Organic marketing","AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_8vcifQ97l1yDB/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Making OM Easier","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"How to make organic marketing easier and save time by scheduling your posts ahead of time.","points":["Work smart and hard: scheduling keeps you consistent without having to stop and post manually every day","Platforms to schedule on: TikTok Studio, Instagram and YouTube","TikTok: download the TikTok Studio app, go to Post, then Schedule Post (it works from the app, not the computer)","You can schedule up to about 7 days out. For example, sit down on Friday, make 7–8 posts and schedule them across the next few days","Use the time you save to grow your brand and find better products","Instagram and YouTube may need a short wait before scheduling unlocks, while TikTok Studio lets you schedule right away","If scheduled posts aren't getting consistent views, switch back to posting manually"],"topics":["Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_jXSl7mGWRWrzF/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Using Ai to Help W/ OM","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"How to use AI (Claude or ChatGPT) to come up with organic marketing video ideas when you're running out of them.","points":["Start with a clear prompt: tell the AI you do organic marketing for your brand, which platforms you post on (YouTube Shorts, Snapchat Spotlight, etc.) and that you need ideas","Explain what your brand is, what it does and who your target audience is (e.g. streetwear for ages 16–25)","If the first ideas aren't good, ask it to make them more engaging","Describe or share examples of videos that work (yours or competitors'), such as B-roll of the product with engaging captions like \"which fit would you wear?\"","If the AI drifts off and starts writing scripts, bring it back to your original prompt","Ask it to turn your best ideas into 25 similar content ideas, and paste in your top 5–10 performing videos for reference","Be patient while it works, then pick the ideas that fit your brand (e.g. \"I wore this to...\" or \"rate my fit\" style videos)"],"topics":["Organic marketing","AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_nUbiYBR6ZlF50/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Using OM to Scale","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"The biggest mistakes people make with organic marketing, and how one viral post can scale your store when the right funnels are in place.","points":["Mistake 1, expecting fast results: treat each post like a lottery ticket. The more consistently you post, the better your odds","One video with a million views, plus email/SMS funnels to capture contacts and bring buyers back, can turn into six figures over time","Mistake 2, over-producing posts: spend no more than about 15 minutes per video. An hour on one video could have been 10 videos","Mistake 3, very low-quality posts: blurry screenshots from Google, Alibaba or Zendrop won't go viral. Aim for decent, not perfect","Mistake 4, not using the \"three W's\": make content people can comment on, react to and interact with, like the top videos on your own feed","Mistake 5, not posting enough: post 3–5 decent videos a day. A member who stayed consistent for over a month with no results eventually had a video take off and changed his business","Mistake 6, too many hashtags: stick to 3–5 relevant ones (e.g. fitness, viral, fyp, gym). Avoid tags that make your product easy for competitors to find"],"topics":["Website","Organic marketing","Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_Dra3y4IvdbUFX/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Tracking Audience W/ OM","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"How to figure out your target audience from the comments on organic marketing videos, plus creative marketing ideas from real examples.","points":["Look at who is commenting on organic videos for your product, or similar ones: names, profile pictures and how they write show their likely gender, age and lifestyle","Example: a self-care product's comments were mostly women around their 20s to middle age, likely city or apartment dwellers, so that's who to target with Facebook ads","Example: a protein/fitness product's comments skewed toward men","Use these patterns to confirm your audience before you spend money on ads","Clever trick: writing your shop name on your hand while demoing the product means viewers see your brand repeatedly in the video","Realistic-looking AI videos can work for organic marketing, but use real people for paid ads","Get creative with your hooks (e.g. calling out overpriced alternatives) to grab attention"],"topics":["Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_oCpEKB3rQG3Ik/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"OM Within FB Groups","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"How to use Facebook groups for community-driven, genuine marketing of your store.","points":["Why it works: groups are already niched down, so you reach people likely to care about your product. Facebook skews older, and that audience tends to buy what they need.","Find groups: search niche keywords (e.g. home decor, fitness, pets, moms), pick active groups (1,000+ members who post regularly), join 10–15 to start, and read each group's rules carefully.","Follow the 80/20 rule: spend ~80% of your time adding real value (answering questions, helpful tips) and only ~20% promoting.","Mention products naturally when they genuinely solve someone's problem, be upfront that it's your store, and offer a link if they want it.","Make sure you have stock and can deliver before promoting.","Content that converts: product-in-use demos, honest reviews, problem → solution stories, educational posts (e.g. ab workouts), and genuine limited-time offers.","Suggested timeline: weeks 1–2 pure value, week 3 casually share your story, week 5+ share products when relevant. Build relationships, not just sales.","Avoid: promoting right after joining, copy-paste posts that look like a bot, breaking group rules, and being pushy.","Track engagement (likes, comments, shares), conversations and website clicks (Shopify shows traffic sources)."],"topics":["Organic marketing","Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_KYVFTv0tyRrk7/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to use higgsfield","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"A quick walkthrough on using Higgsfield to make AI videos that feature your avatar and promote your product.","points":["Starts from research: find a video on another dropshipper's page to use as a reference for format and style","Shows how to download or copy that reference video so it can be brought into Higgsfield","Covers using Higgsfield with your avatar AND your product, not just a talking avatar","Explains how to fit the generated video into what you're already doing with your content and ads"],"topics":["AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_zQuP4qp0FsWvI/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"More with higgsfield","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"How to use AI to create UGC-style organic marketing videos for your product.","points":["Keep filming your own content too, for extra videos and to save money, but AI UGC can add a lot of volume","Search TikTok for UGC creator videos in your niche (e.g. \"UGC creator protein powder\") to find examples you like","Download the video, or watch it and describe it to Claude in detail, scene by scene with timestamps (what's shown, the setting, the hands, how the product is used)","Ask Claude to generate a script for your own product based on that description that you can paste into an AI video tool (Claude is recommended over ChatGPT for scripts)","In the AI UGC software, choose an avatar (avoid overused ones), upload a picture of your product and generate the video","You can also pick existing templates, click recreate with your product, and chop up different creatives into new videos"],"topics":["Organic marketing","AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_sIjc8MTfI6FhB/"},{"kind":"pdf","title":"Some higgsfield ai secrets","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"Quick tips for getting better results and saving credits in Higgsfield and Arcads.","points":["Render at lower resolution (1280 or less) to save credits","The better you describe the video you want to Claude/ChatGPT, the better the prompt it writes","Keep prompts organized but not overloaded, or the AI gets confused","Go prompt by prompt; don't generate more than ~30 seconds of video at once"],"topics":["AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_DUdmep7TI8FpJ/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"OM sauce (call)","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"A live masterclass with Q&A on how organic marketing and paid ads work together, and why you should use every marketing channel instead of picking just one.","points":["Q&A: do both MEO and content creation. Content should only take 20–30 minutes a day","Q&A: get comfortable with one store (dropshipping or clothing brand) before starting a second","Q&A: once samples arrive, start marketing with 2–3 videos posted across all platforms","Q&A: your product page should be a professional account. A personal page can be more casual","Organic is your free test: post anything (funny, random, AI) and see which videos get traction and retention, then run those as paid ads","If nothing pops organically, make a simple ad that shows how your product solves a problem. One good ad (made yourself or with AI) can run for months","Organic builds brand recognition and trust. Look at what competitors aren't doing and do it, like big retailers that market everywhere","Start with organic on a small budget, then put early profits into ads (around $5/day for a 3-day test)","Marketing is about reach: a decent product with great marketing beats a great product nobody sees","You don't have to show your face. Use friends, a UGC creator or AI, though showing yourself (e.g. modeling your clothing) builds more trust","Q&A: you don't need a certain follower count before running ads. Ads bring followers too","AI ad tools let you test products and make ads while you wait for samples"],"topics":["Calls","Organic marketing","Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_boXnhiS8faEps/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"UGC sauce (call)","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"A live masterclass with Q&A on finding, contacting and working with UGC creators to promote your product.","points":["Q&A: track a creator's results before raising their pay. A simple contract can require about 5 videos a week, no competitor work or mentions, and videos left up for a minimum period","Q&A: for clothing models, send the item, have them take their own photos, and get written permission to use them in marketing","Q&A: collect contacts with pop-ups, orders and a lock screen. Keep one offer for email and phone, and start with email","Q&A: before launch, use a Klaviyo landing page on your domain to collect early-access signups, then point the domain to your store when it opens","Q&A: foreign-language labels on outer packaging aren't a big deal. Add a thank-you card with a coupon for the next order to drive repeat buys and referrals","UGC creators are like affiliates who know how to push products. Even if you're good at marketing, a creator can bring your first back-to-back orders","Don't sleep on small creators: they're cheaper, easier to work with, and can blow up with your product on their page. Big creators often charge high prices","Reach out by DM and email. For known creators, also look for a second account or people close to them","In your first message, don't name an exact rate. Mention perks (e.g. paying up front), show other creators you've worked with, and let them name their price first so you don't overpay","Follow up persistently, since your first message may have been missed. Vary the message after a few tries","When negotiating with a creator who has their own business, be careful about sharing your revenue numbers","Teaser: new AI modules are coming, and e-commerce is still growing, not saturated"],"topics":["Calls","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_QqgVp6vh4l0P6/"},{"kind":"pdf","title":"Organic Content Playbook","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"The 3 W's, content ideas, when and how often to post, hashtags, hooks and calls to action.","points":[],"topics":["Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_z5a95IpymAtbk/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Scaling With Paid Ads","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"How to scale with Facebook/Meta ads the MEO way: validate first, then test with data and scale only proven winners.","points":["Meta (Facebook/Instagram) is one of the biggest ad platforms. Learn how it works before spending.","Non-negotiables before scaling: a validated product and audience, solid organic marketing (~100–120 posts, just 2–3 a day), some testimonials, and a product price that leaves room for ad costs (ideally $35+).","Rule 1, data first, budget later: start with 3–5 ads, test one interest per ad set (e.g. gym-goers), and begin low at ~$5–$10/day.","Key metrics: CPM (cost per 1,000 impressions, lower is better), CTR (aim for 2%+; low CTR means the wrong audience or a weak creative, so hook people in the first 2 seconds), and add-to-cart vs. checkout (many add-to-carts but few purchases points to pricing, shipping or checkout issues; use discount codes and follow-up emails).","Rule 2, scale winners, not hopes: if one product gets clearly more organic traction, put ad money behind that one. Don't reinvent what's working.","Targets: cost per purchase low relative to your price (roughly $5–$15) and ROAS around 1.3–3x to start.","Scaling: duplicate winning ad sets (ABO), use campaign budget (CBO) or Advantage+ shopping, and raise budgets only 20–30% every 24 hours. Never double overnight.","Refresh creatives every 3–5 days to avoid ad fatigue, and monitor results closely in the first 72 hours.","Mindset: the first hurdle is the hardest. Stay disciplined with organic before ads, and grow in every area of life, not just money."],"topics":["Paid ads","Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_9UCZGf9rs6D1A/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Watching Comps Meta Ads","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"How to use the Facebook (Meta) Ad Library to see what ads your competitors are running and get ideas for your own.","points":["Open the Facebook Ad Library, select \"All ads\", and search keywords for your niche (e.g. fitness, skincare)","Check how long an ad has been running: one that's been live for months is probably a winning, profitable ad","You only need one great ad or marketing video that you can keep running","Make ads in a similar style for your own product, but don't copy or take their ads directly (for legal reasons)","Look for common patterns across winning ads in your niche and write them down","Ads using real people tend to outperform obvious AI ads","Do this research for any niche, even if you're building a brand"],"topics":["Product research","Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_lhhIESychISDh/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Running Facebook Ads (UPDATED 2026.)","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"A step-by-step walkthrough of setting up a Facebook/Meta sales ad campaign in Ads Manager.","points":["In Ads Manager, click Create and choose the Sales objective. Traffic, awareness and engagement are mainly for hyping a drop before launch.","Choose manual setup (or Advantage+), and set the conversion event to Purchase.","Connect your Meta Pixel through the Facebook & Instagram app in Shopify.","Audience size: avoid too narrow or too broad. Use what organic marketing taught you about your audience.","Locations: commonly the US, UK, Canada and Australia for English stores. Target specific regions only for seasonal reasons (e.g. summer clothes in the opposite hemisphere).","Budget: start small (~$5/day) until you know your analytics.","Schedule: start at 12 a.m. the next day (gives Meta time to process) and set an end date 7–10 days out so you don't overspend.","Only scale budget once an ad is clearly outperforming. Skip Meta's auto-generated ads.","Connect your brand's Instagram and Facebook pages.","Creative: a single image or video works best (Canva can help with design). Primary text, headline and description should be short and clear (e.g. restock news, limited stock, a first-orders discount).","Use a Shop Now call to action, add your promo code, and use a clean display link (your domain)."],"topics":["Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_jzF8ZxAEycUAP/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"In Depth FB Ads","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"More on Facebook (Meta) ads: how to set up multiple creatives within a campaign so you can test them against the same audience and budget.","points":["Click Create and keep the buying type on Auction (Reservation is for more advanced, predictable buys)","Review the campaign objectives (awareness, traffic, engagement, app promotion, leads, sales) and pick the one that matches your goal, e.g. Sales","Advantage+ lets Meta's AI help with targeting if you want it","Think of the campaign as a folder: duplicate ad sets inside the original campaign to run different creatives side by side","Name everything clearly (e.g. \"Water gun – testing creatives\", \"Water gun 2\") so you can tell your tests apart","Use A/B (creative) testing to compare versions. An incomplete ad shows a warning marker on the side","Use the ad preview to check how it will look, and connect both your Facebook and Instagram profiles for more reach","Choose the right call to action for your goal (e.g. Shop Now or Buy Now for sales, sign-up for leads), and turn on partnership ads if you're running with creators"],"topics":["Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_lFpgvLyz4I9nU/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"DIFFERENT AI'S FOR DIFFERENT THINGS","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"A live masterclass with Q&A on which AI tools to use for different parts of your e-commerce business, plus member wins and shipping troubleshooting.","points":["Q&A: when a Zendrop order stops updating in transit, contact support, wait up to a full 30 days, then reship or refund the customer if it still hasn't arrived","Refunds still apply when a customer never received their product","Know which AI fits which job, instead of using one tool for everything","Higgsfield: videos (UGC, organic content, some paid ads)","Claude: website coding, email copy, video scripts, hooks and content ideas","ChatGPT: images (ad images, product photo shoots), prompts, copywriting and general business questions","Lovable: mainly front-end websites and landing pages, and it can connect to Shopify","Base44: front-end websites plus custom software (e.g. inventory or UGC creator tracking) that can connect to Shopify or Wix","Using AI the right way saves time and money compared with paying people for copywriting, prompts or UGC","Member win: adding clear CTAs in captions quickly turned a stagnant store into daily sales. Tell viewers where to buy","Post on Snapchat and YouTube Shorts too, and add bundle or add-on products (no more than 5–6) to raise average order value"],"topics":["Calls","AI tools","Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_sJnFF7mQdjeEH/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to Utilize AI w/ FB Ads","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"A tour of Facebook (Meta) Ads Manager after its newer AI-powered update, and how to build a sales campaign step by step.","points":["The structure: a campaign is the folder, ad sets are the pages inside it, and each ad is one specific page","Create a campaign on Auction with the Sales objective, give the folder a clear name, and leave the default settings","Start with about a $5 budget to warm up a new ad account","Turn on A/B testing to compare up to seven creatives over about seven days and see which performs best","At the ad set level, aim to maximize conversions (or website visits), and optionally let Meta adjust media and text for different customers","Set an end date or leave it running, and build or adjust your audience and demographics","Ad transparency isn't necessary. Use placements to focus on platforms like Instagram if you want","Use the ad preview, add your website URL, then upload your creative. Image ads suit branded stores, while video ads suit dropshipping or problem-solving products","Meta's new AI features can generate extra creative versions from your images","Don't stress if the campaign score is a bit low, as long as everything else is set up correctly"],"topics":["Paid ads","AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_FcfpILzQvg5bX/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Maxxing customers/paid ad sauce","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"A live masterclass with Q&A on making paid ads that convert. The main idea is that every ad should show how your product solves a problem.","points":["Biggest mistake: showing what a product does without showing how it improves the buyer's life. Show the problem and the fix, or just the fix in a relatable way","Photo ads (e.g. a supplier image) work for awareness, like before a launch date or if you're already a big brand. For sales, use a video that solves a problem","Awareness campaigns: a picture with your logo, no offer. Sales campaigns: problem-solving creative plus an offer such as a discount","Ways to make the creative: order a sample and film it yourself (one good hour-long shoot can run for a year), hire a UGC creator (some do it for around $10 plus the product), or ask friends","AI ads only work if they look completely real (e.g. Higgsfield). Fake-looking AI makes people think it's a scam and lowers conversions","Start with small creators (around 1,000 followers): they reply more and charge less. Many small creators beat one expensive celebrity post when you're starting","Some people will promote for free product. If they genuinely like it, it often keeps showing up in their videos","Even basic products can be framed as solving a need (warmth plus style for a hoodie). Use AI to brainstorm angles","Q&A: new members should start with the welcome/mindset and beginner modules","Q&A: if a Klaviyo pop-up isn't showing, check the timer, try a different browser, re-publish in Shopify or contact Klaviyo support","Q&A: Meta's tools only connect to Facebook and Instagram, not TikTok"],"topics":["Calls","Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_UHZVHncIl6XtV/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"More paid ad sauce","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"A live masterclass with Q&A ranking the types of paid ad creative that drive sales, plus how to research and test winning ads.","points":["Q&A: to use an AI voiceover in CapCut, screen-record the audio and add it as the video's background audio. Run the AI-voice version as a new ad next to your original and compare","Q&A: if Instagram won't schedule posts, post manually and space posts out (about every 2 hours)","Organic can be goofy test content, but paid ads should be serious and answer \"why should I buy this?\"","Rough sales ratings: photo/awareness ads 2–3/10, comparison ads (yours vs. other brands) ~7/10, problem-and-solution videos ~8/10, problem-and-solution video testimonials ~9/10","Photo ads mostly build awareness. They work better for big brands or looks-based products like clothing","Video testimonials should be 30 seconds to 1 minute. A plain star review is much weaker (~4/10)","Get real testimonials from happy customers, e.g. an automated email inviting an honest video review in return for a coupon","Ask people around you what makes them want to buy, or hesitate, after watching your ad","Use the Facebook Ad Library for inspiration: ads a competitor has run for 3–6+ months are likely profitable. Take inspiration from the angle without copying exactly. Shop Hunter can show some stores' analytics","Testing: run 2–4 creatives at about $5–10/day each, then put more budget into (or duplicate) the one with the best click-through rate","Early on, reinvest most profit (around 85%) back into the business. If you're selling out, put it into inventory. If you have extra stock, put it into ads (not financial advice)","Q&A: light brightness tweaks to videos are fine, but skip cheesy filters","Ends with members sharing their weekly wins"],"topics":["Calls","Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_t2kYUUtKCHRHo/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Secret Paid ad INFO. (CALL)","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"A live masterclass with Q&A on the difference between organic marketing (\"practice\") and paid ads (\"game time\"), plus inventory and Meta Ads questions.","points":["Organic content should entertain. People scrolling TikTok, Instagram, etc. want to be entertained, not shopped at","Paid ads are shown to people the platforms already think will buy (based on what they've searched across apps). Ads should focus on selling, not getting likes","Organic is practice: post freely, learn which creatives people like, and don't over-produce it. Still keep it clean and not unprofessional","Organic's three jobs: views, branding/familiarity and finding your target audience. Familiar brands convert better when you later run ads","Ad buyers often check your organic pages to see if you're legit, and organic comments give free feedback","Q&A: platforms track browsing and search activity (cookies) to show related ads. SEO also helps you rank in search","Q&A: when products are selling, scale the current model before upgrading. Put roughly 70–75% of funds into inventory and keep a reserve for ads (not financial advice)","Q&A: get creators to make videos aimed at a different audience, and consider a separate Facebook/Instagram page for AI content (e.g. Higgsfield)","Q&A: Higgsfield beginners can start on a ~$20/month plan with about 250 credits, enough for a handful of videos to remix in CapCut","Q&A: when restocking, compare air vs. sea shipping. Air is faster but cuts margin, and bigger orders can lower unit cost","Q&A: Meta's \"high cost\" or \"limited creative diversity\" warnings are often upsells. If an ad is already getting sales, don't edit it mid-run. Note changes for the next ad"],"topics":["Calls","Organic marketing","Paid ads","Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_RBWGm9D7eWH4g/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Different creatives with Paid Ads. (CALL)","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"A live masterclass with Q&A on how creatives (the video or picture in your marketing) should differ between organic and paid ads, plus scaling, inventory and email advice.","points":["Q&A: in videos and ads, show what YOUR version of the product does differently, so buyers see why it's worth more than an Amazon alternative","Organic works best with video (or slideshows). Aim for about 3 posts a day, with at least 7 out of 10 being videos you'd actually expect on your feed","Paid ads can be pictures or videos, but every ad (10/10) should look like a real ad with a clear offer, since you're spending money on it","Before running ads, check the Facebook Ad Library. If competitors run very different creatives from yours, switch it up","Don't reinvent the wheel: if winning ads are short 10-second clips, don't make 10-minute videos. Use the \"5% change\" approach (new location, reworded caption) instead of copying exactly","Start with your own creativity. If ~50–100 posts get little traction, take more inspiration from competitors","Give a creative a fair test (around $100+, depending on order value) before judging it. A strong creative matters more than perfect targeting","Q&A: Ad Library doesn't show performance, but long-running ads are a good sign. Shop Hunter can show some stores' sales","Q&A: when an ad gets sales, scale by putting more budget into it. Follower count isn't important. Post ad creatives organically too, and boost strong organic videos as ads","Q&A: unboxing ads should show the product in the first 3 seconds. Static ads need the product name, a brief description and a clear CTA","Q&A: Shopify may label TikTok traffic as \"direct\". When scaling, a lower margin (~25%) can be OK. Weigh express vs. regular shipping for restocks and use pre-orders if needed","Set up email marketing now: abandoned-cart flows alone can recover a meaningful share of carts. Also reply to comments with a CTA","Q&A: faceless content works (hands-only videos, or showing just your neck for jewelry). Masked personas fit streetwear brands","Q4 is the biggest season. Run holiday sales on evergreen products rather than making holiday-only products"],"topics":["Calls","Organic marketing","Paid ads","Email & SMS"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_xE7tvDFj1xIfs/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Scaling Meta Ads Without Breaking the Algorithm","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"A simple Meta (Facebook) ads strategy for reading your campaign results and scaling winning ads without breaking the algorithm.","points":["\"Learning phase\" means Meta is still gathering data and figuring out your audience. An ad can be completed or not delivering but still switched on, so check both","Cost per result is what it costs to get one purchase. Judge it against your product price and margin (e.g. ~$48 can be fine on a $200–300 product)","Turn off ad sets with a much higher cost per result and move that budget into the winners, or split it between the remaining good ones","Scale budgets gradually: about 10–30% per day (50% at most). Big jumps push the ad to new audiences and can spike your cost per result","Don't react emotionally to one great day. Wait until an ad is clearly profitable before raising its budget","Use a 7-day click attribution setting and leave Meta's automatic creative \"enhancements\" (like added music) off, since they can throw off conversion rates","Name your campaigns to stay organized, and remember a new ad can take a little while to show delivery","Key metrics to watch: cost per result, results, budget, CTR and ROAS"],"topics":["Organic marketing","Paid ads","Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_peDYhbphhN2wC/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Reading Ad Metrics & Raising Your Budget","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"How to read the key metrics in Facebook Ads Manager and decide which ads to keep on, kill or give more time.","points":["CPM (cost per 1,000 impressions): $20–30 is normal; very cheap isn't always good, it can mean a cheap audience","CPC under $2 is fine, under $1 is solid; CTR 2% is fine, 2–3%+ is strong","CPA (cost per result) is what each sale costs you; compare it to your own break-even, e.g. $30 price − $10 COGS = $20 max","ROAS 2.0+ is good; also watch add to carts, checkouts initiated and frequency","Start testing at $20–50/day; around $10 spend, check for clicks and add to carts, but let a new ad account warm up a few days","Real examples: great CPC and CTR but no add to carts after $14 = killed; bad CTR but sales at a $20 CPA = kept on","If several similar ads flop, the near-copies will likely flop too; make iterations of your winners"],"topics":["Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_pIEUwCHNVPPt6/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Reading Your Paid Ad Metrics (CV)","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"How to read the key metrics in Facebook Ads Manager and decide which ads to keep running or turn off, using real campaign examples.","points":["CPM (cost per 1,000 impressions): about $20–30 is typical. Very cheap isn't always good, since it can mean a lower-value audience","CPC (cost per click): under $2 is fine, under $1 is solid","CTR (click-through rate): 2% is fine, 2–3%+ is solid","Cost per result (CPA): what each sale costs you. Compare it to your break-even (price minus cost of goods). If CPA gets near break-even, you're not making profit","Also track add to carts, checkouts initiated, purchases, ROAS (2.0+ is good) and frequency (how many times each person sees the ad)","Everyone's targets differ because margins differ. Pricier products can afford a higher CPA and more testing time","Testing: start around $20–50/day. After about $10 spend, check for clicks and add to carts. Give new ad accounts a few days to warm up before judging","Good CPC and CTR mean nothing without add to carts and purchases. An ad can be engaging to watch without making people want to buy","Examples: ads with strong clicks but no add to carts, or $40 spent with no purchase, get turned off. An ad with weak CTR but a profitable CPA stays on","If several similar ad variations are failing, the rest probably will too. Make new variations of your winners, since winning ads fade over time"],"topics":["Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_j4EYhXml2LvE8/"},{"kind":"pdf","title":"Paid Ads Cheat Sheet","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"Meta campaign setup, benchmarks (CPM, CPC, CTR, ROAS), when to kill or keep an ad and how to scale.","points":[],"topics":["Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_KbY5pwNUZPhkB/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"PUMPIING your ads ($$) call","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"A masterclass on not burning money with ads: how to read your ad numbers, which ads to scale or kill, and how to raise budget safely.","points":["Check your ad analytics every day, but for data, not emotions; never run an ad and ghost it for a week","Raise budget only 10–30% a day; jumping from $120 to $1,000/day confuses the algorithm and it shows the ad to anyone","Low CTR = bad ad. High CTR but no sales = a website problem (missing reviews, testimonials, trust)","Structure: campaigns → a couple of ad sets → 3–5 ads each; compare CTR and ROAS across them","Scale the one with good ROAS even if CTR is only decent; good ROAS with low CTR needs a harder call to action, same concept","Kill ads with both low CTR and bad ROAS (e.g. 0.25x) and move that budget to the winners; ROAS matters more than conversion rate","Give an ad 5–7 days for the learning phase, let Andromeda pick the audience, and keep it to about 5 creatives per ad set (mix images and videos); put a strong creative in its own ad set if Meta starves it","Q&A on AI video: 720p is fine if your images are sharp, upscale in CapCut instead of paying to render, Kling 4.0 and Seedance tips, and use ChatGPT for reference images"],"topics":["Calls","Paid ads","Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_NiU3iqLgqbPCK/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to email market (Klaviyo)","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"How to create good email marketing content, using the Shopify Messaging app (the same ideas work in Klaviyo).","points":["Install the free Shopify Messaging app to practice email campaigns before moving to Klaviyo","Brand every email: add your logo and store name, pick a clean, readable font, set your colors, and add a footer with your address and social links (TikTok, IG)","For content ideas, subscribe to other brands' emails (e.g. GLD) and study how they market, from product promos to deals like \"2 for 1 Tuesday\"","Not every email needs a discount. Think of emails as reminders for people who meant to come back or never checked out","You can paste a competitor's email into ChatGPT and ask it to rewrite the idea in your own words for your product","Every email should have a clickable button (e.g. \"Shop Now\") and a product image","Simple email ideas: \"We just restocked\", \"Sale ending soon\", or seasonal hooks like \"Feeling cold?\""],"topics":["Website","Organic marketing","Email & SMS"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_uRARnGpepOT0U/"},{"kind":"pdf","title":"Best ways to raise CVR with email marketing","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"How to write marketing emails people open and click, to raise your conversion rate.","points":["Subject lines: short, curious, one relevant emoji (e.g. \"Your nighttime routine is missing this 👀\"); test different ones","Give a reason to buy: a clear offer (discount, bundle, free shipping) and one obvious button like Shop now","Mix promo emails with tips, product demos and customer stories; only use deadlines when they're real","Use real customer photos, videos and reviews (with permission) to answer \"how does it work?\" and \"will I like it?\"","Before sending: is there one clear reason to care and one easy next step?"],"topics":["Website","Email & SMS"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_EgxMzu6gW4Xgk/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"The Daily Activities for Your Store","where":"Dropshipping · Logistics","summary":"The daily tasks you should do to run your store, which take about an hour and 20 minutes a day.","points":["Push out marketing and check on it (~35 min): post organic videos, DM creators, run Facebook ads, then see which creators replied, who your organic content is reaching, and which ads perform best","Stay updated on products and trends (~30 min): scroll a burner TikTok account whose feed is full of other dropshippers and brands, and look for trending topics you can tie into your store (e.g. looksmaxxing, or a hat with a fan when everyone's talking about the heat)","Check your email and watch competitors (Facebook Ad Library, etc.). If they jump on a new product or marketing trend, you should too","Customer service: answer customer emails quickly, such as \"where's my order?\" (AI can help draft replies)","Check Shopify (~5–10 min) to make sure orders are shipping","Top two priorities: pushing out marketing and staying on top of trends and products, since these drive sales and scaling","At minimum, do the free stuff daily (organic content and DMing creators). Getting a sample to film your own videos helps a lot"],"topics":[],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_MQmukrHLIqwHt/lessons/lesn_VhfmVn8afo9Zc/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Fulfillment models sauce","where":"Dropshipping · Logistics","summary":"A live masterclass on the different fulfillment models you can use for your store, plus member Q&A on setup, shipping addresses and creator outreach.","points":["Q&A: while your website is being built, study marketing (Facebook Ad Library, competitors) and start posting so you have visitors ready at launch","Dropshipping is a fulfillment model, not a business model: you never touch the product, and the supplier ships straight to the customer","Best dropshipping apps with auto-fulfillment: Zendrop, CJ Dropshipping and AutoDS (AliExpress can work when starting, but often needs manual order spreadsheets)","B2B then B2C: the supplier ships stock to you and you ship to customers. More work and requires an MOQ (minimum order quantity), but good for branded or customized products","3PL (third-party logistics): the supplier ships to a 3PL that packs and ships for you, usually around $2 per package and best once you have high order volume","Warehouse: you own or rent the space and hire packers, often cheaper than a 3PL at scale","Recommended path: start with dropshipping to learn, then move to B2B/3PL/warehouse as you scale or brand your products, so packing doesn't eat the time you need for marketing","You can use multiple suppliers across platforms, which is handy when testing products","If you ship orders yourself, use a P.O. box as the return address for privacy and returns (LLC registration still needs a real address)","When creators ask for details, explain the problem your product solves so they know they're promoting something trustworthy"],"topics":["Calls","Organic marketing","Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_MQmukrHLIqwHt/lessons/lesn_LWuSzrzQlP7qT/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Quick Lesson on Shipping Rates","where":"Dropshipping · Logistics","summary":"A quick example of how to set up simple shipping rates for your store.","points":["Instead of complex calculated shipping, you can set up simple shipping zones and flat rates","Example setup: free shipping on orders of $150+, economy (1–5 lbs) at $12, economy (5–70 lbs) at $21, and international economy at $43","Around $43 is a reasonable average for international shipping given current costs"],"topics":["Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_MQmukrHLIqwHt/lessons/lesn_t119u5dBBIjc3/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to Test a Supplier on Zendrop","where":"Dropshipping · Logistics","summary":"How to check and test suppliers on Zendrop before adding their products to your store.","points":["Search your niche on Zendrop and scroll past the first results to find products","Open a product and check its details, especially the shipping country (e.g. United States) and average shipping time (e.g. 2 days)","Tip: create a separate Zendrop account for product research so you see fresh product suggestions","Zendrop suppliers are generally legit, since they want repeat business","Red flag: \"suppliers\" who message you out of nowhere on WhatsApp, with no platform behind them to verify they're real","Zendrop and AutoDS are B2C dropshipping (they ship straight to your customer), while Alibaba is mostly B2B (shipped to you first), though some Alibaba suppliers also dropship"],"topics":["Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_MQmukrHLIqwHt/lessons/lesn_VDvRkCRaatENL/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Verifying Alibaba Suppliers","where":"Dropshipping · Logistics","summary":"How to verify that Alibaba suppliers are legit before you order, for both branded products and dropshipping.","points":["Alibaba is mostly B2B: wholesalers ship stock to your business. Some suppliers will also dropship straight to customers if you ask","On a product page, scroll down to the supplier card and check the store rating (around 4.5+ is good), the reorder rate (about 20–30% is strong) and the response time","A response time of several hours is normal, since suppliers are across the world. Be patient with them","Store reviews aren't product reviews. A product with few or no reviews can still be fine, since there are far fewer business buyers than consumers","Also check how long they've been in business and how many orders they've shipped (e.g. to North America)","Use \"Send inquiry\" to ask about customizing the product, such as your logo, color, formula or packaging","Check the MOQ (minimum order quantity) and negotiate it, and the price, down. Many suppliers will work with you","Alibaba's secured payments, easy returns and money-back protection help if a supplier doesn't deliver","Start with a sample and test the market before placing a bulk order"],"topics":["Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_MQmukrHLIqwHt/lessons/lesn_jbmOlvT2JvK1D/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to Hire Safely and Smartly","where":"Dropshipping · Logistics","summary":"How to hire people for your store safely and smartly, and what to look out for.","points":["You can hire on Fiverr or similar sites, or hire family and friends, but above all make sure they're good, trustworthy people","Don't give full access right away. Start with limited permissions and increase them only after people prove themselves","Customer service hires: look for clear communication, quick responses and the ability to stay calm. Ask how they'd handle an angry customer whose package is late","Media buyers: ask what budgets and niches they've managed, their testing strategies and how they handle losing campaigns. Watch for people who blame the algorithm for everything","Start with a small paid test task (product research, competitor research, data entry or customer service). A one-hour test can save months of headaches","Look for reliability: fast responses, showing up on time and following instructions exactly","Protect your store with contracts, NDAs and confidentiality agreements","Use separate logins for each person, and remove access or change passwords when someone leaves"],"topics":["Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_MQmukrHLIqwHt/lessons/lesn_TPmUsofBueQhW/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"What Actually is Branding?","where":"Dropshipping · Logistics","summary":"What branding your dropshipping store and products means, along with the pros, cons and when to do it.","points":["Branding makes your store look and feel like a real business. At minimum, your store should have a name and logo","Branding the product means having your supplier put your logo on the item itself","Branding the product isn't mandatory, but branding your store is pretty necessary","Pros: people recognize your product in ads, you build a stronger brand presence (like Gatorade), a more loyal fan base, less competition, and more money","Cons: it can cost a little more per piece (e.g. ~$0.10–$0.20), and you may have to hold inventory if your supplier won't brand and dropship","Message your supplier (e.g. on Zendrop) to ask if they can add your logo and how much extra it costs per piece. The added value also lets you raise your price","Before branding, make sure it's the right product by getting some wins first (sales or strong interest like \"where can I get this?\")","Ask yourself if you're ready to hold inventory. If your supplier can't brand it, you may need to source the same product on Alibaba"],"topics":["Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_MQmukrHLIqwHt/lessons/lesn_NSpsf0lp2ohm4/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to Brand","where":"Dropshipping · Logistics","summary":"How to customize and brand a product as your own through an Alibaba supplier.","points":["Do this after you've found a winning product. It adds personality and a brand to your store","Find the product on Alibaba, click \"Chat now\", and ask if they'll let you customize it and what the price difference is","Be patient, since suppliers are often in very different time zones","Ask for their MOQ (minimum order quantity). When starting out, look for the lowest MOQ so you can sell at your own pace (e.g. clothing is often around 50)","Customization MOQs are usually higher than regular ones, but you can negotiate (e.g. \"Can I start with 100 since I'm just starting out?\")","Decide your max MOQ in advance and compare suppliers until you find one that fits. Check their reviews too","Have your logo and brand name ready for them to put on the product","Always order a sample first before placing a bulk order"],"topics":["Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_MQmukrHLIqwHt/lessons/lesn_1f86Qk8cY7pW3/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"5 Biggest Mistakes Ppl Make in MEO","where":"Dropshipping · Logistics","summary":"The 5 biggest mistakes people make when starting (or running) their e-commerce store.","points":["Mistake 1, not sleeping on your ideas: take action, but before you finalize a logo, name or product (ordering samples, launching marketing), wait overnight and re-check it with fresh eyes","Mistake 2, asking \"will it work?\" The real question is \"will you work?\" With 8 billion people in the world, even a tiny fraction is a huge potential audience","Mistake 3, a limited mindset: no brand has sold to everyone, and new online shoppers appear every day. Don't be scared off because a big brand sells something similar. Out-market them instead","Mistake 4, focusing too much on how your store looks: when starting, you just need a decent store, a pop-up to collect contact info and a good domain","Mistake 5, limiting your marketing: post on every platform you can and do anything that puts your product in front of more people"],"topics":["Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_MQmukrHLIqwHt/lessons/lesn_bnhktFbWhQRCR/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to Fix Your Mistakes","where":"Dropshipping · Logistics","summary":"An intro to the \"mistakes\" section of the course and how to get the most out of it.","points":["The upcoming videos focus on common mistakes, some the mentor made himself and some he sees members make often","A big part of mentorship is learning what not to do, saving you time, money and energy","Avoiding these mistakes helps your store succeed faster","Watch all the videos and take notes, adding a \"don't do this\" note under the matching section (website, marketing, etc.)"],"topics":[],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_MQmukrHLIqwHt/lessons/lesn_xCdQzsEwaOWpx/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Classic Mistakes to Avoid in Ecommerce","where":"Dropshipping · Logistics","summary":"A live call on four big mistakes to avoid in e-commerce, plus Q&A on CTAs, suppliers and scaling ads.","points":["Mistake 1, spending too much time on website looks: focus on systems instead. Put your product on the home page, use clear Add to Cart/Buy Now buttons and a fast checkout, and add a pop-up. Marketing gets the sales, not fancy design (look at how simple Amazon makes buying)","Mistake 2, being inconsistent with organic: it's free ads (1,000 views is roughly $1,500 of ad spend saved), it shows you your audience, and it's practice for making good creatives before paid ads. You don't need to show your face","Mistake 3, putting off CRM: collect emails and phone numbers from day one. Big brands use pop-ups, and even 1% of a 10,000-person list buying adds up. Klaviyo gives you 250 contacts free. Test emails landing in junk is normal","Mistake 4, comparing yourself to others: \"don't let your plate get cold looking at someone else's food.\" Learn from competitors, but don't jump between products or business ideas chasing what others are doing","Q&A: add a private supplier as a fulfillment location in Shopify's shipping profiles (no plan upgrade needed)","Q&A: put CTAs in your bio and comments (e.g. reply to a top comment with \"link in bio\") rather than heavily in captions, which platforms may suppress","Q&A: a ~5% click-through rate on Meta is excellent (average is about 1.2%). Scale the budget by no more than 10% per day and test more creatives with different models","Set goals around freedom and value, not just a number, and get wins outside of money too (gym, faith, personal growth)"],"topics":["Calls","Paid ads","Suppliers & shipping","Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_MQmukrHLIqwHt/lessons/lesn_r9dad7mKtHClR/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"What to do if Problems do Come Up","where":"Dropshipping · Logistics","summary":"What to do when technical issues or other problems come up while running your store.","points":["The mentor's personal first step is to pray, then work out what kind of problem it is","Decide whether it's a technical issue (e.g. Shopify showing an error when adding payments or an app) or a personal/account issue (e.g. you got logged out or forgot your password)","Technical issue: contact the company involved, such as Shopify, your payment processor, or your supplier app (Zendrop, AutoDS)","Personal or setup issue: ask your personal coach or post in the Discord, since someone has likely dealt with it before","Fastest fix: do both. Contact the company's support and post in the Discord at the same time","The team will help research unusual issues, but they're business mentors, not tech support, so some problems will still need the platform's support team"],"topics":[],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_MQmukrHLIqwHt/lessons/lesn_necu6kDthNDJk/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Influencers are Your Best Friends","where":"Dropshipping · Scaling","summary":"Why influencers are your best friends when starting out, and how to reach out and partner with them.","points":["Search TikTok for influencers in your niche (e.g. \"fitness influencers\", \"skincare tips\", \"pet advice\") and look for creators who post consistently","DM them as a company: introduce your brand and what you specialize in, and offer a sponsorship","Many influencers want to earn more but don't have their own products, so there are thousands open to deals","You don't have to pay up front. Give them a custom Shopify discount code and a percentage of revenue (e.g. 25%) from sales made with it","Or offer a flat rate per video (e.g. $50 for five videos, or about $25 per video)","ChatGPT can help write outreach messages, but tailor the script to each person (a dedicated script module is coming)","The bigger goal isn't only sales: a trusted influencer in your niche builds credibility, so people trust your other marketing more"],"topics":["Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_csjsbyldmmm1R/lessons/lesn_KThR22zqKzzHx/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Using AI To Reach Out to Creators","where":"Dropshipping · Scaling","summary":"How to use AI (ChatGPT or Claude) to write outreach messages to creators so they'll promote your product.","points":["Prompt the AI to write a text, DM or email asking a creator to promote your product, and tell it what you're offering","Offer ladder: start with free product for a video, then money plus product, then commission plus product. Big creators may want payment up front","Ask the AI to make it eye-catching and natural, keep it short, and revise it until it sounds right","Explain what your brand stands for and why it fits the creator's audience","Use \"we\"/\"our\" if you have a team, which can make the outreach feel more established","Don't name an exact price or commission rate in the first message. Say you're open to paying up front or offering commission, then negotiate once they reply","Mention the long-term opportunity if their content performs well, and end with \"reply back if interested\"","An intriguing opener (e.g. \"Did you hear about this?\") can get more creators to open the DM"],"topics":["Organic marketing","AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_csjsbyldmmm1R/lessons/lesn_ZwOrkl1eXs63I/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"The Key to Customer Lifetime Value (CLV)","where":"Dropshipping · Scaling","summary":"Mastering customer lifetime value (CLV) and retention so your store earns sustainable income instead of constantly chasing new customers.","points":["The common mistake: spending heavily on ads for one-time buyers who never return, like filling a leaky bucket","Keeping a customer is 5–25x cheaper than getting a new one, and repeat customers drive most revenue","CLV = average order value × purchase frequency × customer lifespan (e.g. $50 × 3/year × 2 years = $300)","Knowing your CLV lets you confidently spend more to acquire a customer, even breaking even on the first sale","Strategy 1, upsells and cross-sells: volume discounts, bundles, premium versions, and post-purchase one-click add-ons (apps like CartHook, Zipify OCU, or Shopify's built-in feature)","Strategy 2, subscriptions: find a consumable product in your niche (treats, refills, supplies) and offer subscriber perks with easy skip or pause options (Recharge, Bold)","Strategy 3, email/SMS: welcome series, abandoned cart, post-purchase and win-back flows, and review requests 7–10 days after delivery. Keep texts short, time-sensitive and easy to opt out of","Strategy 4, community: a private Facebook group, Discord or Instagram broadcast list with tips, Q&As, early access and UGC contests","Tools: Klaviyo/Omnisend (email), Postscript/Attentive (SMS), Smile.io/LoyaltyLion/Yotpo (loyalty), and GA4 or Triple Whale (analytics)","90-day roadmap: month 1, analytics, email flows and bundles; month 2, upsells, loyalty and SMS tests; month 3, subscriptions, community and a value ladder","Focus on one retention strategy at a time, and test and measure everything"],"topics":["Email & SMS"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_csjsbyldmmm1R/lessons/lesn_UdtLdeoCR4uD4/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Post-Purchase Upsells to Raise AOV","where":"Dropshipping · Scaling","summary":"How to set up post-purchase upsells to raise your average order value (AOV), shown with the Kitchen Upsell app.","points":["An upsell offers customers a deal on more product right after they buy, e.g. buy for $40, then get offered a second one at around 50% off","The app used here is Kitchen Upsell (14-day free trial), though other upsell apps work too","Create the upsell manually (or try the AI option), and preview it in mobile view","Use a post-purchase upsell page rather than a thank-you page upsell, since customers are more likely to buy before they leave checkout","Choose which products trigger the offer and set the upsell discount, after checking your cost of goods so you still profit","Skip downsells when you're starting out, since too many offers can make your store feel cheap","Keep the design simple, use a clean color (green signals a deal), and turn on reviews next to the product for credibility","Once you're getting regular daily sales, you'll notice customers taking the upsell"],"topics":["Website","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_csjsbyldmmm1R/lessons/lesn_1p5JamFPMaZpF/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Using a PB to Create Trust","where":"Dropshipping · Scaling","summary":"How to build a personal brand that creates trust and helps scale your dropshipping store.","points":["Trust beats reach: 1,000 people who trust you are worth more than a million who don't. Shoppers are skeptical of faceless stores with generic supplier videos","Put a real person behind the store: your face, your hands or your voice, plus genuinely engaging with comments","Content that converts: problem/solution stories, behind-the-scenes (testing, packing, quality checks), honest before/afters and free educational tips that feature your product","Short-form video (TikTok, Reels, Shorts) gives huge free reach. Spend 30 minutes a day making 3–5 videos of 15–60 seconds. Longer YouTube videos can tell your story and build deeper trust","Be relatable and raw: minimal editing, a conversational tone, and talk to viewers like friends","Transparency is your secret weapon: unboxings, honest reviews that mention flaws or shipping times, and showing how you resolve customer issues","Trust path: discovery, then visiting your profile, connecting, following, purchasing and coming back","30-day launch: week 1, find your niche and film 20 pieces of content; week 2, post daily across platforms; week 3, refine what works; week 4, scale with UGC, email and micro-influencers","Set up a personal account that promotes your store (face photo, clear bio, call to action linking to the store), respond to every comment, and post even if it feels uncomfortable at first"],"topics":["Website","Organic marketing","Suppliers & shipping","Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_csjsbyldmmm1R/lessons/lesn_QZS7bCQldNjPV/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Mixing that PB w/ Your Website","where":"Dropshipping · Scaling","summary":"How to mix a personal brand with your store using a two-account strategy to build trust.","points":["Most shoppers need to trust a brand before they buy. A human face (yours, a friend's, or even just your hands and voice) separates you from faceless stores","Two-account strategy: a personal account that humanizes the business, plus a professional brand account (logo, product showcases, promotions)","Personal account setup: a clear face photo, a 2–3 sentence authentic bio about what you help people with, and a link to your store","Content that connects: life stories, your workspace and packing, business wins and losses (e.g. switching to a better supplier), and everyday moments","Platform tips: Instagram for lifestyle posts with some business, TikTok for unpolished authentic content, X for quick thoughts that can go viral, LinkedIn for business connections","Subtle selling: mention your store naturally (\"just added this product I can't stop using\"), with about 90% authentic content and 10% business","Timeline: month 1, just share your story and post consistently; months 2–3, start weaving in your business; after that, promote more openly","Action steps: set up the account, post 4–5 times daily, and genuinely engage with 10+ people in your niche every day"],"topics":["Website","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_csjsbyldmmm1R/lessons/lesn_1p0Y6bIjrfoSp/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Don't Waste Your Energy on These","where":"Dropshipping · Scaling","summary":"The things people waste too much energy on when starting or running an e-commerce store.","points":["Over-designing your website: customers come for the product, not your color scheme. Keep the site clean and functional and move on","Over-perfecting organic content: spending 15+ minutes on a 5-second video leads to burnout. Focus on pushing out consistent, valuable content instead of perfect videos","Over-perfecting your product: make sure the design and quality are good, but don't obsess over tiny details. Your energy is better spent elsewhere","Listing too many products: you only need 1–3 products. Stores that sell everything (dog beds, treadmills, etc.) burn out before they ever get to marketing","Upselling is worth your energy, especially when you're first starting out"],"topics":[],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_csjsbyldmmm1R/lessons/lesn_TZcgumjPk42b2/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Avoid These 3 Mistakes","where":"Dropshipping · Scaling","summary":"Three e-commerce deal breakers that kill sales, and how to fix each one.","points":["Mistake 1, ignoring your audience: organic marketing is for finding your audience. Focus on one niche so customers feel connected to your brand, like Nike or Jordan","Broad targeting leads to low click-through and conversion rates, while competitors win by targeting one audience well (even Amazon started with just books)","Be an audience detective: use Facebook ads data, Google Analytics and surveys to see where your buyers live, then focus influencers and ads on those areas","Build detailed customer personas from the profiles of people engaging with your content (age, location, interests, pain points). A personal brand in your niche helps here too","Mistake 2, an over-complicated checkout: too many products or unclear steps cause cart abandonment","Turn on as many payment options as possible in Shopify (PayPal, Apple Pay, etc.), allow guest checkout, and keep forms short","Mistake 3, neglecting mobile: most social traffic shops on phones, so check your site in mobile view, fix oversized text and unclear prices, and test the full checkout on your own phone"],"topics":[],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_csjsbyldmmm1R/lessons/lesn_o4KIbY2g3ACxc/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Raising AOV SAUCE (CALL)","where":"Dropshipping · Scaling","summary":"A recap of how to raise your average order value (AOV), followed by a mindset discussion on protecting your business and choosing who you share it with.","points":["To raise AOV, provide more value: if ad costs are eating your margins, give customers reasons to spend more per order","Bundles: offer extra products or multiples of the same product at a discount, but make sure the discounted price still sits well above your cost","Carry more than one product, since a one-product store limits what a ready-to-spend customer can buy","Shipping protection: an optional $2–$3 add-on at checkout that covers replacing lost packages","Gifts and mystery boxes: price them to fit your audience and your product's price point","Protect your store: keep your best products and store details private, since people (even friends) may copy your products. Trademark your brand when you can","Showing your face is fine early on, and you can switch to UGC creators or AI UGC later","Don't share your revenue with people who aren't genuinely supportive. Surround yourself with like-minded people, like the community in the Discord","Ignore doubters, stay focused on your goals, and find the working hours (like late at night) when you're most locked in"],"topics":["Calls","Mindset","Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_csjsbyldmmm1R/lessons/lesn_Bdx2fuO1y4j89/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Raising budget/ look at metrics on ad","where":"Dropshipping · Scaling","summary":"How to read the key metrics in Facebook Ads Manager and decide which ads to keep on, kill or give more time.","points":["CPM (cost per 1,000 impressions): $20–30 is normal; very cheap isn't always good, it can mean a cheap audience","CPC under $2 is fine, under $1 is solid; CTR 2% is fine, 2–3%+ is strong","CPA (cost per result) is what each sale costs you; compare it to your own break-even, e.g. $30 price − $10 COGS = $20 max","ROAS 2.0+ is good; also watch add to carts, checkouts initiated and frequency","Start testing at $20–50/day; around $10 spend, check for clicks and add to carts, but let a new ad account warm up a few days","Real examples: great CPC and CTR but no add to carts after $14 = killed; bad CTR but sales at a $20 CPA = kept on","If several similar ads flop, the near-copies will likely flop too; make iterations of your winners"],"topics":["Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_csjsbyldmmm1R/lessons/lesn_LH0EWHoklYb8t/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to Ride Trends","where":"Dropshipping · Scaling","summary":"How to spot and ride trends early in your niche, and how to test and launch trend products without overspending.","points":["Successful entrepreneurs spot trends 3–6 months early. Being first gives you an edge, better supplier pricing and higher margins before prices rise","Building on a trend with a twist (e.g. a new beanie style during a beanie trend) can make you an authority in that space","Trend-watch systems: burner TikTok accounts that follow 100+ accounts in your niche, monitoring hashtags, tracking competitor launches, and tools like Exploding Topics or BuzzSumo","Look at seasonal and long-term growth patterns so you can launch before competitors catch on","Join communities in your niche to hear about trends early","Post on every platform, but focus on value. After 100–140 posts, start improving quality","Partner with 10–20 smaller niche creators with strong engagement before celebrities, and send them samples before launch","Test in small batches (50–100 units or pre-orders) and aim to sell out quickly once your marketing foundation is set","Watch your KPIs: sell-out rate, profit margin and return rate (under 10%), and gather customer feedback","Make content shareable: quick unboxings, show the product in use so viewers can picture owning it, offer multiple colors, and film from flattering angles","If you're still on your first product, focus on mastering product research, your store and marketing before using these advanced strategies"],"topics":["Product research"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_csjsbyldmmm1R/lessons/lesn_2Of8Sp9ALhjhD/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Identifying Then Watching Competitors","where":"Dropshipping · Scaling","summary":"Why and how to watch your competitors in e-commerce, using them for motivation and opportunities rather than envy.","points":["Why: know where your pricing stands (don't massively underprice or overprice compared with competitors), and notice when competitors shift products","Keep an abundance mindset: \"if they can do it, I can too.\" Use competitors as motivation, never jealousy","Find gaps you can improve on, like extra colors, better materials or features customers complain about in their reviews","Defensive strategy: check their website regularly (e.g. screen-record it every couple of weeks) to spot new sales, offers or changes, and act fast when something is working","Know your direct competitors (same product, check them 3–4 times a week), indirect competitors (similar marketing or products) and fast-rising new ones","Track prices, SEO keywords, reviews and social conversations (e.g. Reddit). This can be done manually in about an hour a week, or delegated","Five things to monitor: pricing, site experience (e.g. pop-ups collecting email, phone and name), shipping speed and costs, product selection, and how they promote","Example: Target closely tracks Walmart's pricing to stay competitive","Stay legal and ethical: don't copy logos or trademarked designs, impersonate employees or bribe anyone for information. Learn from their strategy and make it your own"],"topics":["Mindset","Product research"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_csjsbyldmmm1R/lessons/lesn_BguhxuMG9hcjz/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Playing Mind Games","where":"Dropshipping · Scaling","summary":"How the wording on your store, down to a single word, affects buying psychology, using a real store's homepage banner as the example.","points":["After Black Friday, the store's banner changed from a fall sale to a seasonal \"winter cozy sale\" that matches its best-selling hoodies","The wording should match the feeling of the product you're featuring. A \"summer sale\" next to hoodies would feel wrong to shoppers","\"Winter\" alone makes people think of puffer coats, so combining it with \"cozy\" fits hoodies better","One wrong word (e.g. naming a style or feature some shoppers don't want) can make people scroll past everything","Sell the feeling behind the product, not just the item, and keep your best sellers front and center with messaging that fits the season"],"topics":["Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_csjsbyldmmm1R/lessons/lesn_VAUn4Gcaa4c3B/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Understanding Your Audience, who is on what?","where":"Dropshipping · Scaling","summary":"Which age groups are on which social platforms, so you know where to push hardest for your product's audience.","points":["TikTok: reaches both very young and older audiences","Snapchat: younger to middle-aged audience","Pinterest: young adults (around 21–23)","Facebook and Messenger: mainly older users (around 40–50+)","Threads and YouTube Shorts: mainly teens and younger users","Instagram: a middle mix of ages","Match your niche to the platforms where its audience is strongest (e.g. fitness leans middle ages, skincare reaches both young and older)","Still post every video on all platforms, since anyone can be anywhere and a video might go viral on one platform but not another. It only takes seconds to repost"],"topics":[],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_csjsbyldmmm1R/lessons/lesn_ual5B68X7JOOv/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Branding your store","where":"Dropshipping · Scaling","summary":"A live marketing masterclass with member Q&A on fulfillment, store setup, brand recognition and how to find and scale winning ad creatives.","points":["Q&A: if a new supplier's version looks slightly different but works the same, email customers to let them know before it ships","Q&A: for a chargeback over a defective item, ask for photo evidence, then contact your supplier (or Alibaba) for a replacement or refund","Q&A: get a professional business email (e.g. support@yourstore.com) through GoDaddy. Routing it into Gmail is fine","Q&A: with AliExpress, send order details to the supplier manually, or automate it with AutoDS","Must-have automations: abandoned cart, welcome email, and a post-purchase thank-you with a coupon for the next order","Sold out? Keep posting and add a pop-up to collect emails and phone numbers so you can text and email everyone when you restock. Reinvest profits into more inventory","Organic marketing builds recognition (like Sprite billboards), which makes people far more likely to choose your brand and raises conversion rates","In ads, focus on the creatives (video/image) and text. The ad setup itself is mostly automated now","A winning ad shows a high click-through rate. Clicks and site visits show interest even before sales follow","Once a creative wins, don't reinvent it: recreate it with a new setting or person, and put more budget behind it","Test 2–3 creatives at about $5/day each for 3–5 days, then scale the one with the most clicks. Raising the budget reaches more people in that niche","Avoid \"first 10 orders get X% off\" messaging, which can make it look like nobody is buying. A time-limited offer creates urgency without that downside","For pre-orders, state the shipping timeline clearly in the title, description and emails"],"topics":["Calls","Paid ads","Suppliers & shipping","Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_csjsbyldmmm1R/lessons/lesn_z7ZQVtXkkYa20/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to fix, sessions but no sales. (CALL.)","where":"Dropshipping · Scaling","summary":"How to diagnose and fix a store that gets lots of sessions but no sales.","points":["If people are visiting, your product and marketing are working. The problem is the gap between landing on your site and checking out, so find where they drop off","Leaving on the landing page: usually a messy or untrustworthy-looking site or no clear way to buy. Add a big \"Add to Cart\"/\"Buy Now\" button right under the product","Adding to cart but not checking out: usually distraction (phone notifications) rather than price. Set up an automated add-to-cart reminder email","Abandoning checkout: usually unexpected shipping costs or hidden fees, or not having a card handy. Keep shipping fair and set up an abandoned-checkout email flow","Shipping protection should be an optional toggle, roughly no more than 10% of the product price","Benchmarks: a 5–6%+ add-to-cart rate is healthy, the average e-commerce conversion rate is around 1%, and 10%+ is exceptional","Organic traffic naturally converts lower than ads, since it reaches a broader, less targeted audience","Lots of sessions with no sales can also mean you're targeting the wrong demographic","Products that need trust (like supplements) may take several visits before someone buys, so collect emails and SMS to follow up","When running ads for a single product, link straight to that product page"],"topics":["Calls"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_csjsbyldmmm1R/lessons/lesn_FVI9j02rZ9L99/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Creating Bundles","where":"Dropshipping · Scaling","summary":"An updated walkthrough of creating product bundles in Shopify.","points":["Install a bundles app from the Shopify App Store","Create bundle → pick the products → name it (e.g. bundle of 2) and save","The bundle becomes its own product you can list","Show it on your home page next to the single products so people compare prices and pick the bundle"],"topics":["Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_csjsbyldmmm1R/lessons/lesn_sj6ylafCxaIzx/","also":["Clothing Brand · Scaling"]},{"kind":"lesson","title":"WELCOME","where":"Welcome","summary":"A welcome to the Inner Circle, what to expect, and your first steps.","points":["This is a mentorship plus a network of hungry people: coaches, DiMaggio and other members","Expect honest guidance, not just what you want to hear (think of a fitness coach)","Ground rules: show up, be on time, come to calls with specific questions, keep info confidential, don't copy members' websites, respect everyone","It's simple, not easy; the goal is to help you skip costly mistakes","Your first steps: 1) watch the full Welcome and at least 5–20 Mindset videos, 2) go through the Skool (beginner group) classroom for the fundamentals, 3) then come into the Inner Circle modules to scale","Write down your top 3 goals","Introduce yourself in the Discord; your personal coach will also reach out"],"topics":["Getting started"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_2CFjPPKn1ezSol/app/courses/cors_70JCTEmB5heRG47ewhUozB/lessons/lesn_Nuvr4dP99D9i0sP7B5Kz2/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"When first starting in here watch this","where":"Welcome","summary":"Why you need a reason bigger than yourself to stay consistent.","points":["Goals that are only for yourself are easy to set but hard to stick to","A reason bigger than you (family, someone you love) gives you urgency and drive","Urgency doesn't mean rushing; it means working every day, spiritually, mentally or physically","With a big enough reason you keep going no matter what the early results look like","E-commerce success is real and possible; don't hold a limited mindset"],"topics":["Getting started","Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_2CFjPPKn1ezSol/app/courses/cors_70JCTEmB5heRG47ewhUozB/lessons/lesn_6U6LNvZkaQah4qQGtuQLTg/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Just in case you didn't watch","where":"Welcome","summary":"The full Inner Circle walkthrough: what it is, how it works and what to do next.","points":["Joining is a big first step; a mentor saves you the time, energy and money of learning alone","The Inner Circle is the advanced group with limited spots; the Skool (beginner group) teaches the basics","You learn lifelong skills (marketing, websites, paid ads) you can use in any business","Where to start: Welcome → Mindset → Skool classroom → Inner Circle modules","Your personal coach reaches out within about 24–48 hours; introduce yourself and write down your goals and vision","Take notes; this isn't Netflix","Use your coach for things not in the modules: product ideas in your niche, marketing strategy, life advice, website reviews","Watch the modules before asking questions; double-text your coach if there's no reply in 48 hours","DiMaggio is usually active 11 AM–5 PM Mountain Time; message him on Whop, and use the Discord for most questions","Be patient: this isn't get-rich-quick. Enjoy the process and don't give up"],"topics":["Getting started"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_2CFjPPKn1ezSol/app/courses/cors_70JCTEmB5heRG47ewhUozB/lessons/lesn_IzLz571xdeov7/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How the coaching works","where":"Welcome","summary":"How coaching works and where to go when you have a question.","points":["Always watch the modules first so you don't waste your own time","In Skool, the Introduction lessons are mandatory; then pick your path (Dropshipping or Clothing Brand), then move into the Inner Circle","Question order: 1) ask in the Discord, 2) bring it to a weekly call (held 3x a week), 3) text your personal coach","Write down your questions during the week and bring them to a call","Coaches usually reply within 24–48 hours; they have lives too, so use all your resources","The most successful members are the most active in the Discord"],"topics":["Getting started"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_2CFjPPKn1ezSol/app/courses/cors_70JCTEmB5heRG47ewhUozB/lessons/lesn_t1ZZTHGWSmnoV/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Get Organized: Your First Steps (Read This)","where":"Welcome","summary":"Read this before you go any further. It tells you exactly what to do and in what order. YOUR PERSONAL COACH WILL REACH OUT SOON","points":["Your personal coach, Ryder, will reach out to you within about 24–48 hours","When he does, reply and introduce yourself: who you are, where you're at, and what you want to build","If you haven't heard back within 48 hours, double-text him. That's not spamming","YOUR PATH, IN ORDER","1. WELCOME (you're here): finish every video in this section first. Watching some of the Mindset videos too will help a lot","2. THE SKOOL: you get access to the Skool community with your mentorship. Go through its classroom modules; they teach the fundamentals. Try to finish this before Ryder reaches out","3. DROPSHIPPING INNER CIRCLE: once you have the basics down, move on to the Dropshipping modules here on Whop","HOW TO STAY ORGANIZED","Keep one notes doc or notebook, with a section for each module","Take notes on every video; don't just watch","Write down your top 3 goals and why you want each one","Keep a running list of questions. Ask in the Discord first, bring anything left to a weekly call, then ask your coach","Mark each lesson complete as you finish it, so you always know where you left off","Download the Key Terms PDF (next lesson) and keep it open while you watch"],"topics":["Getting started"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_2CFjPPKn1ezSol/app/courses/cors_70JCTEmB5heRG47ewhUozB/lessons/lesn_rLbnqoAlnSBYi/"},{"kind":"pdf","title":"Key Terms","where":"Welcome","summary":"Every important word from the modules, defined in plain English.","points":[],"topics":["Getting started"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_2CFjPPKn1ezSol/app/courses/cors_70JCTEmB5heRG47ewhUozB/lessons/lesn_AJ7YkUGNWAbzU/"},{"kind":"pdf","title":"First 30 Days Checklist","where":"Welcome","summary":"Week-by-week checklist for your first month.","points":[],"topics":["Getting started"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_2CFjPPKn1ezSol/app/courses/cors_70JCTEmB5heRG47ewhUozB/lessons/lesn_mwPukgbw30AYQ/"},{"kind":"pdf","title":"Get Organized: Your First Steps","where":"Welcome","summary":"How to set up your notes, calendar and workspace before you start.","points":[],"topics":["Getting started"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_2CFjPPKn1ezSol/app/courses/cors_70JCTEmB5heRG47ewhUozB/lessons/lesn_4gmRz2fi10a0F/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Community Rules #1","where":"Welcome","summary":"The community rules.","points":["Respect everyone: no hate speech or disrespect, and report anyone who breaks this","Everything shared in here stays in here: modules, resources, screenshots, calls and discussions","No spamming; make every message add value, and ask good questions that aren't already in the modules","Don't share, save or download the course materials","Don't copy other members' designs or brand names; inspiration is fine","Watch the modules somewhere private","These rules are part of the terms you agreed to when you joined"],"topics":["Getting started"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_2CFjPPKn1ezSol/app/courses/cors_70JCTEmB5heRG47ewhUozB/lessons/lesn_11cTe6IOLia0BYNFrUN4mU/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"HOW WE PROTECT YOUR INFO","where":"Welcome","summary":"How the Inner Circle keeps the information private so it stays valuable.","points":["Members are screened so the group is made up of serious people","Anyone who joins to leak or sabotage gets removed","Content posted online without permission gets taken down","Don't share your login; the account is for you only, and unusual logins can be reviewed","The info is protected so it doesn't get saturated and keeps working for members"],"topics":["Getting started"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_2CFjPPKn1ezSol/app/courses/cors_70JCTEmB5heRG47ewhUozB/lessons/lesn_H8RTn4PZY0igJ/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"COACHES","where":"Welcome","summary":"Meet the coaches and how to work with them.","points":["Beginners are usually paired with Ryder, who's great at the step-by-step basics","More advanced members are usually paired with Josh; most people keep the same coach as they grow","Coaches are vetted for both results and character","Expect replies within 24–48 hours; if not, double-text (that's not spamming)","Keep questions short and simple","Watch the modules before asking; coaches can't re-teach a whiteboard module over text","Introduce yourself to your coach"],"topics":["Getting started"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_2CFjPPKn1ezSol/app/courses/cors_70JCTEmB5heRG47ewhUozB/lessons/lesn_nI4UIZpXZ73Tq/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"(MANDATORY WATCH)","where":"Welcome","summary":"The mindset to have when you get a mentor (mandatory watch).","points":["A mentor guides you around the mistakes you can't see yet; DiMaggio lost about $37K to mistakes when he started","Judge it by what you save, not just what you gain","It's an investment in lifelong skills (websites, marketing, sales) you can use in any business","Part of the value is being around hungry people; network in the Discord","Be coachable: watch every module, take notes and never assume you already know it all"],"topics":["Getting started","Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_2CFjPPKn1ezSol/app/courses/cors_70JCTEmB5heRG47ewhUozB/lessons/lesn_8f0k21b4SCsiM/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Fixing Your Mindset About This","where":"Welcome","summary":"Seeing the mentorship as an investment, not an expense.","points":["Many people spend freely on entertainment but hesitate to invest in themselves","Your most valuable asset is your time","Value comes from the mistakes you avoid, e.g. a bad supplier costing you thousands","While you watch, write down the mistakes you would have made without this video","You also gain connections and new modules as they're added"],"topics":["Getting started","Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_2CFjPPKn1ezSol/app/courses/cors_70JCTEmB5heRG47ewhUozB/lessons/lesn_Gu2Cg5OZfvMww/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"have this mindset when getting a mentor","where":"Welcome","summary":"Why having a mentor is worth it, and the mindset to bring into the program.","points":["A mentor saves you time, energy and money","Your time is your most valuable asset","Figuring everything out alone leads to costly mistakes","Think about what you save, not just what you gain","Reach out to DiMaggio or your coach whenever you need help"],"topics":["Getting started","Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_2CFjPPKn1ezSol/app/courses/cors_70JCTEmB5heRG47ewhUozB/lessons/lesn_Il7eGwdkZZX0V/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How it works!","where":"Welcome","summary":"How the program is structured, level by level.","points":["Level 1: the Skool (beginner group) teaches the foundation; you can make money here too","Level 2: the Inner Circle's advanced modules help you scale","Level 3: MEO, which covers ringchaining (connecting stores), selling stores and the agency model","Pick your path (Dropshipping or Clothing Brand), then go to MEO","The same SOPs are used throughout, so your store is set up for MEO later","Take notes, watch the modules before asking questions, and send in module requests","Perfect each step and don't rush; detach from results and do the daily work"],"topics":["Getting started"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_2CFjPPKn1ezSol/app/courses/cors_70JCTEmB5heRG47ewhUozB/lessons/lesn_A5ZXpI9xJX1oC/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to search modules","where":"Welcome","summary":"How to use Search The Modules to find the exact lesson you need.","points":["Every module's notes are summarized in one searchable tool","Search a problem, like paid ads, abandoned carts, winning products or \"I want to quit\", and it lists every lesson that helps","Press Open lesson to jump straight to that module","Use it whenever you're stuck instead of digging through every course"],"topics":["Getting started"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_2CFjPPKn1ezSol/app/courses/cors_70JCTEmB5heRG47ewhUozB/lessons/lesn_BIkavUuybhKef/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to join discord!!","where":"Welcome","summary":"What the Discord is for and how to join it.","points":["Use it for networking, asking questions, introducing yourself and mindset advice","Get store reviews from peers, share winning products, hop on calls and motivate each other","Post every win, even small ones: a finished website, your first sessions, your first sale","Don't give misleading advice; say when something is just your opinion","Keep profanity low, speak positively, and no racism or sexism","Keep your enrollment details private","Don't be shy; your next business partner could be in there","To join: go to the Home tab on Whop and open the Discord"],"topics":["Getting started"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_2CFjPPKn1ezSol/app/courses/cors_70JCTEmB5heRG47ewhUozB/lessons/lesn_b6EWU2mn74baX/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How coaching works pt.2","where":"Welcome","summary":"Tips for getting the most out of coaching.","points":["Watch the modules first, then ask high-quality follow-up questions","Question order: 1) the Discord (coaches and members all see it), 2) extra-questions calls, 3) your personal coach","Don't depend on one person; use all your resources and re-ask if a question gets missed","Respect coaches' time: they're real people with businesses and lives","Expect replies within 24–48 hours (usually faster)","Take notes and stay active"],"topics":["Getting started"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_2CFjPPKn1ezSol/app/courses/cors_70JCTEmB5heRG47ewhUozB/lessons/lesn_IEq7M1mhs2gpB/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Mindset (IT STARTS NOW!)","where":"Mindset","summary":"Why mindset comes first, before you build anything.","points":["Most success is spiritual and mental; fix your mindset before chasing results","Don't go in asking \"what if this doesn't work?\" Ask \"how can I make this work?\"","Find a purpose bigger than yourself; then you need discipline, not motivation","E-commerce is a low-cost opportunity: sell a $100 product to 10,000 people and that's $1M","Treat it like a real business, not a side experiment with minimal effort","Don't do it only for the money; learn to love discipline, knowledge and the process","Don't quit too early; your breakthrough could be a week away","If others are doing it, you can too; think with abundance"],"topics":["Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_EPD0j2av9itWEs/app/courses/cors_7xXaUTzQbs3dIBgU2TiKNW/lessons/lesn_72f1136QQw3mxOxVLoBtTu/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Mentorship Mindset","where":"Mindset","summary":"The right mindset for getting the most out of your mentorship.","points":["The game plan works; the real question is whether you'll do the work","No excuses: members with less experience than you have made it work","The most successful members watch the modules, ask a few high-quality questions, take action and stay consistent","Don't expect coaches to do it for you, and don't blame others when you didn't put in the work","Expect people around you to doubt you; don't let it kill your motivation","Don't be the fish that's led to the water but won't swim; you have 50 hours of modules","You're learning high-income skills you can use for life"],"topics":["Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_EPD0j2av9itWEs/app/courses/cors_7xXaUTzQbs3dIBgU2TiKNW/lessons/lesn_Mp2HsjrBESgGK/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Don't tell ppl you're doing this!","where":"Mindset","summary":"Why you should keep your goals and this business to yourself when starting out.","points":["Most people won't understand your vision, even people close to you","Telling everyone invites doubt and negativity that can kill your motivation","Everything is a risk, including a job; don't let other people's fears limit you","You don't have to lie, just keep it quiet and keep working","If someone keeps bringing negativity, set a boundary; respect them, but distance yourself if it continues","Listen to real advice, not doubt"],"topics":["Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_EPD0j2av9itWEs/app/courses/cors_7xXaUTzQbs3dIBgU2TiKNW/lessons/lesn_crKwpB5ZymIdu/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Mindset when getting Mentor. (WATCH THIS)","where":"Mindset","summary":"The mindset to have when you get a mentor (mandatory watch).","points":["A mentor guides you around the mistakes you can't see yet; DiMaggio lost about $37K to mistakes when he started","Judge it by what you save, not just what you gain","It's an investment in lifelong skills (websites, marketing, sales) you can use in any business","Part of the value is being around hungry people; network in the Discord","Be coachable: watch every module, take notes and never assume you already know it all"],"topics":["Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_EPD0j2av9itWEs/app/courses/cors_7xXaUTzQbs3dIBgU2TiKNW/lessons/lesn_UxmfMdmfX1Q9A/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"The Power Of Faith","where":"Mindset","summary":"Why faith and big belief matter in business.","points":["You have to believe your goals are possible before you see any proof","If someone else has done it, it isn't delusional; it's possible for you too","Don't wait for your first sale to believe it can work","Picture the life you want in detail; the gap between you and it is your daily actions","When things feel lost, keep faith that you're building a foundation","Don't let people talk you into the \"safe route\""],"topics":["Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_EPD0j2av9itWEs/app/courses/cors_7xXaUTzQbs3dIBgU2TiKNW/lessons/lesn_4qMPpfnPRoJai1iJ0bgHOj/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Don't quit","where":"Mindset","summary":"Why consistency beats quitting early.","points":["Most people want results but quit after a few days, at the gym and in business","Giving a business a couple of months and then quitting is the wrong mindset; doctors and lawyers train for years before earning","Some people succeed fast, but if it takes longer you may be close to a bigger breakthrough","Quitting right before the breakthrough is the worst mistake","Sometimes success looks like sitting at your computer for 12 hours and not giving up","E-commerce is growing; don't look back and say you quit"],"topics":["Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_EPD0j2av9itWEs/app/courses/cors_7xXaUTzQbs3dIBgU2TiKNW/lessons/lesn_Za5XOtcFX9KpV/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"ECOM SAUCE + LIFE ADVICE","where":"Mindset","summary":"Raising your average order value (AOV), plus life advice on protecting your business and dealing with doubters.","points":["Raise AOV by adding value: bundles, \"add another for less\" offers and more products on your site","A one-product store limits big spenders and makes ads cost more per sale","Offer optional shipping protection (a small fee that guarantees delivery)","Mystery boxes and gifts can work; price them to fit your audience","Keep your best stores private: people (even friends) can copy your products and site","Showing your face is fine early on; you can switch to UGC or AI UGC creators later","Only share your numbers with people who need to know","Distance yourself from people who belittle you with \"how's that little business going?\" or pocket-watch you; stay humble but be direct","Surround yourself with like-minded people; that's what the Discord is for","Find your focus time; late nights or early mornings have fewer distractions"],"topics":["Mindset","Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_EPD0j2av9itWEs/app/courses/cors_7xXaUTzQbs3dIBgU2TiKNW/lessons/lesn_nKVgbUgPjMufK/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"don't let doubt hurt your dream","where":"Mindset","summary":"How to handle people who doubt your dream.","points":["People often doubt you because they doubted themselves","Ask yourself: has this person achieved what you want? If not, why take their advice?","Stay respectful, but don't let doubt change your direction","Parents often doubt out of love and a different generation's view; don't hold it against them","Friends who hate on you are different; distance yourself from that negativity","Don't expect average people to understand your goals; telling them often kills your drive"],"topics":["Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_EPD0j2av9itWEs/app/courses/cors_7xXaUTzQbs3dIBgU2TiKNW/lessons/lesn_D7jrMRkCogDWi/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"They may hate && doubt, don't stop!!","where":"Mindset","summary":"Not caring what other people think about your goals.","points":["When you share a goal, people often doubt you, usually from their own insecurity","No one else can see the vision you've been given","Countless businesses never happened because someone listened to \"do you really think that'll work?\"","Only take advice from people who know the subject and are where you want to be","You don't have to argue; say \"okay\" and go make it happen","If a vision won't leave your head, keep chasing it"],"topics":["Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_EPD0j2av9itWEs/app/courses/cors_7xXaUTzQbs3dIBgU2TiKNW/lessons/lesn_5dPlguEti8eAEghVee18FE/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"GOALS AND HISTORY","where":"Mindset","summary":"How to write goals that sharpen your vision.","points":["Mindset comes before any marketing strategy","Write down who you want to be in 5–6 years, not just a number by a certain age","Be specific: your dream car and its specs, who you want to help and how","Write the life you want, not the life you see others living","Next to each goal, write why you want it","Be bold with your goals, pray about them and put in the work"],"topics":["Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_EPD0j2av9itWEs/app/courses/cors_7xXaUTzQbs3dIBgU2TiKNW/lessons/lesn_3hFwz6inSi7pzDuim3C5MZ/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"ABUNDANCE!","where":"Mindset","summary":"Thinking with abundance instead of scarcity.","points":["There are billions of people; you only need a tiny fraction of them to buy to build a big business","Even big brands have reached only a small percentage of the world, so there's room for you","Watch your words: say \"how can I make more?\", not \"I don't know if I can\"","Treat costs like samples as investments that push you to earn more","Abundance doesn't mean being careless with money"],"topics":["Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_EPD0j2av9itWEs/app/courses/cors_7xXaUTzQbs3dIBgU2TiKNW/lessons/lesn_vOr3gM78MOKj5/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"KEEP FAITH!!","where":"Mindset","summary":"Keeping faith during the early grind when results are slow.","points":["The start often feels like the same work every day with no big results; that's where most people quit","Keep going regardless of results, like early Amazon","Consistency itself is rewarding, and success means more when there was a process behind it","Failure is only when you give up; like learning to skate, you fall and get back up","Don't give it a couple of weeks and call it a scam"],"topics":["Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_EPD0j2av9itWEs/app/courses/cors_7xXaUTzQbs3dIBgU2TiKNW/lessons/lesn_lr7kZ35jMTepQ/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Don't Focus on Results","where":"Mindset","summary":"Focusing on your inputs instead of your results.","points":["Basing your effort on daily sales will drain you and kill your discipline","Early on, detach from the money; focus on the work you're putting in","Don't stare at your dashboard for sales; check it to see what you can improve (sessions, content)","Building takes time: website, product, marketing","If results are slow, be patient and keep going; that's where most people give up","Galatians 6:9: you'll reap a harvest at the proper time if you don't give up"],"topics":["Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_EPD0j2av9itWEs/app/courses/cors_7xXaUTzQbs3dIBgU2TiKNW/lessons/lesn_KByjSIj7vGp1p/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Testimony from student/ the mindset it took","where":"Mindset","summary":"A successful student shares the mindset that got him results.","points":["He didn't give up, even when results didn't come at first","He watched videos more than once, took notes and reviewed them","He was coachable: he set his ego aside and followed his coach's directions","He focused on learning skills, not quick money","He posted many organic videos (75+) before his first sale and stayed consistent for months","The mentorship saved him time and money, including steering him away from a bad supplier"],"topics":["Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_EPD0j2av9itWEs/app/courses/cors_7xXaUTzQbs3dIBgU2TiKNW/lessons/lesn_GI0WuHSQ6kkVL/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Mindset of month!","where":"Mindset","summary":"A member spotlight: an 18-year-old student who started from scratch.","points":["He'd never built a store before joining, and was nervous it might be a scam","Weekly calls and real support built his confidence","A tip from a call fixed his \"lots of sessions, no sales\" problem and got his first sale the next day","Organic marketing only: videos reaching 500K+ views and products selling out batch after batch","He ignored doubt from people close to him and let results speak","The skills transfer: he used them to help a family member's business","Advice: do your research, but know there's real one-on-one help here"],"topics":["Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_EPD0j2av9itWEs/app/courses/cors_7xXaUTzQbs3dIBgU2TiKNW/lessons/lesn_xUuntAp4fLp3i/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Mindset to achieve","where":"Mindset","summary":"Four things the most successful members do.","points":["1) Have a big enough reason. Motivation goes up and down; discipline comes from a real reason (God, family, future generations, giving back), not just material things","Turn seeing others' success into motivation, not envy","Avoid \"dopamine talking,\" where you talk about goals without doing the work","2) Get up and work every day, no matter how you feel","3) Really lock in on the modules: take notes and understand them","4) Apply what you learn; learning without action is scamming yourself","Ask questions; that's what your coach and the Discord are for"],"topics":["Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_EPD0j2av9itWEs/app/courses/cors_7xXaUTzQbs3dIBgU2TiKNW/lessons/lesn_2zrUHwylreiZ1WWp8f8XqE/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Creating BIGGER reason","where":"Mindset","summary":"Why you need a reason bigger than yourself to stay consistent.","points":["Goals that are only for yourself are easy to set but hard to stick to","A reason bigger than you (family, someone you love) gives you urgency and drive","Urgency doesn't mean rushing; it means working every day, spiritually, mentally or physically","With a big enough reason you keep going no matter what the early results look like","E-commerce success is real and possible; don't hold a limited mindset"],"topics":["Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_EPD0j2av9itWEs/app/courses/cors_7xXaUTzQbs3dIBgU2TiKNW/lessons/lesn_18zWYmB7ay2J1qlkEQDlXv/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"do u really want it?","where":"Mindset","summary":"Do you really want it bad enough?","points":["Nobody will want your success more than you do","People close to you may not want it for you, often out of love: they like you how you are or want you to play it safe","Every business is hard; trials are normal, so don't run back to the safe route at the first one","Chasing fast money gets fast results that don't last; build real skills instead","Hopping between businesses keeps you stuck; pick one thing and go all in","The blueprint only works if you do the work every day"],"topics":["Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_EPD0j2av9itWEs/app/courses/cors_7xXaUTzQbs3dIBgU2TiKNW/lessons/lesn_yAxzPneDWG544/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How hungry are you?","where":"Mindset","summary":"How hungry you are, and rewiring how you think.","points":["Rich people aren't different from you; they fixed their mindset, locked in and had a purpose bigger than money","There's gold in consistency; nobody knows every step when they start","DiMaggio's early brand: months of marketing, rough early drops, and not quitting when it got hard","See the mentorship as an investment in yourself, then actually use it: watch, take notes, act","Nobody can teach hunger; write down why you joined and remind yourself every day","Reject the \"what if this doesn't work?\" thought; it shows up at every level"],"topics":["Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_EPD0j2av9itWEs/app/courses/cors_7xXaUTzQbs3dIBgU2TiKNW/lessons/lesn_6sfKNiXau7h1b/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"LOCK IN!! !","where":"Mindset","summary":"Two habits that make people successful: believing before you receive, and always learning.","points":["Mark 11:24: whatever you ask for in prayer, believe you've received it","Act like the person who already has what you're working toward","Always be willing to learn; ego (\"I already know this\") stops growth","See losses as lessons: reflect on what you could do better first, then adjust","Even experienced entrepreneurs keep learning from others, including newer members","Ask questions and request modules anytime"],"topics":["Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_EPD0j2av9itWEs/app/courses/cors_7xXaUTzQbs3dIBgU2TiKNW/lessons/lesn_60tTBaY1yFcql/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Having ur back against wall","where":"Mindset","summary":"Why having your back against the wall can be a blessing.","points":["Many people settle when life is hard; your drive to keep going makes you different","Be grateful that you want more","It's normal to feel lost while others seem to be winning","Keep fighting and keep your faith, even when you don't know what's next","Many successful people started with their back against the wall","Remember why you joined and keep pushing"],"topics":["Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_EPD0j2av9itWEs/app/courses/cors_7xXaUTzQbs3dIBgU2TiKNW/lessons/lesn_gaNNBSccwKT0o/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"It's a business.-","where":"Mindset","summary":"Treating this like a real business, not a get-rich-quick scheme.","points":["As a business owner you'll face trials; a mentor reduces mistakes but doesn't remove them","The 1% do what the other 99% won't","The model works; the question is whether you'll work it","Give your business time, the way people give college four years","Be patient and don't be hasty; some people win in months, others take longer","The 1% aren't different from you; they keep going"],"topics":["Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_EPD0j2av9itWEs/app/courses/cors_7xXaUTzQbs3dIBgU2TiKNW/lessons/lesn_b4Io7RiWUdk3H/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Things I notice about successful people","where":"Mindset","summary":"Two habits that make people successful: believing before you receive, and always learning.","points":["Mark 11:24: whatever you ask for in prayer, believe you've received it","Act like the person who already has what you're working toward","Always be willing to learn; ego (\"I already know this\") stops growth","See losses as lessons: reflect on what you could do better first, then adjust","Even experienced entrepreneurs keep learning from others, including newer members","Ask questions and request modules anytime"],"topics":["Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_EPD0j2av9itWEs/app/courses/cors_7xXaUTzQbs3dIBgU2TiKNW/lessons/lesn_3U7rw0eL7Hbr3/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Delusion @ it's finest!!","where":"Mindset","summary":"The kind of unshakable belief successful people have.","points":["Successful people believe their plans will work even when they're down","They don't let other people's opinions stop them, within moral limits","Believe your future self already has it, even if a thousand people say otherwise","Don't let results or inner doubt shake that belief","This is the faith many people had before they became successful"],"topics":["Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_EPD0j2av9itWEs/app/courses/cors_7xXaUTzQbs3dIBgU2TiKNW/lessons/lesn_gAetPj2MUUoS4/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Don't ever stop!!","where":"Mindset","summary":"The only way to fail is to give up.","points":["Success in business means nothing without a strong mindset","Failing isn't trying something that doesn't work yet; it's stopping","People who say \"I tried it, it doesn't work\" usually just quit","If others in the same or worse position made it, it can work for you","Talk back to the version of you that wants to quit after a few weeks","Stay consistent so you're still there when the big opportunity comes"],"topics":["Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_EPD0j2av9itWEs/app/courses/cors_7xXaUTzQbs3dIBgU2TiKNW/lessons/lesn_Xq1LnQ1QMgIE4/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"POSITIVE!","where":"Mindset","summary":"Keeping a positive, grateful mindset.","points":["You can't expect a positive life while thinking and speaking negatively","Half full or half empty? Notice which you see","Be grateful even in tough times (your Wi-Fi, tech and opportunities)","Positive doesn't mean ignoring problems; ask \"how can we fix it?\"","Come to God with gratitude, even when praying about hard things"],"topics":["Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_EPD0j2av9itWEs/app/courses/cors_7xXaUTzQbs3dIBgU2TiKNW/lessons/lesn_OCyJoTk6p6WTG/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to deal with doubt","where":"Mindset","summary":"How to handle people who doubt your dream.","points":["People often doubt you because they doubted themselves","Ask yourself: has this person achieved what you want? If not, why take their advice?","Stay respectful, but don't let doubt change your direction","Parents often doubt out of love and a different generation's view; don't hold it against them","Friends who hate on you are different; distance yourself from that negativity","Don't expect average people to understand your goals; telling them often kills your drive"],"topics":["Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_EPD0j2av9itWEs/app/courses/cors_7xXaUTzQbs3dIBgU2TiKNW/lessons/lesn_xCx2SYeHQGeIw/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Stop telling people goals!!","where":"Mindset","summary":"Why you should stop telling people your goals.","points":["Not everyone wants to see you win, even people who seem close","Talking about goals gives you the reward before you've done the work (\"dopamine talking\")","Talking without action looks bad; let your results speak","Affirmations don't need an audience","People may copy you, get envious or try to talk you out of it","Tell advice apart from negativity; parents often push the safe route out of love","Prove it to yourself first, then share it once it's working"],"topics":["Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_EPD0j2av9itWEs/app/courses/cors_7xXaUTzQbs3dIBgU2TiKNW/lessons/lesn_Q4vmzUJrkRXiE/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Controlling Emotions!","where":"Mindset","summary":"Not letting your emotions control your actions.","points":["Business and life will test your emotions: frustration, sadness, anger","If someone can push you to act out of character, they have control over you","Feeling emotions is fine; acting on them badly (quitting, lashing out) has consequences you live with","Turn emotion into fuel: when you're mad or sad, work harder, work out, pray or read","Money is a byproduct of being a good person and doing the work"],"topics":["Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_EPD0j2av9itWEs/app/courses/cors_7xXaUTzQbs3dIBgU2TiKNW/lessons/lesn_hDKv7ZoPONwvH/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Make God your foundation","where":"Mindset","summary":"Making God the foundation of your business and life.","points":["1 Corinthians 3:7: it's God who makes things grow, not the one who plants or waters","Early success can make you think you did it alone; stay humble","Luke 1:37: with God nothing is impossible","We're made to create (businesses, art, music); have faith that it will work out while you put in the work","Material success without God feels empty","Revenue will go up and down; faith and persistence carry you through","Pray, read the Bible and make God your \"CEO\""],"topics":["Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_EPD0j2av9itWEs/app/courses/cors_7xXaUTzQbs3dIBgU2TiKNW/lessons/lesn_zwCpih2T2jutM/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"2 Bible verses that can really help with your journey","where":"Mindset","summary":"Two Bible verses that help on the entrepreneur journey.","points":["Mark 11:24: when you pray, believe you've already received it","Live like the person who already has it; become that person first","Luke 1:37: with God nothing is impossible","When people call your goals unrealistic, remember that verse","Read the Bible and pray daily; it can change your life"],"topics":["Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_EPD0j2av9itWEs/app/courses/cors_7xXaUTzQbs3dIBgU2TiKNW/lessons/lesn_hSWKWHSerDVrv/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"BIBLE VERSES THAT HELP WITH BUSINESS","where":"Mindset","summary":"Romans 15:13 (NIV) May the God of hope fill you with all joy and peace as you trust in him, so that you may overflow with hope by the power of the Holy Spirit.","points":["2 Peter 3:9 (NIV)","The Lord is not slow in keeping his promise, as some understand slowness. Instead he is patient with you, not wanting anyone to perish, but everyone to come to repentance.","Deuteronomy 31:6-8 (NIV)","Be strong and courageous. Do not be afraid or terrified because of them, for the Lord your God goes with you; he will never leave you nor forsake you.” Then Moses summoned Joshua and said to him in the presence of all Israel, “Be strong and courageous, for you must go with this people into the land that the Lord swore to their ancestors to give them, and you must divide it among them as their inheritance. The Lord himself goes before you and will be with you; he will never leave you nor forsake you. Do not be afraid; do not be discouraged.”","Joshua 1:8-9 (NIV)","Keep this Book of the Law always on your lips; meditate on it day and night, so that you may be careful to do everything written in it. Then you will be prosperous and successful. Have I not commanded you? Be strong and courageous. Do not be afraid; do not be discouraged, for the LORD your God will be with you wherever you go."],"topics":["Mindset","Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_EPD0j2av9itWEs/app/courses/cors_7xXaUTzQbs3dIBgU2TiKNW/lessons/lesn_QNWR58HzyHYV9/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Be hungry for God!!","where":"Mindset","summary":"Staying hungry for God when life gets busy.","points":["Discipline and devotion matter more than motivation","Set aside time every day to pray and thank God, even when you're busy","Real peace comes from your relationship with God, not money","Write down what's holding you back from being close to God, and cut it off","God's guidance comes from love, like good parents setting rules"],"topics":["Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_EPD0j2av9itWEs/app/courses/cors_7xXaUTzQbs3dIBgU2TiKNW/lessons/lesn_hdZvFOX9s6YbD/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Be for God!!","where":"Mindset","summary":"Actually living for God, not just saying it.","points":["Faith isn't a Sunday-only thing; make time for God every day","Hunger for God the way you hunger for food","Don't live lukewarm, praying in the morning and then living against it all day","Watch who you spend time with and what you do","Choose a side: prioritize God over fun","A life close to God is better than any material success"],"topics":["Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_EPD0j2av9itWEs/app/courses/cors_7xXaUTzQbs3dIBgU2TiKNW/lessons/lesn_JoGctkwLtPQDB/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"trust the process with God!!","where":"Mindset","summary":"Trusting the process, with God, in business.","points":["Business takes faith; there will be trials (held payments, shipping problems, mistakes)","Really trusting the process means you don't complain when things go wrong","This isn't get-rich-quick; you're building skills you can pass down","Don't business-hop; every business has a process, so stick with one","The hard times become the story and the testimony; the journey is what makes the destination meaningful","Use your resources: the Discord and your coaches"],"topics":["Mindset","Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_EPD0j2av9itWEs/app/courses/cors_7xXaUTzQbs3dIBgU2TiKNW/lessons/lesn_dE0nbyUNKSuHV/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Act in faith. -- mark 11:24. (NIV)","where":"Mindset","summary":"Acting in faith (Mark 11:24).","points":["Mark 11:24: whatever you ask for in prayer, believe you've received it, and it will be yours","After praying, act like you already have it: no stressing, overthinking or fear","Acting from scarcity makes it harder to receive what you're praying for","There's power in believing before you see it; even a little faith counts"],"topics":["Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_EPD0j2av9itWEs/app/courses/cors_7xXaUTzQbs3dIBgU2TiKNW/lessons/lesn_PgjmkZdRm8pCS/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"CLOTHING BRAND (LISTEN)","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"A quick intro to the Clothing Brand section and who it's for.","points":["This section is for people who want to move from dropshipping to holding their own inventory","Even if you're still dropshipping, the branding lessons here will make your brand stronger","The goal: help you grow your clothing brand into a more established business"],"topics":["Clothing brand"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_Dik6XzkQctxNV/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Take action","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"How to get the most out of the advanced modules by taking action and/or notes as you go through each category.","points":["Two ways to work through the modules: take action while you watch, or take notes first and act afterwards. Either is fine as long as you act","If you act as you go: at the end of each category (e.g. Website, Product), go back through every module and check your store has everything it covers","If you take notes first: make sure you have notes on every module in the category before moving on","Taking notes is recommended either way, since it keeps you organized","Don't skip through videos. The modules are built to save you time, money and energy by helping you avoid mistakes, and one tip could scale your store"],"topics":["Clothing brand"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_DHzp0eqHedPdF/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to make a sale with banner","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"How to set up a simple Black Friday (or any holiday) sale theme on your Shopify store using an announcement banner and updated sale prices.","points":["The store doesn't need a fancy redesign. It just needs to make clear there's a sale","In the theme editor, add an announcement bar at the top with a message like \"Black Friday Sale\" and when it ends","Change the homepage heading (e.g. \"Shop Black Friday\") so the sale is the first thing people see","Put your products on sale with a compare-at price, but make sure you still make a profit on every item","The announcement bar can also show social proof, like a real recent customer's repeat purchase","The same approach works for any holiday sale","Marketing matters more than the website: promote the sale with organic videos and ads"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_3dWVOX50AjXR4kmJm366PG/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Website Sales Funnel","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"A step-by-step breakdown of the customer journey on your website, from first visit to checkout, and what you need at each step.","points":["Customers find you through Google, Instagram, TikTok, Facebook, Pinterest, Snapchat, YouTube, etc., then land on your homepage","Have a pop-up that collects contact info (email or phone, not both, plus maybe first name). Most people don't buy on their first visit, so this lets you reach them later","Real urgency (genuinely limited stock or a sale with a real end date) can encourage people to act sooner. Keep it honest","Show 3–4 products (no more than ~5) so visitors can find the right one without too many choices. Start small and add products gradually","Add to cart, then view cart. This is the moment to upsell a matching item (e.g. a beanie with a hoodie), ideally with a small discount","At checkout, customers either buy (shipping info, payment, order confirmation) or leave","Common reasons people leave: shipping costs, deciding they don't need it, comparing other options, or simply forgetting. That's why collecting contact info and following up matters","Future modules cover upsells, post-purchase steps and the \"no conversion\" follow-up funnel"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_7KHzk9qbbMCJCR4O26YzQy/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to find Font for logo","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"How to find the right font for your logo and brand, browsing a free font site (like DaFont) by category.","points":["Match the font to your niche and vibe: professional/fitness brands need clean fonts, streetwear can use bolder, distressed or vintage styles","Browse categories (Western, distorted, groovy, comic, old-school, etc.) and download fonts that fit. Check each font's license before using it commercially","Some styles (like blurred or ripped effects) look great but can be hard to print well, so test them with your supplier","Make sure the logo is easy to read. A hard-to-read font won't stick in people's heads","Dropshipping: you don't need a crazy logo (even AI-made is fine). Use it on your profile picture, website and emails to give the store a brand feel so it doesn't look like a generic dropshipping store","Clothing brand: put more work into a unique logo and put it everywhere, including on the products"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_1s63AL3XKwUPXYeyN4XeBV/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Watching Competitors","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"Why and how to watch your competitors in e-commerce, using them for motivation and opportunities rather than envy.","points":["Why: know where your pricing stands (don't massively underprice or overprice compared with competitors), and notice when competitors shift products","Keep an abundance mindset: \"if they can do it, I can too.\" Use competitors as motivation, never jealousy","Find gaps you can improve on, like extra colors, better materials or features customers complain about in their reviews","Defensive strategy: check their website regularly (e.g. screen-record it every couple of weeks) to spot new sales, offers or changes, and act fast when something is working","Know your direct competitors (same product, check them 3–4 times a week), indirect competitors (similar marketing or products) and fast-rising new ones","Track prices, SEO keywords, reviews and social conversations (e.g. Reddit). This can be done manually in about an hour a week, or delegated","Five things to monitor: pricing, site experience (e.g. pop-ups collecting email, phone and name), shipping speed and costs, product selection, and how they promote","Example: Target closely tracks Walmart's pricing to stay competitive","Stay legal and ethical: don't copy logos or trademarked designs, impersonate employees or bribe anyone for information. Learn from their strategy and make it your own"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Mindset","Product research"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_7dP9qO1YakoQ6y6veWzOZc/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Abandoned cart","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"What abandoned carts are and how to set up an automated abandoned-cart email that brings customers back.","points":["An abandoned cart (a \"soft order\") is when someone adds a product to their cart, wants to buy, then leaves","It doesn't always mean they don't want it. Often it's shipping cost or price, and most of the time they simply forget","Set up an automated abandoned-cart flow with Shopify Flow, Klaviyo or Postscript, so emails go out on their own","Write casually, not stiff and corporate (e.g. \"We see you left your cart hanging\"). Overly formal emails can feel scammy","Include an offer, like a coupon code to pick their order back up","Add a reasonable time limit so they act. Around 10–15 minutes works, while 30 seconds is unrealistic. Make sure the code really expires when you say it does","Formula: friendly text + the offer + a reason to act now. Done consistently, this raises your conversion rate"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Email & SMS"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_voaZV90Dc3KdI/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Reviews","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"Using a Shopify review app (Judge.me) to collect and show real customer reviews.","points":["Lets customers rate products after they get them, and is mostly set up so reviews come from people who actually bought","Good reviews from satisfied customers build trust and can raise your click-through rate","Show reviews with eye-catching widgets and testimonial sliders on your store","You can send coupons or referral rewards to encourage customers to leave a review and buy again","Import reviews and sync them to Google and Meta so they show up when people search for you","Reviews double as feedback (e.g. requests for a new color), which can guide new products"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_d86hy5iFNV1Gf/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Website & Trust!","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"A live masterclass on two website essentials, collecting customer contacts (CRM) and building trust, plus why long-term skills matter more than quick sales.","points":["A sign-up offer (e.g. 10% off) can turn around 30% of visitors into contacts, while a plain newsletter box might get only ~2.5%. Giving up a small discount pays off over time","Example: emailing 300 contacts instead of 25 about a $100 product makes a huge difference in revenue","A store with a strong contact list can sell for noticeably more, so think about collecting contacts in your ads and everywhere else","Trust is essential. Without it, visitors leave even if they like the product","Show genuine customer reviews and testimonials. Never invent fake reviewers. Apps like Judge.me let buyers review after they receive the product","Keep testimonials clean and organized. Let people see the product first, then the reviews below it","If a site makes you wonder \"is this legit?\", it's missing trust signals. Make sure yours doesn't","Focus on building real skills (contact collection, sales psychology, trust) rather than chasing a sale tomorrow. These skills work in any business, and a well-built store can sell for several times its yearly earnings","Q&A: you can bring contacts from one store to a new one by exporting and importing them, as long as customers agreed to receive promotional emails"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Website","Email & SMS"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_XR98CsMJTaGDd/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Website Maxxing!","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"The non-negotiables every store's landing page needs to build trust and convert, plus Q&A on reviews, AI in ads, suppliers and testing creatives.","points":["Testimonials/social proof: show that other people use the product and it works. You don't need hundreds; 3 or so on the homepage is enough to start.","About Us page: give your brand a deeper meaning. People buy the persona, not just the product (Nike sells feeling fit). Explain what problem you solve and why you exist.","FAQ: answer the questions people would ask (what it does, shipping time, where it ships from). Five or six good ones save time and speed up purchases.","Contact page: shows you're a real business, not a quick cash grab.","What the product solves: address objections head-on (e.g. a grip product that prevents blisters).","Real urgency when you're running a sale (e.g. free shipping for the next 24–48 hours).","Keep your refund/return policy in the footer.","Don't obsess over colors and wording. Once the essentials are in place, marketing is what drives sales, and a website is useless without traffic.","Reviews when you're new: collect genuine reviews from people who've actually used the product. Never make reviews up.","AI in ads: keep paid ads mostly real content (roughly 25% AI max). Never use celebrities' or athletes' likeness with AI without permission, as you can get sued.","Strategy: use organic content to validate, then put money behind what works in paid ads. You don't need to buy followers.","Testing creatives: launch several around the same time, track analytics, then raise the budget on winners and replace losers.","Suppliers: order samples from your actual supplier so you see what customers get. For dropshipping, Zendrop or CJ usually beat Alibaba.","Business setup (LLC vs. sole proprietorship) varies by state, so talk to a tax advisor."],"topics":["Clothing brand","Website","Paid ads","AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_oFtQjxls0FYtb/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How I do my organic marketing!","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"A walkthrough of DiMaggio's own brand page, showing real organic marketing examples and the ideas behind them.","points":["Organic content built the brand's audience (100K followers). The goal is reaching your target audience and building contacts.","Once you have contacts, you can email them weekly or monthly and keep selling to the same customers.","Simple content works: videos filmed at home got tens of thousands of views. You don't need a studio.","Content ideas that performed: wearing or showing the product in everyday settings, product shots with a nice backdrop (like a sunset), outfit/styling videos, AI-edited product clips, seasonal angles (back to school, Christmas), and \"we're dropping this for $X\" hooks.","A big creator or streamer wearing your product can drive huge attention.","Engagement tip: comments and replies boost reach, so engage with your audience.","One free viral video (600K views) can be worth a lot in orders and new contacts.","As you grow, combine organic posting with Facebook ads and email marketing.","The point is constant reminders: when people see your product repeatedly, they eventually buy."],"topics":["Clothing brand","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_1cJTX2hzqfDmFiyRIyD8lZ/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"More in depth in Organic Marketing","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"A deeper framework for organic marketing: how to make content people engage with, how to analyze results, and how to turn viewers into customers.","points":["Use the 3 W's in every video: What can people comment on? What will they get from the product? Why should they buy (from you)?","Spark comments: ask questions (\"would you wear this?\"), run comment giveaways, or do something unexpected with the product that gets people talking.","Show the benefit, not just the product: what problem does it solve or what experience does it give (e.g. a water toy that cures summer boredom)? Show it rather than plastering text.","If a competitor sells the same product, use a different marketing angle to reach a different audience.","Analyze every post with the \"3 major keys.\" If it got engagement, look at what people are talking about and make more content around that.","If it didn't get engagement, check analytics (watch-through rate), list 5 reasons it may have flopped, and honestly ask whether you'd engage with it. Then revise.","Reply to comments, positive or negative. Negative comments still push your content out. Answer objections and point people to your store (e.g. with a code).","Make your page and landing page obvious so interested viewers can buy easily.","Whether they buy or not, collect their info. Follow up non-buyers with a discount through email/SMS automations (Klaviyo, Postscript)."],"topics":["Clothing brand","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_3qVaegJj3TXrXkfX9GoXmk/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"OM Funnel","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"","points":[],"topics":["Clothing brand","Website","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_6hbUuSwf8Gm1sA236OpGs4/","pending":true},{"kind":"lesson","title":"step by step OM!","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"A live demo of how quick and simple organic marketing videos can be, using a hoodie and a diffuser as examples (the \"starter organic method\").","points":["It doesn't need to be perfect. Each example video took about 3 minutes to make.","On TikTok, tap + and pick a trending/recommended sound, then film the product simply (e.g. lay a hoodie on the floor and pan over details like a custom chain).","Even simple videos need the 3 W's. The caption does the heavy lifting early on, e.g. \"Have you ever seen a hoodie with a custom chain?\" or \"POV: this was your night light growing up.\"","Give people something to comment on. Doing something unexpected with the product drives comments (a friend got huge views letting commenters suggest what to put in a diffuser).","Film in your phone's camera app instead of inside TikTok so you can reuse the same clip across platforms with different captions.","ChatGPT can help generate captions.","15 minutes a day can produce ~5 videos, which means 5 chances to go viral. Consistency matters more than effort per video.","The goal isn't only sales: it's getting your product familiar in potential customers' heads."],"topics":["Clothing brand","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_3Tpv6GPH0qfqeHjVwsmwwM/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to get marketing ideas","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"How to do market research on TikTok to see how others market products in your niche and get ideas for your own content.","points":["Search \"TikTok made me buy it\" or \"buy now\" + your niche (e.g. \"buy now fitness\") to find products and see how they're being marketed.","Study what grabs attention: strong hooks, POV-style videos, quick demos (e.g. testing a product underwater), and replying to comments with videos.","Note which videos get lots of comments, even negative ones like \"that's useless.\" Comments mean reach.","What not to do: screen-recording your website as \"organic\" content performs poorly.","Be skeptical of self-proclaimed \"Shopify experts\" in comment sections.","Engage with these videos (like, comment, watch) so the algorithm fills your For You page with similar stores, giving you a constant stream of trends and marketing ideas.","You'll notice sellers copying each other's winning formats (like the same demo style). That's market research in action."],"topics":["Clothing brand","Product research","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_2pyhLTXzphnEsu11ufi9Fz/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Scaling with FB ad (using MEO / own method)","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"How to scale with Facebook/Meta ads the MEO way: validate first, then test with data and scale only proven winners.","points":["Meta (Facebook/Instagram) is one of the biggest ad platforms. Learn how it works before spending.","Non-negotiables before scaling: a validated product and audience, solid organic marketing (~100–120 posts, just 2–3 a day), some testimonials, and a product price that leaves room for ad costs (ideally $35+).","Rule 1, data first, budget later: start with 3–5 ads, test one interest per ad set (e.g. gym-goers), and begin low at ~$5–$10/day.","Key metrics: CPM (cost per 1,000 impressions, lower is better), CTR (aim for 2%+; low CTR means the wrong audience or a weak creative, so hook people in the first 2 seconds), and add-to-cart vs. checkout (many add-to-carts but few purchases points to pricing, shipping or checkout issues; use discount codes and follow-up emails).","Rule 2, scale winners, not hopes: if one product gets clearly more organic traction, put ad money behind that one. Don't reinvent what's working.","Targets: cost per purchase low relative to your price (roughly $5–$15) and ROAS around 1.3–3x to start.","Scaling: duplicate winning ad sets (ABO), use campaign budget (CBO) or Advantage+ shopping, and raise budgets only 20–30% every 24 hours. Never double overnight.","Refresh creatives every 3–5 days to avoid ad fatigue, and monitor results closely in the first 72 hours.","Mindset: the first hurdle is the hardest. Stay disciplined with organic before ads, and grow in every area of life, not just money."],"topics":["Clothing brand","Paid ads","Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_6scnCHeXDCVpF0LuWpElVv/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to STALK competitors","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"This video is for drop shipping, but you can do the same process just to see how people are promoting hteior products, so you can implement that in your brands marketing. Do this with other brands though, not drop- shipp","points":["This video is for drop shipping, but you can do the same process just to see how people are promoting hteior products, so you can implement that in your brands marketing.","Do this with other brands though, not drop- shipping products.","What this video covers: The \"burner account\" method for watching competitors and filling your feed with product-marketing ideas.","Make a new TikTok account and only interact with videos that promote products: follow, like and comment on them.","Within a short time, your For You page fills with other sellers' product videos, even from accounts you never followed.","Study how they promote: hooks, trends they're riding, who they're targeting (e.g. a mini laundry machine aimed at college students).","One viral video can put a store on the map and bring in contacts and sales for a long time.","Viral product videos are often simple and quick (5–15 minutes to make) and don't need to be professional.","Use these insights to make videos in a similar style for your own product."],"topics":["Clothing brand","Product research","Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_79gbgGlxUUSAvQuEisqCII/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"It's not as hard as u think to OM","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"Why organic marketing is easier than people think, shown through real stores that grew by reposting simple videos.","points":["One store posts the same short clip over and over, just changing the caption or intro. It's filmed once on a phone in about 3 minutes, no heavy editing","Post consistently, every day, even when early videos get little traction. Their videos only started blowing up after lots of posts","Treat each video like a lottery ticket: the more you post, the better your chances of a hit","Don't push \"buy now!\" hard in organic content. It can feel sketchy and hurt your brand. Just show the product","A video that blows up can become a Facebook ad and keep making sales long after","Example: a comb seller with one viral video and lots of consistent posting","Get a sample, film yourself or someone using it, and reuse that footage for months","The real value of a viral video is the contacts you collect (email/phone), which you can market to for years. That's a key difference with MEO vs. regular e-commerce"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_5U9u4g4zyI8cWCuowFgPe2/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Mistakes People Make In Marketing","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"The biggest mistake in organic marketing, which is not doing enough of it, and why marketing deserves most of your effort.","points":["No marketing means zero sales. Even Jeff Bezos wouldn't make sales from a store nobody knows about.","Marketing should get the majority (~80%) of your attention in e-commerce, not a token 1% effort.","Posting once a week and expecting big results won't work. Consistency is key.","Big brands (e.g. Balenciaga) market everywhere, all year: Instagram, TikTok, Pinterest and Snapchat posts, influencers, paid ads on multiple platforms, plus email and SMS.","If the biggest brands do all of this constantly, a new store can't expect success from minimal effort.","Bottom line: if nothing is being pushed out to people, they can't buy your product."],"topics":["Clothing brand","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_307fosxyVJzwms1V14fPCT/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Creating SOPs for Your Store","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"How to create SOPs (standard operating procedures) for your store so tasks can be handed off and scaled, using the first several SOPs from one of the mentor's stores as an example. (Despite the title, this video is about SOPs, not the organic marketing algorithm.)","points":["Keep an SOP index/tracker: each SOP gets an ID, name, department (product, operations, tech, fulfillment, support, marketing, finance), version number, status (active, deactivated, draft) and review cycle","Suggested review cycles: product monthly, suppliers and store setup quarterly (e.g. before holidays), paid ads weekly, support and fulfillment monthly, more often as you grow","Each SOP should explain why it exists, what success looks like (consistent results and smart spending, not just money) and which tools are needed","Set access levels for each team member and keep logins secure","Anyone using your SOPs should sign an agreement not to share them or take over your products and accounts. Get proper legal advice on the contract","Example marketing SOP steps: order a sample, set up all platforms, study competitors for inspiration, post consistently, and expect results only after ~150 posts","A burner TikTok/YouTube account that follows competitors helps you watch them daily","MEO can be outsourced fast. Even at large scale you only need a small team (around 4 people), with most of them on marketing","You don't need SOPs on day one, but set them up early. You'll be glad you did when the store gets big"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_rvE8G4BahvM3K/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"POST EVERYWHERE!","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"The key to organic marketing: don't be a perfectionist, and post everywhere to reach as many people as possible.","points":["Don't overthink each post. Get content out as much as possible.","Post on every platform: TikTok, Instagram, YouTube, Facebook, Snapchat, Pinterest and more.","You don't need different videos for each platform. Make one video and post it everywhere, and use Instagram/Facebook cross-posting.","A worse-looking store can outsell yours simply by reaching more people. Reach is the game.","More eyes means more sales: 1 million viewers beats 10,000.","With organic, reaching a broad audience costs nothing. Narrow niche targeting matters most when you're paying for ads."],"topics":["Clothing brand","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_0hNTzCmZwtgf2/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Viral Organic Marketing Examples (dropshipping, still good sauce!)","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"Real examples of viral organic marketing for dropshipping products, and what made them work.","points":["Example: a dashboard de-icing gadget marketed to people in cold climates. The videos showed it in use on a frosty morning, which made the niche instantly relatable.","Show what the product does, not just the product. Demonstrations get far more engagement than simply holding it up.","Target your niche directly (e.g. people dealing with cold weather) and use relatable everyday scenarios.","Hooks that spark debate drive comments and reach, but avoid anything that disrespects or alienates part of your audience.","Timely hooks like \"best product of [current year]\" and putting the store link in the comments (not just \"link in bio\") can help.","Volume matters: the creator's first big viral video came after roughly 50–100+ posts. Keep posting.","Once traffic comes in, it's all about getting more people to the site, like adding more customers to a restaurant that's already built."],"topics":["Clothing brand","Organic marketing","Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_5WFTwDbGe71Tu/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"ALL TYPES OF MARKETING!","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"A rundown of the main types of marketing and ads you can use for your store, and which ones are worth your time.","points":["Organic content: make your own videos, with or without showing your face (hands-only on a tripod, or have a friend film), and later hire creators on Fiverr","Showing your face early helps build a brand and trust; you can step back from the camera as the store grows","Use or wear your product in public so it works like a walking billboard","Influencers: DM micro-influencers (some will post for free product), negotiate low and be willing to walk away","Bigger influencers on a contract (e.g. keep videos up for 6 months) can pay off long term through the customer contacts you collect","Paid platforms: Pinterest (niche/lifestyle), Snapchat (younger audience), Google Ads (better for big brands, focus on SEO instead), Meta ads (Facebook, Instagram, Threads/Messenger) mainly for sales, and TikTok ads for repeated exposure across platforms","Main recommendation: start with organic videos plus influencers, and use Facebook and TikTok for paid ads"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_bhaofZVDS50vG/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"OM (how to make it easier pt.1)","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"How to make organic marketing easier and save time by scheduling your posts ahead of time.","points":["Work smart and hard: scheduling keeps you consistent without having to stop and post manually every day","Platforms to schedule on: TikTok Studio, Instagram and YouTube","TikTok: download the TikTok Studio app, go to Post, then Schedule Post (it works from the app, not the computer)","You can schedule up to about 7 days out. For example, sit down on Friday, make 7–8 posts and schedule them across the next few days","Use the time you save to grow your brand and find better products","Instagram and YouTube may need a short wait before scheduling unlocks, while TikTok Studio lets you schedule right away","If scheduled posts aren't getting consistent views, switch back to posting manually"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_d5826IAx7nv6e/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"WHAT EACH TYPE OF MARKETING IS FOR!","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"The four goals of marketing and why each one matters as much as sales.","points":["Sales: people visiting your site and buying.","Traffic: getting people onto your website.","Contacts: getting visitors to leave their email or phone number.","Awareness: people becoming familiar with your brand.","They all depend on each other. You can't get sales without traffic and awareness, or contacts without traffic.","The biggest mistake is going into e-commerce focused only on sales. Treat it like a real business and work on all four.","Act with gratitude for progress (e.g. 100 sessions is a win). Visitors today may come back and buy later, especially if you collected their contact info."],"topics":["Clothing brand"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_F297kmj6B4RwN/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"different types of marketing","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"A breakdown of what each type of marketing is best for, rated on awareness, contacts, website traffic and potential sales.","points":["Organic marketing: highest for awareness. Contacts, traffic and sales are medium and depend on consistency, going viral and having a link in your bio. Use it to build awareness and fund your ads, not mainly for sales","Seeing your product organically gives people a break from obvious ads and makes them more likely to buy later","UGC creators: top-tier awareness because their audience already trusts them. Traffic is lower, but conversion is very high. Think of it as organic marketing on steroids","Email/SMS: very high traffic and potential sales, since these people already know your brand and are being reminded to buy","Paid ads: not mainly for awareness, but highest for traffic and direct sales","Summary: organic = awareness, UGC = half awareness and half sales, email/SMS and paid ads = sales","Awareness is the most underrated one. It builds longevity and makes your store worth more if you ever sell it"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_DDH2iKrsoC6eY/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Running FB ad","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"A step-by-step walkthrough of setting up a Facebook/Meta sales ad campaign in Ads Manager.","points":["In Ads Manager, click Create and choose the Sales objective. Traffic, awareness and engagement are mainly for hyping a drop before launch.","Choose manual setup (or Advantage+), and set the conversion event to Purchase.","Connect your Meta Pixel through the Facebook & Instagram app in Shopify.","Audience size: avoid too narrow or too broad. Use what organic marketing taught you about your audience.","Locations: commonly the US, UK, Canada and Australia for English stores. Target specific regions only for seasonal reasons (e.g. summer clothes in the opposite hemisphere).","Budget: start small (~$5/day) until you know your analytics.","Schedule: start at 12 a.m. the next day (gives Meta time to process) and set an end date 7–10 days out so you don't overspend.","Only scale budget once an ad is clearly outperforming. Skip Meta's auto-generated ads.","Connect your brand's Instagram and Facebook pages.","Creative: a single image or video works best (Canva can help with design). Primary text, headline and description should be short and clear (e.g. restock news, limited stock, a first-orders discount).","Use a Shop Now call to action, add your promo code, and use a clean display link (your domain)."],"topics":["Clothing brand","Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_3siAAoYZvkGgim399hxhhE/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"FB ads pt.2","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"An in-depth walkthrough of Facebook (Meta) Ads Manager, from reading your dashboard to setting up and publishing a campaign.","points":["Opportunity score: Meta's points rating for your ad setup. Under 60 is bad, and about 73–75 is the sweet spot","Campaign dashboard: turn campaigns on and off, and name each one after the product and budget so tests stay organized","Avoid duplicating winning ads unless you have a big budget, because Meta will push you to spend more. Create new ones instead","A/B testing runs two creatives against each other for a set time and keeps the winner","Key columns: results, cost per result (CPR), amount spent, impressions and reach. Keep attribution on a 7-day click","Campaign goals: awareness (before launch), traffic, engagement, leads (collect contacts) and sales (once the product is live)","Budgets: daily or lifetime. New ad accounts should start small (about $5/day per test) so Meta trusts the account","Ad sets act like folders inside a campaign (e.g. an image version and a video version of the same product)","Targeting: narrow by location, age and interests, using what your organic marketing showed you about who your audience really is","Placements: choose where the ad shows and exclude platforms that don't fit your audience","Creative: single image, video or carousel. Add your website URL, and use instant forms if you're collecting leads","After you publish, the ad may sit in draft/pending while Meta reviews it, which is normal"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_dSRvCtq6raiQu/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Which Ages Are on Which Platforms","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"Which age groups are on which social platforms, so you know where to push hardest for your product's audience.","points":["TikTok: reaches both very young and older audiences","Snapchat: younger to middle-aged audience","Pinterest: young adults (around 21–23)","Facebook and Messenger: mainly older users (around 40–50+)","Threads and YouTube Shorts: mainly teens and younger users","Instagram: a middle mix of ages","Match your niche to the platforms where its audience is strongest (e.g. fitness leans middle ages, skincare reaches both young and older)","Still post every video on all platforms, since anyone can be anywhere and a video might go viral on one platform but not another. It only takes seconds to repost"],"topics":["Clothing brand"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_t0i5Xqt4RVvgo/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to set up email/sms (KLAVIYO)","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"A walkthrough of setting up Klaviyo for email and SMS marketing and sending your first campaign.","points":["Sign up, answer the setup questions (your business, where your store is hosted) and add your business address","Set the sender email your customers will see","Choose your channels: email and SMS (WhatsApp and push notifications are also available)","Start on the free plan until you're sure you like it, and connect your Shopify store","The home dashboard shows how your emails and SMS are performing, and the built-in guides help with things like sign-up forms","To create a campaign: choose email, select or import your audience list, then write the subject line and preview text","The subject line is what catches their eye, so make it strong. The preview text shows underneath it","Pick a template, edit it, and send it to your list"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Paid ads","Email & SMS"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_44kvrIvdXmRPG/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Ai help u with OM!","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"How to use AI (Claude or ChatGPT) to come up with organic marketing video ideas when you're running out of them.","points":["Start with a clear prompt: tell the AI you do organic marketing for your brand, which platforms you post on (YouTube Shorts, Snapchat Spotlight, etc.) and that you need ideas","Explain what your brand is, what it does and who your target audience is (e.g. streetwear for ages 16–25)","If the first ideas aren't good, ask it to make them more engaging","Describe or share examples of videos that work (yours or competitors'), such as B-roll of the product with engaging captions like \"which fit would you wear?\"","If the AI drifts off and starts writing scripts, bring it back to your original prompt","Ask it to turn your best ideas into 25 similar content ideas, and paste in your top 5–10 performing videos for reference","Be patient while it works, then pick the ideas that fit your brand (e.g. \"I wore this to...\" or \"rate my fit\" style videos)"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Organic marketing","AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_T8uLO7aK3gQYD/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to have ai help you with products","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"How to use AI to create UGC-style organic marketing videos for your product.","points":["Keep filming your own content too, for extra videos and to save money, but AI UGC can add a lot of volume","Search TikTok for UGC creator videos in your niche (e.g. \"UGC creator protein powder\") to find examples you like","Download the video, or watch it and describe it to Claude in detail, scene by scene with timestamps (what's shown, the setting, the hands, how the product is used)","Ask Claude to generate a script for your own product based on that description that you can paste into an AI video tool (Claude is recommended over ChatGPT for scripts)","In the AI UGC software, choose an avatar (avoid overused ones), upload a picture of your product and generate the video","You can also pick existing templates, click recreate with your product, and chop up different creatives into new videos"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Organic marketing","AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_u1srn9aPg6Vqc/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"(scroll down)","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"HOODIES, SHIRTS, PANTS Text this number on whatsapp > + 923365649000","points":["This supplier is Pakistani. Turnover time is great great communication and trusted.","Here are some other TRUSTED supplier for all kinds of different clothes","+923086527522","+86 159 4838 4472","+8619173941572","TEXT THEM ON WHATS APP!"],"topics":["Clothing brand"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_4a1hqhFTbs0FpcXgufT1bb/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Verify Alibaba suppliers","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"How to verify that Alibaba suppliers are legit before you order, for both branded products and dropshipping.","points":["Alibaba is mostly B2B: wholesalers ship stock to your business. Some suppliers will also dropship straight to customers if you ask","On a product page, scroll down to the supplier card and check the store rating (around 4.5+ is good), the reorder rate (about 20–30% is strong) and the response time","A response time of several hours is normal, since suppliers are across the world. Be patient with them","Store reviews aren't product reviews. A product with few or no reviews can still be fine, since there are far fewer business buyers than consumers","Also check how long they've been in business and how many orders they've shipped (e.g. to North America)","Use \"Send inquiry\" to ask about customizing the product, such as your logo, color, formula or packaging","Check the MOQ (minimum order quantity) and negotiate it, and the price, down. Many suppliers will work with you","Alibaba's secured payments, easy returns and money-back protection help if a supplier doesn't deliver","Start with a sample and test the market before placing a bulk order"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_yaxRArlo0c5SR/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"WINNING PRODUCT","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"More pointers on what makes a winning product, including the psychology behind why people buy.","points":["It's something people need, not just want. Market it as solving a real problem (e.g. a diffuser for congestion, or one that helps clear skin)","It makes someone's life better, like electric toothbrushes or hiring a maid. Highlight that on your site (FAQ, testimonials)","Even a winning product won't sell if you don't market how it improves people's lives","Small upgrades create loyalty. Example: adding a satin lining to hoodie hoods (protects hair) built a loyal fan base that keeps coming back and tells friends","It's hard to find in stores. If people can buy it at Walmart for a few dollars, it won't sell at a markup","It doesn't need to be totally unique, just a version people can't easily get (e.g. a teddy bear that records and plays back a loved one's voice)"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Product research"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_08bRdayATBppH/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Don't make this mistake!!","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"Three e-commerce deal breakers that kill sales, and how to fix each one.","points":["Mistake 1, ignoring your audience: organic marketing is for finding your audience. Focus on one niche so customers feel connected to your brand, like Nike or Jordan","Broad targeting leads to low click-through and conversion rates, while competitors win by targeting one audience well (even Amazon started with just books)","Be an audience detective: use Facebook ads data, Google Analytics and surveys to see where your buyers live, then focus influencers and ads on those areas","Build detailed customer personas from the profiles of people engaging with your content (age, location, interests, pain points). A personal brand in your niche helps here too","Mistake 2, an over-complicated checkout: too many products or unclear steps cause cart abandonment","Turn on as many payment options as possible in Shopify (PayPal, Apple Pay, etc.), allow guest checkout, and keep forms short","Mistake 3, neglecting mobile: most social traffic shops on phones, so check your site in mobile view, fix oversized text and unclear prices, and test the full checkout on your own phone"],"topics":["Clothing brand"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_RwVmc4XTpWDNR/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Customizing product","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"A quick clothing brand tip on customizing pieces with your Alibaba supplier.","points":["You're not limited to products exactly as listed. Find a base item (e.g. pants) from your supplier","Besides adding your logo, send the supplier an inquiry asking to customize it, like changing colors or details on certain parts","Customizing makes your pieces unique to your brand instead of the same item everyone else sells"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_IuTcRqLvBgXLc/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Creating a PB","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"How to build a personal brand that creates trust and helps scale your dropshipping store.","points":["Trust beats reach: 1,000 people who trust you are worth more than a million who don't. Shoppers are skeptical of faceless stores with generic supplier videos","Put a real person behind the store: your face, your hands or your voice, plus genuinely engaging with comments","Content that converts: problem/solution stories, behind-the-scenes (testing, packing, quality checks), honest before/afters and free educational tips that feature your product","Short-form video (TikTok, Reels, Shorts) gives huge free reach. Spend 30 minutes a day making 3–5 videos of 15–60 seconds. Longer YouTube videos can tell your story and build deeper trust","Be relatable and raw: minimal editing, a conversational tone, and talk to viewers like friends","Transparency is your secret weapon: unboxings, honest reviews that mention flaws or shipping times, and showing how you resolve customer issues","Trust path: discovery, then visiting your profile, connecting, following, purchasing and coming back","30-day launch: week 1, find your niche and film 20 pieces of content; week 2, post daily across platforms; week 3, refine what works; week 4, scale with UGC, email and micro-influencers","Set up a personal account that promotes your store (face photo, clear bio, call to action linking to the store), respond to every comment, and post even if it feels uncomfortable at first"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Website","Organic marketing","Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_4vMdpyRsaSyLcEAqoJdRtS/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"leveraging personal brand (clothing brand & dropshipping)","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"How to mix a personal brand with your store using a two-account strategy to build trust.","points":["Most shoppers need to trust a brand before they buy. A human face (yours, a friend's, or even just your hands and voice) separates you from faceless stores","Two-account strategy: a personal account that humanizes the business, plus a professional brand account (logo, product showcases, promotions)","Personal account setup: a clear face photo, a 2–3 sentence authentic bio about what you help people with, and a link to your store","Content that connects: life stories, your workspace and packing, business wins and losses (e.g. switching to a better supplier), and everyday moments","Platform tips: Instagram for lifestyle posts with some business, TikTok for unpolished authentic content, X for quick thoughts that can go viral, LinkedIn for business connections","Subtle selling: mention your store naturally (\"just added this product I can't stop using\"), with about 90% authentic content and 10% business","Timeline: month 1, just share your story and post consistently; months 2–3, start weaving in your business; after that, promote more openly","Action steps: set up the account, post 4–5 times daily, and genuinely engage with 10+ people in your niche every day"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Website","Organic marketing","Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_69EftmLyEefPxpEONYseZD/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"MISTAKES!","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"An intro to the \"mistakes\" section of the course and how to get the most out of it.","points":["The upcoming videos focus on common mistakes, some the mentor made himself and some he sees members make often","A big part of mentorship is learning what not to do, saving you time, money and energy","Avoiding these mistakes helps your store succeed faster","Watch all the videos and take notes, adding a \"don't do this\" note under the matching section (website, marketing, etc.)"],"topics":["Clothing brand"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_4DlivwbTzWQaJ/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"5 top mistakes","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"The 5 biggest mistakes people make when starting (or running) their e-commerce store.","points":["Mistake 1, not sleeping on your ideas: take action, but before you finalize a logo, name or product (ordering samples, launching marketing), wait overnight and re-check it with fresh eyes","Mistake 2, asking \"will it work?\" The real question is \"will you work?\" With 8 billion people in the world, even a tiny fraction is a huge potential audience","Mistake 3, a limited mindset: no brand has sold to everyone, and new online shoppers appear every day. Don't be scared off because a big brand sells something similar. Out-market them instead","Mistake 4, focusing too much on how your store looks: when starting, you just need a decent store, a pop-up to collect contact info and a good domain","Mistake 5, limiting your marketing: post on every platform you can and do anything that puts your product in front of more people"],"topics":["Clothing brand"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_EilIger9ByFqr/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to solve- Tech issues","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"What to do when technical issues or other problems come up while running your store.","points":["The mentor's personal first step is to pray, then work out what kind of problem it is","Decide whether it's a technical issue (e.g. Shopify showing an error when adding payments or an app) or a personal/account issue (e.g. you got logged out or forgot your password)","Technical issue: contact the company involved, such as Shopify, your payment processor, or your supplier app (Zendrop, AutoDS)","Personal or setup issue: ask your personal coach or post in the Discord, since someone has likely dealt with it before","Fastest fix: do both. Contact the company's support and post in the Discord at the same time","The team will help research unusual issues, but they're business mentors, not tech support, so some problems will still need the platform's support team"],"topics":["Clothing brand"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_qIj33IVfA43K6/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Using email flows to get customers","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"","points":[],"topics":["Clothing brand","Email & SMS"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_6fWaUoQ4JgZuiSm5mhhr6C/","pending":true},{"kind":"lesson","title":"CLV (Customer Lifetime Value)","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"Mastering customer lifetime value (CLV) and retention so your store earns sustainable income instead of constantly chasing new customers.","points":["The common mistake: spending heavily on ads for one-time buyers who never return, like filling a leaky bucket","Keeping a customer is 5–25x cheaper than getting a new one, and repeat customers drive most revenue","CLV = average order value × purchase frequency × customer lifespan (e.g. $50 × 3/year × 2 years = $300)","Knowing your CLV lets you confidently spend more to acquire a customer, even breaking even on the first sale","Strategy 1, upsells and cross-sells: volume discounts, bundles, premium versions, and post-purchase one-click add-ons (apps like CartHook, Zipify OCU, or Shopify's built-in feature)","Strategy 2, subscriptions: find a consumable product in your niche (treats, refills, supplies) and offer subscriber perks with easy skip or pause options (Recharge, Bold)","Strategy 3, email/SMS: welcome series, abandoned cart, post-purchase and win-back flows, and review requests 7–10 days after delivery. Keep texts short, time-sensitive and easy to opt out of","Strategy 4, community: a private Facebook group, Discord or Instagram broadcast list with tips, Q&As, early access and UGC contests","Tools: Klaviyo/Omnisend (email), Postscript/Attentive (SMS), Smile.io/LoyaltyLion/Yotpo (loyalty), and GA4 or Triple Whale (analytics)","90-day roadmap: month 1, analytics, email flows and bundles; month 2, upsells, loyalty and SMS tests; month 3, subscriptions, community and a value ladder","Focus on one retention strategy at a time, and test and measure everything"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Email & SMS"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_4jLZ3s2AhajgcNMTYj1elg/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to Ride Trends Early","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"How to spot and ride trends early in your niche, and how to test and launch trend products without overspending.","points":["Successful entrepreneurs spot trends 3–6 months early. Being first gives you an edge, better supplier pricing and higher margins before prices rise","Building on a trend with a twist (e.g. a new beanie style during a beanie trend) can make you an authority in that space","Trend-watch systems: burner TikTok accounts that follow 100+ accounts in your niche, monitoring hashtags, tracking competitor launches, and tools like Exploding Topics or BuzzSumo","Look at seasonal and long-term growth patterns so you can launch before competitors catch on","Join communities in your niche to hear about trends early","Post on every platform, but focus on value. After 100–140 posts, start improving quality","Partner with 10–20 smaller niche creators with strong engagement before celebrities, and send them samples before launch","Test in small batches (50–100 units or pre-orders) and aim to sell out quickly once your marketing foundation is set","Watch your KPIs: sell-out rate, profit margin and return rate (under 10%), and gather customer feedback","Make content shareable: quick unboxings, show the product in use so viewers can picture owning it, offer multiple colors, and film from flattering angles","If you're still on your first product, focus on mastering product research, your store and marketing before using these advanced strategies"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Product research"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_50CCOyEgWH65dtx20zXuDY/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Don't Put Too Much Time And Energy Into This!!","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"The things people waste too much energy on when starting or running an e-commerce store.","points":["Over-designing your website: customers come for the product, not your color scheme. Keep the site clean and functional and move on","Over-perfecting organic content: spending 15+ minutes on a 5-second video leads to burnout. Focus on pushing out consistent, valuable content instead of perfect videos","Over-perfecting your product: make sure the design and quality are good, but don't obsess over tiny details. Your energy is better spent elsewhere","Listing too many products: you only need 1–3 products. Stores that sell everything (dog beds, treadmills, etc.) burn out before they ever get to marketing","Upselling is worth your energy, especially when you're first starting out"],"topics":["Clothing brand"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_2jpDoTknOwT9YEQpTyACxy/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Embedding Base44 in Shopify","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"How to embed something you built on Base44 into your Shopify store.","points":["In Shopify, create a new page (or section) where you want the Base44 build to show up","Ask Claude or ChatGPT to create an iframe embed code for you","Copy the link to your Base44 project and put it into the iframe code","Paste the iframe code into Shopify, save, and click preview to check that it shows up","If it glitches, delete the code and paste it in again","If you get a \"not available\" error, go to Base44's dashboard, then Security settings, and turn off the setting that blocks embedding, then reload","Base44 can't be added as a Shopify app (they're likely competitors), so embedding is the workaround. Wix makes embedding easier"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Website","AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_t31fh11rtRoVM/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Daily!","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"The daily tasks you should do to run your store, which take about an hour and 20 minutes a day.","points":["Push out marketing and check on it (~35 min): post organic videos, DM creators, run Facebook ads, then see which creators replied, who your organic content is reaching, and which ads perform best","Stay updated on products and trends (~30 min): scroll a burner TikTok account whose feed is full of other dropshippers and brands, and look for trending topics you can tie into your store (e.g. looksmaxxing, or a hat with a fan when everyone's talking about the heat)","Check your email and watch competitors (Facebook Ad Library, etc.). If they jump on a new product or marketing trend, you should too","Customer service: answer customer emails quickly, such as \"where's my order?\" (AI can help draft replies)","Check Shopify (~5–10 min) to make sure orders are shipping","Top two priorities: pushing out marketing and staying on top of trends and products, since these drive sales and scaling","At minimum, do the free stuff daily (organic content and DMing creators). Getting a sample to film your own videos helps a lot"],"topics":["Clothing brand"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_cNHpN3lasCV4P/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"using AI to help you reach out to creators","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"How to use AI (ChatGPT or Claude) to write outreach messages to creators so they'll promote your product.","points":["Prompt the AI to write a text, DM or email asking a creator to promote your product, and tell it what you're offering","Offer ladder: start with free product for a video, then money plus product, then commission plus product. Big creators may want payment up front","Ask the AI to make it eye-catching and natural, keep it short, and revise it until it sounds right","Explain what your brand stands for and why it fits the creator's audience","Use \"we\"/\"our\" if you have a team, which can make the outreach feel more established","Don't name an exact price or commission rate in the first message. Say you're open to paying up front or offering commission, then negotiate once they reply","Mention the long-term opportunity if their content performs well, and end with \"reply back if interested\"","An intriguing opener (e.g. \"Did you hear about this?\") can get more creators to open the DM"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Organic marketing","AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_0OJhA4sxZVRzj/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Setting zones/rates","where":"Clothing Brand","summary":"A quick example of how to set up simple shipping rates for your store.","points":["Instead of complex calculated shipping, you can set up simple shipping zones and flat rates","Example setup: free shipping on orders of $150+, economy (1–5 lbs) at $12, economy (5–70 lbs) at $21, and international economy at $43","Around $43 is a reasonable average for international shipping given current costs"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_2TCEE6vN1sFfaa4OgiNUTM/lessons/lesn_ag2OVf8BxVHBv/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"If you want to start a CB, Listen to This","where":"Clothing Brand · Logistics","summary":"A quick intro to the Clothing Brand section and who it's for.","points":["This section is for people who want to move from dropshipping to holding their own inventory","Even if you're still dropshipping, the branding lessons here will make your brand stronger","The goal: help you grow your clothing brand into a more established business"],"topics":["Clothing brand"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_F4Kfw9VUiiTlb/lessons/lesn_GFSDIOVgKjfo5/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Take Action While You Learn","where":"Clothing Brand · Logistics","summary":"How to get the most out of the advanced modules: either take action as you go or take notes, and check off every section.","points":["Two ways to learn: take action while you watch, or take notes and act after; both work as long as you act","If you're doing as you go, go back at the end of each category (website, product…) and check you applied every module","If you're taking notes, make sure you have notes on every module in that category","Don't skip through videos; the mistakes covered here can save you a lot of time and money"],"topics":["Clothing brand"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_F4Kfw9VUiiTlb/lessons/lesn_RcwdKBC34hxMe/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Normal Mistakes","where":"Clothing Brand · Logistics","summary":"Intro to the mistakes section: the next videos cover common mistakes that hold people back.","points":["A big part of a mentorship is learning what not to do, to save time, money and energy","Some of these are mistakes the coach made, others he sees all the time","Add them to your notes under the right section (website, marketing…) as \"don't do this\""],"topics":["Clothing brand"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_F4Kfw9VUiiTlb/lessons/lesn_yRtSe4Mv3XhNE/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"The Top 5 Mistakes I see","where":"Clothing Brand · Logistics","summary":"The top 5 mistakes new store and brand owners make.","points":["Not sleeping on ideas: before finalizing a logo, name, design or product (ordering samples, starting marketing), wait a night and recheck it","Asking \"will it work?\" instead of \"will you work?\": with billions of people online, you just need to get in front of enough of them","A limited mindset: other brands selling the same thing doesn't cap you; new online shoppers appear every day","Over-focusing on how the store looks: you only need the right systems (a good store, a pop-up to collect contacts and a real domain)","Limiting your marketing: post on every platform you can and put the product in front of as many people as possible"],"topics":["Clothing brand"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_F4Kfw9VUiiTlb/lessons/lesn_wtp3FYHqlGRFc/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Verifying Alibaba Suppliers","where":"Clothing Brand · Logistics","summary":"How to check that an Alibaba supplier is legit, customize their product, and negotiate minimum order quantities.","points":["Alibaba is mostly B2B wholesale (ships to you); message a supplier to ask if they'll dropship straight to customers","Check store rating (4.5+ is good), reorder rate (~20–30% is strong) and years in business; product reviews matter less since buyers are businesses","A ~5-hour response time is normal for suppliers across the world; give them time","Use Send inquiry to customize anything: logo, color, cut, ingredients, distressing","Check the MOQ (minimum order quantity) before ordering, and negotiate both MOQ and price","Example: 1,000 units at ~$1 plus ~$0.14 for a logo is about $1,000 to start your own skincare line; start with a sample","Alibaba's secured payments, easy returns and money-back protection let you test new suppliers safely"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_F4Kfw9VUiiTlb/lessons/lesn_JCkZfnPTCthUW/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"What is a Winning Product","where":"Clothing Brand · Logistics","summary":"What actually makes a winning product: something people need, that makes life better, and that they can't just grab at Walmart.","points":["Sell it as a need, not a want; people buy needs faster (e.g. pitch a diffuser for a runny nose or clear skin)","It should make the buyer's life better, like electric toothbrushes or maids; use that in your FAQ and testimonials","Example: switching to satin-lined hoodie hoods (so hoods don't mess up hair) built a loyal fan base for a few cents more per unit","When a product meets a real need, buyers come back and tell friends","It should be hard to find locally in that exact form (e.g. not just a teddy bear, but one that records a loved one's voice)"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Product research"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_F4Kfw9VUiiTlb/lessons/lesn_4idV1tKFxghX8/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Customizing Your Product","where":"Clothing Brand · Logistics","summary":"How to customize any product on Alibaba for your clothing brand.","points":["Search for the item (e.g. pants) and open a supplier listing","Use Send inquiry to ask for changes: colors, cut-offs, a different look, or your logo","Suppliers will usually find a way to make whatever you ask; the sample may cost a bit more"],"topics":["Clothing brand"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_F4Kfw9VUiiTlb/lessons/lesn_SNjtZlk1YpbJT/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Creating a Personal Brand","where":"Clothing Brand · Logistics","summary":"How a personal brand builds the trust that faceless dropshipping stores lack, and a 4-week plan to launch one.","points":["Faceless stores with stolen supplier videos lose many buyers to trust concerns; reaching 1,000 people who trust you beats a million who don't","You don't need your face; your hands, voice and honest opinions also show a real person behind the store","Content that converts: problem → solution stories, behind-the-scenes (testing, packaging, quality checks), before/after and testimonials from friends or family","Give free value (e.g. \"3 ways to sleep better\") and work the product into it","Short-form video (TikTok, Reels, Shorts): 3–5 raw, conversational 15–60s videos a day beats spending on ads right away","Be transparent: show flaws and real shipping times, and share how you fix customer issues","Run two accounts: a personal brand account (face photo, bio, link to the store) and the store account","4 weeks: film 20 pieces of content → post daily everywhere → double down on what works → add UGC, email and micro-influencers"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Website","Organic marketing","Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_F4Kfw9VUiiTlb/lessons/lesn_mP62BRvr7gWbP/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Leveraging Your Personal Brand","where":"Clothing Brand · Logistics","summary":"How to use a personal brand account to funnel trust and sales into your store.","points":["Your store needs a face, yours or a friend's, or at least your hands and voice","Two-account strategy: a personal account that humanizes you, and a professional brand account (logo, product showcases, promos)","Personal account setup: clear face photo, a 2–3 sentence authentic bio, and a link to the brand","Share life stories, your workspace and packaging, wins and losses, and everyday moments, so people buy the person behind the product","Platform tips: lifestyle posts on Instagram, raw unpolished TikToks, short thoughts on X","Sell subtly with a 90/10 split: 90% authentic content, 10% business (\"just added this to my store\")","Weeks 1–4 just share your story; months 2–3 start weaving in the business; post 4–5 times a day and engage with 10+ people daily"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Website","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_F4Kfw9VUiiTlb/lessons/lesn_2gc3Od7jKap3R/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Setting your Zones/Rates","where":"Clothing Brand · Logistics","summary":"A simple way to set up shipping zones and rates in Shopify.","points":["Set rates by your fulfillment location and zone","Example: free shipping on orders $150+","Economy 1–5 lb: $12; economy 5–70 lb: $21","International economy: about $43"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Website","Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_F4Kfw9VUiiTlb/lessons/lesn_9AWXhznNNRcm3/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"The Sales Funnel","where":"Clothing Brand · Website Maxxing","summary":"A walkthrough of the sales funnel: every step from a customer finding your store to checkout, and what you need at each step.","points":["Discovery: Google, Instagram, TikTok, Facebook, Pinterest, YouTube… → your landing page","On the landing page you need a pop-up to collect email or phone (ask for just one at first) and an urgency notice (\"selling out soon\", limited-time offer, X people viewing) in the top banner","Keep 3–4 products (no more than 5); too many choices means no choice","Add to cart → view cart → upsell: offer matching items (e.g. a beanie with a hoodie), like Target's buy-one-get-one deals","Checkout: shipping and payment info → order confirmation","People leave because shipping is too high, they rethink the spend, they keep shopping, or they simply forget; that's fine if you captured their contact to follow up"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_wSlwG8m885Pu4/lessons/lesn_wKXkFSbob0zmA/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Finding a Font for Your Logo","where":"Clothing Brand · Website Maxxing","summary":"How to pick a font for your logo that fits your niche, using DaFont.","points":["Match the font to your niche and customer: streetwear/vintage vs. fitness vs. professional vs. Western","Browse dafont.com by style (distorted, groovy, Western, comic) and download the ones you like","Dropshipping: you mostly need a clean logo for your profile picture, website and emails; AI-made logos are fine","Clothing brand: work harder on a unique logo and put it everywhere on the product","Give a dropshipping store a brand personality so it doesn't look like a scam (dropshipping is a fulfillment model, not a business)","Avoid fonts that are hard to read"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Product research","Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_wSlwG8m885Pu4/lessons/lesn_yJwzB526LSx9C/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Banners","where":"Clothing Brand · Website Maxxing","summary":"How to set up a Black Friday (or any holiday) sale banner on your Shopify store.","points":["Add an announcement bar at the top: e.g. \"Black Friday Sale – ends soon\"","Change the homepage heading to something like \"Shop Black Friday\"","Set a compare-at price and a sale price so the discount is obvious, but keep a profit; don't sell below cost","Social proof banner: e.g. \"Carson just bought his second hoodie\" from recent customers","The look matters less than marketing it: post organic videos and run ads for the sale"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_wSlwG8m885Pu4/lessons/lesn_Iux26Px9udfoo/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Competition Within The Space","where":"Clothing Brand · Website Maxxing","summary":"How and why to watch your competitors: pricing, product moves, gaps you can fill, and what to track each week.","points":["Know where you stand on price; if you sell at $20 and competitors sell at $60, you can likely raise yours","When competitors switch products or add features (like Hydro Flask), take notes so you don't get left behind","Watch out of motivation, not jealousy: \"if they can do it, I can too\"","Find gaps: more colors, better features, complaints in their reviews (slow shipping, wrong sizes) you can fix","Screen-record competitor sites every couple of weeks; if something is making them money, move on it fast","Watch direct competitors 3–4 times a week, plus indirect ones using the same marketing","Track pricing, site experience (pop-ups collecting phone, email, first name via Klaviyo or Postscript), shipping speed, product selection and promotions","Stay legal: borrow strategies, never copy logos or trademarked designs"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Product research"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_wSlwG8m885Pu4/lessons/lesn_EgG6H5ZkOIiBx/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Avoid Abandoned Carts","where":"Clothing Brand · Website Maxxing","summary":"How to win back abandoned carts with an automated email that pairs an offer with a realistic deadline.","points":["An abandoned cart is a \"soft order\": they wanted it but something stopped them, usually shipping cost, price, or they simply forgot","Set up an automated abandoned cart flow with Shopify Flow, Klaviyo or Postscript","Write casually, not like a corporation: \"You left your cart hanging, here's a coupon to pick it back up\"","Give the offer (a coupon code), then the scarcity: \"only good for the next 10–15 minutes, limited stock\"","Don't overdo it: 30 seconds is unrealistic, and 30 minutes is too loose; 10–15 minutes works best"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Email & SMS"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_wSlwG8m885Pu4/lessons/lesn_8IYlioWKqJ6Qa/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Getting Reviews","where":"Clothing Brand · Website Maxxing","summary":"Why to install the Judge.me reviews app and how it builds trust and repeat sales.","points":["Judge.me lets real buyers leave reviews after they get the product","Show reviews with eye-catching widgets and testimonial sliders","Send coupons or referral rewards to get more reviews and repeat orders","Sync reviews to Google and Meta so they show up when people search for you","Reviews double as feedback, e.g. customers asking for a pink version you can then make"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_wSlwG8m885Pu4/lessons/lesn_cypGrLKDDkFNM/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Building Trust in Your Store","where":"Clothing Brand · Website Maxxing","summary":"A call on collecting customer contacts (CRM) and building trust on your store, so it sells now and is worth more when you sell it.","points":["A real offer (e.g. 10% off or a giveaway) might capture ~300 of 1,000 visitors vs. ~25 from \"join our newsletter\"; emailing 300 buyers of a $100 product adds up fast","A strong CRM can make your store sell for ~35% more; always ask \"how can I collect contacts?\"","Testimonials: import your supplier's real reviews for the same product, or have friends and family try it and leave honest reviews (Judge.me collects reviews from real buyers)","Keep reviews clean and organized, placed after the product, not before it","Focus on building skills (CRM, trust, sales psychology), not just the next sale; stores sell for multiples of annual profit","Q&A: moving contacts from one store to another by exporting and importing a spreadsheet, and telling people they're signing up for promo emails"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Website","Email & SMS"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_wSlwG8m885Pu4/lessons/lesn_fdMMNvxWJFm01/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Website Maximization Masterclass","where":"Clothing Brand · Website Maxxing","summary":"A masterclass on the non-negotiables every landing page needs to feel trustworthy, and why marketing still matters more than design.","points":["Testimonials/reviews: a few is enough (2–3 on the homepage); import supplier reviews for the same product or ask friends for honest reviews, never make them up","About Us page: give the store a deeper purpose and persona, because brands sell a persona (Nike = fit, Balenciaga = mysterious)","FAQ: answer the 5–6 questions people ask most (what does it do, how fast is shipping) to save emails and speed up sales","Contact page at the bottom so people know you're a real business","Show what problem the product solves (e.g. blisters → gym grips) to handle objections","Scarcity during sales: \"selling out soon\" or free shipping for the next 24–48 hours","Don't obsess over colors and wording; marketing gets people to the site","Q&A: no need to buy followers; organic first then push winners to paid (≤25% AI in ads); don't use AI celebrity endorsements; Zendrop/CJ over Alibaba for dropshipping; check LLC rules with a tax advisor"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_wSlwG8m885Pu4/lessons/lesn_GlrMZPUoZNt3f/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Inbedding Base44 With Shopify","where":"Clothing Brand · Website Maxxing","summary":"How to embed a site you built on Base44 into your Shopify store.","points":["Ask Claude or ChatGPT to write an iframe embed code","Copy the published link from your Base44 app and put it in the iframe","Paste the iframe into a custom code section on a Shopify page and preview it","If it shows \"not available\", go to Base44 → Dashboard → Settings → Security and toggle the setting off, then reload","Base44 can't be added as a Shopify app, so this is the workaround (Wix is easier to embed into)"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Website","AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_wSlwG8m885Pu4/lessons/lesn_n95sxz0qs87Jh/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"The Point of Marketing","where":"Clothing Brand · Marketing","summary":"Why marketing is everything when you start: zero traffic means zero sales, so get in front of as many people as possible, everywhere.","points":["Your store won't magically show up in search; you have to build momentum, and early traction creates social proof","Cast a wide net, then double down on the audience and channels that respond","Start with your personal network: post your story on your own socials, ask friends and family to share (offer a free gift)","Old-school still works: flyers, business cards, local events, car magnets and stickers with your URL or IG handle","Partner up: micro-influencers with a promo code, cross-promos with non-competing brands, affiliate programs","Content and SEO: blog posts (e.g. \"how to sleep better\" linking to your product), demos, unboxings, testimonials, podcasts, UGC, Reddit and Facebook groups","Try at least 3 channels, check analytics daily and adjust; every store started at zero"],"topics":["Clothing brand"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_mTGUmKFzrElYs/lessons/lesn_NVbLQTHDTR5MJ/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"All Types of Marketing","where":"Clothing Brand · Marketing","summary":"An overview of every type of marketing you can use, organic and paid, and which ones matter most.","points":["Organic videos: your face helps at first, or just your hands on a tripod; later you can hire a Fiverr creator","Be a walking billboard: wear or use your product in public so people ask about it","Micro-influencers: DM them and offer free product; if they want pay, negotiate low and be ready to walk away, unless it's a big influencer worth a contract","Big-influencer deals pay off long-term because you collect their audience's contacts","Paid: Pinterest (niche audiences), Snapchat (younger buyers), Google Ads (better once you're a big brand; use SEO until then), Meta (Facebook/Instagram/Threads) and TikTok","Seeing your product on TikTok and then Meta makes people more likely to buy","Main ones to focus on: organic videos and influencers, plus Meta and TikTok ads"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_mTGUmKFzrElYs/lessons/lesn_hqEE3b8dSFfsQ/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"What Diff Marketing is For","where":"Clothing Brand · Marketing","summary":"What each marketing channel is actually good for: awareness, contacts, traffic or sales.","points":["Organic: great for awareness; contacts, traffic and sales are medium and depend on consistency and going viral; use it to fund ads","UGC creators: the best awareness (a trusted face in the niche), lower traffic, but high conversion when they promote it","Email/SMS: very high traffic and sales because people already know you; just reminders to buy","Paid ads: built for traffic and sales, weaker for awareness","Awareness is the most underrated: it builds longevity and makes your store worth more when you sell"],"topics":["Clothing brand"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_mTGUmKFzrElYs/lessons/lesn_5mOZCp4aroF1O/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"What Each Type of Marketing is Good For","where":"Clothing Brand · Marketing","summary":"Why sales, traffic, contacts and awareness all matter equally, and why chasing only sales is a mistake.","points":["Sales = people buying; traffic = people reaching your site; contacts = email/phone collected; awareness = people getting familiar with your brand","Each depends on the others; you can't get sales without traffic and awareness","Don't go into ecommerce focused only on money; build all four","Be grateful for sessions and contacts even without sales; visitors often come back later and buy"],"topics":["Clothing brand"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_mTGUmKFzrElYs/lessons/lesn_oHR2mntkUSPn2/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Mistakes Commonly Made in Marketing","where":"Clothing Brand · Marketing","summary":"The biggest organic marketing mistake: not marketing enough and expecting sales anyway.","points":["No marketing = zero sales; even Jeff Bezos couldn't sell from a store nobody knows about","Marketing should get about 80% of your attention","Posting once a week and expecting millions doesn't work; stay consistent","Big brands like Balenciaga do it all, nonstop: IG, TikTok, Pinterest, Snapchat posts, influencers, ads on every platform, email and SMS"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_mTGUmKFzrElYs/lessons/lesn_BOu2HNByFcaLK/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Marketing to the Right Audience","where":"Clothing Brand · Marketing","summary":"Which age groups each social platform reaches, and how to match your product to the right ones.","points":["TikTok: very young and older; Snapchat: young to middle; Pinterest: young adults (~21–23)","Facebook and Messenger: older (40–50+); Instagram: middle","Threads and YouTube Shorts: teens and under 18","Map your product: e.g. fitness → middle ages; skincare → young and older","Go hardest where your audience is, but still post the same video everywhere; any platform can go viral"],"topics":["Clothing brand"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_mTGUmKFzrElYs/lessons/lesn_0oWxo72TWXmuh/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Setting Up Email/SMS for Marketing","where":"Clothing Brand · Marketing","summary":"How to set up Klaviyo for email and SMS marketing on your Shopify store.","points":["Sign up, enter your business details and address, and set a sender email","Choose email and SMS (push notifications optional); start on the free plan","Connect Shopify, then use the guided setup to make a sign-up form","Create a campaign: pick or import your audience list","Write a strong subject line and preview text (e.g. \"Shop now\" / \"Offer ends soon\") to catch their eye","Pick a template, edit it and send; the dashboard shows how your emails perform"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Website","Email & SMS"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_mTGUmKFzrElYs/lessons/lesn_lZrCuoXriMjVl/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"POST EVERYWHERE!","where":"Clothing Brand · Marketing","summary":"A reminder to post your organic content on every platform instead of being a perfectionist.","points":["Post on TikTok, Instagram, YouTube, Facebook, Snapchat, Pinterest and more; reach is a long game","A worse-looking store can outsell yours just by reaching more people","You don't need 7 different videos: make one and post it everywhere (use Facebook/IG cross-posting)","Organic reach is free, so go broad; narrow your audience only when you're paying for ads"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_mTGUmKFzrElYs/lessons/lesn_YaciT6sEKZIF2/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Leveraging Cross Platform Marketing","where":"Clothing Brand · Marketing","summary":"How to subtly promote one store's product inside another store's content (cross-promotion for MEO).","points":["Example: promoting one product while wearing your own brand's hoodie in the video","Viewers who don't want the main product may still want the hoodie","Keep it subtle; don't push two products at once, and wait until a product is popping off","Like Dr Pepper logo ads, the goal is recognition; people trust a brand they've seen before","Works in organic and paid content; viewers don't realize both stores are yours"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Organic marketing","Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_mTGUmKFzrElYs/lessons/lesn_uFeTTFsdeDCGB/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"More Marketing Review","where":"Clothing Brand · Marketing","summary":"Breaking down real viral product videos to see what makes winning organic marketing work.","points":["Example: an electromagnetic anti-frost tool, with the video showing exactly what it does for people in cold weather","Show the product working, not just holding it up; every good organic post is relatable","Target the right audience, even in ways that spark comments and engagement","Put the domain in the comments instead of hard-selling \"link in bio\"","The viral video came after 50–100+ posts; consistency is what wins","One creator's store likely makes six figures a month from this approach"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Website","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_mTGUmKFzrElYs/lessons/lesn_P57bGEjqkQt62/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Using Competitors for Leverage","where":"Clothing Brand · Marketing","summary":"How to train a fresh TikTok account so your For You page fills with competitors' product videos you can learn from.","points":["Make a new account and only like, follow and comment on accounts promoting products","Soon your feed is full of sellers and viral product videos, even outside your niche","Study how they promote, which trends they follow, and their hooks","One viral video can put your store on the map, especially if you collect contacts","Many of these videos are simple and took 5–15 minutes; they don't need to be professional"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Product research","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_mTGUmKFzrElYs/lessons/lesn_ISM5Rpk3H14Gg/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Finding Marketing Ideas","where":"Clothing Brand · Marketing","summary":"How to find marketing ideas by searching TikTok for how others sell products in your niche.","points":["Search \"TikTok made me buy it\" or \"buy now\" + your niche (e.g. fitness)","Note hooks that grab attention in the first second and formats like replying to comments","Look at what gets comments, even controversial angles","Interact with those videos so more seller content shows up on your For You page","Spot trends many sellers copy (e.g. sticking a product on everything); they're doing market research on each other too"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Product research","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_mTGUmKFzrElYs/lessons/lesn_oR0I9dw2Eu7kI/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Patterns Within Winning Ads","where":"Clothing Brand · Marketing","summary":"Spotting the patterns in winning competitor ads: present the problem first, then the solution.","points":["Example: disposable face towels that open with the bacteria problem in normal towels before introducing the product","Example: a travel bag ad opening with \"Who else overcomplicates airport travel?\"","Winning ads present the problem, then show the product as the solution and how it's different","Just showing the product only works for huge brands like Balenciaga or Gymshark","Use this structure when making your Facebook ads"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Product research","Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_mTGUmKFzrElYs/lessons/lesn_YBCNXPySdqSbU/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"OM Isn't as Hard as You Think, Here's Why","where":"Clothing Brand · Marketing","summary":"Proof that organic marketing doesn't need fancy editing: real stores reposting the same simple video with new captions.","points":["A seller likely making ~$100K/month posts the same 3-minute phone recording over and over, changing only the caption or intro","They post constantly; early videos flopped until the brand caught on, and now they average ~30K views","Don't shove \"buy now\" in people's faces; it makes the store feel sketchy","Once a video blows up (e.g. 4M views), run it as a Facebook ad","Treat each video like a lottery ticket; consistency is what eventually hits","Collecting contacts matters more than one-off sales: 100K contacts beats $200K from one viral video"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_mTGUmKFzrElYs/lessons/lesn_mJFmpYzahtgwk/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"The Whole Point of OM","where":"Clothing Brand · Marketing","summary":"What organic marketing is really for: awareness and sessions now, sales over time.","points":["Organic brings lots of sessions but fewer sales; it's mainly awareness and collecting contacts","Real example: about $30K in sales from organic over ~2.5 weeks, after hundreds of posts","Sales start when people see you over and over","It's free: why spend $50 to reach 1,000 people on ads when 10 extra minutes can reach 2,000?","Even 500-view videos are potential buyers; Facebook ads are where you'll see more direct sales"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_mTGUmKFzrElYs/lessons/lesn_kSCGIVb6PDwtk/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Why to Prioritize OM > Paid Ads","where":"Clothing Brand · Marketing","summary":"Why to start with organic marketing before paid ads: it gives you free audience data so you don't burn money.","points":["Paid ads without data is guessing, and you might target teens when older buyers want it, or the reverse","Before ads, know at least 3 things: where buyers are (country, state, city), when they're active and their age range","Switch to a creator (or business) account to see the same analytics Facebook would give you, for free","The people engaging with your organic posts are your buyers; trust the algorithm","Use the push-and-go method: validate with organic, then push winners with paid","Post 3–5 times a day; it's only about 20 minutes"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Organic marketing","Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_mTGUmKFzrElYs/lessons/lesn_7oJgzInsJr5au/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"OM More In Depth","where":"Clothing Brand · Marketing","summary":"Three deeper keys to organic marketing: stay consistent, learn your real audience, and make videos people engage with.","points":["Your first videos probably won't go viral; the algorithm wants to see consistency. Spend no more than 15–30 minutes on each of your first ~10 videos","TAE (target audience engagement): after ~11 posts, check who's actually engaging; it may not be who you expected","Write down 5 traits of your ideal buyer (age, location, interests like rap artists, occasions like birthdays) and use them for content ideas","Never delete videos; the algorithm uses the data, and videos can blow up weeks or months later","WWW: Would this make me buy? After 11–15 videos, judge each video honestly","Give people something to comment on: captions (\"imagine having this in college\"), trending audio, questions, giveaways (\"comments without a reply get it free\")","Ask: would I watch this fully or scroll past? Make it more entertaining"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Mindset","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_mTGUmKFzrElYs/lessons/lesn_fBUYOTJveiK0i/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"The OM Algorithm","where":"Clothing Brand · Marketing","summary":"How to \"POK\" your store: write SOPs (standard operating procedures) for everything that works, so others can run it. (Note: despite the lesson title, this video covers SOPs, not the OM algorithm.)","points":["Keep an SOP index with IDs (e.g. SOP-002), department, version (1.0, 2.0…), status (active, draft, deactivated) and review cycle","Review cycles: product research monthly (TikTok, Meta Ad Library), suppliers quarterly, store setup quarterly (holidays), paid ads weekly, support and fulfillment monthly","Each SOP explains why it exists, what success looks like (not just money), the tools and access levels, and when it's used","Everyone using your SOPs must sign a contract not to share them or take over the product or accounts","Example marketing steps: order a sample, set up all platforms, replicate 2–3 competitor videos a day; don't expect results until ~150 posts","With MEO you can have things outsourced within ~2 months; even at $1M/month you need about 4 people"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_mTGUmKFzrElYs/lessons/lesn_SThCaF80truHH/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to Make OM Easier","where":"Clothing Brand · Marketing","summary":"How to make organic marketing easier by batching and scheduling your posts.","points":["Use TikTok Studio (app) → Post → Schedule post; you can schedule up to about 7 days out","Batch 7–8 videos in one sitting (e.g. Friday) and schedule them over the next few days","Instagram and YouTube also allow scheduling (sometimes after a short wait)","Use the saved time for finding products and other marketing","If scheduled posts don't get consistent views, go back to posting live"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_mTGUmKFzrElYs/lessons/lesn_I9iwJYKVhleIY/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How I do my OM","where":"Clothing Brand · Marketing","summary":"A look at the coach's own brand account (100K real followers) and the simple organic videos that worked.","points":["Early videos were filmed in his room and still got ~45K views; simple beats perfect","Recent posts: hoodies laid out at sunset, outfits, a streamer wearing the hoodie, back-to-school and Christmas angles","One post hit ~600K views for free and may bring ~$100K including the contacts collected","Engagement tricks: \"comments with no replies\" giveaways and $1-drop teasers","Once you have an audience, contacts + weekly emails + Facebook ads do much of the work","Seeing your brand daily keeps reminding people to buy"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_mTGUmKFzrElYs/lessons/lesn_r0qABcCm4al4a/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Doing OM Step by Step","where":"Clothing Brand · Marketing","summary":"A step-by-step demo of making quick \"starter\" organic videos for a clothing brand and a dropshipping product.","points":["Find a trending sound (press + on TikTok for recommended sounds)","Simple setup: lay the product on the floor and film it; each video took about 3 minutes","Film in your phone's camera app, not inside TikTok, so you can reuse clips on every platform with new captions","Use the 3 W's: give people something to comment on, e.g. \"Have you ever seen a hoodie with a custom chain?\" or \"Imagine having this as a night light when we were younger\"","Comment-driven ideas work (a dropshipper got huge views doing what commenters asked, like putting Sprite in a diffuser)","15 minutes a day = 5 videos = 5 chances to go viral; ChatGPT can write captions"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Organic marketing","Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_mTGUmKFzrElYs/lessons/lesn_8YyRrEFJufLJs/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Running an OM Social Media","where":"Clothing Brand · Marketing","summary":"Pros and cons of TikTok, Instagram and YouTube Shorts for organic marketing, and how often to post.","points":["TikTok pros: people comment the most, posts blow up bigger (followers, shares, saves), and detailed analytics. Cons: need ~1,000 followers for a bio link, bot comments, and videos can take weeks to pop (never delete them)","Instagram pros: bio link from day one, pinned comments (\"go to my page to shop\"), scheduling later on. Cons: slower traction, fewer insights, the occasional hater (who actually boosts your video)","YouTube Shorts pros: very fast traction, good engagement, longer videos later. Cons: no DMs, no sharing to stories, people rarely share","Don't make new videos per platform; post the same one everywhere","It's free promotion that takes 4–5 minutes; post about 2–3 times a day per platform, but no more than ~5 so you don't look like a bot"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_mTGUmKFzrElYs/lessons/lesn_GceNvSWLXDeKW/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"AI to Help With OM","where":"Clothing Brand · Marketing","summary":"How to use Claude (or ChatGPT) to come up with organic video ideas for your brand.","points":["Start the prompt with context: you do organic marketing for your brand on YouTube Shorts, Snapchat Spotlight, etc., and you're running out of ideas","Describe what the brand is and your target audience (e.g. streetwear, ages 16–25)","If the ideas are weak, ask it to make them more engaging, or describe your best videos and competitors' videos (B-roll + engaging questions like \"Which fit would you wear?\")","If it drifts into writing scripts, pull it back to the original prompt","Ask it to turn what you like into 25 similar content ideas, e.g. \"fit in the city\", \"I wore this to the gym\", \"my mom rated my fit\""],"topics":["Clothing brand","Organic marketing","AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_mTGUmKFzrElYs/lessons/lesn_lgMNYePHDvwVL/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Scaling With OM","where":"Clothing Brand · Marketing","summary":"The 6 most common organic marketing mistakes, and how one viral video can turn into a six-figure payday with the right funnel.","points":["Expecting results fast: treat posts like lottery tickets; one video with 1M views → buyers + ~10K contacts → repeat sales by email/SMS over the years","Making high-quality posts too early: don't spend more than ~15 minutes per video; one hour could be 10 videos","Posting low-quality content: blurry screenshots from Google, Alibaba or Zendrop won't go viral; aim for decent","Not using the 3 W's: give people something to comment on and react to","Not posting 3–5 decent videos a day; a student who kept posting for weeks with no results finally had one video change his life","Too many hashtags: stick to 3–5 relevant ones (e.g. #fitness #viral #fyp)"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Website","Organic marketing","Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_mTGUmKFzrElYs/lessons/lesn_LPmEmu2k1ho3C/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Scaling with FB Ads","where":"Clothing Brand · Marketing","summary":"How to scale with Facebook (Meta) ads once your foundation is solid: the numbers to hit and the scaling methods that work.","points":["Build the foundation first: do organic before paid so you know who your real audience is (who comments, follows and likes)","Aim for an AOV of $35+ (ideally $50–100); good marketing lets you charge more than competitors","Your store must look legit and organized; 1,000 visitors and 2 checkouts points to design or pricing","Have 3–5 profitable ad sets before scaling, ideally after ~100–120 organic videos","Start at $5–10/day, 3–4 creatives, one interest per ad set (e.g. gym, outdoors)","Targets: CTR above 2%, cost per purchase under ~$10–15, ROAS 1.3–3x; watch add to carts (abandoned checkout above ~45% means fix price, shipping or follow-ups)","Scale winners, not hopes: raise budget 20–30% every 24 hours (never double overnight), refresh creatives every 3–5 days to avoid ad fatigue","Scaling methods: CBO, duplicate winning ad sets (ABO), and Advantage+ shopping campaigns"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Paid ads","Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_mTGUmKFzrElYs/lessons/lesn_YdCN6qjgkBMlr/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Running FB Ads","where":"Clothing Brand · Marketing","summary":"A walkthrough of setting up a Facebook (Meta) sales campaign in Ads Manager, step by step.","points":["Create → choose the Sales objective (use traffic or awareness only to hype a drop before launch)","Manual setup; your Meta pixel connects through Shopify's Facebook & Instagram app; set the conversion event to Purchase","Audience: not too narrow, not too broad (don't target \"dogs and cats\"); use what organic taught you","Locations: US, UK, Canada, Australia for English stores, or the opposite hemisphere for seasonal clothes","Budget: start around $5/day; start at 12 AM the next day and set an end date 7–10 days out","Skip the AI-suggested ads; connect your Facebook page and Instagram; use a single image or video","Write primary text, headline (\"Just restocked – selling out soon\"), description (e.g. 10% off the first 10 orders), Shop Now button, promo code and a short display link"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_mTGUmKFzrElYs/lessons/lesn_7TecujsOwCrsJ/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"FB Ads Pt. 2","where":"Clothing Brand · Marketing","summary":"Part 2 of running Meta ads: the opportunity score, reading the campaign dashboard, and building a campaign with detailed targeting.","points":["Opportunity score: Meta rates your ad setup; below 60 is bad, ~73 is the sweet spot","Name campaigns by product and budget; test new products at $2–5/day","Avoid duplicating winners if money is tight (it pushes you to spend more); create new ones instead","A/B testing pits creatives against each other for a set time and keeps the winner","Dashboard: results, cost per result, amount spent, impressions vs. reach; keep attribution at 7-day click","Objectives: awareness before a drop, sales during it, traffic or leads for contacts","New ad accounts should start low (~$5/day) to build trust with Meta","Use organic data to narrow targeting by location, age and interests (e.g. Atlanta Braves fans 35+); exclude young placements like Threads for older audiences","Instant forms collect leads inside Meta; always add your URL and connect your CRM (Shopify Email, Klaviyo, Postscript)"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_mTGUmKFzrElYs/lessons/lesn_vOTQkxVDqa3Re/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Watching Comp Meta Ads","where":"Clothing Brand · Marketing","summary":"How to use the Facebook Ad Library to study competitors' ads and spot winners.","points":["Facebook Ad Library → All ads → search keywords in your niche (e.g. fitness, skincare)","An ad running for months (e.g. since February, 3+ months) is likely a winner","Make a similar ad for your product, never copy theirs","Write down patterns you see repeated across ads (hooks, problem/solution, testimonials)","Real-person ads usually beat AI ones; most winners use real people"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Product research","Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_mTGUmKFzrElYs/lessons/lesn_DRIe9VuXEhpev/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Even More FB Ad Sauce","where":"Clothing Brand · Marketing","summary":"How to set up multiple creatives and ad sets in one Meta campaign and A/B test them.","points":["Create → Auction buying type → pick an objective (awareness, traffic, engagement, leads, sales)","A campaign is a folder; inside it, duplicate ad sets to test different creatives (e.g. \"water gun testing creatives 1, 2…\")","Duplicate into the original campaign, an existing one, or a new one","Use A/B (creative) testing to compare names, images or copy","Connect both your Facebook and Instagram profiles for more reach","Pick the conversion event (purchase, add to cart, leads) and a Shop Now call to action"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_mTGUmKFzrElYs/lessons/lesn_t6WKj7BYZOTGn/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to analyze fb ad (CAOCH SAUCE)","where":"Clothing Brand · Marketing","summary":"How to read the key metrics in Facebook Ads Manager and decide which ads to keep on, kill or give more time.","points":["CPM (cost per 1,000 impressions): $20–30 is normal; very cheap isn't always good, it can mean a cheap audience","CPC under $2 is fine, under $1 is solid; CTR 2% is fine, 2–3%+ is strong","CPA (cost per result) is what each sale costs you; compare it to your own break-even, e.g. $30 price − $10 COGS = $20 max","ROAS 2.0+ is good; also watch add to carts, checkouts initiated and frequency","Start testing at $20–50/day; around $10 spend, check for clicks and add to carts, but let a new ad account warm up a few days","Real examples: great CPC and CTR but no add to carts after $14 = killed; bad CTR but sales at a $20 CPA = kept on","If several similar ads flop, the near-copies will likely flop too; make iterations of your winners"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_mTGUmKFzrElYs/lessons/lesn_gmdb72NAPsNH5/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Pumping ads. ($$)","where":"Clothing Brand · Marketing","summary":"A masterclass on not burning money with ads: how to read your ad numbers, which ads to scale or kill, and how to raise budget safely.","points":["Check your ad analytics every day, but for data, not emotions; never run an ad and ghost it for a week","Raise budget only 10–30% a day; jumping from $120 to $1,000/day confuses the algorithm and it shows the ad to anyone","Low CTR = bad ad. High CTR but no sales = a website problem (missing reviews, testimonials, trust)","Structure: campaigns → a couple of ad sets → 3–5 ads each; compare CTR and ROAS across them","Scale the one with good ROAS even if CTR is only decent; good ROAS with low CTR needs a harder call to action, same concept","Kill ads with both low CTR and bad ROAS (e.g. 0.25x) and move that budget to the winners; ROAS matters more than conversion rate","Give an ad 5–7 days for the learning phase, let Andromeda pick the audience, and keep it to about 5 creatives per ad set (mix images and videos); put a strong creative in its own ad set if Meta starves it","Q&A on AI video: 720p is fine if your images are sharp, upscale in CapCut instead of paying to render, Kling 4.0 and Seedance tips, and use ChatGPT for reference images"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Paid ads","Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_mTGUmKFzrElYs/lessons/lesn_w3Z883waDyaIf/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Do These Daily for Success","where":"Clothing Brand · Scaling","summary":"The daily routine for running your store, about 1 hour 20 minutes a day.","points":["Push out marketing and check on it (~35 min): post organic videos, DM creators, check ads and which audience you're reaching","Stay on top of products and trends (~30 min): scroll your burner TikTok of sellers and spot trends to capitalize on (e.g. a hat with a fan when it's hot)","Watch competitors in the Facebook Ad Library; if they jump on a new product or trend, you should too","Customer service: answer emails (AI can draft replies)","Check Shopify order status (~5–10 min) to make sure orders ship","Most important: marketing and staying updated on trends. At minimum do the free stuff daily (organic + DMing creators), ideally with your own sample footage"],"topics":["Clothing brand"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_MAv8LYK1raB8K/lessons/lesn_00n2651iIF5Xs/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Setting up Email/SMS Flow","where":"Clothing Brand · Scaling","summary":"How to set up Klaviyo for email and SMS marketing on your Shopify store.","points":["Sign up, enter your business details and address, and set a sender email","Choose email and SMS (push notifications optional); start on the free plan","Connect Shopify, then use the guided setup to make a sign-up form","Create a campaign: pick or import your audience list","Write a strong subject line and preview text (e.g. \"Shop now\" / \"Offer ends soon\") to catch their eye","Pick a template, edit it and send; the dashboard shows how your emails perform"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Website","Email & SMS"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_MAv8LYK1raB8K/lessons/lesn_k7Z4j4uDz1ueJ/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"All About CLV","where":"Clothing Brand · Scaling","summary":"How to raise customer lifetime value (CLV) and retention, so customers are worth far more than one sale.","points":["Getting a new customer costs 5–25x more than keeping one; repeat buyers drive most revenue","CLV = AOV × purchases per year × years as a customer (e.g. $50 × 3 × 2 = $300)","With CLV in mind you can afford to break even on the first sale (e.g. $30 to acquire → $120 profit over time)","Upsells and cross-sells: volume discounts, bundles, premium versions, post-purchase one-click add-ons, warranties (CartHook, Zipify OCU, Shopify's built-in)","Subscriptions for consumables (supplements, beauty, pet food) with discounts, free shipping and easy pause/skip (Recharge, Bold); even durable-goods stores can add a subscription box","Email/SMS: welcome series, 3-part abandoned cart, post-purchase thank-you + tutorial + cross-sell, win-back after 2 months, review requests 7–10 days after delivery; SMS under 160 characters, sent 11am–2pm or 6–8pm","Community: private Facebook group, Discord or IG broadcast list with tips, early access and UGC contests","Tools: Klaviyo/Omnisend/Mailchimp, Postscript/Attentive, Smile.io/LoyaltyLion/Yotpo, Google Analytics 4 or Triple Whale. 90-day plan: analytics + flows + bundles → upsells + loyalty + SMS → subscriptions + community"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Email & SMS"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_MAv8LYK1raB8K/lessons/lesn_5oL7l8i1uNQVp/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Focus on The Stuff That Works","where":"Clothing Brand · Scaling","summary":"How to spot and ride trends early, test them cheaply, and scale what works.","points":["Entrepreneurs see trends 3–6 months early; being first (like rhinestone hoodies) means bigger margins before suppliers and competitors catch on","Add a twist to a trend (e.g. a pink furry beanie riding the beanie trend; satin lining to justify a higher price)","Trend-watch systems: burner TikTok accounts following 100+ sellers in your niche, hashtags, competitor launches, tools like Exploding Topics, and seasonal search patterns","Being in a community like this one helps you hear about trends first","Focus on content quality (before/after, transitions) after your first ~100–140 posts","Partner with 10–20 smaller niche creators (5–10% engagement) instead of celebrities; send samples before launch","Test small: 50–100 units or pre-orders, aim for a 72-hour sellout once experienced, and track sellout rate, margin (>15%) and returns (<10%)","Content tips: unboxings, show the product in use, offer multiple colors, and film from flattering angles"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Product research","Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_MAv8LYK1raB8K/lessons/lesn_onjFYkpoUMAzO/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Don't Waste All Your Energy on This","where":"Clothing Brand · Scaling","summary":"Three things beginners waste too much energy on, and one more to avoid.","points":["The website: customers come for the product, not your color scheme","Over-polishing organic videos: spending 15+ minutes on a 5-second clip leads to burnout; push out value, not perfection","Over-perfecting the product: get the design and quality right, then stop tweaking","Listing too many products: you need 1–3, not dog beds and treadmills in one store"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Getting started"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_MAv8LYK1raB8K/lessons/lesn_6LnTHpSVCyXBe/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Scaling Purely With OM","where":"Clothing Brand · Scaling","summary":"The 6 most common organic marketing mistakes, and how one viral video can turn into a six-figure payday with the right funnel.","points":["Expecting results fast: treat posts like lottery tickets; one video with 1M views → buyers + ~10K contacts → repeat sales by email/SMS over the years","Making high-quality posts too early: don't spend more than ~15 minutes per video; one hour could be 10 videos","Posting low-quality content: blurry screenshots from Google, Alibaba or Zendrop won't go viral; aim for decent","Not using the 3 W's: give people something to comment on and react to","Not posting 3–5 decent videos a day; a student who kept posting for weeks with no results finally had one video change his life","Too many hashtags: stick to 3–5 relevant ones (e.g. #fitness #viral #fyp)"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Website","Organic marketing","Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_MAv8LYK1raB8K/lessons/lesn_zx86DfaMiibQP/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Using AI To Reach Out to UGC Creators","where":"Clothing Brand · Scaling","summary":"How to use AI to write DMs that get UGC creators to reply and promote your product.","points":["Prompt: ask AI to write a short, eye-catching TikTok DM asking a creator to promote your product","Offer ladder: free product for a video → money + product → commission + product; big creators may want money upfront","Say what your brand stands for and why it fits their audience (e.g. a grind-mentality brand)","Use \"we\" so it reads like a real company; keep it short","Don't state the exact price or commission up front; say you're \"willing to pay upfront\" so they reply and you can negotiate","A curiosity opener (\"Did you hear about this?\") gets more opens"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Organic marketing","AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_MAv8LYK1raB8K/lessons/lesn_KiF3ojt4IxRuo/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Using Other Influencers to Market for You","where":"Clothing Brand · Scaling","summary":"How to get influencers in your niche to market your product for you.","points":["Search your niche (fitness, skincare, pet advice) for creators who post consistently, e.g. 29K or 182K followers","DM them as a company: \"we specialize in X and would like to sponsor you\"","You don't have to pay upfront: give them a custom Shopify code and a share of revenue (e.g. 25%)","Or offer a flat rate, e.g. $25–50 for a set of videos","The goal isn't only sales; a trusted voice in the niche makes your own marketing more believable"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Product research","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_MAv8LYK1raB8K/lessons/lesn_FnN8mchHlQ9Mg/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Using AI to UGC","where":"Clothing Brand · Scaling","summary":"How to make AI UGC videos by recreating a real UGC creator's video with your product.","points":["Search TikTok for a UGC creator video in your niche to use as a reference","Describe the video second by second to Claude (e.g. 0–10s: protein powder pushed next to a smoothie; 10–11s: milk poured, scoop taken) and ask it to write a script for an AI video tool","Claude is recommended for scripts","In the AI UGC tool, pick an avatar (avoid overused ones), add your product image and generate","You can also recreate existing templates with your product, then cut up clips for organic marketing","Still film some content yourself for extra footage and to save money"],"topics":["Clothing brand","Organic marketing","AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_QBd0F1ojcAMcVg/app/courses/cors_MAv8LYK1raB8K/lessons/lesn_3Pl7ixjPxYKy2/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Before you start, listen to this!","where":"MEO","summary":"What MEO is and what you need before starting this section.","points":["A basic overview of MEO","Master the e-commerce basics first, before advanced strategies like ringchaining","Ideally scale your first store (or be able to explain the process simply) before watching","These videos are short and advanced, with no beginner material","Be ready to apply everything you learn directly to your store"],"topics":["Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_5IlwoO5vQQAZ1I/app/courses/cors_uxsGaAxG2Lnsz/lessons/lesn_87dWWeL4hXRt8/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"CRITERIA BEFORE STARTING","where":"MEO","summary":"The requirements before starting MEO (multi-store e-commerce) and why you should master single-store e-commerce first.","points":["Don't jump straight into MEO. The dropshipping and clothing brand modules already teach MEO concepts within a single store","Before MEO you should understand marketing, systems and how to run one store profitably","Jumping in early is like throwing a beginner straight into an NFL game. You need the practice first","The MEO section has fewer modules because it's focused on systems: ringchaining, parking (POK), exiting/selling stores and more. The videos get straight to the point","Think of it like a house: the foundation (regular e-commerce) takes the longest, and MEO is the furniture you add once it's built"],"topics":["Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_5IlwoO5vQQAZ1I/app/courses/cors_uxsGaAxG2Lnsz/lessons/lesn_kJUm9APslkl4m/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Understanding MEO","where":"MEO","summary":"What MEO (multi e-com operations) is and why it's described as \"the real estate of e-commerce\".","points":["MEO means building online stores as digital assets: grow a store's profits, then outsource it or sell it, instead of running every task yourself","Like real estate investors who don't maintain every property, MEO owners hire specialists (marketing, customer service, logistics) and focus on scaling a portfolio","Benefits: multiple income streams, assets across different niches (less risk), and repeatable systems you can apply to new stores","Two models: outsourcing (keep ownership, a team runs the store, you collect profit, like renting to tenants) or store flipping (scale to steady monthly revenue, then sell)","Store-building process: find a profitable niche and product, build a professional store, drive targeted traffic, optimize products/pricing/suppliers, document SOPs, hire a team, and find buyers","Key skills: niche selection, simple repeatable systems, team management and understanding analytics","Common challenges and fixes: find reliable help on sites like Upwork or Fiverr, keep quality up with detailed SOPs, pick evergreen high-margin niches, and build store value with 3–4 products and several traffic sources","Action plan: master regular e-commerce first, start with one store, build your network, then choose your MEO path (outsourcing is recommended)"],"topics":["Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_5IlwoO5vQQAZ1I/app/courses/cors_uxsGaAxG2Lnsz/lessons/lesn_1abtRU2zVZl75eO4Z0XAuH/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"CONTACTS FOR MEO!","where":"MEO","summary":"Why collecting customer contacts (email and SMS) is the backbone of MEO, and how to grow and use your contact list across multiple stores.","points":["Your contact list connects all your stores. Most visitors don't buy the first time, so contacts let you reach them again for months or years","Email marketing is known for a strong return on spend, and SMS gets much higher open rates than email","Ways to collect contacts: exit-intent pop-ups (10–15% off or free shipping), clear opt-ins at checkout, spin-to-win style offers, and useful freebies like guides or size charts","Only market to people who agreed to it, don't spam, and follow email/SMS consent rules (not legal advice)","Ask buyers within 24 hours of purchase to join SMS updates (e.g. order tracking). Try to collect both email and phone","Benchmarks: aim for 2–3% of visitors subscribing, expect SMS opt-ins to grow slower, and target ~15% monthly list growth","With subscriber consent, cross-promote related stores in similar niches (e.g. supplements and fitness apparel). Tag subscribers by purchase and browsing behavior","Build separate stores instead of piling every product into one. Several stores each doing well are worth more when you sell them","Hire a good copywriter (often paid on a percentage) with e-commerce, Klaviyo/Shopify and analytics experience to run campaigns across all stores","Roadmap: build to ~5,000 contacts per store, then set up automations (welcome, abandoned cart, post-purchase), then cross-promote stores, then bring in help to manage it all"],"topics":["Scaling & MEO","Email & SMS"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_5IlwoO5vQQAZ1I/app/courses/cors_uxsGaAxG2Lnsz/lessons/lesn_6m079tyWy0spp0ILLdigzI/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Choosing good exit niche!","where":"MEO","summary":"How to choose a niche with a good \"exit\" (resale) value for MEO, using Shop Hunter to study successful stores.","points":["Use Shop Hunter to see competitor stores' sales, product launch dates, store age and how many ads they run","Women's beauty is one of the strongest niches: it grows a store quickly and still sells well. Men's niches (like fitness or cars) can offer strong long-term exit value too","Many stores doing big numbers were started years ago and only recently took off. MEO and e-commerce are a long game, not get-rich-quick","Example: a simple gray hoodie store running just a few ads is building a customer list that should grow sales a lot over time","Contact lists drive long-term growth. Most visitors don't buy right away, and regular emails/SMS bring them back when they're ready. Established stores can earn a big share of revenue from email and SMS","Some stores make steady sales with zero ads thanks to loyal customers and products people need","Products that look different and aren't saturated (eye-catching creatives) can outsell basic products in crowded niches","Shoppers often need to visit about three times before they trust a store. Longevity and loyal customers raise what a store can sell for"],"topics":["Scaling & MEO","Product research"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_5IlwoO5vQQAZ1I/app/courses/cors_uxsGaAxG2Lnsz/lessons/lesn_Qtltum9J5wguB/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to POK (SOPs for Your Store)","where":"MEO","summary":"How to create SOPs (standard operating procedures) for your store so tasks can be handed off and scaled, using the first several SOPs from one of the mentor's stores as an example. This is what \"POK\" (turning your store into a system others can run) is built on.","points":["Keep an SOP index/tracker: each SOP gets an ID, name, department (product, operations, tech, fulfillment, support, marketing, finance), version number, status (active, deactivated, draft) and review cycle","Suggested review cycles: product monthly, suppliers and store setup quarterly (e.g. before holidays), paid ads weekly, support and fulfillment monthly, more often as you grow","Each SOP should explain why it exists, what success looks like (consistent results and smart spending, not just money) and which tools are needed","Set access levels for each team member and keep logins secure","Anyone using your SOPs should sign an agreement not to share them or take over your products and accounts. Get proper legal advice on the contract","Example marketing SOP steps: order a sample, set up all platforms, study competitors for inspiration, post consistently, and expect results only after ~150 posts","A burner TikTok/YouTube account that follows competitors helps you watch them daily","MEO can be outsourced fast. Even at large scale you only need a small team (around 4 people), with most of them on marketing","You don't need SOPs on day one, but set them up early. You'll be glad you did when the store gets big"],"topics":["Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_5IlwoO5vQQAZ1I/app/courses/cors_uxsGaAxG2Lnsz/lessons/lesn_itP2CwQUITQKc/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"WHAT IS RINGCHAIN?","where":"MEO","summary":"A short intro to ringchaining (RCH) in MEO.","points":["Ringchaining means connecting all your MEO stores together so they work like one agency","Use the SOPs from each store so the connected stores line up and stay in the same or related niches","The next videos cover different ways to ringchain. They may seem varied, but they all come back to linking your stores together"],"topics":["Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_5IlwoO5vQQAZ1I/app/courses/cors_uxsGaAxG2Lnsz/lessons/lesn_RGyBV31BT1K9z/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Ringchaining (RCH) Pt.1 - SOPs","where":"MEO","summary":"Part 1 of ringchaining your store: documenting everything with SOPs so the store doesn't depend on you and can be handed off, scaled or sold.","points":["Ringchaining is a full system for documenting how your store works. A store that depends on you is hard to scale and hard to sell","SOPs are the core: write down exactly what's working so you can hire specialists to run each part","Track marketing (posting schedules, campaigns, ad creatives that worked), email systems (templates, flows, what brings customers back) and anything else driving sales. Buyers want to see what worked from the start","Process: sort tasks into categories (marketing, ads, email, customer service, inventory), write step-by-step SOPs for every task, hire category specialists, then monitor and refine","Good SOPs are clear, include screenshots or screen recordings, set realistic timelines and include troubleshooting tips for common issues","Avoid being vague, assuming people already know things, leaving out examples, or making procedures messy","Example categories: social media, inventory/reordering, email and customer inquiries, customer service and complaint handling, and day-to-day maintenance","Hiring: write clear role descriptions, test candidates (or a friend) on the SOP first, choose people who follow instructions well (e.g. via LinkedIn or Fiverr), and consider percentage-based pay to keep them invested"],"topics":["Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_5IlwoO5vQQAZ1I/app/courses/cors_uxsGaAxG2Lnsz/lessons/lesn_MUd61fHZB3aVY/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Ringchaining (RCH) Pt.2 - Email Templates","where":"MEO","summary":"Part 2 of ringchaining: using email templates to cross-promote your other MEO stores to the email list you've built.","points":["As one store builds its email list (whether you or an outsourced marketer/copywriter runs it), use those emails to also promote your other stores","Email layout: put 2 best-sellers at the top, then about 3 more products from the same store below","At the very bottom, feature about 3 products from another one of your stores in the same or a related niche (e.g. a face roller under a self-care humidifier email)","Make it clear those products are on a different website, e.g. a \"featured from our other stores\" section","Keep each store focused on one niche so it becomes known for that product, like a brand, instead of a do-everything store","Cross-promoted products must fit the same audience. Unrelated products hurt conversion and waste emails","The goal is that when customers think of that niche (e.g. self-care around Black Friday), they think of your stores","Building separate stores in related niches, instead of one big store, usually adds up to more value when you sell them. Use exit profits to fund and scale the next store"],"topics":["Scaling & MEO","Email & SMS"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_5IlwoO5vQQAZ1I/app/courses/cors_uxsGaAxG2Lnsz/lessons/lesn_3MorWZO0ihBzm/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Selling Your Store #1 (How to Exit)","where":"MEO","summary":"How to fully exit (sell) an e-commerce store: when to sell, how stores are valued, how to prepare and how the sale process works. (General education, not legal or financial advice.)","points":["Don't rush to sell as soon as a store turns a profit. Letting an outsourced team keep growing it (like rental property building equity) can make it worth far more later","Reasons to sell: personal (burnout, new opportunities) or strategic (growth has plateaued, cash out at peak value, no resources to scale further)","Valuation methods: SDE multiple (common for small businesses), EBITDA multiple (larger businesses) and revenue multiple (high-growth companies)","What raises your price: consistent revenue growth, strong profit margins, traffic from many sources, high repeat-customer rate, documented systems and automations, and clean financial records","Be careful about revealing your systems too early to potential buyers, since some may just want to copy them","Prep timeline (6–12 months): organize financials, document SOPs, optimize growth and reduce reliance on you, get a valuation, review legal/trademark items, then list with a broker or marketplace","Selling options: business brokers (often for stores worth $500K+, fee around 10–15%), online marketplaces, or reaching out directly to competitors and strategic buyers","Buyers will check financials (tax returns, statements), traffic and marketing data, operations/suppliers, and legal ownership. Keep your taxes and records in order","Deal terms to understand: cash at close, seller financing, earnouts, non-compete clauses and transition support","Mistakes that kill sales: poor record keeping, overpricing, selling during a downturn, and a business that depends too much on the owner"],"topics":["Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_5IlwoO5vQQAZ1I/app/courses/cors_uxsGaAxG2Lnsz/lessons/lesn_ZaJ6Cdms2cNqC/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"The best way to exit","where":"MEO","summary":"The easiest way to exit (sell) your store: listing it on Flippa and working with a business broker.","points":["Flippa is a marketplace for selling online businesses, mostly e-commerce stores (plus SaaS), with listings up to millions of dollars","Use \"Meet your brokers\" to schedule a call with a broker who can help sell your store","A broker is the middleman between you and the buyer: they find the buyer willing to pay the most and handle much of the paperwork, like a CPA handles your taxes","Brokers usually charge a percentage of the sale (sometimes a flat fee), depending on their experience and the size of the deal. That small cut saves you a lot of time","Why sell? To fund more stores: use the cash to hire people, start and scale new stores faster, then sell those for even more","If a store has long-term brand potential, it can be worth holding. If it's a plain dropshipping store, selling and reinvesting can make more sense"],"topics":["Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_5IlwoO5vQQAZ1I/app/courses/cors_uxsGaAxG2Lnsz/lessons/lesn_sBuzQsYwSUdrv/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Prep to SELL","where":"MEO","summary":"How to prepare an MEO store to sell for the highest price, and what buyers look for. (General education, not legal or financial advice.)","points":["In MEO the big money is often in selling the store. Profitable stores commonly sell for around 2–5x annual profit (EBITDA)","Start preparing 12–24 months before listing (or longer). You keep earning from the store while you build its value","What buyers value: consistent revenue over 1–3 years, customers from many channels, strong repeat-purchase rates, a good reputation, quality products and solid long-term supplier relationships","Keep clean financials: P&L statements, balance sheet (inventory, cash, liabilities), tax returns and cash-flow records. Talk to a CPA","Make the business run without you: document SOPs for inventory, customer service, marketing, fulfillment and reporting","Targets: ~25–40% yearly revenue growth, 15%+ repeat customers, 15–25% net margin, no single traffic channel over ~40%, and no single customer over ~15% of revenue","Cut unnecessary expenses, negotiate good supplier terms and stay on good terms with suppliers","Clear up deal breakers: trademark/IP ownership, over-reliance on one platform, legal and tax compliance, website accessibility","Exit team: an e-commerce business broker (often 10–15% commission, but can raise the sale price), an attorney for contracts, and a CPA","Sale process: pre-marketing (valuation, asking price, target buyers), marketing (inquiries and NDAs), buyer due diligence, closing (2–4 weeks) and 30–90 days of transition support","Don't rush the exit. Focus on fundamentals and removing yourself from day-to-day operations"],"topics":["Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_5IlwoO5vQQAZ1I/app/courses/cors_uxsGaAxG2Lnsz/lessons/lesn_acqtBjIUFSCPN/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Why this store makes so much","where":"MEO","summary":"A teardown of a successful skincare store (reportedly doing 100K+/month), showing why its website converts so well.","points":["Top banner: a sale offer (extra 20% off bundles) tied to subscribing, so the discount also collects contacts","A \"science behind it\" page builds trust, which matters especially for supplements and skincare","Results and testimonials (customer reviews plus clinical data) take up most of the page. The product itself only takes a small part","Clearly shows what the product does and how it makes the customer's life better in simple steps","The About page tells a story (nature plus science) to connect emotionally, and plant/nature imagery makes the brand feel natural and trustworthy","A money-back results guarantee adds security for new buyers. The video suggests adding one once your store is established","A focused product range instead of hundreds of products. Branded stores should start with just 1–3","Professional product images (possibly AI-enhanced) and gift bundles round out the store","Use stores like this for inspiration on structure, not to copy them"],"topics":["Scaling & MEO","Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_5IlwoO5vQQAZ1I/app/courses/cors_uxsGaAxG2Lnsz/lessons/lesn_6UGXBNpDEynMr/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Let us know!","where":"MEO","summary":"Please do this form if you have modules u want us to do! https://5nmfitdbxyy.typeform.com/to/Td6q3SgZ","points":[],"topics":["Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_5IlwoO5vQQAZ1I/app/courses/cors_uxsGaAxG2Lnsz/lessons/lesn_RZMRkLaKSYY7F/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Start and Listen Here","where":"MEO · Logistics","summary":"What MEO is and what you need before starting this section.","points":["A basic overview of MEO","Master the e-commerce basics first, before advanced strategies like ringchaining","Ideally scale your first store (or be able to explain the process simply) before watching","These videos are short and advanced, with no beginner material","Be ready to apply everything you learn directly to your store"],"topics":["Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_5IlwoO5vQQAZ1I/app/courses/cors_pdLO1Mu9YYY6D/lessons/lesn_NiHkykh44VLXx/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"The Criteria Before Starting","where":"MEO · Logistics","summary":"What you need before starting the MEO section, and why the course teaches regular ecommerce first.","points":["The dropshipping and clothing brand modules already teach MEO-ready habits on one store","Before MEO you should understand systems, marketing and how to be profitable","Jumping straight into 3 stores at once is like throwing a kid into an NFL game","The MEO modules are shorter and direct: ringchaining, POK/SOPs and exiting; they just connect the pieces","Like a house: the foundation (regular ecom) takes longest; furnishing it (MEO) is faster"],"topics":["Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_5IlwoO5vQQAZ1I/app/courses/cors_pdLO1Mu9YYY6D/lessons/lesn_tdLAw0PDBb6yw/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Actually Understanding MEO","where":"MEO · Logistics","summary":"What MEO (multiple ecom operations) really is: treating online stores like real estate you build, outsource or sell.","points":["Build a store up, then outsource the daily work and focus on scaling a portfolio, not managing one store","Ecom as real estate: build a digital asset, raise its profit, then \"rent\" it to operators or sell it","Hire specialists (customer service, marketing, logistics); Elon Musk's skill is hiring, not doing everything","Repeatable systems: a winning product and ad can launch a second store with the same contacts","Two models: outsourcing (keep ownership, collect profit like a landlord) or flipping (sell for ~30–40x monthly profit)","Process: profitable niche → professional store → traffic → optimize products and suppliers → write SOPs → hire a team → find buyers","Key skills: niches, systems, team management, analytics. Hire on Upwork, FreeUp or Fiverr; use 3–4 products in one evergreen, high-margin niche","Start with one store; don't open several at once"],"topics":["Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_5IlwoO5vQQAZ1I/app/courses/cors_pdLO1Mu9YYY6D/lessons/lesn_GfQsWc2smaU6l/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Contacts for MEO","where":"MEO · The MEO Sauce","summary":"Why collecting contacts is the core of MEO, and how to grow and use your email and SMS lists across stores.","points":["Email returns ~$42 per $1 spent; SMS gets ~98% open rates; most visitors won't buy the first time, so follow up","Use exit-intent pop-ups (10–15% off or free shipping), pre-checked SMS opt-ins at checkout, spin-to-win and free guides or size charts","Cross-store programs: sign up for Store B's list and get perks at both; 90% of one store's buyers should hear about your related stores","Invite buyers to SMS order updates within 24 hours of purchase; aim for 2–3% conversion and ~15% monthly list growth (SMS opt-ins are only ~0.5–1%)","Tag subscribers by behavior and cross-promote only related niches (e.g. streetwear → sneaker crease protectors; protein → fitness apparel)","Build separate stores rather than adding everything to one; two $10K/month stores are worth more at exit","Hire one ecommerce copywriter for all stores (often on percentage) who knows Klaviyo and reads analytics","Roadmap: ~5,000 contacts per store in months 1–5 → automations (welcome, abandoned cart, post-purchase) → cross-promote and hire a manager past 30K contacts → a small team by months 10–12"],"topics":["Scaling & MEO","Email & SMS"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_5IlwoO5vQQAZ1I/app/courses/cors_WIt01Sj3cNGgJ/lessons/lesn_O17SArpDUL8fA/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Choosing a Good Exit Niche","where":"MEO · The MEO Sauce","summary":"How to pick a niche with a strong exit, using Shop Hunter to study stores that are printing right now.","points":["Women's beauty is a top niche: it grows stores fast and exits well (\"beauty is women's version of cars\"); men's niches can have more longevity","Use Shop Hunter to see competitors' daily/weekly sales, ad counts, and when the store and product were created","Examples: an eye-care product doing ~$77K/week; a plain gray hoodie with 3 ads doing ~$2K/week; briefs doing $2K/week with zero ads","Many winning stores were created years earlier (2017–2018) and only now pop off; contact lists drive 65–75% of their revenue","It usually takes ~3 visits before someone trusts a store; keep collecting contacts and following up","Products that look new and unsaturated outsell basic ones (one did $39K/week vs. $2K/week)","MEO is a longevity game; a steady store can sell for hundreds of thousands"],"topics":["Scaling & MEO","Product research"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_5IlwoO5vQQAZ1I/app/courses/cors_WIt01Sj3cNGgJ/lessons/lesn_Nq7M4KidRpR38/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Selling Lesson 1","where":"MEO · The MEO Sauce","summary":"Lesson 1 on selling your store: when to exit, how stores are valued, and what buyers check.","points":["Don't rush to sell when it first turns profitable; have operators build equity (like tenants paying off a house) and sell later for more","Sell at peak value, when growth flattens, not to cover burnout","Valuation methods: SDE × 2–4 (small businesses), EBITDA × 3–6, or revenue × 0.5–2 for high-growth stores","What raises your price: steady growth, ~20%+ net margins, many traffic sources, repeat customers 30%+, SOPs and automations, clean financial records","Be careful sharing your automations and systems with \"buyers\" who may just want to copy them","Prep timeline: 6–12 months for financials and SOPs → growth optimization → valuation and legal → go to market via marketplace, broker (~10–15% fee, best for $500K+) or direct outreach to competitors","Due diligence: 2–3 years of financials or tax returns, CAC/LTV, traffic sources, supplier capacity, legal/IP. Deal terms: cash at close, seller financing, earn-outs, non-competes, transition support","Mistakes: messy records, overvaluing, selling in a downturn, the business depending on you, hiding problems, rushing"],"topics":["Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_5IlwoO5vQQAZ1I/app/courses/cors_WIt01Sj3cNGgJ/lessons/lesn_YmgWNhM9JxUU7/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"The Best Way to Exit a Store","where":"MEO · The MEO Sauce","summary":"How to sell (exit) your store through Flippa and a broker.","points":["Flippa is a marketplace where ecommerce stores (and SaaS) are listed and sold, some for $1.5M+","Click \"Meet your brokers\" and book a call; the broker is the middleman who finds the highest-paying buyer and handles paperwork","Brokers usually take a percentage (sometimes a flat fee), worth it for the time saved","Why sell: e.g. sell a $5K/month store for ~$300K, then use the cash to hire, open new stores or get a warehouse","Keep a branded store with long-term potential; sell unbranded dropshipping stores to scale faster"],"topics":["Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_5IlwoO5vQQAZ1I/app/courses/cors_WIt01Sj3cNGgJ/lessons/lesn_cdqxi1o86EWSO/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Preparing to Exit","where":"MEO · The MEO Sauce","summary":"How to prepare an MEO store for a top-dollar exit over 12–24 months.","points":["The real money is in selling the business; ecom stores typically sell for 2–5x annual EBITDA (up to ~7x)","Know your \"why\" for selling, e.g. to fund a new store in a better season","Buyers pay for consistent revenue over 1–3 years, diverse traffic (no channel above ~35–40%), repeat customers (15–25%+), good supplier relationships and clean books","A rare private supplier with great pricing can add tens of thousands to the price","Keep P&L statements, balance sheets, tax returns and cash-flow statements; write SOPs for every process","Build a business that runs without you (75/25 rule if your personal brand is involved)","Targets: 25–40% yearly growth, 15–25% net margin, cut unnecessary expenses; no single customer over ~15% of revenue; reduce platform dependency","Exit team: an ecommerce broker (10–15% fee but often 20–40% higher price), an attorney and a CPA. Process: pre-market → NDAs + inquiries → due diligence → close in 2–4 weeks → 30–90 days of transition support"],"topics":["Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_5IlwoO5vQQAZ1I/app/courses/cors_WIt01Sj3cNGgJ/lessons/lesn_uIXWlQO63c3it/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"What is Ringchaining?","where":"MEO · The MEO Sauce","summary":"A quick intro to ringchaining: connecting all your MEO stores together.","points":["Ringchaining means linking your stores so they work together like an agency","Use each store's SOPs so stores in the same niche support each other","The next videos show different ways to ringchain"],"topics":["Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_5IlwoO5vQQAZ1I/app/courses/cors_WIt01Sj3cNGgJ/lessons/lesn_WrW2g1PshJOqA/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Ringchaining (RCH) Pt.1 - SOPs","where":"MEO · The MEO Sauce","summary":"Ringchaining part 1: documenting your store in SOPs so specialists can run it and you can scale to more stores.","points":["A store that depends on you is hard to scale and hard to sell","Write down what works: marketing (posting schedules, ad templates, winning creatives, pop-up shops), email flows and retention tactics, customer service rules, inventory reorders","Organize SOPs by category (e.g. marketing, with ads as its own sub-category)","Make SOPs clear with screenshots or screen recordings, realistic timelines and troubleshooting tips; don't be vague or assume knowledge","Big brands don't have the CEO running TikTok; specialists follow SOPs","Hire specialists by category (LinkedIn, Fiverr, friends), test them on a sample task, prefer quick learners, and pay on percentage so they care","Monitor and refine your SOPs before handing them off"],"topics":["Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_5IlwoO5vQQAZ1I/app/courses/cors_WIt01Sj3cNGgJ/lessons/lesn_fIvcWUCx05O96/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Ringchaining (RCH) Pt.2 - Email Templates","where":"MEO · The MEO Sauce","summary":"Ringchaining part 2: using email templates to cross-promote your other stores in the same niche.","points":["Have your copywriter promote your other stores, not just the one they work on","Email layout: 2 best sellers at the top, ~3 more products below, all from this store","At the bottom, 3 products from a ringchained store in the same niche (e.g. humidifier buyers → face humidifier or face roller), labeled \"from our partner stores\" to borrow trust","Each store should be known for one thing; don't become an Amazon-style everything store, and never cross-promote unrelated niches","Customers who like one of your stores will trust the others, so at Black Friday all your stores are top of mind","Separate stores sell for more as separate assets than one combined store (e.g. $150K + $250K vs. $150K)","Sell stores to fund the next one, rinse and repeat until stores profit $40–50K+ a month"],"topics":["Scaling & MEO","Product research","Email & SMS"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_5IlwoO5vQQAZ1I/app/courses/cors_WIt01Sj3cNGgJ/lessons/lesn_fflczp3Ntnr9t/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to POK (SOPs for Your Store)","where":"MEO · The MEO Sauce","summary":"How to \"POK\" your store: write SOPs (standard operating procedures) for everything that works, so others can run it.","points":["Keep an SOP index with IDs (e.g. SOP-002), department, version (1.0, 2.0…), status (active, draft, deactivated) and review cycle","Review cycles: product research monthly (TikTok, Meta Ad Library), suppliers quarterly, store setup quarterly (holidays), paid ads weekly, support and fulfillment monthly","Each SOP explains why it exists, what success looks like (not just money), the tools and access levels, and when it's used","Everyone using your SOPs must sign a contract not to share them or take over the product or accounts","Example marketing steps: order a sample, set up all platforms, replicate 2–3 competitor videos a day; don't expect results until ~150 posts","With MEO you can have things outsourced within ~2 months; even at $1M/month you need about 4 people"],"topics":["Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_5IlwoO5vQQAZ1I/app/courses/cors_WIt01Sj3cNGgJ/lessons/lesn_EqAClZCGBn9fs/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Why this Store Makes so Much","where":"MEO · The MEO Sauce","summary":"Breaking down a skincare/supplement store likely doing $100K+/month: what it does right on its site.","points":["Top banner: \"Extra 20% off bundles when you subscribe\"; it trades a discount for contacts, not just a sale","A \"science\" page builds trust, which is essential for supplements and skincare","Results and testimonials (including measurable data) take up more of the page than the product itself","Clearly show what it does in simple steps and why it makes life better","Emotional About page and nature imagery (plants = natural, safe)","Money-back guarantee, added once they had loyal repeat customers, not at the start","A small, focused product range plus bundles; product photos likely AI-enhanced from simple shots"],"topics":["Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_5IlwoO5vQQAZ1I/app/courses/cors_WIt01Sj3cNGgJ/lessons/lesn_aFp2XNmD3L29Z/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Store & Content Feedback Q&A","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"A live Q&A where members get feedback on their stores and content.","points":["Store and bio review: Stacking too many discounts (50% off + 10% off + free shipping) looks desperate or scammy. Rewrite the bio around building a fan base in your niche, and keep your videos focused on one clear message about what the product does.","Website help: Either watch the full website-building tutorial in the group or have the admins build it for you.","Ads: Use TikTok for younger audiences, Facebook/Instagram for older ones, and both for unisex products. Start at $5-10/day, not $100. Test several creatives, find the winner, then rebuild a new ad around it and target the audience that converted.","Suppliers vs. winning products: Winning products are niche-specific research done for you. A supplier covers many categories and matters most for clothing, where upfront stock costs money.","Ad creative: Video of a real person using the product works best because it answers buyer doubts before they reach your site.","Targeting on TikTok: Captions and hashtags like \"romantic gift\" pull in women. If you're selling to men, write hooks aimed at them, like \"imagine your girl's reaction when you get her this.\"","Mindset: Feeling embarrassed when people you know see your early content is normal. Keep posting, because once the views and sales come, that changes."],"topics":["Calls","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_vTLiucDBHVkhw/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Q&A: Scaling Ads & Building Your First Store","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"An open Q&A on scaling ads, marketing, and building your first store.","points":["Scaling a winning ad: Don't jump your budget 10x overnight, because reach and quality drop. Duplicate the ad (or make a similar version) aimed at the same audience, then raise budgets gradually.","Pricing comes from marketing: Brands like Balenciaga can charge more because of their marketing. The more people see and relate to your product, the higher you can price it. Market everywhere you can.","Where to advertise: Focus paid ads on Meta and TikTok first. Facebook reaches an older audience. Pinterest and Snapchat can come later.","Organic content: Keep it raw and relatable (phone footage, not pro cameras) so viewers can picture themselves using it. Use viewer comments as ideas for your next video. People buy the feeling and identity behind a product, especially with clothing.","Finding your niche: Ask how the product helps the customer (e.g. blue-light glasses help sleep, so the niche is health and wellness).","Clothing tips: Start with regular screen print. Rhinestones and puff print can work once you find a quality supplier, and add care instructions to the product description.","Reviews and trust: Add reviews to your store, and use a Shopify app to automatically request reviews 1-2 weeks after delivery (never before the customer has the product).","Other questions: You don't need to show your face; showing the product in your hands works. Reposting other people's videos is risky, so stitch them or edit them into your own. Under-18 sellers may need a parent or guardian on the account, and supplements need legal/FDA checks first."],"topics":["Calls","Paid ads","Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_ZcNeKUlvJK5Gt/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Why Your Contact List (CRM) Matters (Call)","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"A short Q&A, then a key lesson on why collecting customer contacts (your CRM) matters more than one-off sales. The main teaching starts around the halfway point.","points":["Biggest beginner mistakes: Rushing into testing and samples without thinking it through, not getting a mentor, and not taking time to learn real skills on your first store.","Store setup questions: You can use multiple suppliers on one Shopify store (e.g. AutoDS and Zendrop). Order samples to check quality. Connect a GoDaddy domain in your site builder's settings, or buy one directly to skip the DNS setup. Use Canva or Photoshop for labels.","Content without the product in hand: Use supplier videos, ask the supplier for custom footage, or edit existing creator videos. Watch every module and take notes so you know the roadmap.","Getting add-to-carts but no sales: That's a good sign. Collect contact info so you can follow up later. Run a big limited offer (e.g. \"35% off for the next 10 buyers\") and raise the base price to keep the same margin. Test two or three new creatives on the product that's working.","The funnel: All marketing (ads, organic, UGC) should send people to your landing page or store. The first thing they see should be a pop-up offering a discount in exchange for their email or phone number.","Why CRM is gold: An email/SMS list keeps paying. Even a 0.1% conversion rate on 10,000-100,000 contacts can mean thousands per email. It's easier to get an existing customer to reorder than to find new ones, and a store with a big contact list sells for much more.","Next steps: Set up welcome flows and holiday campaigns in Klaviyo (free up to 250 contacts) or Shopify, and look for every way to collect contacts in your marketing."],"topics":["Calls","Email & SMS"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_EB8NroH3ACnvM/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Extra Questions: Product Research, Ad Budgets & Store Setup","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"A relaxed extra-questions call covering product research, ad budgets, store setup, and when a product has run its course.","points":["Branded samples: If a supplier's sample shows another company's branding, look for an unbranded version of the same product.","Marketing a product you don't have yet: Order a sample and film it yourself, send it to a creator, or stitch and edit an existing video.","Product research: Scroll TikTok (e.g. #TikTokMadeMeBuyIt) and track products in a spreadsheet. Look for strong engagement and what people say in the comments, and check competing stores' sales on Shop Hunter. If others already sell it, don't avoid it; just market it harder.","Start with organic: Post organically first to learn who your target audience is, then add paid ads on TikTok, Meta, YouTube, or Pinterest.","Ad budgets: Ignore the \"$50/day minimum\" advice when you're new. Start around $5/day so the platform trusts your account and finds the right audience, then step up gradually ($10, $15, $20, $50 and beyond).","One product vs. several: Market your single strongest product the hardest, but stock a few related products in your niche so visitors can buy more than one thing.","When a product is \"dead\": When it becomes much harder to get traction and views than it used to be.","Launching your store: Don't rush it. Make sure it looks professional, has a pop-up, and uses a real domain. .store, .shop, or .com all work; avoid anything that looks off, like .org.","Prep work: Collect your store information now so you're ready when the team builds your site. Ask your personal coach for help with store building and winning products."],"topics":["Calls","Product research"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_jTug0bowAiWz8/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"New Strats: Bundles, Scalable Products & Shipping","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"A Presidents' Day extra-questions call on bundles, picking scalable products, shipping times, and how organic content supports sales.","points":["Bundles: People only use bundles when the discount is meaningful. If nobody's choosing yours, try roughly doubling the discount.","Pick products you can scale: Think about scaling before you choose a product, not after. Look for things people need and buy repeatedly (beauty, health, supplements, fitness), because those work with subscription models. A \"want\" like a water gun is much harder to build on. Give a product 2-3 months before switching.","Organic posts with no comments: That's normal early on, but make videos people can interact with: better captions, questions, or giveaways like \"tag a friend to win this.\"","Shipping times: If your supplier ships fast (e.g. 3-5 days from California), advertise it on your product page. Show a delivery range like \"3-5 days\" instead of exact dates. Slower overseas shipping is fine if you're upfront about it and send an order confirmation email.","Pre-orders (clothing): Make sure your supplier is reliable and you know the process first. Customers will email constantly if orders are late, so order samples and test everything.","Reusing supplier videos: Fine on your website, but for organic posts, edit them (crop, new sound, different edits) so they don't look copied.","Suppliers: Amazon works but cuts into your margins. Zendrop, AutoDS, or AliExpress are usually better. If a product won't import into Shopify, try a different supplier or product to find the issue.","What organic is for: It's mainly to learn your audience and build trust, not direct sales. People often check your social pages after seeing an ad, so consistent posts act as social proof and can help your paid ads convert."],"topics":["Calls","Organic marketing","Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_NQ4Ackg15UMET/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Ad Creative Call: Modeling Competitor Ads","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"How to model your ads on competitors, come up with new video ideas, and why marketing matters more than price. It also covers the MEO idea of building stores to sell.","points":["Research competitor ads: Search your product in the Facebook Ad Library and on Shop Hunter to see who sells it and how they advertise. Copy what works, either with the same product or a similar one. Don't try to reinvent the wheel.","Choosing your first ad: Either run your best-performing organic video as an ad, or make a new ad modeled on competitors' ads.","Fresh video ideas: Show different reasons and situations for using the product (after football practice, after work, back to school, college), each aimed at a different type of buyer.","Organic before ads: The coach posted organically for 6-7 months before running ads. Familiarity means people recognize the product later, so you spend less to get each sale.","Don't race to the bottom on price: Even if TikTok Shop sells something cheaper, strong marketing and branding let you charge more. Most of the modules focus on marketing for this reason.","Physical stores and franchises: Not recommended for beginners. A physical store limits your reach and adds rent, theft, and maintenance costs. Franchises need a lot of money upfront.","The MEO model: Like flipping real estate, build up a store's value (sales, brand, customer list) so you can sell it for far more than years of product profits, then reinvest.","When to switch products: Only if, after a few months, people show no interest (no likes or comments). If one product works, add more. Keep 3-4 products on your store.","Other notes: Use social proof like athlete reviews on your homepage, stay consistent while your business compounds, and cut out distractions like gaming and scrolling."],"topics":["Calls","Product research","Paid ads","Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_fS0mGHWAqT2e8/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"MISTAKES NOT TO MAKE CALL!","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"The three biggest store mistakes to avoid, plus how to know when you're ready to switch from organic to paid ads.","points":["Mistake 1, no custom domain: A URL like yourstore.myshopify.com looks like a scam and hurts conversions, especially in emails and texts. Buy a cheap domain on GoDaddy or Shopify.","Mistake 2, obsessing over the website: A beautiful store with no visitors makes nothing. Unlike a restaurant, an online store gets no foot traffic, so most of your effort should go into marketing (organic and paid). The broader your reach, the more buyers you'll find.","Mistake 3, too many products: Thousands of products overwhelm shoppers (like a huge restaurant menu). Start with around 10 or fewer, and feature one product per email.","Pop-ups and announcement bars: Offer a strong discount (e.g. 20% off, with prices raised to match) in exchange for an email, and keep an announcement bar as a reminder. Emails get more sends; texts convert better but can feel pushy.","First store not working? It's usually the product, not the store. Swap in a new product and rerun ads before starting over.","When to start paid ads: The product gets some organic traction (2,000-5,000 views), it's something people need or can be marketed that way, and you know your audience from looking at who comments.","Samples: Order samples of your 2-3 strongest products (check supplier order counts and reviews) to see the product firsthand and film content.","Other Q&A: For clothing, one-color screen print is usually cheaper than DTG, and price well above cost (e.g. $70-80 on a $30 cost). Google Ads and SEO are better once you're bigger; focus on social media ads first."],"topics":["Calls","Organic marketing","Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_39iyxTu19sPBN/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Q&A: Getting Started the Right Way (+ Life Advice)","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"A long, open Q&A on getting started the right way. The second half turns into life advice about faith, friendships, and staying focused while you grind.","points":["Dropshipping vs. MEO: Dropshipping isn't a business model on its own; it's a fulfillment method (your supplier ships to the customer). A dropshipping store is already a form of MEO.","Getting started: Watch every module and take notes by category (marketing, sales, etc.) before building, so you have a clear plan and don't have to backtrack.","How many products: Five is the upper limit at launch. Start with 2-3, build your contact list, then release the rest. Connect related stores (e.g. supplements + workout gear + a nutrition app) so they promote each other.","Branding: For branded products, customize items from suppliers like Zendrop, AliExpress, or Alibaba with your logo. Pick a name first, then a logo that fits it, and use a bundle app for bundles.","Start posting now: You don't need a finished website to start posting organic content. Practice making videos, then use the best ones for ads.","Keep your store simple: You only need a supplier app and a CRM like Klaviyo (free up to 250 contacts) to start, plus Meta ads later. Add bundle apps and extras only after you're making sales (around $5K). Too many apps just add monthly costs.","Clothing drops: Start with pre-made stock (look for suppliers with low minimum orders of 5-10 pieces) so early customers get fast shipping and trust you. Save pre-orders for when you're bigger.","Scaling up: Once a product is proven, brand it. Use a warehouse only for branded products; unbranded products can stay on dropshipping at any size (Shein is a huge example).","Competitor ads: Remake winning competitor ads with about a 5% change (your product, your own twist). Run a $5/day test for 7 days before raising budgets.","Life advice: Keep God first, read the Bible daily (Genesis and Proverbs are good starting points), and surround yourself with people as hungry as you, including this community. Don't let a demanding job swallow the time you need to build. Plan your day in writing, take breaks and walks to avoid burnout, and use apps like Opal to block distracting social media."],"topics":["Calls","Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_1yeKb16sNNyjA/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Extra Questions: First Sales, Content & Clothing Brands","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"An extra-questions call on content volume, getting your first sales, product videos without samples, advertorials, ad platforms, and starting a clothing brand.","points":["Personal brand + clothing brand: Use your own face to launch, then shift to influencers and other people over time so the brand isn't tied only to you.","Quantity vs. quality: When starting out, post two quick videos a day instead of one polished one. Once videos take off, move to one high-quality video posted on every platform.","After importing products: Connect Zendrop or CJ Dropshipping (they auto-fulfill orders), import your products, then follow the marketing modules. Your store gets no traffic unless you market it, through organic posts or $5/day Facebook ads.","Videos without a sample: Use AI-generated video (organic only, not ads), send the product to a UGC creator, or ask your supplier for custom footage.","Advertorials and blogs: Start with ads that go straight to the product and show the problem it solves. Advertorials work better once you have winning ads, or for organic and SEO (e.g. a free blog post in your bio that mentions the product).","Market everywhere: Organic, paid ads, SEO, and offline all add up. Seeing your brand in more than one place builds trust.","Ad platforms: Meta (Facebook/Instagram) drives the most sales. TikTok boosts are for views and followers, so use TikTok's Ads Manager for sales. Snapchat works for products aimed at people under 25, and running ads on several platforms helps rather than hurts.","Supplier relationships: Be friendly and check in occasionally. Once you have order history, ask for lower prices or minimums.","Calls to action: Under 1,000 followers on TikTok, ask viewers to engage. On other platforms, point them to the product.","Clothing brand basics: Start with a meaning or values, then find a name that fits (AI can help brainstorm). Model photos work best for ads; mockups are fine for organic.","Other notes: Peptides and supplements need to be done the legally correct way (a module is coming). The coach isn't a CPA, so get tax advice from an accountant. Text your personal coach for quick questions and save calls for bigger reviews."],"topics":["Calls","Organic marketing","Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_UZwZHhYY3BJXJ/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Extra Questions: Store Builds, Posting Everywhere & Notes","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"An extra-questions call on getting your store built, posting on every platform, and why taking detailed notes on the modules matters.","points":["Store build by the agency: You only need to pick your niche, not a product. After the store is handed over, you add products yourself (Zendrop/CJ need your own accounts), and the niche can't be changed by the team. Use the step-by-step video if you want to build future stores yourself.","Build it yourself or pay? Decide whether your time is worth more than the cost. The agency's build is more professional on the back end.","One store first: Even if Shopify lets you open more, master one store before starting others.","Organic mindset: Don't fixate on how long until a video goes viral. Focus on getting better. When you plateau, find the video that did best and recreate it in a new way.","Post everywhere: Put the same video on TikTok, Instagram, YouTube Shorts, and more. It takes a few extra minutes and could go viral on a different platform. Mix product-focused videos with fun, engagement-driven ones.","Practical tips: Before 1,000 TikTok followers (no bio link), use \"follow for more\" and send people to Instagram. Blurry videos usually come from the camera or editing app, so try another editor like InShot. List realistic shipping times in your description; most customers don't mind a 1-2 week wait.","Legal questions (trademarks, etc.): Ask a business attorney; the coach can't give legal advice.","Starting capital: Begin with dropshipping so you don't tie money up in inventory, then move to a branded product with your own stock once you have capital.","Take notes: Write down everything from the modules, organized by topic (organic, Facebook ads, product research, etc.). Skipping details means rewatching later and missing things that matter once sales start.","Off-topic: A brief chat on starting with a smaller rental property and financing rather than paying cash (not financial advice), and why the coach avoids sports betting: business results are something you can control."],"topics":["Calls","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_1lm4ecjfww4s7/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Q&A: Taxes, Dropping Products & Out-Marketing Everyone","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"A short Q&A on taxes and when to drop a product, followed by a mindset talk on marketing harder than everyone else and why dropshipping isn't \"saturated.\"","points":["Taxes: Get a CPA (certified public accountant) early. The coach got one in year one. Write-offs vary by state, so ask your own accountant rather than relying on general advice.","When to drop a product: Not after a week. Give it at least a month and a half of consistent posting. Drop it only if nobody is engaging (no likes, follows, DMs, or questions) and ads send no one to your site. If people are engaging, keep going; strong marketing can sell almost anything.","Switching products in the same niche: Keep your old videos up; they make your account look more trustworthy.","Market like you mean it: An online store has no foot traffic, so post on every platform, every day. If you have time to scroll or game, you have time to post more. The coach posted up to 30 videos a day starting out, and \"creative block\" is mostly a mindset problem.","Business is like a war: Put in the hardest work at the start, before results show up, and it pays off for years. Don't wait for sales to start going hard.","\"Dropshipping is saturated\" is a cope: Dropshipping is just a fulfillment method (Uber and Airbnb are middlemen too). New products, trends, and strategies come out constantly, and having a mentor means you skip the mistakes that cost others time and money.","What's coming: A more active Discord with voice chat and regular winning-product drops, plus new in-app shops (Instagram, Snapchat, etc.) following TikTok Shop that should make buying from organic content much easier. Get good at organic now so you're ready.","Other notes: A Photoshop/Pixlr basics module was requested; Canva tutorials are already in the course."],"topics":["Calls","Mindset","Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_kziQSmj7Qdvz1/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Sales Info: Turning Visits Into Sales & Contacts","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"The end of a call on turning store visits into sales and contacts, then a talk on faith, balance, and staying consistent with daily posting.","points":["Visits but no sales: Add urgency and a clear call to action. Make sure your pop-up gives something in return for an email or phone number, like 10-25% off (raise the product price to match so you don't lose margin).","Why contacts matter: With a customer's contact info you can follow up on abandoned carts, win back people who didn't buy, and get past buyers to order again.","Hide the \"Shopify\" look: Buy a custom domain (GoDaddy or Shopify, about $1-2) so your URL shows your brand, not myshopify.com.","Faith and balance: The coach shares how keeping God first has carried him through uncertain times. Hard seasons often shape who you become, so aim to be strong in every area (spiritual, emotional, physical, business), not just one. Bible studies and a \"getting closer to God\" video are planned.","Staying consistent: Posting daily comes down to how badly you want it. Take Sundays off to avoid burnout. Reusing clips from your camera roll should keep content to about 15 minutes a day, less time than a Netflix episode.","Call times: Most calls run on Mountain Time, so convert to your time zone."],"topics":["Calls","Mindset","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_WYCKopbImIJf7/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to Join the Discord (Call)","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"The launch of the Inner Circle Discord, with step-by-step help joining it, plus a few quick store questions at the start.","points":["How to join: In Whop, go to Home and click \"Join the Discord,\" then connect your Discord account to claim access (it works on desktop; refresh if you see an error). Create a Discord account first if you don't have one, and add a profile picture so people know you're not a bot.","What's in the Discord: Announcements (new products and strategies), Introductions, a Questions channel for store and website reviews from the group, a Wins channel, a general Inner Circle chat (not just business), and a Networking voice channel where you can jump in anytime to work, share screens, or review each other's stores.","Why it matters: It's a safer space than open communities (no fake \"admins\" pitching website builds) and a 24/7 network of like-minded people. You might find a partner, someone who has already sold your type of product, or an organic marketer to work with on commission. Treat it like school and show up daily.","First steps: Introduce yourself (name, goals, what kind of store you're running, hobbies). You can still text your personal coach and the coach as usual.","Store questions: Find your products first and tell the team your niche for the build. Zendrop and CJ Dropshipping auto-fulfill orders; AliExpress needs manual ordering. If you don't have a store yet, you can still browse products in Zendrop before connecting.","While you wait for samples: Study competitor videos so you're ready to film when the product arrives.","Organic and paid together: Use what organic teaches you (audience, which videos work, how long to run them) to set up paid ads, starting around $5/day.","Digital products count too: Any online product, like a course, is e-commerce."],"topics":["Calls"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_iG5mgDt1bXhFe/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"5/22/26 - Seasonal Ads, Fresh Organic & Holiday Sales","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"Refreshing winning ads for the season, keeping organic videos fresh, framing products as problem-solvers, and cashing in on holidays like Father's Day.","points":["Seasonal ad refresh: If your ad mentions something out of season (like high school in summer), keep the same video but swap in a seasonal caption (pool, gym, summer with friends).","Duplicating winning ads: You can duplicate an ad after it finishes running. Raise the budget a little each time it keeps performing (e.g. $5, then $7-10).","Keep organic videos fresh: Don't repost the exact same clip. In CapCut, mirror the video, move the text, change the filter, or add a new intro so viewers and TikTok see it as new.","UGC creators: Ship them the product or a sample, and have them sign a contract first so they can't back out after receiving it.","Problem + solution: Every winning ad shows a problem and how your product solves it. Frame products as something people need (e.g. a breathable tee for hot summer days instead of \"a cool shirt\").","Ride the holidays: Father's Day is about a month away. Find dad-friendly products from your supplier and market them hard now. Keep your store built around a lasting niche so it keeps selling after the holiday.","Store settings: A promo code in the ad itself increases clicks. A \"no returns\" policy is fine starting out; just replace defective items. Black is usually the best-selling color, and adding pink can help attract women.","Shipping: Clothing suppliers should ship within 14 days, even on bulk orders. If yours is much slower, find another on Alibaba, or list the real shipping time on your site.","Platforms: TikTok works best for reach and paid Meta ads for sales. Snapchat Spotlight is a strong new channel for organic views. Check where your visitors come from under Sources in Shopify analytics.","Other notes: Listing on Amazon is possible (a module is coming), but you lose your customer contacts and pay high fees. Q1 is usually slower for e-commerce, and Q4 is the busiest."],"topics":["Calls","Organic marketing","Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_vBmkOBNfXzIHf/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Organic Marketing Deep Dive (Call)","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"A deep dive on organic marketing: posting everywhere, getting comments, using organic results to guide paid ads, and putting a new spin on competitors' products. Also covers TikTok Shop vs. your own store.","points":["Post on every platform: TikTok, Instagram Reels, YouTube Shorts, Snapchat, Facebook, even Pinterest. Organic is free, and each post is another \"lottery ticket\" that could go viral.","Get people commenting: Views alone won't push a video far. Give viewers something to react to, like a relatable hook (\"imagine having this in college\") or a video where the product appears naturally instead of a hard sell. Use calls to action like \"follow for more\" or \"releasing at 1,000 followers\" until you can add a bio link.","Collect contacts, not just sales: Gather emails and phone numbers from every visitor so you can market to them again. A good email list can bring in money for years.","Organic funds paid ads: Use organic analytics (age, location, active hours) to set up targeted Facebook or Pinterest ads. The coach's team puts about 20% of organic revenue back into ads. Don't jump straight to paid ads without knowing your audience.","Find products: Search #TikTokMadeMeBuyIt and filter by \"this week\" to see what's trending, then market the same product to a different audience or with a different angle.","The 3% change: Keep a proven product but rename or reframe it (e.g. an \"ice roller\" sold as an \"eye bag killer\") or improve it slightly. For clothing, take inspiration without copying designs.","Coach's examples: A $8 muscle stimulator resold for $50-60, and an ice roller that made around $2,000 from three short videos.","TikTok Shop vs. your own store: TikTok Shop is easier (no website or customer service) but you can only promote it on TikTok, payouts take about 15 days, and you can't collect customer contacts. Your own Shopify store pays out in 1-2 days and builds a long-term customer list."],"topics":["Calls","Product research","Organic marketing","Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_7uDzNksFttWzR/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"5/29/26 - Extra Questions: AI Tools, Meta Ads & Multiple Stores","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"An extra-questions call on AI content tools, Meta ad setup, running more than one store, product research tools, and selling on Amazon vs. your own store. It ends with a group discussion about the supplement and peptide market.","points":["AI content: Text-to-video tools like Sora, combined with ElevenLabs for voiceovers, can produce realistic organic videos when you don't have the product on hand yet.","Meta ads: The campaign setup in the modules is still current. One Meta ad runs on both Facebook and Instagram, and you can make different creatives for each platform's audience.","Running two stores: It's fine (e.g. a fitness store and a beauty store), especially if two people share the work. Apply what you learn from one store's ads (e.g. problem-solving videos beat plain photos) to the other.","Website builders: AI builders like Lovable only design the front end. Shopify still handles payments, checkout, and SEO.","Amazon/Walmart vs. your own store: Marketplace apps can list your products on Amazon and others for extra sales, but fees are high (about 20%), you often still need ads, and you don't get the customer contacts. A member who did six figures on Walmart said almost no customers came back. Your own store builds a customer list you can sell later.","Repeat customers: Health and wellness products people reorder (supplements, sleep aids, red-light therapy) beat one-time purchases.","Product research: The Facebook Ad Library shows what competitors are running and for how long; ads running for months usually mean a winning product. Zendrop shows how saturated a product is.","When to brand: Wait until you have a loyal customer base that keeps coming back through your emails, then switch from white-label to branded.","UGC and creators: For supplements, real people vouching for a product build trust. Use several smaller creators instead of one big one, offer commission, and follow up persistently across TikTok, Instagram, X, and email.","Peptides: Members discussed this fast-growing market. The coach is still researching it and flagged serious legal, payment-processing, and health risks. Do your homework and get proper legal advice before going near it.","Klaviyo SMS module: Now available. Check the Discord announcements."],"topics":["Calls","Product research","Organic marketing","Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_v8283oe3nggO6/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"SYSTEMS!! (CRM & Email Masterclass)","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"A masterclass on the \"systems\" behind a profitable store, mainly your CRM (customer relationship management) and email strategy.","points":["Marketing beats website polish: Plenty of stores with average websites make $100K/month because of their marketing. Before tweaking your site, ask yourself whether you did all your marketing for the day.","A decent website vs. the right systems: A decent site just looks good. Systems are what happens after someone visits: how they get into your CRM, what offer gets them there, and what they receive afterward.","CRM means relationship: Collecting emails isn't enough. Send a welcome email when people sign up and a thank-you email after they order. Treat customers like people, not just sales, the way a friendly store keeps you coming back.","Make an offer they can't refuse: Sign-up rates depend on the offer. A newsletter gets maybe 1%; a big discount gets far more. Raise the product price to match so a 30% offer doesn't cut your margin.","Customer service is part of CRM: Send tracking numbers and updates (like Amazon does). If an order will be late, contact the customer, and consider labeling slow-shipping items as pre-order.","Email plan: Set up one automated welcome email, then send scheduled emails to your whole list about four times a week. Write them all on Saturday and schedule them in Klaviyo. Avoid stacking extra automations so people don't get duplicate emails.","Email tips: Change subject lines every time, personalize to your audience's interests, and tie emails to seasons or trends. Subscribe to competitors' lists with a spare email for ideas, and use AI to draft emails in your brand's voice.","Email vs. SMS: Start with email to build familiarity. Add SMS once customers know your brand, since unexpected texts feel spammy.","Promote your discount: Mention the sign-up discount in video captions. On TikTok without a bio link, send people to your Instagram to order.","Challenge: Grow in every area, not just money. Work out or read the Bible after the call."],"topics":["Calls","Email & SMS"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_m10pum8OBuxkx/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"6/26/26 - Masterclass: Ad Creative That Converts","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"A masterclass on ad creative: why your ads need to show the problem your product solves, awareness vs. sales campaigns, and getting content from UGC creators.","points":["Show the problem and solution: A plain product photo rarely sells unless you're a big brand like Nike. Show how the product makes life better, either by showing the problem and the fix or by showing the fix in action (e.g. \"Workouts not working? Add this protein after the gym\").","Awareness vs. sales: Product photos and logos work for awareness campaigns, like teasing a launch date. Sales campaigns need real selling tactics: solve a problem and add an offer like \"10% off for the next 10 buyers.\"","Creative is everything: The video people see first matters more than your title or description. If it doesn't grab attention or show how the product works, they'll scroll past.","How to get creatives: Order a sample and spend an hour making one strong ad that can run for months. Or pay UGC creators (often about $10 per video plus the product) or friends to film it. Script it yourself or with AI.","AI ads: Use them only if they look completely real (e.g. Higgsfield). Fake-looking AI ads make people think it's a scam.","Working with creators: Start with small creators (around 1,000 followers) who are more likely to reply and charge less. Ten small creators posting regularly beat one expensive celebrity post. Free products or promo codes can work too, and happy customers often post for free.","Every product solves something: Even a basic hoodie can be framed as a need (stay warm and look good). Use AI to brainstorm angles if you're stuck.","Getting started: Create a new Meta business account to run ads, even with nothing posted on Facebook. Watch the Welcome and Mindset sections, then the beginner school, while you wait for your coach.","Troubleshooting: If your Klaviyo pop-up isn't showing, check the timer, test in a different browser, republish in Shopify, or contact Klaviyo support."],"topics":["Calls","Organic marketing","Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_qjUgqw6lc8ppZ/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"6/30/26 - Extra Questions: Email Setup, Ad Budgets & Suppliers","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"An extra-questions call on store settings, email setup and deliverability, running winning ads longer, ad budgets, picking products, and clothing suppliers.","points":["Away from your store: If you can't ship for a while, update the product description with the real shipping timeline. Shopify doesn't force you to fulfill within a few days.","Seasonal niches: Car products like all-season floor mats can work if you target buyers in regions with real seasons. Long-term, a store that covers every season lasts longer than one tied to summer only.","Business email: Set up a branded email through your domain provider (e.g. GoDaddy) and connect the DNS records. If your campaign emails land in junk, check that your domain and DNS are connected correctly, and ask Shopify support for help.","Reviews: Only use reviews for the exact same product. Ask your supplier which brands have sold it, or get UGC creators and friends to review it. Three to five reviews on the homepage is a good start.","Klaviyo flows: Set up a welcome email (the most important one), abandoned cart, browse abandonment, and a post-purchase flow. AI can walk you through building custom flows.","Winning ads: If an ad keeps getting add-to-carts and good click-through, keep running it and raise the budget a little. Duplicate it to test new audiences, or make a similar version for the same audience. Around 30 seconds is a good length.","Ad budget: Start at $5/day; about $100 in an ad account covers a week of tests. Only spend money you can afford to test with. Testing more ad sets cheaply matters more than total spend, and doing organic first makes ads convert faster.","Finding products: Follow the product research modules step by step. Any product people need can win if it's marketed well.","Clothing suppliers: Alibaba and Instagram suppliers work. Send an inquiry with a mockup (AI can make one from your logo) showing logo placement. Local suppliers cost more but are often faster and easier to test. Ask suppliers for examples in your specific niche.","Business partners: If a partner isn't pulling their weight, write out the pros and cons, give them a short window to contribute, and consider forming your own LLC before having the conversation."],"topics":["Calls","Paid ads","Email & SMS","Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_pUOMStnKOt9Fs/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How Facebook Ads Are Built & A/B Testing (Whiteboard)","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"A whiteboard lesson on how Facebook ads are built (creative + audience), how to test each one, and A/B testing headlines, plus quick store questions.","points":["Build out your niche: In a fitness store, add related products that cover other gym needs (gym shirts, gym bags) so customers can buy more.","When to launch ads: When you're new, start ads Friday-Sunday (ideally Sunday) so they're settled into the algorithm by the work week.","Bundles: Stay on a bundle app's free trial as long as you can. Keep your top products available in bundles to raise average order value, but don't force people to buy the bundle.","Samples: Order samples from Amazon for faster shipping. Ordering straight from the supplier is still useful for seeing the exact product.","Organic mix: Engagement-bait videos with the product in the background work. About one in every four or five posts should focus directly on the product (\"imagine coming home to this diffuser\"), or run a separate account for direct product content.","Ad = creative + audience: The creative is what people see (video, image, carousel); the audience is who sees it. A great creative fails with the wrong audience and vice versa. Test two creatives and two audiences.","Diagnose what's working: Run the same creative to different audiences to see if the audience is the problem. Keep a winning creative and swap audiences based on impressions. Watch impressions first, then average order value.","A/B test headlines: Facebook can run two titles in one ad (e.g. \"Buy now\" vs. \"On sale, limited time\") and automatically favor the winner, which saves money compared to testing each separately.","Target sensibly: Don't advertise hoodies in Arizona summer or beach products in Wyoming. Audience is arguably more important than the creative. Organic marketing helps you discover audiences you'd never have guessed.","Community: Be active in the Discord. Ask questions, request reviews, and encourage each other like a team."],"topics":["Calls","Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_PQe6Gbn45oW7c/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"7/3/26 - Masterclass: Organic + Paid Working Together","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"A short masterclass on how organic content and paid ads work together, and why you need to out-market your competitors, plus a few quick questions.","points":["Quick answers: You can do MEO and content creation together, since content should only take 20-30 minutes a day. Master one store (dropshipping is a good learning ground with no inventory) before starting a clothing brand. Once samples arrive, post 2-3 videos a day on every platform. Use a professional account for your product page; a personal account can be casual.","Organic is your test lab: Post anything (funny, goofy, AI) to see what gets traction. It costs only time and shows you which video to turn into a paid ad.","If nothing goes viral: Make one strong ad that clearly shows how your product solves a problem. Thirty minutes of work can produce an ad that runs for months.","Why organic still matters: It builds brand recognition and trust, so your ads convert better. Skipping it means leaving sales on the table.","Beat your competitors: Business is competitive. Find what competitors aren't doing (organic, more ads, better customer service) and do it. Big brands market everywhere: ads, email, texts, sponsorships.","Scale your marketing: Start with organic if your budget is tight, then put early profits into $5/day ad tests. Once your store is built, success mostly comes down to how many people you reach.","Faces and followers: You don't have to show your face; friends, UGC creators, or AI can appear instead. For a clothing brand, showing yourself and others wearing it builds trust (e.g. filming at your boxing events). You don't need a follower count before running ads.","AI ad module: A new module shows how to make realistic AI ads (around $10/month for a few ads) so you can test products while waiting for samples.","Peptides: The coach said he isn't comfortable selling them for moral reasons."],"topics":["Calls","Product research","Organic marketing","Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_VplferUtdUxbH/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"7/7/26 - Extra Questions: Store Layout, Organic & New Members","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"An extra-questions call on store layout, tech troubleshooting, organic posting, AI content, and advice for brand-new members.","points":["Business setup: If you have an EIN, use it for your business accounts (ask a tax professional about the specifics).","Market one product: Push your single best product to drive traffic to the store, instead of making daily videos for every product.","Homepage layout: Show your products directly on the homepage with add-to-cart or buy-now buttons. Making shoppers click through extra pages lowers conversions.","Clothing drops: Sell a few pieces first, then release an exclusive item. It brings back past customers alongside new ones.","AI vs. real videos: If you use AI videos for organic, mix in real ones too so your brand stays trustworthy.","Store won't load after edits: If you changed theme code (especially on a site built with Lovable), the theme may be broken. Rebuild the site and reconnect it to the same Shopify account and domain, avoid editing code directly, and use Shopify support (phone or live chat) for tech issues.","Facebook Ad Library: Expect to see the same ads repeatedly; winning ads often run for months.","Building apps with AI: AI can build the front end, but the back end usually still needs a developer. It's worth trying if a product pairs well with an app.","Posting everywhere: Post the same video with the same caption on every platform you can (TikTok, Instagram, Pinterest, X, Snapchat, etc.).","Tips for new members: Take detailed notes organized by topic, show up to calls, keep God first, and think long-term. You're building real skills in sales and marketing, not chasing quick money."],"topics":["Calls","Organic marketing","AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_qUHfAJLPiLNvY/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"7/9/26 - Brand Recognition & Winning Ad Creatives","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"A Friday masterclass on brand recognition and spotting winning ad creatives, plus Q&A with a member who sold out his first batch and hit $1K in his first month.","points":["Supplier changes: If a new supplier's version looks slightly different (e.g. logo placement) but works the same, email customers to let them know before it ships.","Chargebacks and defects: Ask the customer for photos or video. If the product really is defective, contact your supplier for a replacement; if they don't respond, escalate through Alibaba.","Fulfillment: With AliExpress you may have to send order details manually. AutoDS or Zendrop automate it.","Klaviyo flows: Abandoned cart, welcome, and a post-purchase thank-you with a coupon for the next order are the must-haves. An unused-discount reminder is optional but can lift conversions.","Sold out? Keep going: Keep posting (2-3 times a day is fine), add a \"get notified when it's back\" pop-up to collect emails and phone numbers, and reinvest profits into bigger inventory orders (e.g. 40-45 units next time) instead of spending them. Clearly label pre-orders in the title and description, and keep customers updated by email.","Pop-up offer: Around 20-25% off (35% max), with prices raised to match.","Recognition sells: Like Sprite billboards, the more people see your brand (organic posts plus ads), the more likely they choose you over an unknown competitor.","Ads: what to focus on: Meta automates most of the setup. Your job is the creative (video/image) and text. A meme can work once people know your brand.","Spotting a winner: Look at click-through rate and store visits, not just sales. When a creative wins, don't reinvent it; make similar versions (new scenery, a different person). Test two or three creatives at about $5/day each for three to five days, then put more budget behind the best one. Raising the budget mostly increases reach within that audience.","Urgency without looking empty: Instead of \"first 10 orders get 35% off\" (which suggests nobody's buying), try \"35% off for the next 24 hours.\""],"topics":["Calls","Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_hE7zmR8NCe6Bu/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"7/14/26 - Q&A: Business Setup, Pre-orders & Niching Down","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"A long start-of-week Q&A covering business setup, getting started step by step, handling pre-orders when you sell out, niching down, and advice on partners and mindset.","points":["Business setup: An LLC isn't required to take payments but is common for liability. Use a bank account kept separate from your personal money. You can run several brands under one parent LLC, or form separate LLCs if you plan to become an S-corp later.","Getting started: Watch the Welcome videos, then the beginner school, then the advanced modules. Find your niche and a product before you build or pay for a store, and stock 3-5 products in that niche. Inner Circle Plus members get a free store build; others can build it themselves or use the half-price agency (about $150).","Voiceovers: AI voices like ElevenLabs text-to-speech can sound more polished. Run the new version as a separate ad at the same time as the old one for a fair test.","Selling out on pre-order: Make \"pre-order\" prominent in the title and description, and consider removing an automated email that repeats it (it can make buyers second-guess). Add a pop-up to collect emails from people who'd rather wait for stock. Before a big reorder from a new supplier, pay for an express sample to check quality, then raise your order size (e.g. 50 units).","Winning products: Pick something people need (or can be marketed as a need) that solves a problem. Gym accessories like grips and wrist wraps are a good example.","Niche down: A narrow niche (e.g. combat sports instead of general fitness) builds a more loyal customer base, and your emails stay relevant to what people bought.","Starting capital: With about $1K, start with dropshipping to learn marketing, or brand a simple product. A 9-5 can fund ads and samples; reinvest rather than spending on extras.","Suppliers: Prioritize suppliers who communicate well over the cheapest price or star rating.","Partners: Try running a store solo first so you know the real workload. With a partner who isn't contributing, part ways respectfully (offer to buy them out or let them buy you out), or keep them involved as an affiliate.","Mindset: The coach's advice to his younger self: expect to need more consistency than you think. Customers often need to see you several times before buying. Mentors save you from costly mistakes."],"topics":["Calls","Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_yTW2CyN5Pn72X/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"7/15/26 - Landing Page Non-Negotiables","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"A masterclass on the non-negotiables every store landing page needs to earn a visitor's trust, plus Q&A on reviews, AI ads, and ad testing.","points":["1. Testimonials: Show that real people use and like the product. Three or four on the homepage is enough, even from friends or people close to you.","2. About Us: Explain the deeper reason behind your brand and what problem you solve (e.g. a gym grip made because lifters keep getting blisters). People buy the persona, not just the product: Nike sells feeling fit, Balenciaga sells mystery. A strong About page lifts conversions.","3. FAQ: Answer the questions people keep asking (what it does, shipping time, discounts, where it ships from). It saves you emails and helps impatient shoppers buy faster. Five or six questions is plenty; ask friends what would stop them from buying.","4. Contact page: Put it in the footer so visitors know you're a real business they can reach.","5. Show the problem it solves: Use before/after images or copy that answers objections like \"do I really need this?\"","6. Urgency: When running a sale, add a time limit like \"free shipping for the next 24-48 hours\" so people don't leave and forget to come back.","Refund policy: Keep it in the footer; few people read it.","Don't overthink the design: Colors and wording matter far less than marketing. Get these basics in place, then focus on bringing people to the site.","Reviews when starting out: Use supplier reviews for the exact same product, your own honest review, or friends' honest reviews. Never make reviews up.","AI in ads: Keep AI to about 25% of paid ad content; organic can use more. Never use celebrities or people who haven't signed a contract in AI ads, since that can lead to lawsuits.","Ad testing: Launching about seven creatives across a week works. Track results, then raise the budget on the winners or replace the losers.","Other tips: Organic validates content, then push what works with paid ads (the push-and-go method). For dropshipping, use suppliers like Zendrop or CJ. An LLC vs. sole proprietorship depends on your state, so ask a tax advisor. On Instagram you can add a bio link at any follower count, so buying followers isn't needed."],"topics":["Calls","Website","Paid ads","AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_6Mnrr7W0kurf0/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"7/17/26 - Paid Ad Creatives That Convert","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"A masterclass on what makes a paid ad convert, with ratings for different creative styles, how to study competitors' ads, and where to reinvest profits.","points":["Organic vs. paid creative: Organic can be loose and fun (\"test videos\"). Paid ads are seen by potential buyers asking \"why should I buy this?\", so they need to be clear and serious about what the product does.","Creative types, rated for sales: Plain product picture (awareness only): about 2-3/10 unless you're a huge brand or selling something bought for looks. Problem + solution: about 6/10 as a picture, 8-9/10 as a video. Comparison vs. other brands: about 7/10. Video testimonials: about 9/10, especially when the person shows the problem and how the product solved it. Star-review screenshots: about 4/10.","Getting testimonials: Ask people what would make them buy or hold back. Automate a post-purchase email offering a coupon for a 30-60 second video review; it also drives a repeat order.","AI voiceovers: Test an AI voiceover against your own voice as a separate ad running at the same time. Don't extend the old ad until you see which one wins.","Testing creatives: Start with three or four creatives at $5-10 each. Put more budget behind (or duplicate) the one with the best click-through rate.","Study competitors: In the Facebook Ad Library, look for ads from mid-size competitors in your niche that have been running for 3-6+ months, since that usually means they're profitable. Borrow the angle (e.g. testimonial format) but don't copy exactly. Shop Hunter shows revenue for dropshipping stores. Billion-dollar brands play a different game, so don't model their ads.","Reinvest early: Put most early profit back in (the coach suggests around 85%, not financial advice). If you're selling out, buy more inventory; if inventory is sitting, spend more on marketing.","Small tips: If Instagram won't schedule posts, post manually and space them out. TikTok filters carry over when you save and repost elsewhere. Simple brightness tweaks are fine, but skip gimmicky filters."],"topics":["Calls","Product research","Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_WrYDn0BWxzFeD/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"7/21/26 - Extra Questions: Working With Creators","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"An extra-questions call focused on working with creators (commission deals and contracts), getting started as a new member, clothing samples, and applying these marketing skills to other businesses.","points":["Member win: One member who spent months on organic first, then started ads about six weeks ago, is now getting regular sales. Find your audience and hero products before paying for ads.","Creator deals: Start with the lowest offer (commission only), then commission plus free product, and only offer upfront pay for creators really worth it. Give them a unique discount code so you can track their sales, and set it so it can't be stacked with your pop-up discount. Commission is a percentage of the sales they drive.","Always use a contract: Several members sent free products and got nothing back. Draft a simple agreement (AI can help; there's a template in the modules) covering what they'll post and for how long, and have them e-sign it with DocuSign or PandaDoc. DMs aren't a binding agreement. Have an attorney review contracts once you're bigger.","If a creator's video flops: With their consent, you can still run their video as a paid ad. Brief them on what to say to improve results.","New members: Watch the intro video sent when you join, finish the beginner school (the foundation), then move to the advanced modules. Learning ads here saves paying agencies to run them for you.","LLCs: Set one up through your state's filing site or the IRS. Rules differ by state, so check with a professional.","When to run paid ads: Once organic shows you who your audience is. Boosting a TikTok post (about $15 for 10,000+ views) is good for exposure, while ads manager is better for sales.","Clothing samples: A $100 sample fee is normal; unit prices drop in bulk. Spell out exact materials and logo method (screen print vs. embroidery). Pay more for better quality to protect your brand.","Scaling and other businesses: Stay focused on a store that's working before starting another; later, connect related stores so they promote each other. Rent a warehouse before buying one. These marketing skills also work for service businesses, which should target older decision-makers on Facebook."],"topics":["Calls","Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_HWWjeUKKgVRiv/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"7/22/26 - Fulfillment Models Masterclass","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"A masterclass on the different ways to fulfill orders (dropshipping, holding your own stock, 3PLs, and warehouses), plus tips on shipping addresses, pre-orders, and reaching out to creators.","points":["While your store is being built: Study marketing, check competitors in the Facebook Ad Library, and practice making content so you're ready to drive traffic on day one.","Dropshipping is a fulfillment method, not a business: Your supplier ships straight to the customer, so you never touch the product. Zendrop, CJ Dropshipping, and AutoDS auto-fulfill orders; AliExpress often needs manual spreadsheets. Best for beginners and unbranded products.","Holding stock (supplier to you to customer): You order in bulk and ship orders yourself. It's more work but cheaper per unit, and it's the usual route for branded or custom products. Suppliers set minimum order quantities because one-off pieces cost them more, which is why samples are pricey.","3PL (third-party logistics): Your supplier ships bulk stock to a 3PL that packs and ships each order (roughly $2 per package). Worth it once you're packing hundreds of orders and charging well above that per order.","Warehouse: Rent or own space and hire people to pack. Often cheaper than a 3PL at scale.","Scale your fulfillment: Packing 1,000 orders a day yourself leaves no time to grow, so upgrade as volume increases. You can also use multiple suppliers, e.g. testing a product from AliExpress and another from Zendrop.","Return addresses: If you ship orders yourself, use a P.O. box as the return address instead of your home (an LLC still needs a real address). Dropship suppliers add their own return address.","Sold out? Run pre-orders, show the realistic wait time clearly (even on the homepage banner), and keep collecting contacts.","Reaching creators: Have a copy-paste pitch that explains what your product solves, what makes you different, and what you're offering. Match the offer to their reach (a discount code may be enough for small creators). Get the contract signed before sending any product or payment.","When you're selling out: Keep pushing the cheaper version that's already winning and save upgraded variants for later, like a new phone model. Be on every platform, and avoid scammy offers or celebrity-likeness ads that risk your reputation and lawsuits."],"topics":["Calls","Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_9LPGGG8ZFanWy/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"7/25/26 - Short Clip (End of Call)","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"What this clip covers: A short (about 2-minute) clip from the end of a call. It isn't a full lesson. A member follows up on a story shoutout the coach offered, and the coach says he'll post another one; text him as a rem","points":["What this clip covers: A short (about 2-minute) clip from the end of a call. It isn't a full lesson.","A member follows up on a story shoutout the coach offered, and the coach says he'll post another one; text him as a reminder.","For members who work weekday hours and can't make live calls: watch the recorded calls in this course and stay active in the Discord."],"topics":["Calls","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_zPF5l4VHtotrZ/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"7/28/26 - Extra Questions: Member Wins & New Member Advice","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"An extra-questions call with member wins (repeat sales and a 1-million-view video), advice for new members, staying productive while traveling, and handling fraud risk and conversion rates.","points":["Member wins: A member who stayed consistent is now waking up to orders, and another hit 1 million views in 24 hours by remaking a past winning video with a new hook. Results often show up all at once after a long grind.","Building while you learn: You can have your store built as you go through the modules or gather everything first. Either works. Start with dropshipping so you aren't holding inventory.","Course path for new members: Finish the beginner school first (basics), then move to the Inner Circle modules, where the advanced systems are. Take notes and focus on gaining skills, not just the money.","Pick a niche first: Choose a focused niche before products so your store isn't scattered (toy cars, dog food, remotes). Your coach can suggest winning products, and you can ask the Discord.","Digital products count: E-books and planners are e-commerce too. Add a call-to-action for your product at the end of your YouTube or TikTok content, and use UGC creators to promote music, fragrances, or other products.","Productivity while traveling: Get up earlier than everyone else and knock out your posting and work first, then enjoy the trip.","Inventory over new tactics: If you're selling out on organic, put money back into inventory (not financial advice) and keep doing what's working rather than switching fulfillment or marketing. Slower dropship shipping (about 30 days) can hurt your reputation.","Understanding analytics: View counts can take days to update. Organic traffic converts lower than ads because many visitors are just curious, so judge conversion by unique visitors, not sessions. You can see TikTok analytics with creator tools; no business license needed.","Fraud and chargebacks: Shopify's \"medium risk\" flag often means billing and shipping addresses differ. Watch for multiple or back-to-back orders from a new customer. If disputed, submit proof of delivery. The coach uses a no-refunds policy with exchanges for damaged items to cut down on abuse.","Meta ads module: The newer Facebook ads video in the modules reflects the latest Ads Manager."],"topics":["Calls","Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_CnjoYmPLwvCgh/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"7/29/26 - Outreach Masterclass + Website Review","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"A masterclass on outreach, the biggest advantage online stores have, plus a live website review and an explanation of email marketing beyond automations.","points":["Captions matter: Keep your content tied to the product. A generic caption like \"how was your day?\" attracts an audience that has nothing to do with what you sell. A member hit 1 million views in 24 hours by clearly showing what his product solves while keeping it engaging.","Cheaper competitors (e.g. on Amazon): Not a dealbreaker. There are 8 billion people with different preferences, and many have never seen your competitor. Strong offers and personality set you apart.","Outreach is everything: A physical store depends on location; an online store can reach anyone. Don't wait for traffic. Organic (TikTok, YouTube, Instagram, Snapchat, Facebook), paid ads (Meta, TikTok, plus Pinterest/Snapchat later), and UGC creators are your main channels.","Post every video everywhere: Each extra platform takes about 30 seconds. Space posts out through the day so they don't look like spam.","Priorities: Once your site and product are \"good enough\" (around 8/10), stop endlessly tweaking or researching and focus on how many more videos you can make. A competitor with a worse site can out-earn you by out-marketing you.","Website review takeaways: Make the add-to-cart/buy-now button big and a contrasting color. Show the image before the price. Get a custom domain. Check that the store displays correctly in other currencies, or stick to USD. Don't show a public 10% code in the banner when your pop-up offers 25% for an email; keep only the pop-up offer so visitors give you their contact info.","Reading ad results: Beyond click-through rate, watch average order value and conversion rate. If many people see the ad but few buy, change the creative or the audience. Test a new creative on the same audience.","Pre-order vs. email-only when sold out: Keeping pre-orders going brings in cash to buy more inventory, while the pop-up still captures people who'd rather wait.","Email marketing: Automate at most a couple of welcome emails, then write and schedule regular campaigns yourself every 24-48 hours (about 5 minutes each, done in one weekend session). Most emails can simply remind people of your products; save discounts for about one in five. A content-focused email module is coming.","Business partners and AI tools: A partner can split marketing and back-end work, but it isn't required. New AI site tools (e.g. Base44) are coming; get traffic first."],"topics":["Calls","Website","Organic marketing","Email & SMS"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_UQGh4GlCN1YHZ/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"7/31/26 - Finding & Recruiting UGC Creators","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"A short masterclass on finding and recruiting UGC creators, plus Q&A on creator contracts, collecting contacts, pre-launch pages, and packaging.","points":["Creator contracts: Start small (e.g. $200/month) and raise pay once you can track their results. Spell out terms: around five videos a week, no competitor products or mentions, and videos stay up at least 160 days. Have models sign a release covering ads and marketing.","Collecting contacts: Use pop-ups, checkout, and a pre-launch page. Make one offer (e.g. 25%) for email and phone together; starting with email only is fine.","Pre-launch page: Instead of Shopify's default \"coming soon\" page, point your domain to a Klaviyo landing page that collects emails for early access, then switch it to your store at launch.","Packaging: Chinese labeling on the outer package isn't a problem; customers care about the product. Add a thank-you card with a time-limited coupon for their next order to drive repeat purchases.","Why creators matter: Don't assume you can do all the promotion yourself. The coach's biggest jump in orders came from gifting product to a creator back when he had only about 12K followers. That creator kept wearing it as he grew, without being paid.","Target small creators: Look for people gaining traction in your niche before they blow up. Big creators often have big egos and high prices (e.g. $3K for one photo).","How to reach them: DM and email. For bigger creators, check for a second or business account through their tags and following.","Don't name your price first: Pitch the perks (e.g. paid upfront, not just commission, plus other creators you've worked with and why your brand fits their audience), then ask what they normally charge. Naming a number first can mean overpaying (e.g. $75 when they'd accept $10).","Keep following up: One message isn't enough. Reach out repeatedly until they reply or tell you to stop; change the wording after a few attempts.","Don't reveal your numbers: When a creator asks about your revenue, stay vague (\"we've had sales and are scaling\"). Sharing big numbers raises their price and can invite copycats."],"topics":["Calls","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_D86LF3fa66kwq/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"8/4/26 - Q&A: Beating Procrastination & Staying Consistent","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"A mindset-heavy Q&A on beating procrastination, staying consistent, and what separates members who succeed, plus quick tips on ads, AI site tools, and starting a second store.","points":["Telling your parents: Many parents don't understand online income. Consider staying quiet until you have results to show.","Use the resources: The members getting results show up to calls, text their coaches, and stay active in the Discord. One member got his first sale the morning after asking the right question on a call.","Ad tips: When advertising one product, link the ad straight to that product page; when promoting the whole store, link to the store. Try a video featuring two products (like a store commercial). A recorded voiceover with a strong ending is driving one member's sales.","Base44 (AI site builder): It can build or edit your store, but the code still needs to be integrated with Shopify. Use it alongside a professionally built site; intro modules are in the school, with an advanced one coming.","Beating procrastination: If you don't act on your vision, someone else will. Every \"I'll do it tomorrow\" delays your success and can cost money later. Let other people's wins motivate you, not discourage you; the coach just started earlier, and you have the same information.","Get uncomfortable: The coach succeeded partly because he had no backup plan, posting 20 videos a day. Cut back on comfort habits like gaming and sports, and remember the process is what makes the win meaningful.","Member story: Steve's first product (a shaker cup) flopped. He kept going, switched products, improved his marketing, ran $5/day ads using the push-and-go method, and just had his best month. He credits his athlete mindset, not caring what people think, his faith, and the mindset modules.","Starting a second store: If your first store runs mostly on its own, start another, ideally in a related niche, or even a \"competitor\" store selling the same product with the same tactics. Avoid niches with heavy payment-processing headaches.","Content ideas: Mix informational, problem-solving videos (\"want to work out at home? use this\") with humor, and paste your best-performing ideas into AI to generate 30-60 more."],"topics":["Calls","Mindset","Paid ads","AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_embu0egSqs49N/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"8/11/26 - Extra Questions: Live Store & TikTok Reviews","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"An extra-questions call with live reviews of members' stores and TikTok content, plus tips on shipping times, TikTok Shop, promoting posts, and shipping protection.","points":["Shipping times: Your supplier's listed time (e.g. 2 days from a U.S. warehouse) is roughly what customers will see. In the product description, list something like \"2-5 business days (U.S.)\" and \"2-4 weeks international.\" Most orders come from the U.S., Canada, and the U.K.","TikTok Shop: Listing your product is free. Apply as a TikTok Shop seller, show you're a real business, and connect your store. It's a strong move if you're already getting big organic views. A how-to module is coming.","Store review (a cleaning-products store doing big organic views): Add a pop-up (e.g. Klaviyo) right away so you collect emails from people who aren't ready to buy. Put your winning product first on the homepage, and add products with add-to-cart buttons directly on the homepage. If you promote several products, link your bio to the homepage, not one product, to raise average order value.","Content review (a TikTok product demo): Strong problem-solution format. Use trending sounds instead of generic business audio, and use thinner, standard captions instead of bold, bubbly ones. Post two to three times a day on every platform, and consider Facebook for an older audience.","Growing a second product: On a broad niche account (e.g. cleaning), mix in a second product now and then. If it takes off, it can get its own account.","TikTok Promote: A cheap way to get views and test content (about $5 for 4-5K views). Use TikTok Ads Manager when your goal is sales.","Ad help: A media buyer taking around 5% of the sales they drive is a good deal; agencies usually take 10-25%.","Shipping protection: Offering optional shipping protection for a few dollars works as long as it's optional and priced fairly. Don't gouge customers.","Email setup: A basic Shopify email forwarding to your personal inbox is fine for now; set up a branded email later. Abandoned-cart emails and discount codes can work together without hurting deliverability.","Mindset: Keep your business quiet from doubters until you have results. Paying for a mentorship pays off when friends see results they can't easily copy."],"topics":["Calls","Website","Organic marketing","Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_wjMWZ2MXH42fU/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"8/12/26 - Organic (Practice) vs. Paid Ads (Game Time)","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"A masterclass on the difference between organic content (\"practice\") and paid ads (\"game time\"), plus Q&A on SEO, scaling inventory, AI content, and Meta's ad recommendations.","points":["Organic is practice: On TikTok, Instagram, and Snapchat, people want entertainment. Your organic videos can be loose and mid-quality (even AI); just don't make them look sloppy, since buyers check your organic pages to see if you're legit. Don't over-produce them.","Why organic matters: It builds your brand name and familiarity, shows you your target audience, and teaches you which creatives people like. Even critical comments give useful feedback.","Paid ads are game time: Platforms already show ads to people likely to buy, so ads should be polished and focused on selling, not just entertaining. Familiarity from organic makes ads convert better (people pick the brand they recognize). You can skip organic, but you'll be less prepared.","SEO and tracking: When people search for something, apps use tracking (cookies) to show related ads elsewhere. Good SEO also helps you appear in Google results. More SEO modules are coming.","Scaling inventory: When selling out, put most of your money (around 70-75%, not financial advice) into more stock and keep about 15% for ads. Scale the current product to a big customer base before launching an upgraded version. Sea shipping is much cheaper than air for big orders; split the difference if you need some stock fast.","Reaching new audiences: Have creators make content aimed at a different demographic (e.g. older buyers on Facebook/Instagram) than your own younger-skewing videos. A separate AI-content account (e.g. made with Higgsfield, starting around $20/month) is another option.","Meta's \"high cost\" warnings: Meta pushes you to add images and carousels. If your single video ad is already getting sales, leave it alone and test new creatives separately. Targeting the whole U.S. is fine. If the shop-now button covers your text in Stories, put text at the top next time.","Take notes and apply them: These skills work in any business. Stay active in the Discord, since your network is valuable."],"topics":["Calls","Organic marketing","Paid ads","AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_EbypJVOaA0ctE/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"8/14/26 - What Makes a Winning Product","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"A masterclass on what makes a \"winning product,\" how to tell whether a product or your marketing is the problem, and how to stand out from big competitors. It ends with a warning about pyramid schemes and a quick content review.","points":["You make a product win: No product is a guaranteed winner. A great product fails without strong marketing, a good website, and real effort, while a simple product (even a toothbrush) can win with great marketing and ambassadors. Practice your skills before chasing the \"perfect\" product.","Saturation is the real risk: If anyone can grab the same item at Walmart, you're competing with billion-dollar companies and must out-market them. It's not impossible, but it's harder. Products people haven't seen before, or upgraded versions, face less competition.","Upgrade an existing winner: Take a product that already sells and make it better, like a hoodie with a satin-lined hood, acid wash, and custom embroidery. Look for a supplier that sells an upgraded variant; a dollar or two more per unit can put you in your own lane.","Product or marketing? Don't give up after a week. Low interaction (no comments, questions, or even haters) and a very low click-through rate after reaching lots of people point to the creative, offer, or product. Test new creatives (new locations, creators, angles) before deciding the product is dead. Visitors who leave without buying still means there's interest.","Winning-product checklist: A large enough market, healthy profit margin, an emotional \"I need that\" reaction, a benefit you can show within 10-20 seconds, an instantly clear reason to buy, a clear difference from competitors, and room for bundles, quantity breaks, or subscriptions.","Ads should show problem + solution: Let buyers picture themselves using it. Market the specific benefit (\"satin hood protects your hair\") instead of generic features (\"thick hoodie\").","Beware of pyramid schemes: If someone compliments you and pitches an \"opportunity\" where you pay big money and only earn by recruiting others, walk away. Real businesses build skills and assets you can sell.","Content review: For a gym product with big-brand competition, keep posting, put ad money behind your most-viewed video, use your logo as the profile picture, and use a small TikTok promote to reach 1,000 followers.","Weekend challenge: Find a non-financial win, like the gym, prayer, a phone-free walk, or a cold shower."],"topics":["Calls","Product research","Website","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_X6WJtkJSKKU2Y/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"8/16/26 - Bible Study (Proverbs 1-3)","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"The group's first Bible study call. There's no e-commerce content; members take turns reading Proverbs chapters 1-3 (NIV) and sharing what each passage means to them.","points":["Proverbs 1:1-7, the purpose of wisdom: \"The fear of the Lord is the beginning of knowledge.\" The coach admits he used to get offended when people taught him things. The lesson is to welcome advice and correction instead of resisting it. One member's tip: there are 31 chapters in Proverbs, so read one a day each month.","Proverbs 1:8-19, don't give in to peer pressure: When people try to pull you into wrong things, don't go along. Bad company corrupts good character, so be intentional about who you spend time with.","Proverbs 1:20-33, wisdom calls out: Wisdom is available to everyone, through parents, friends, and even strangers, but it's up to you to listen. Ignoring repeated warnings leads to consequences. The coach shares how three different people warned him about a friend group at 16; he listened and avoided the trouble they later got into.","Proverbs 2:1-11, seek wisdom like treasure: You have to actively want and search for understanding, not half-heartedly. Members talked about not being \"lukewarm\" and living out faith privately, not just for image. God protects and guides those who seek Him.","Proverbs 2:12-22, wisdom protects you: Wisdom keeps you away from people and temptations that look fun in the moment but cause harm. The group framed God's guidance as coming from love, not control.","Takeaway: Stay humble, be open to correction, choose your circle carefully, and seek wisdom daily."],"topics":["Calls","Mindset","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_lkmcwqnujTW84/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"8/18/26 - Extra Questions: Breaking a Sales Plateau","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"An extra-questions call on breaking through a sales plateau, turning big organic views into sales with calls to action, supplier payment terms, and which platforms give the best return on organic posting.","points":["Stuck at the same sales level: If paid ad budget and organic effort stay the same, sales stay the same. Post more consistently (one or two videos a day on TikTok, not one every few days), keep funding your winning ads, and make sure your email campaigns are going out regularly to drive repeat buyers. Most buyers order quietly; DMs are often other businesses wanting to collab.","Turn views into sales: A member getting 300-800K views per video had no call to action. Add one in the caption (\"in stock now,\" no pushy \"buy now\"), reply to comments on recent viral videos with where to buy (\"get yours at the link in our bio\"), and use Stories with polls, giveaways, and reminders. Keep CTAs out of the on-screen text so it doesn't feel pushy.","Supplier payment terms: Pay only about 30-35% upfront, and require photos showing your logo, the full quantity, and ready-to-ship status before paying the rest. One member received 60 units without his logo because he paid in full first. Report issues to get a refund, and hold back unbranded stock from influencers.","AI vs. real content: You can mix AI and real organic videos. For paid ads, keep it about 75% real people and only around 15% AI, especially for products buyers need to trust.","Store builds: The agency builds the front end and focuses on back-end SEO; you set up your own domain because of the DNS and privacy settings.","Best platforms for organic: Snapchat Spotlight and YouTube Shorts are handing out views right now, alongside TikTok and Instagram. Snapchat can take a month to take off. Post the same videos everywhere and spread posts through the day.","Shipping vs. margin: When starting out, choose the higher-margin supplier, then reinvest into ads and switch to faster shipping later.","Mindset: Hitting a plateau is normal. Stay consistent; sales compound as more people see your product repeatedly.","Personal brand: Use the same push-and-go approach. \"Day in the life\" videos documenting your e-commerce journey perform well for small creators."],"topics":["Calls","Organic marketing","Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_kHUDnD9bmTvLF/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"8/19/26 - AI Tools Masterclass","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"An AI tools masterclass breaking down which tool to use for each part of your business, plus a shipping troubleshooting question and member wins.","points":["Shipping delays: if a supplier package stalls or shows as expired in tracking, give it about 30 days, then reship or refund. Refunds apply if the product never arrived.","Higgsfield: generating UGC-style content and videos.","Claude: website code, email copywriting, scripts, hooks and video ideas.","ChatGPT: product images and photo shoots, copywriting and business ideas.","Lovable: front-end website design that connects to Shopify.","Base44: front end plus custom internal software (e.g. inventory tracking, UGC creator tracking), connects to Shopify or Wix.","Key takeaway: pick the right tool for each job. You don't need to connect Claude or ChatGPT directly to your store.","Member win: adding clear CTAs to captions led to a $375 day right after getting feedback. Next step is expanding to Snapchat and YouTube Shorts.","Raising AOV: add complementary products (e.g. lifting straps) while keeping the store focused at 5–6 products max."],"topics":["Calls","AI tools","Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_wcd5FRaiHsvIJ/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"8/21/26 - High-Converting Homepage Structure","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"A short masterclass on how to structure a high-converting homepage or landing page, section by section.","points":["Hero section: lead with your current offer (e.g. a sale or bundle deal), ideally over an image of a model using the product, plus a clear Shop Now button.","Next section: show your winning products right away. That's what most visitors came for.","Then: models and testimonials (reviews, ratings, fit photos) so shoppers can picture the product working for them.","Category sections: useful once you have multiple product lines. Skip them if you're starting with just a few products.","Lower down: extra offers and trust points like free shipping, fast delivery and warranties, then influencer or creator features (swap in UGC or AI creators if you don't have big names yet).","Footer: an email capture pop-up to grow your list.","Why it works: gives quick shoppers a clear path to checkout while answering common objections as people scroll.","Q&A: link in bio should go to the homepage if you promote several products on one account, or straight to the product page if it's one product per account or ad.","Q&A: AI-generated models can work, as long as they look realistic and show how the product is used."],"topics":["Calls","Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_4gZb465BPwuPW/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"8/25/26 - Q&A: Where to Start + Live Store Review","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"An open Q&A on where new members should start, how to be creative with marketing the right way, and a live store review focused on conversion rate.","points":["Where to start: new members should work through Introduction to E-commerce and the dropshipping modules first. Save the multi-store/advanced modules for after your first store is running.","Dropshipping vs. a brand: start with dropshipping (minimal capital) to learn suppliers, listings and selling, then move into a clothing brand once you have the knowledge.","Selling in short videos: show what problem the product solves. A simple format of hook, then why/what, then a call to action works well.","Different platforms favor different videos. Post everywhere and focus on your inputs, not short-term results.","Creativity: be creative in how you promote the product, not just in being entertaining. Entertainment-only content brings followers and sessions, but not buyers.","Think like a consumer: ask \"would I buy from this video?\" and use the 5% rule (small changes vs. competitors, e.g. a new camera angle).","Seasonal angles: winter (stay home), bulking season, summer (avoid crowded gyms), spring (summer physique). One product, four different hooks.","Store review: email pop-ups should give the discount code only after the email is entered (aim for ~3–6% form conversion).","Avoid auto-adding upsells to the cart, add urgency like a \"sale ending soon\" banner, and send traffic to the product page when you promote a single product.","Low conversion (~0.25–0.3%) is normal for organic-only traffic. Once you have traction, small TikTok ads (even $5/day) can retarget warm viewers.","Ad setup: 2–3 creatives per campaign, mixing a video and images to see what performs.","Branding: make your social handle match your brand name, and post more videos vs. photos (AI UGC creators via Higgsfield can help — covered in the marketing modules)."],"topics":["Calls","Getting started","Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_ZkN7gucP1b61C/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"8/26/26 - Common Website Mistakes","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"A beginner-friendly masterclass on the most common website mistakes that hurt trust and conversions.","points":["Mistake 1 — no custom domain: a default \"myshopify\"-style URL hurts trust and is hard to remember. Buy a domain (e.g. on GoDaddy or Shopify). If .com is taken, try .shop or a niche extension like .apparel.","Mistake 2 — no clear path to checkout: don't make shoppers dig for the product. Put the offer at the top, a Shop Now button in the hero section, and the product (with an add-to-cart button) on the homepage. Shopify Sidekick can set this up.","The \"under-10\" test: if a young kid can't figure out how to buy from your site, it isn't simple enough for the average shopper.","Mistake 3 — unprofessional look: use clean, high-quality imagery and stick to 2–3 brand colors that suit your target audience instead of a rainbow of clashing colors.","Mistake 4 (advanced) — no incentive: a plain price with no sale, free shipping or bundle gives people a reason to wait. Even a small discount, free shipping, BOGO or a pop-up code can lift conversions.","Pricing tip: end prices in .99 (e.g. $49.99 instead of $50.99).","Feature your winning product: if an ad is driving demand for one product, make it front and center. Keep the homepage to 1–3 winners and use categories for larger catalogs.","Challenge: set goals for mental, spiritual and physical health too, not just financial ones."],"topics":["Calls","Getting started","Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_ouxkKXoDL418W/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"9/1/26 - Extra Questions: Platforms, TikTok Shop & Q4 Sales","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"An extra-questions call covering store platforms, beginner strategy, social account issues, TikTok Shop, staying productive, and Q4 sale ideas.","points":["Wix/Base44: you shouldn't need to buy a new Wix business plan for each site. Check you're logged into the right account and contact Wix support if it persists.","Platforms: Base44 can build the front end, with Shopify or Wix as the back end. Using both can help, but one is fine.","Products: start with low-ticket products until you get the hang of it, then move into higher-ticket items.","Beginners: take notes, apply what you learn, and watch every module fully, even if you have prior e-commerce experience. Pace yourself (a few modules at a time) to avoid brain fog.","Scaling approach: start small, learn, then double down and replicate what works, similar to building a real estate portfolio.","Banned social account: contact the platform's support, try a fresh account after a few days, and keep pushing other platforms meanwhile.","TikTok Shop: add the product link to roughly 1 in every 8–9 videos so views don't tank. Some shoppers trust TikTok Shop more than a new website. Flash sales plus free shipping help (module coming).","Productivity: instead of one long block, split work into shorter sessions across the day, change your environment (coffee shop, park), and prioritize sleep.","Google Ads vs. social: Google is crowded with competitors comparing prices. Good SEO acts like a free Google ad, but the course mainly uses organic social media marketing.","Finding suppliers: try Zendrop, CJ or AutoDS (see the product research modules). Find your niche and product before building the store.","Q4 sales: use back-to-school, Halloween or a \"fall sale\" angle. A countdown timer (1–24 hours, resetting per visit) can add a few percent in conversions."],"topics":["Calls","Getting started","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_kn3O9lG5m6BQu/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"9/2/26 - Creatives Masterclass: Organic vs. Paid","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"A masterclass on creatives (the videos and images behind your marketing): how organic and paid creatives differ, when to lean on competitors for ideas, plus Q&A on scaling, inventory and email marketing.","points":["Organic vs. paid: organic works best with video (slideshows are okay). Aim for about 3 posts a day and make roughly 7 in 10 something you'd actually see on your feed. Paid ads can use images or video, but every ad should look like a real ad worth buying from.","Before running ads: check the Facebook Ad Library to see what competitors run. Ads that have been live a long time are usually working. Shop Hunter can also give clues.","Don't reinvent the wheel: if competitors win with short 10-second product videos, don't switch to long explainers or low-quality photos. Use the 5% change rule instead (new location, tweaked wording).","Creativity first: start with your own ideas. If ~50–100 posts get little traction, look to competitors and creators for inspiration.","Testing ads: you won't judge a creative on $2. If $100+ gets few clicks (depending on AOV), switch it up. The creative matters more than the audience.","Scaling: when an ad works, put more money into it. Follower count matters far less than sales. Reuse winning organic videos as ads and post ad creatives organically too.","Unboxing ads: show the product within the first 3 seconds. Static ads should include the product name, a clear CTA and a short brand description.","Inventory: when chasing a monthly goal with stock running low, faster shipping on a smaller batch (and pre-orders if needed) can prevent sell-outs. While scaling, margins can dip temporarily (the modules recommend ~35% long term). Not financial advice.","Shopify analytics: TikTok traffic often shows up as \"Direct\" in the Growth/acquisition reports.","Email marketing: set up abandoned-cart and welcome flows ASAP. Email can drive 30–40% of revenue and many abandoned carts can be recovered.","Faceless content: fine for streetwear or jewelry (film hands, neck shots, etc.). Run holiday-themed sales, but keep the products themselves evergreen.","When to start paid ads: once you've practiced creatives organically and understand your target audience (check who likes, comments and follows). No follower minimum."],"topics":["Calls","Product research","Organic marketing","Email & SMS"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_N0kPy6R0GZ70c/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"9/4/26 - Testing Products With AI Videos","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"A masterclass on testing products with AI-generated videos before (or while) ordering a sample, plus Q&A on offers, competitor partnerships and fulfillment.","points":["The old way: order a sample (supplier or Amazon), wait 2–4 weeks, then film and market it to check demand.","The new way: screenshot the product, have Claude write a detailed prompt (what it is, who it's for, the vibe you want), then generate realistic UGC-style videos in Higgsfield or Arcads.","Costs: AI tools run roughly $25–$125+/month for a limited number of videos. A sample costs about the same and gives you unlimited self-filmed content.","Repurposing: one AI video can be cut and remixed into ~10 posts.","Best approach: order the sample and start AI videos while you wait. Run an AI account and a self-filmed account side by side, and if AI videos show demand, use the sales to fund real samples.","Downsides of AI-only: you don't know the product's quality. Trust- or results-based products (supplements, skincare) still need real people or UGC creators. Simple products (water bottles, headsets, ab rollers) work well with AI.","Skincare creatives: show results (before/after), how to use it, and review-style static images.","Variants: products with color or size options need to go through the product page. A direct-to-checkout button isn't possible there.","Offers: price competitively and use free shipping over a threshold or a small gift incentive. Prize/giveaway entries rarely drive real buyers.","Competitor partnership offers: verify their numbers first (screen-share the dashboard). Don't merge a fast-growing store. If anything, test a separate new store together.","Fulfillment: better supplier pricing comes from a consistent relationship and volume. A 3PL (~$2/package) or your own warehouse takes packing off your plate once orders get heavy."],"topics":["Calls","Product research","AI tools","Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_FGj0giwrIwtBh/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"9/8/26 - Extra Questions: AI Organic, Suppliers & Store Review","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"An extra-questions call on AI organic marketing, product variants, supplier quality issues, affiliates, and a live store review.","points":["Getting started: you can start building your site before your coach reaches out. Shopify is best for beginners (free trial), Wix if you're more tech-savvy. Watch the school modules first, then the Inner Circle for advanced material.","AI organic: running AI-generated UGC (e.g. Higgsfield) on a separate account alongside real videos is one of the most time-efficient ways to market right now. Most viewers care what the product does, not whether the video is AI.","AI tips: give Claude full context about your product to get better prompts, render in lower quality and upscale to save credits, and split/remix clips to repurpose them.","Viral ≠ sales: some \"cool\" products get millions of views but convert poorly. Look for products with real demand.","Avoid \"no-ship\" or bait-and-switch tactics. They're dishonest and get stores and accounts banned.","Faulty units: test several samples before assuming a defect, contact the supplier with clear details, and update your product page if specs (like battery life) differ. Don't overpromise.","Affiliates/UGC: commission-only deals are great when available. Use a contract or pay after videos are delivered, and find more affiliates through TikTok Shop Seller Center.","Variants: for products that depend on car make/model (e.g. steering wheel covers), create separate products per brand with model variants. Start with the most popular models and use spec searches and listing photos to give your supplier references.","Clothing brand: order samples of your main pieces first, then sell from mockups once you trust the supplier.","Store review: add genuine customer reviews, and only reveal the discount code after the shopper enters their email."],"topics":["Calls","Website","Organic marketing","AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_vpkmM6GS4Naj9/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Raising AOV Recap + Protecting Your Business","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"A quick recap of how to raise average order value (AOV), then a mindset discussion on protecting your store from copycats and keeping your business private.","points":["Raise AOV by adding value: if ad costs are eating your margins, give customers more reasons to spend.","Bundles: offer multi-buys or \"add another, get 50% off.\" Make sure the discounted price still sits comfortably above your cost.","More products: a one-product store caps what high-intent buyers can spend, which pushes your cost per customer up. Add complementary products.","Shipping protection: a small optional add-on ($2–3) at checkout. Lost packages are rare, and you reship if one is lost.","Gifts and mystery boxes: price them relative to your products and audience.","Protect your store: keep your best stores private, even from people close to you. Products and stores can be copied quickly (even by pasting a site into AI), so trademark your brand when you can.","Faceless marketing: consider keeping your face out of your store's content once it's visibly successful. AI UGC and hired creators make that easier.","Don't share revenue: only those who need to know (like the IRS) should know your numbers. Steer conversations away from \"how much are you making?\"","Surround yourself with supportive, like-minded people. The community Discord exists for that, and you shouldn't let doubters discourage you.","Productivity: many members find late-night or early-morning sessions more focused, with fewer distractions."],"topics":["Calls","Mindset","Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_F0JDCnq4uwa3K/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"9/9/26 - Base44 Masterclass","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"A masterclass on Base44, an AI tool that builds website front ends and custom software, plus Q&A on faceless branding, selling a store, and AI-driven dropshipping.","points":["What Base44 does: builds the front end of your store (homepage/landing page) from a text prompt and integrates with Shopify or Wix, which handle the back end (checkout, payments).","Shopify vs. Wix: both work as the back end. Shopify has a free trial, so test both and pick what you're comfortable with.","Custom software: Base44 can build internal tools, e.g. a daily task calendar that texts reminders (via WhatsApp) and rolls unfinished tasks over, or alerts for late orders.","Product research alerts: you can have Base44 build a system that notifies you of new products in your niche every couple of weeks.","Use it to mock up design ideas quickly before building on Shopify. It's one of the more affordable and beginner-friendly AI builders.","Faceless branding: keep your face off your main brand account to avoid copycats. A separate personal account can promote the product instead.","Suppliers: AutoDS and Zendrop for dropshipping, Alibaba for bulk/B2B (covered in the school modules).","Selling a store: branded stores are worth more if you hold and scale them for at least a year. Buyers often pay a multiple of annual revenue, and a broker helps find buyers. Covered in the multi-store modules.","Growth comes from fixing small leaks: email flows, CTAs, paid ads, UGC creators and TikTok Shop all compound.","AI dropshipping: emotional/story-driven AI videos can convert well for quick revenue, but they don't build a lasting brand. Use them sparingly alongside real content.","When to avoid AI: products people consume or that require high trust are better served by real people and UGC creators."],"topics":["Calls","Website","AI tools","Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_QGqnCgVpDK0Oj/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"9/11/26 - 4 Common E-commerce Mistakes","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"A masterclass on four common e-commerce mistakes that hold stores back, plus Q&A on CTAs, supplier setup and scaling a first paid ad.","points":["Mistake 1 — over-perfecting the website: colors and banners matter less than the buying system. Put the product on the homepage with a clear Add to Cart/Buy Now CTA (take notes from Amazon). Marketing drives sales, not a perfect site.","Mistake 2 — inconsistent organic posting: organic is free advertising (a million views is worth thousands in ad spend), teaches you who your audience is, and is practice for paid ads. You don't need to show your face.","Mistake 3 — neglecting CRM: collect emails and phone numbers with pop-ups and offers. A large list lets you sell to past visitors without paying to reach them again. Klaviyo's free tier covers your first 250 contacts.","Mistake 4 — comparing yourself to others: learn from competitors, but don't chase every shiny opportunity or business model. Focus on scaling what you already have.","Test emails often land in junk. Subscribe with a separate email to see what customers receive.","Private supplier on Shopify: add them as a fulfillment location under shipping profiles (no plan upgrade needed). Give shipping access only if you trust them.","CTAs on organic posts: heavy \"buy now\" captions can limit reach. Put CTAs in your bio and comments, or reply to a top comment with \"link in bio.\"","Scaling a winning ad: ~1.2% CTR is average for e-commerce ads, so a high CTR is a great sign. Increase budget by no more than ~10% per day and keep testing creative variations that match your target audience.","Goals: focus on providing value and freedom rather than chasing a number, and set wins outside of money too (gym, faith, health)."],"topics":["Calls","Paid ads","Suppliers & shipping","Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_EAzswRQSz6ffK/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"9/25/26 - Fixing Sessions But No Sales","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"A masterclass on diagnosing a store that gets lots of sessions but few or no sales: where shoppers drop off and how to fix each leak.","points":["Lots of sessions means the product and marketing are working. The problem is between landing on the page and checking out, so find where people abandon.","Leaving the landing page: usually the site's fault. Common causes are no clear way to buy, a messy or untrustworthy look, or a surprising price. Put a big Add to Cart/Buy Now button right under the product.","Add-to-cart benchmarks: ~5–6% is normal, and 10–15% is very strong. Below 5% points to a landing page issue.","Add to cart, then no checkout: usually distraction (phone notifications) rather than price. Set up an automated add-to-cart reminder email.","Abandoned checkout: usually unexpected costs (high shipping or taxes) or no card on hand. Use an abandoned-checkout email flow (e.g. Klaviyo).","Keep shipping honest: don't inflate flat-rate shipping far above real cost. Shipping protection should be an optional toggle and no more than ~10% of the product price.","Conversion rate: ~1% is the e-commerce average, especially for ad traffic. Organic traffic often converts lower (0.1–0.35%) because it reaches a broader, less targeted audience.","Wrong audience: hundreds of daily sessions with zero sales often means you're targeting the wrong demographic.","Trust-based products may need several visits before the first purchase. Be patient and keep collecting emails and SMS contacts.","Q&A: send ad traffic to the product page if you're promoting one product, or the homepage if you're promoting several."],"topics":["Calls"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_BgfVzZdTt4eEu/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"9/29/26 - Extra Questions: Where to Start & Discipline","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"A beginner-focused extra-questions call on where to start, store setup tools and supplier basics, mixed with a lot of mindset talk about discipline, delayed gratification and avoiding burnout.","points":["Where to start: begin with \"Introduction to this Community\" in the school, choose one path (dropshipping, clothing brand, etc.), take notes, and work through the modules in order. The school covers beginner basics, and the Whop Inner Circle is the advanced mentorship.","Pick your niche first. It makes building the website much easier. Start with 2–6 products (1–2 winners plus add-ons that raise AOV).","Building the site: use the done-for-you agency (ownership transfers to your email), build it yourself on Shopify (free trial) or Wix, or use AI builders like Base44 or Lovable for the front end.","One Shopify account can hold multiple stores, and each new store needs its own domain.","CJ Dropshipping: set up a shipping/delivery profile in Shopify with the supplier's warehouse and a flat or calculated rate.","Finding winning products: search \"TikTok made me buy it\" + your niche, use a burner account that follows dropshippers, and browse Zendrop (a third-party supplier marketplace).","AI videos (Higgsfield/Kling): you can model a prompt after a video you like, but don't copy the exact prompt for the exact same product. A step-by-step guide is in the Discord announcements.","Budget: dropshipping can start with roughly $50–$100. Brands cost more (see the intro modules).","Testing: five days isn't enough to judge a product. Test longer or run a second product on a separate page.","Pricing vs. competitors: incentives (BOGO, free shipping) and better marketing can beat a lower price.","Mindset: rely on discipline over motivation, put the hard things first (delayed gratification), take Sundays off to rest, and use a 9–5 as a stepping stone to fund your business."],"topics":["Calls","Getting started","Mindset","Suppliers & shipping"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_Ys3fv9Onfx9lw/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Content Ideas That Turn Views Into Sales","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"A masterclass on proven content and creative ideas that turn views into sales (a better \"per mille,\" i.e. revenue per million views).","points":["Problem → solution: make viewers aware of the problem first, then show how your product solves it. Scrollers won't connect the dots on their own.","Show why it's worth it: \"value videos\" that justify the price, ideally replying to comments asking why it costs so much.","Funny niche content: memes or relatable humor that still lead to the product solving a problem. Good for Facebook ads and organic.","How-to/demonstration: show exactly how to use the product, especially for older audiences. You can also add a how-to section on your site.","Founder story: share the meaning behind the brand to build trust and longevity. Most useful for brands, less so for quick product tests.","Match your UGC creator to your target audience: shoppers want to picture themselves using the product, so use creators (real or AI) who look like your buyers, and test different creator types.","Turn hate comments into content: reply with videos that address the objection many silent viewers share.","Always give a clear next step: tell viewers exactly what to do (\"check our page to get yours\") and don't assume they'll find the link.","Common-mistakes content: help existing customers get results, which drives repeat purchases. Past customers convert far better than new ones.","Entertaining hooks: attention-grabbing openings (including AI characters) drive views. Pair them with a clear product message.","Ad policy tip: avoid exaggerated before/after images on Facebook, especially for weight loss or muscle-gain products.","Platforms: Facebook for paid ads, TikTok/Instagram/Snapchat Spotlight/YouTube Shorts for organic. Google Ads suits service businesses or very competitive pricing. Run AI and real content on separate accounts.","Top 4 to focus on: problem→solution, showing why it's worth it, matching your creator to your audience, and entertaining hooks."],"topics":["Calls","Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_3msoBGKL6zVJT/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"10/6/26 - Extra Questions + Meet New Coach AB","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"An extra-questions call for many new members, plus the introduction of a new coach (AB), who runs jewelry and home decor stores and will help in the Discord and on weekly calls.","points":["TikTok Shop carts: you can't email TikTok Shop cart abandoners, but running a Flash Sale (percent or flat discount for a limited time) re-surfaces the product to people who had it in their cart.","Website + TikTok Shop: having both is a strong combo. Link your site in your bio and use TikTok Shop product links in videos for one-tap buying.","Sample shipping: 1–2 weeks from overseas is normal (use a US supplier for speed). While you wait, practice AI UGC videos and study competitors.","AI video tools: Higgsfield and Arcads. A Higgsfield motion-transfer model can recreate the motion of an existing video with your own product, so you can test products quickly.","Building your store: done-for-you agency (about $150 for members), build it yourself on Shopify, or use Base44 (front end only, smarter than Lovable). Beginners usually start with Shopify (free trial).","Domains: buy after the site is built, from GoDaddy rather than Shopify (much cheaper).","Suppliers: Zendrop, CJ Dropshipping, Alibaba. Overseas suppliers are fine if you state delivery times. Google Trends shows when and where a product is trending.","Learning path: finish the school modules before the Inner Circle. Watch one category at a time, and choose your niche now.","Testing: you can test products with videos before launching the website, especially with AI organic.","Meta ads: before/after static images get restricted. Show results subtly instead (e.g. a creator with clear skin promoting a skincare serum).","No traction after 100+ posts usually means weak hooks, low quality, or content that looks too much like an ad. Share an example in the Discord for review.","Marketing tone: match your audience (professional for health/wellness, more personality for streetwear or biker products). Showing your face sometimes is fine, and AI creators can fill the rest.","Safety: be wary of anyone DMing you offering to build your store. Only work with official coaches."],"topics":["Calls"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_tMB8ufLbhbpbA/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"10/7/26 - Why Visitors Don't Check Out (Pt.1)","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"The end of a masterclass on why visitors don't check out, followed by Q&A on funnels, AI video tools, suppliers, repurposing content, seasonal products and a quick store review.","points":["Don't blame yourself for every abandoned cart: if your site checks all the boxes, many shoppers just need a reminder email or another ad touchpoint. A ~5% add-to-cart rate is a healthy minimum.","Funnel: the path a shopper follows from ad or video, to product page, to cart, to checkout. Make each step obvious.","Reviews: build genuine reviews from real customers over time.","Delegating: if family helps pack orders, pay hourly rather than per order.","Before your sample arrives: upload a product photo to Higgsfield and create AI UGC. The basic plan (~$19/mo) is enough to start, and rendering at 720p saves credits.","Do the AI content yourself first. Hire or offer revenue share only after you have proof of concept.","Flopped video? Repurpose it (new hook, re-edit in CapCut) rather than reposting the same file. Post each video across all platforms, and use snaptik.app to download without watermarks.","Alibaba is B2B and won't auto-fulfill orders. Use Zendrop, CJ Dropshipping or similar to connect a supplier to Shopify.","Store review: feature the product on the homepage above the reviews, make every section a CTA, and make the pop-up's email field clearly visible.","New coach AB was in members' shoes six months ago and specializes in paid ads and multi-store setups.","AI hook formats (like 3-2-1 countdowns) can work in ads too. Few people do this, which makes it an opportunity.","Mixing AI and real footage is a good split test. Keep separate accounts, and upload creatives to your Facebook creative library to run them from your business page.","AI video length: 30–60 seconds is the sweet spot, ~15 seconds for ads.","Seasonal products: add holiday items (e.g. Christmas) starting around early November, earlier if you're prepping ad accounts."],"topics":["Calls","Website","Organic marketing","AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_MZ1TjofxARPbk/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"10/7/26 - Why Visitors Don't Check Out (Pt.2)","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"The end of a masterclass on why visitors don't check out, followed by Q&A on funnels, AI video tools, suppliers, repurposing content, seasonal products and a quick store review.","points":["Don't blame yourself for every abandoned cart: if your site checks all the boxes, many shoppers just need a reminder email or another ad touchpoint. A ~5% add-to-cart rate is a healthy minimum.","Funnel: the path a shopper follows from ad or video, to product page, to cart, to checkout. Make each step obvious.","Reviews: build genuine reviews from real customers over time.","Delegating: if family helps pack orders, pay hourly rather than per order.","Before your sample arrives: upload a product photo to Higgsfield and create AI UGC. The basic plan (~$19/mo) is enough to start, and rendering at 720p saves credits.","Do the AI content yourself first. Hire or offer revenue share only after you have proof of concept.","Flopped video? Repurpose it (new hook, re-edit in CapCut) rather than reposting the same file. Post each video across all platforms, and use snaptik.app to download without watermarks.","Alibaba is B2B and won't auto-fulfill orders. Use Zendrop, CJ Dropshipping or similar to connect a supplier to Shopify.","Store review: feature the product on the homepage above the reviews, make every section a CTA, and make the pop-up's email field clearly visible.","New coach AB was in members' shoes six months ago and specializes in paid ads and multi-store setups.","AI hook formats (like 3-2-1 countdowns) can work in ads too. Few people do this, which makes it an opportunity.","Mixing AI and real footage is a good split test. Keep separate accounts, and upload creatives to your Facebook creative library to run them from your business page.","AI video length: 30–60 seconds is the sweet spot, ~15 seconds for ads.","Seasonal products: add holiday items (e.g. Christmas) starting around early November, earlier if you're prepping ad accounts."],"topics":["Calls","Website","Organic marketing","AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_puiE9AbXYbKR9/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"10/9/26 - Live Website Reviews (Mobile)","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"A live website review masterclass. Several members' stores are critiqued on mobile (where ~95% of shoppers are) to show what drives trust, urgency and conversions.","points":["New members: start with the introduction in Whop, then the school basics, then the Inner Circle modules.","Social proof is essential: shoppers want to see that other people use the product. Add product images with a person using or holding it, plus genuine customer reviews on both the homepage and the product page.","Make incentives impossible to miss: free shipping, bundle savings and discounts belong right next to the price and bundles, not just in a top banner. Badges (e.g. \"Most Popular\" via a bundles app) help raise AOV.","Create honest urgency: a visible sale end date or limited-time offer works better than \"free shipping always,\" which tells shoppers they can come back later. Avoid contradictory messages.","Homepage structure: hero banner with a background image and offer, then your winning products with a direct Add to Cart button. Details can live on the product page.","Clear CTAs: use \"Shop Now,\" \"Buy Now\" or \"Add to Cart\" rather than vague wording like \"Unlock yours.\"","Pop-ups: keep them centered and don't let them make the site look like a different page. Offer a clear incentive (e.g. 10–25% off for an email) instead of \"join our newsletter.\"","Pricing and titles: use familiar price endings (e.g. $89.99, not $88.95) and keep product titles short (3–4 words).","Domain: replace the default myshopify URL with a custom .com or .shop domain.","Viral but no sales: early on, conversion is low (~0.4% organic is common). Repetition across multiple videos brings viewers back to buy.","Niche timing: it's late for Halloween, so focus on Christmas or holiday-friendly angles for Q4.","Suppliers: if CJ quality is lacking, try Zendrop, TeamDrop or the private suppliers being added to the modules.","Store policies: have a clear refund/return policy on every store, and handle damaged items properly."],"topics":["Calls","Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_MbwIAukRizm3a/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"10/13/26 - Extra Questions: Scaling a Winner & Raising Ad Budgets","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"An extra-questions call covering first steps for new stores, scaling a winning product with new accounts, and safely raising Meta ad budgets.","points":["Subscription products are fine, but never auto-enroll customers; let them opt in","To remove the \"AI generated\" label on TikTok, run the video through CapCut and repost it; in your bio put your niche, a call to action and maybe a slogan","Test 2–3 products a week; once your store is built, watch competitors and start making AI videos (Higgsfield) to warm up marketing","With consistent $1K days on a seasonal winner, max out the working account (3 posts/day) and open new accounts testing different hooks","A student hit a 3.2% conversion rate after two small website changes (adding social proof)","Learning style is personal: watch everything then act, or act as you go","Meta: a 3.3% CTR is strong (average ~1.2%); scale budgets 10–30% a day (never over 50%), refresh creatives every 1–2 weeks to avoid ad fatigue, and try image ads too","Seasonal stores: push the seasonal product now; offer paid expedited shipping before Christmas. Show the product within ~5 seconds in AI ads. Keep social account names close to your store name for SEO"],"topics":["Calls","Getting started","Product research","Paid ads"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_xB2RT7DahzPIj/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"10/14/26 - When to Kill a Product (Treat It Like a Winner)","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"A masterclass on when to kill a product, why you should run a niche store, and how to give every product a real test before giving up on it.","points":["Killing a product just means you stop promoting it; you can keep it on the site to help raise AOV","Never name your store after one product (e.g. \"White Humidifier\"); build a niche store (e.g. a wellness store) so you can swap products without rebuilding your domain and logo","Don't judge on one signal (SEO, Google Trends or competitors alone); marketing is the real test","Organic: post 25–50 videos and look for comments, DMs and follows asking where to get it, not just sales","Ads: run 3–5 creatives; a CTR above ~1% means people are interested. Ignore conversion rate while testing, since that's a website problem","\"Treat it like a winner until you find out it isn't\": don't go in expecting your first product to fail, but keep testing others too","Ads need budget, organic needs posts, not time; a UGC creator posting about it is a cheap fast test","Q&A: put the product on your niche store first, then market it; suppliers like Zendrop/CJ fulfill orders automatically once connected; private suppliers vs. apps; you need a real domain, not a .myshopify.com link"],"topics":["Calls","Product research"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_JeSXVzeqQsfh8/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"10/18/26 - Bible Study (Proverbs 14-15)","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"A Sunday Bible study on Proverbs 14–15, about wisdom, hard work, taking correction and staying calm.","points":["Proverbs 14:23: \"All hard work brings profit, but mere talk leads to poverty\"; put work behind what you say","\"Whoever fears the Lord walks uprightly\"; fear God, not people","Proverbs 14:20: the poor feel alone while the rich have many friends","\"Jesus died for us in public, so we shouldn't live for Him in private\"; don't be ashamed to share your faith","Psalm 23:4, James 4:12 (don't judge others) and Jeremiah 29:11 (God has a plan) shared by members","Proverbs 15: \"A gentle answer turns away wrath\"; staying calm is real maturity; correction usually comes from love, so don't despise it","\"Better a little with the fear of the Lord than great wealth with turmoil\"; \"if you get rich without God, you're a poor man with money\"","Call times: Tuesday 5 PM, Wednesday 5:30 PM and Friday 12:15 PM Mountain (check Discord, not Zoom)"],"topics":["Calls","Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_zAhiMxxUTHoSz/","also":["Masterclass Calls · Bible Studies"]},{"kind":"lesson","title":"10/20/26 - Q&A: Higgsfield, Product Testing & New Ad Accounts","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"A Q&A call on Higgsfield and AI video, finding and testing products, competitor timing, and warming up new ad accounts.","points":["Order a sample of your main \"star\" product mainly to check quality (avoid chargebacks); you can market with Higgsfield or test with paid ads while it ships","Higgsfield is the go-to (Arcads is improving); out of credits? buy extra credits without a new plan. Use Kling 3.0 to save credits (Seedance has better motion); ~500 credits ≈ 8 videos","Mixing ~6 seconds of AI with real or UGC footage saves credits and reads as real","AI vs. filming yourself: AI is faster and looks more professional if you can't film well","Test demand with \"comment X to get yours\" captions; use burner accounts to find products","Competition: 2–3 sellers on the exact product is a good sign; check when they started posting and whether it's seasonal (winter products are about to print). Old 2024–2025 winners can be revived with AI","\"5% change\": take a winning product idea in the same niche and change it (e.g. a different projector theme); don't copy creatives. Beware hyped products that don't deliver (like projectors that only light a small spot)","New Meta ad accounts: start at ~$5/day so Meta learns you're legit (big day-one budgets look like scammers); use a business Meta account, not personal. Paid ads also boost organic profile visits","Test about 4–5 products a week max (not 35); one IG account per niche; post ~3 times a day spaced out (morning, afternoon, night)"],"topics":["Calls","Product research","AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_djCPngW4tLtKa/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"10/21/26 - Pumping Ads Without Burning Money (CTR, ROAS & Scaling)","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"A masterclass on not burning money with ads: how to read your ad numbers, which ads to scale or kill, and how to raise budget safely.","points":["Check your ad analytics every day, but for data, not emotions; never run an ad and ghost it for a week","Raise budget only 10–30% a day; jumping from $120 to $1,000/day confuses the algorithm and it shows the ad to anyone","Low CTR = bad ad. High CTR but no sales = a website problem (missing reviews, testimonials, trust)","Structure: campaigns → a couple of ad sets → 3–5 ads each; compare CTR and ROAS across them","Scale the one with good ROAS even if CTR is only decent; good ROAS with low CTR needs a harder call to action, same concept","Kill ads with both low CTR and bad ROAS (e.g. 0.25x) and move that budget to the winners; ROAS matters more than conversion rate","Give an ad 5–7 days for the learning phase, let Andromeda pick the audience, and keep it to about 5 creatives per ad set (mix images and videos); put a strong creative in its own ad set if Meta starves it","Q&A on AI video: 720p is fine if your images are sharp, upscale in CapCut instead of paying to render, Kling 4.0 and Seedance tips, and use ChatGPT for reference images"],"topics":["Calls","Paid ads","Scaling & MEO"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_esnLPd9X9MHvo/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"10/23/26 - Mindset: Skills Over Money + Store Timeline & Live Site Fix","where":"Masterclass Calls · Mastermind","summary":"A Friday mindset masterclass on building skills over chasing money, the full store timeline from niche to exit, and a live website fix for a student with high CTR but no sales.","points":["You can test a first product for about $25–50 (without a sample); if every supplier has no reviews or high shipping, move on","1,200 Higgsfield credits should be 10 ads and ~50 organic posts; use Kling 3.0 or Seedance with your own prompts and images, not pricier direct models","MEO is a real business; like a restaurant, it takes time. Master one skill before jumping to the next (meme coins, etc.)","Money is a byproduct of skill: focus on inputs, not hourly sales checks; like a seed, the roots grow before anything shows. Don't keep a plan B","Store timeline: niche/product (1 day) → niche website (30 min with Base44 to a few days) → sample (~2 weeks, while you post AI videos) → marketing (the hardest skill) → paid ads → sales → CRM emails → optimize → outsource → sell","Hiring UGC creators: tell them the deliverables but let them name the price first (ask what other brands pay them); mix a real creator clip with AI footage","Exit: buyers pay ~4–5x annual profit; AI-made content is easier to hand off than a face-driven store; a ~$18K-revenue store could aim for $30–50K before selling via Flippa","Live fix for 1,180 sessions and 0 sales (13% CTR): the site looked like a tech company. Put purchase options under the product, make images bigger and swipeable, add reviews and a photo of a real person using it, use a lighter color scheme for a 30–60-year-old female audience, and copy a proven product page layout"],"topics":["Calls","Mindset","Product research","Website"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_NFeXfGCXdtvUp/lessons/lesn_pT4mzDx6sxeZw/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"1st Bible Study (8/16/26)","where":"Masterclass Calls · Bible Studies","summary":"The first Inner Circle Bible study, on Proverbs 1–3: loving wisdom, choosing good company, and staying humble.","points":["Proverbs 1:7: \"The fear of the Lord is the beginning of knowledge, but fools despise wisdom\"; welcome advice instead of getting offended","Reading one Proverb a day lines up with the days of the month","Proverbs 1:10: if sinful people entice you, don't give in; bad company corrupts good morals, so watch who you're around (peer pressure)","Wisdom \"calls out in the street\": advice comes from friends, family, even strangers, and ignoring it has consequences","Proverbs 2: you have to really want wisdom, searching for it like hidden treasure; God guards the faithful and protects them from paths that look fun but destroy","Proverbs 3:5–6: trust in the Lord with all your heart and lean not on your own understanding; don't be wise in your own eyes","\"Wisdom is more precious than rubies\"; God's correction comes from love","Pray before taking action; God is the real mentor. Plan: Bible studies on Sundays and sharing a verse in the community each morning"],"topics":["Calls","Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_DUDRBBRKTAU4KF/app/courses/cors_7yVUOj4C3axdW/lessons/lesn_GQdrKKothVQWo/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"The 1% / 99% Analogy (WATCH)","where":"Mindset","summary":"The 1% vs. 99% analogy, advice that changed my mindset, to keep you committed.","points":["99% never take the first step; of those who start, 99% quit when it gets hard","99% end up in a life they're not happy with","The 1% keep going and don't let doubt stop them","Do what the 1% would do: stay committed and never give up"],"topics":["Mindset"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_EPD0j2av9itWEs/app/courses/cors_7xXaUTzQbs3dIBgU2TiKNW/lessons/lesn_JyadrWkflohXx/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Mastering Hooks for More Sales","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"A quick masterclass on hooks, the first 2–3 seconds of a video, using real TikTok examples and how to use them for your product.","points":["No good hook means nobody stays to watch, and no sales","Visual hooks work: setting the phone down to start, holding the product up close, a reaction shot","Put the most dramatic moment at the very start so people stay","Swap a viral video's item for your product and generate the scene with Higgsfield","Turn your scrolling into research: when a video holds your attention, figure out why","Use the Hook Generator tool for your own product"],"topics":["Organic marketing"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_fWeESNlffVbNB/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Quick Higgsfield Guide pt. 1: Prompt Tips for Better Videos","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"How to get good Higgsfield videos on fewer credits by keeping prompts simple and using starting frames.","points":["Kling 3.0, 3 separate 5-second clips at 720p, merged in CapCut","Keep prompts simple; more actions means more mistakes","Use ChatGPT to make a starting frame that already shows the details","Screenshot each clip's last frame to make the next clip's starting frame so the scene stays the same","Aim for 60 credits max per 15-second video"],"topics":["AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_fGrVEnxLVXVMx/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"Quick Higgsfield Guide pt. 2: Using Starting Frames","where":"Dropshipping · Marketing","summary":"A step-by-step demo of the starting-frame method, using a 3-clip video of a girl trying three foods.","points":["ChatGPT makes each clip's starting frame from the last frame of the previous clip","Each clip gets one simple action","Done right, a full video takes about 40 credits"],"topics":["AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_zQ2uG0X7MQhgq/lessons/lesn_cb8hdc4Y5UA4Y/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"High-Converting Announcement Banner w/ Block Generator","where":"Dropshipping · Website Maxxing","summary":"How to build a store announcement banner with Shopify's AI Block Generator that gives customers a reason to buy now.","points":["Give a real reason to buy today, like a discount with a deadline, and match the real discount","Open Sidekick and use the AI Block Generator","Add a countdown timer only for a deal that really ends","Make it sticky at the top and match your theme with a homepage screenshot"],"topics":["Website","AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_ACnEQbqVtBOxl/lessons/lesn_QhxgPyGImnVCt/"},{"kind":"lesson","title":"How to Transfer Your Claude Website to Shopify","where":"Dropshipping · Website Maxxing","summary":"How to have Claude build (or rebuild) your store and upload it to Shopify as a theme .zip file.","points":["Connect Claude to Shopify, describe the store you want, review the preview","Ask for the .zip file, then Online Store → Themes → Import → Upload zip file","It's a real Shopify theme, so you can add products and edit it"],"topics":["Website","AI tools"],"url":"https://whop.com/inner-circle-03/exp_INrF0zE2qByIYs/app/courses/cors_ACnEQbqVtBOxl/lessons/lesn_5Ymh6myhMdbZz/"}],"terms":[{"term":"E-commerce (e-com)","def_":"Selling products online through your own store (Shopify, Wix, etc.).","sec":"The business models"},{"term":"Dropshipping","def_":"A fulfillment method, not a business model: you never touch the product. When a customer orders, your supplier ships it straight to them.","sec":"The business models"},{"term":"Clothing brand","def_":"Your own brand of apparel. You usually hold inventory, design products and build a recognizable name.","sec":"The business models"},{"term":"MEO (multi e-com operations)","def_":"Building online stores as digital assets: grow a store's profits, then outsource it or sell it. Often called \"the real estate of e-commerce.\" Master one store first.","sec":"The business models"},{"term":"Ringchaining","def_":"Connecting your stores so they work like one agency, e.g. cross-promoting your other stores to the email list you've built.","sec":"The business models"},{"term":"POK","def_":"Turning your store into a system other people can run, built on SOPs, so it doesn't depend on you.","sec":"The business models"},{"term":"SOP (standard operating procedure)","def_":"A written, step-by-step instruction for a task (e.g. \"order not received\") so anyone on your team can do it the same way.","sec":"The business models"},{"term":"Exit / selling a store","def_":"Selling your store to a buyer, often through a broker. Stores are usually valued as a multiple of their profit or revenue.","sec":"The business models"},{"term":"Niche","def_":"The specific group of people and problem your store focuses on (e.g. home fitness, pet owners, college students).","sec":"Niches & products"},{"term":"Types of niches","def_":"Problem-solving (fixes a pain point), passion/hobby (gym, gaming, fishing), audience-based (moms, students, cold-climate drivers), evergreen (sells all year) and trending (hot right now, shorter life).","sec":"Niches & products"},{"term":"Winning product","def_":"A product with demand but not overwhelming competition. It solves a problem or excites people, has good margins, ships easily and looks good on video.","sec":"Niches & products"},{"term":"Evergreen product","def_":"Something people buy all year, not just during one season or trend.","sec":"Niches & products"},{"term":"Trending product","def_":"A product that's currently viral. Being early gives you an edge; being late means a crowded market.","sec":"Niches & products"},{"term":"Social proof","def_":"Signs that other people already use and like the product: reviews, testimonials, creator videos, customer photos.","sec":"Niches & products"},{"term":"Sample","def_":"Ordering one unit from your supplier first so you can check the quality and film content with it.","sec":"Niches & products"},{"term":"Product research","def_":"Finding products to sell, often by scrolling TikTok, studying competitors and checking the Facebook Ad Library.","sec":"Niches & products"},{"term":"Supplier","def_":"The company that makes or stocks your product (e.g. Zendrop, CJ Dropshipping, AutoDS, Alibaba).","sec":"Suppliers & fulfillment"},{"term":"B2C vs. B2B","def_":"B2C: the supplier ships straight to your customer (dropshipping). B2B: the supplier ships stock to you first, and you ship to customers.","sec":"Suppliers & fulfillment"},{"term":"MOQ (minimum order quantity)","def_":"The smallest number of units a supplier will sell you in one order. It's often negotiable.","sec":"Suppliers & fulfillment"},{"term":"3PL (third-party logistics)","def_":"A company that stores, packs and ships your orders for a fee, usually once order volume gets high.","sec":"Suppliers & fulfillment"},{"term":"Pre-order","def_":"Selling a product before it's in stock. State the shipping timeline clearly.","sec":"Suppliers & fulfillment"},{"term":"Chargeback","def_":"When a customer disputes a charge with their bank and the money is pulled back. Good customer service prevents them.","sec":"Suppliers & fulfillment"},{"term":"Organic marketing (OM)","def_":"Free marketing: posting content on TikTok, Instagram, YouTube Shorts and other platforms. It builds awareness and shows you who your audience is. Do this before paid ads.","sec":"Marketing"},{"term":"Paid ads","def_":"Paying a platform (Meta/Facebook, TikTok, etc.) to show your ads to a target audience. Best once you know your audience and have content that works organically.","sec":"Marketing"},{"term":"Creative","def_":"The video or image used in your marketing or ads.","sec":"Marketing"},{"term":"Hook","def_":"The first 1–3 seconds of a video, which decide whether people keep watching.","sec":"Marketing"},{"term":"CTA (call to action)","def_":"Telling the viewer what to do next: \"link in bio,\" \"shop now,\" \"comment below.\"","sec":"Marketing"},{"term":"UGC (user-generated content)","def_":"Videos of real people (customers or paid creators) using your product. AI UGC uses AI-generated creators.","sec":"Marketing"},{"term":"Influencer / micro-influencer","def_":"A creator with an audience who promotes your product. Micro-influencers (about 10K–100K followers) often get the best engagement.","sec":"Marketing"},{"term":"Engagement","def_":"Likes, comments, shares, saves and follows: how much people interact with your content.","sec":"Marketing"},{"term":"Algorithm","def_":"The system each platform uses to decide who sees your content. Consistent posting and strong engagement help.","sec":"Marketing"},{"term":"Target audience","def_":"The specific people most likely to buy your product (age, interests, problems).","sec":"Marketing"},{"term":"Impressions","def_":"How many times your ad was shown.","sec":"Paid ad numbers"},{"term":"CPM","def_":"Cost per 1,000 impressions: what it costs to show your ad 1,000 times.","sec":"Paid ad numbers"},{"term":"CPC","def_":"Cost per click: what you pay each time someone clicks your ad.","sec":"Paid ad numbers"},{"term":"CTR (click-through rate)","def_":"The percentage of people who saw your ad and clicked it. Around 2% or more is solid.","sec":"Paid ad numbers"},{"term":"CPA / cost per result","def_":"What each sale (or other result) costs you in ad spend.","sec":"Paid ad numbers"},{"term":"ROAS (return on ad spend)","def_":"Revenue divided by ad spend. A 2.0 ROAS means $2 in sales for every $1 spent.","sec":"Paid ad numbers"},{"term":"Break-even","def_":"The point where you make $0 profit: your price minus product cost, shipping and fees. If your CPA is above it, you're losing money.","sec":"Paid ad numbers"},{"term":"Frequency","def_":"How many times, on average, each person has seen your ad.","sec":"Paid ad numbers"},{"term":"Campaign / ad set / ad","def_":"Meta's structure: the campaign sets the goal, the ad set sets the audience and budget, and the ad is the creative.","sec":"Paid ad numbers"},{"term":"ABO / CBO","def_":"ABO: budget set per ad set. CBO: budget set at the campaign level, and Meta spreads it across ad sets.","sec":"Paid ad numbers"},{"term":"Scaling","def_":"Increasing spend on what's working. Raise budgets slowly (about 20–30% at a time), never double overnight.","sec":"Paid ad numbers"},{"term":"Front end / back end","def_":"Front end: what customers see (homepage, product pages). Back end: checkout, payments and orders (e.g. Shopify, Wix).","sec":"Your store"},{"term":"Landing page","def_":"The page people land on from an ad or link. It should make buying easy.","sec":"Your store"},{"term":"Sessions","def_":"Visits to your store.","sec":"Your store"},{"term":"Conversion rate (CVR)","def_":"The percentage of visitors who buy. Around 1% is average for e-commerce.","sec":"Your store"},{"term":"Add-to-cart rate","def_":"The percentage of visitors who add something to their cart.","sec":"Your store"},{"term":"Sales funnel","def_":"The customer's journey from first seeing your product to checking out, and coming back.","sec":"Your store"},{"term":"AOV (average order value)","def_":"The average amount a customer spends per order. Raise it with bundles, upsells and more products.","sec":"Your store"},{"term":"Bundle","def_":"Selling products together (or multiples of one product) as a package deal.","sec":"Your store"},{"term":"Upsell / post-purchase upsell","def_":"Offering a related or upgraded item before or right after checkout.","sec":"Your store"},{"term":"Shipping protection","def_":"An optional small fee a customer can add to guarantee their package is replaced if lost.","sec":"Your store"},{"term":"Profit margin","def_":"The share of each sale you keep after costs.","sec":"Your store"},{"term":"CRM","def_":"Your customer contact list (emails and phone numbers) and the system you use to manage it.","sec":"Email, SMS & customers"},{"term":"Flow","def_":"An automated series of emails or texts triggered by an action (welcome, abandoned cart, post-purchase, win-back).","sec":"Email, SMS & customers"},{"term":"Abandoned cart","def_":"When someone adds to cart but doesn't buy. An abandoned-cart flow reminds them to finish.","sec":"Email, SMS & customers"},{"term":"Klaviyo / Postscript","def_":"Popular tools for email (Klaviyo) and SMS (Postscript) marketing.","sec":"Email, SMS & customers"},{"term":"Retention","def_":"Getting customers to come back and buy again.","sec":"Email, SMS & customers"},{"term":"CLV (customer lifetime value)","def_":"How much a customer spends with you over time: AOV × purchases per year × years as a customer.","sec":"Email, SMS & customers"},{"term":"Shopify / Wix","def_":"Platforms for building and running your online store.","sec":"Tools you'll hear about"},{"term":"Base44 / Lovable","def_":"AI tools that build website front ends (and custom tools) and connect to Shopify or Wix.","sec":"Tools you'll hear about"},{"term":"Claude / ChatGPT","def_":"AI assistants for writing scripts, emails, product descriptions and content ideas.","sec":"Tools you'll hear about"},{"term":"Higgsfield","def_":"An AI video tool for making UGC-style product videos.","sec":"Tools you'll hear about"},{"term":"Facebook Ad Library","def_":"A free tool that shows which ads any brand is currently running.","sec":"Tools you'll hear about"}],"topics":["Getting started","Mindset","Product research","Website","Organic marketing","Paid ads","AI tools","Email & SMS","Suppliers & shipping","Scaling & MEO","Clothing brand","Calls"]};
